{"id": "219571", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219571", "title": "Kumbh Mela", "text": "The Kumbh Mela (Sanskrit: \u0915\u0941\u092e\u094d\u092d\u092e\u0947\u0932\u093e \"Kumbhamel\u0101\", Hindi: \u0915\u0941\u0902\u092d \u092e\u0947\u0932\u093e \"Kumbh Mel\u0101\") is the world's largest gathering of humans. It is a Hindu pilgrimage observed every six or twelve years at one of the four locations across India.\nThe festival has been listed as a UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage since 2017.\nThe most recent Kumbh Mela took place at Prayagraj in 2025. The next one will be hosted at Haridwar and Nashik in 2027.\nTypes.\nThere are two types of Kumbh Mela:\nThe Purn Kumbh Mela in Prayagraj is called \"Maha Kumbh Mela\" (Great Kumbh Mela). Contrary to popular belief, it is repeated every 12 years instead of 144.\nDepending on the location, the Kumbh Mela is known by different names:\nThe Prayag Kumbh Mela is held nearly three years after the Haridwar one, and nearly three years before the Ujjain one. The Nashik one is celebrated either in the same year as the Ujjain year or a year earlier.\nSchedules.\nThe Kumbh Mela follows a combination of Hindu solar and lunar calendars, so the exact dates may vary every six or twelve years, except the \"Sankranti\" dates. It has three dates for ritual bathing.\nRituals.\nBathing is the central ritual in the Kumbh Mela. During new moon (\"am\u0101v\u0101sy\u0101\" or \"am\u0101vasy\u0101\"), the Hindu pilgrims welcome and wait for the 13 sadhus to bathe first, which is called \"sh\u0101h\u012b sn\u0101n\" (royal bathing). It is marked by a procession of banners, flags, elephants, horses and musicians, along with the naked monks (\"n\u0101g\u0101 s\u0101dhu\"). Large crowds gather and cheer for this procession of monks, and once the bathing is done, the water becomes open for bathing by the pilgrims.\nMany pilgrims fast and pray as part of their traditions. Some fast for an entire day, while others eat once a day.\nDo.\nThe various activities organised during the Kumbh include religious and spiritual discourses (\"pravachan\"), devotional singing (\"k\u012brtan\"), classical dance and music performances, boat rides, laser shows, art galleries (\"kal\u0101gr\u0101m\") and many others.\nEat.\nThe Kumbh Mela is strictly vegetarian as per Hindu traditions. Volunteers from charitable donations prepare community meals (\"mah\u0101pras\u0101d\") for pilgrims. Besides, there are multiple food stalls with delicacies from different parts of India.\nDrink.\nAlcohol is strictly forbidden, as it's a place of sacred rituals and holy dip.\nSleep.\nMany pilgrims tend to sleep at different camps. However, you may choose to book an accommodation, since all four cities have decent hotel options.\nStay safe.\nAs one of the world's largest gatherings, the Kumbh Mela is very unsafe for those who are not experienced to large crowds, as well as for children and seniors. Deadly stampedes during the Kumbh are commonplace: the enormous crowds frequently panic and defy police. Be aware of your surroundings, especially around the railway station, bridges and rivers' edge.\nStay healthy.\nBasic sanitation is lacking in many areas. Pilgrims not using toilets, flooding, and lack of drainage make diarrhea outbreaks common. Drink and use only bottled water and bring anti-diarrhoea medication.\nRegardless of its sanctity, avoid drinking the river water directly. The rivers of respective locations have been very polluted for decades, especially Ganges and Yamuna rivers.\nRespect.\nNo matter how much you are tempted to click photos of the monks and pilgrims, avoid photographing individuals without consent.\nRelated festivals.\nDue to the immense popularity of the original Kumbh Mela, there have been attempts to promote other pilgrimage sites by organising Kumbh-style festivals.\nRelated annual festivals include:"}
{"id": "219594", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219594", "title": "Sennariolo", "text": "Sennariolo is a village in Oristano province.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe SS 292 Bosa-Oristano allows you to reach Sennariolo.\nBy bus.\nThe ARST 703 bus line stops in Sennariolo.\nGet around.\nGiven its small size, Sennariolo can easily be explored on foot."}
{"id": "219595", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219595", "title": "Bimbilla", "text": "Bimbilla is a historic and culturally rich town serving as the capital of the Nanumba North Municipality in Volta Basin.\nUnderstand.\nBimbilla is a historic crossroads town and the administrative capital of the Nanumba North Municipality in Ghana's Northern Region. Situated in the eastern belt near the Oti River, it serves as a raw, authentic window into northern Ghanaian life. While it functions primarily as a practical transit hub for backpackers moving between Tamale and the Volta basin, it rewards curious travelers with its legendary local hospitality and deep-rooted cultural heritage.\nHistory and royal lineage\nThe Nanumba Kingdom: The town serves as the traditional seat of the Nanumba people, who established a sovereign kingdom in this territory centuries ago.\nOrigins of the name \nLocal lore attributes the state's founding to Naa \u014amantambo. His brother, the founder of the Dagomba state, pointed southward to guide him. This gesture gave birth to the Dagbani terms \"Na\" (King) and \"Nua\" (Hand), which combined to form Nanumba.\nTraditional governance\nBimbilla is governed by the Bimbilla Naa, the supreme overlord of the Nanung traditional council. The local royalty is historically divided between two competing family factions: the Ba\u014b-Yili and the Gbu\u0263ima-Yili.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nVia Tamale Airport: For a faster trip from Accra or Kumasi, fly into Tamale Airport. Once you land, take a taxi to the main Tamale transport station, where you can easily catch a direct trotro or shared car to Bimbilla.\nBy car.\nBimbilla is mostly reached by road along the Eastern Corridor (N2 highway). Most travellers head down from northern transport hubs like Tamale and Yendi, or come up from the Oti Region.\nBy bus.\nBig national bus lines like VIP Jeoun Transport and Intercity STC run routes up the Eastern Corridor from southern cities like Accra or Kumasi. When booking a ticket on a bus heading towards Yendi, simply ask the driver to let you off at the main station in Bimbilla.\nYou can also find a local minibus (trotro) or a shared Sienta at the main stations in Tamale. The route takes you east through Yendi before heading straight down south to Bimbilla. From Yendi is the easiest and most reliable local connection. Trotros run back and forth directly between Yendi and Bimbilla throughout the day. From the south, daily trotros head north from Dambai and Oti-Damanko across the regional border. Travel times on this southern route can change, depending on road construction projects along the N2 highway.\nGet around.\nBy moto-taxi (Mahama Can Do / Pragya) motorized tricycles: These small, three-wheeled passenger vehicles are the most popular way to move around. Locals often call them Pragya or Mahama Can Do. They are highly affordable and can be hailed from almost any roadside.\nMotorbike taxis: Standard motorbikes are also widely available for quick trips. Always negotiate and agree on the fare with the rider before hopping on the back.\nOn foot: The central town area, local markets, and nearby spots are close together. Walking is a great way to take in the town's atmosphere, though it is best to carry water and a hat to protect yourself from the midday sun.\nBy shared TaxiTown taxis: A few local taxis run fixed routes between the town center and outer neighborhoods or nearby villages. You will share the ride with other passengers, making it a very cheap option.\nSee.\nBimbilla is less about typical tourist landmarks and more about experiencing traditional West African culture firsthand.\nDo.\nwith regards to festivals, the people of Bimbilla celebrate;\nBuy.\nBimbilla is an agricultural hub in Northern Ghana, famously called an epicenter for crops like yams, maize, beans, and groundnuts. Trading centers on a couple of main traditional local markets and a specialized commodity hub\nEat.\nFood in Bimbilla is simple, hearty, and highly affordable. The local diet heavily features the region's agricultural staple the yam.\nDrink.\nPeople in Bimbilla enjoy a mix of traditional Northern Ghanaian beverages, popular local juices, and modern soft drinks. Because the Nanumba people are predominantly Muslim, non-alcoholic, grain-based, and spice-infused drinks are the most widely consumed daily beverages.\nThe most common drinks in Bimbilla include:\nSleep.\nAccommodation in Bimbilla is basic and caters mostly to traveling workers, NGO staff, and budget backpackers. Do not expect luxury amenities like swimming pools, but you can find clean, functional rooms.\nGo next.\nBinda\nNasamba\nNakpayili\nWulensi\nKpandai\nYendi"}
{"id": "219599", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219599", "title": "Portland (Oregon)/Northeast", "text": "Northeast Portland comprises the neighborhoods east of the Willamette River and north of Burnside Street and Interstate 84. Alberta Street and several other trendy neighborhoods are here, as is the Convention Center and the Airport. \nUnderstand.\nLocated along Broadway and Sandy Blvd northeast of downtown, Hollywood is a commercial district for the nearby neighborhoods and home to the Hollywood Theater, a historic non-profit theater with an ornate facade showing a variety of independent, second run, and classic films as well as original programming and interactive events. There is also a popular Saturday farmers market in the neighborhood during the warm months.\nTo the north of the former between MLK Blvd and 30th Avenue, Alberta Street has much the same feel as Hawthorne Blvd; a counter-culture/bohemian community that's becoming popular with yuppies. Alberta is home to Last Thursday, said by many locals to be the alternative to First Thursday in the Pearl District and also featuring wine tasting and gallery openings, along with street vending and performance artists. The neighborhood between Alberta Street and Broadway is known as Irvington, and contains many historic Craftsman homes.\nOther neighborhoods to explore include: St. Johns in North Portland featuring the gorgeous St. Johns Bridge, Mississippi Avenue and North Williams.\nGet in and around.\nSee Portland (Oregon)#Get around for getting in from elsewhere in the city. You can also get in from Vancouver across the Columbia River in Washington state. Some Vancouver bus lines cross into Eastside Portland; #60 to Jantzen Beach Center and Delta Park/Vanport, #65 to Parkrose/Sumner Transit Center and #67 to the airport. From each of those, you can switch to a MAX train to get further into Eastside (and on to other parts of Portland).\nThe international airport is in this part of the city.\nPlaces across the Willamette River can be accessed on foot (almost all bridges have sidewalks) or streetcar from Downtown or parts of the northwest. The other MAX lines take you from Downtown and Chinatown across the Steel Bridge. From there, the Yellow Line continues north along the North Interstate and North Denver Avenues up to the Expo Center. The Green, Red and Blue lines go east along the Banfield Freeway to the Gateway Transit Center, stopping at the Rose Quarter, the Convention Center, 7th, 11th, 42nd, 60th and 82nd Avenues. From there they fan out; the Red Line north to the airport, the Blue Line further east to Gresham, and the Green Line south to Clackamas.\nThere's also a fairly dense bus network (network map) taking you to places where rail-based public transit won't.\nWith long distances between places, driving can almost certainly also a good option.\nThe City of Portland has created several maps of bike routes and bike lanes."}
{"id": "219600", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219600", "title": "Portland (Oregon)/Southeast", "text": "Southeast Portland comprises the neighborhoods east of the Willamette River and south of Burnside Street and Interstate 84. Hawthorne Boulevard and several other trendy neighborhoods are here. \nUnderstand.\nHawthorne Blvd, which runs east-west across the river from Downtown, has a broad selection of shops including a menagerie of vintage goods at the House of Vintage and the ornate Bagdad Theater Pub, and is a center of the counter-culture/bohemian community which is dissipating to make way for a variety of upscale businesses. Neighborhoods in or near the Hawthorne District include Buckman, Hosford-Abernethy, Sunnyside, Richmond and Mt. Tabor. The nearby Belmont Street is also worth a visit if you are in the neighborhood, with a similar - if smaller - array of shops and attractions. \nOther neighborhoods to explore include: quaint Sellwood, Inner Southeast a loosely defined neighborhood where bars and music venues have been cropping up amidst the industrial landscape, Foster-Powell, East Burnside and Stark, Division and Clinton Street, and\nGet in and around.\nSee Portland (Oregon)#Get around for getting in from elsewhere in the city. \nPlaces across the Willamette River can be accessed on foot (almost all bridges have sidewalks) or streetcar from Downtown or parts of the northwest. The Orange MAX line takes you to the southern parts of the Eastside along the Willamette River, stopping at OMSI, Clinton, Rhine, Holgate, Bybee and Tacoma. \nThe other MAX lines take you from Downtown and Chinatown across the Steel Bridge. The Green, Red and Blue lines go east along the Banfield Freeway to the Gateway Transit Center, stopping at the Rose Quarter, the Convention Center, 7th, 11th, 42nd, 60th and 82nd Avenues. From there they fan out; the Red Line north to the airport, the Blue Line further east to Gresham, and the Green Line south to Clackamas. The Blue Line stops on Burnside at East 102nd, 122nd, 148th, 172nd, 181st and 188th. The Green Line stops along the 205 at Main, Division, Powell, Holgate, Foster and Flavel.\nThere's also a fairly dense bus network (network map) taking you to places where rail-based public transit won't.\nWith long distances between places, driving can almost certainly also a good option.\nThe City of Portland has created several maps of bike routes and bike lanes.\nSleep.\nSoutheast Portland is relatively devoid of hotels; more hotels are across the 84 in Northeast Portland (esp. by the airport and Convention Center), across the Willamette in Downtown Portland, or just south on I-205 in Clackamas."}
