{"id": "219615", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219615", "title": "Deoband", "text": "Deoband (\u0926\u0947\u0935\u092c\u0902\u0926 \"Devband\") in northern India is famous for the Deobandi movement, an Islamic revivalist movement."}
{"id": "219616", "revid": "1812", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219616", "title": "Govardhan", "text": "Govardhan (\u0917\u094b\u0935\u0930\u094d\u0927\u0928) or Giriraj (\u0917\u093f\u0930\u093f\u0930\u093e\u091c \"Girir\u0101j\") is a key pilgrimage site in Uttar Pradesh, India. Each year, Hindus make pilgrimage to Govardhan from different parts of India and beyond.\nUnderstand.\nAs the legend goes, the people of Vrindavan traditionally worships Indra as the lord of thunder. However, Krishna suggests them to honour the Govardhan hill instead as it supplies grass for cattle. This angers Indra, and he sends and intense rainstorm. Then Krishna picks up the hill and holds it above his head for seven days to protect the people from Indra's wrath.\nSince Krishna declares that he and Govardhan is no different, the Vallabhacharya sect worships the hill, along with the Saligrama.\nDo.\nGovardhan Puja is the main festival in this village. It commemorates the legendary lifting of the hill. Other festivals include Guru Purnima and of course Diwali."}
{"id": "219627", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219627", "title": "La Encrucijada Biosphere Reserve", "text": "La Encrucijada Biosphere Reserve is a protected coastal wetland area in Chiapas, in southern Mexico. It is a series of inland lagoons connected by inland waterways and includes the tallest mangroves in North America, stretching to a height of over 35 meters. \nUnderstand.\nThe protected area covers almost 145,000 hectares along 140 km of Pacific coastline.\nTourist information.\nOfficial information about visiting the reserve is available via the CONANP web site.\nBackground information about the site's ecological importance is available from UNESCO.\nHistory.\nThe reserve was designated a wetland of international importance and added to the list of Ramsar sites in 1996. In 2006, it was designated as a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve. The land is managed for the federal government by CONANP (\"Comisi\u00f3n Nacional de \u00c1reas Naturales Protegidas\").\nFlora and fauna.\nThe reserve is rich in species diversity with several important plant habitats and numerous animals, including more than 5 dozen kinds of reptile and amphibian, 300 bird species, and 100 mammal species (including 68 species of bat) and Mexico's northernmost colony of spider monkeys.\nBesides spider monkeys, mammals include several jungle cats including the reclusive jaguar. \nCrocodiles and caimans float around the lagoons and channels. Turtles are very common in the reserve as some of the Pacific beaches are used as nesting areas and the incoming rivers (there are 11 of them) are home to several species of freshwater turtle. Iguanas and basilisks can be found throughout the region as can a variety of both venomous and non-venomous snakes.\nThe mangrove swamps cover more than 1/5 of the reserve and include five species of mangrove\u02d0 \"mangla colorado\", \"black mangrove\", \"red mangrove\", \"buttonwood mangrove\", and \"white mangrove\". \nThe reserve is known for having Mexico's only Guiana chestnut (\"Pachira aquatica\") forest. It grows in the reserve on a kind of \"floating island\" of built-up organic matter. The trees display beautiful wispy flowers and produces nuts that are eaten by some people, but the plant is most often sold to garden shops, where it is also called the \"money tree\".\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport with commercial flights is in Tapachula.\nFrom Tapachula, take a taxi or bus northwest of the city via federal highway MEX-200 towards the town of Acapetahua, which is about 80 km from Tapachula. The reserve actually begins just 30 km outside Tapachula and covers all of the wetlands that lie between highway MEX-200 and the Pacific Ocean, but there are few public access points. Tour guides hired in Tapachula often know backcountry access points or can take you into the wetlands in small boats called \"pangas\". Tour guides can also be hired in Acapetahua.\nFees and permits.\nCONANP charges visitors a daily entrance fee of M$125.\nSleep.\nBecause of the environmentally sensitive nature of the reserve, campgrounds and backcountry camping are not generally permitted. Local indigenous communities operate some rustic lodging camps where visitors can find rooms, meals, and guide services.\nLodging.\nRustic lodages are available in El Casta\u00f1o, La Palma, and Barra de Zacapulco. "}
{"id": "219629", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219629", "title": "Montolieu", "text": "Montolieu is in Aude. In the foothills of the Montagne Noire near Carcassonne, is a charming village devoted to books and the arts. Its picturesque lanes lead to numerous bookshops, galleries, artists\u2019 studios and the Mus\u00e9e des Arts et M\u00e9tiers du Livre, dedicated to printing, typography and traditional bookmaking. Beyond its cultural appeal, Montolieu offers attractive stone houses, quiet viewpoints and a relaxed atmosphere surrounded by vineyards, wooded hills and the landscapes of the Cabard\u00e8s. It is an excellent destination for book lovers, art enthusiasts and travellers seeking an authentic rural village \nGet in.\nBy bus.\nIt's served by Carcassonne line \u00a0D\u00a0 (Carcassonne - Saissac)\nBy car.\nI's around 20 min drive from carcassonne. From there take \u00a0D6113\u00a0 west, 1 km after Pezens take departamental road \u00a0D629\u00a0 until Montolieu.\nGet around.\nIt's a small village, that has to be seen by foot.\nBuy.\nBookstores.\nMontolieu is famed for hosting many bookstores where buy, sell, admire, etc. I also has a museum."}
{"id": "219650", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219650", "title": "Bagni di Lucca", "text": "Bagni di Lucca is a small spa town in the Lima and Serchio valleys of Tuscany, in the province of Lucca. It is the largest comune by area in Italy, made up of villages scattered through the wooded hills, and is known for its thermal springs, its 19th-century foreign community and the river gorges around it.\nUnderstand.\nThe town grew around natural hot springs that were already in use in the Middle Ages. In the 19th century it became a fashionable resort for English, Russian and other European visitors, and Byron, Shelley and Elizabeth Barrett Browning all spent time here. Traces of that period remain in the English cemetery and the Anglican church, and in the building of the casino, which was one of the earliest in Europe and is no longer in operation.\nToday Bagni di Lucca is quiet and much less visited than the coast or the main Tuscan art cities. The comune covers a very large and mostly mountainous territory, so villages are spread far apart and the surroundings are more forest and river valley than town.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom Lucca take the SS12 north along the Serchio valley, about 27 km and 40 minutes. From Florence the drive is about 1 hour 30 minutes via the A11 to Lucca and then the SS12.\nBy train.\nThe Lucca to Aulla regional line stops at Bagni di Lucca. Trains run several times a day and the trip from Lucca takes around 30 minutes. The station is in Fornoli, a short distance from the main thermal area.\nBy bus.\nRegional buses connect Lucca with Bagni di Lucca and continue up the Serchio valley.\nGet around.\nThe comune is spread over many separate villages, including Ponte a Serraglio, La Villa, Fornoli and Bagni Caldi, and a car is useful for moving between them. Within each village distances are short and walkable.\nDo.\nThermal bathing is the traditional activity, with natural hot springs and steam grottoes carved into the rock. The Lima river running through the town is used for rafting and packrafting between spring and autumn, and several narrow canyons in the surrounding hills are used for canyoning, which combines walking, swimming and short descents on rope. Marked walking trails lead from the villages up towards the Apuan Alps.\nEat.\nLocal cooking follows the Garfagnana tradition, with chestnut flour, farro, wild mushrooms and game appearing on most menus. Much of the eating out happens at agriturismi scattered through the hills rather than in the villages themselves, and many of them serve set menus using their own produce. Booking ahead is usually necessary.\nSleep.\nAccommodation is mostly small hotels, agriturismi and restored houses in the surrounding hamlets."}
{"id": "219653", "revid": "2435153", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219653", "title": "Saboba", "text": "Saboba is the administrative capital of the Saboba District in the northeastern corridor of Ghana in the Volta Basin. It is located along the international border with Togo. Saboba is widely recognized as a major cultural headquarters for the Konkomba people.\nUnderstand.\nThe Saboba district has a total population of 95,683 people (47,172 males and 48,511 females) based on the official 2021 national census. Saboba has a land coverage of about 1,802 square kilometers. \nThe people of Saboba speaks Likpakpaaln with the majority accent being the Bichaabob."}
{"id": "219654", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219654", "title": "Lawra", "text": "Lawra is a city in the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nLawra, a city of 58,000 (2001), is the capital of the Lawra Municipal District in the Upper West Region of Ghana. It is on the border with Burkina Faso. It has two seasons which are the dry and rainy seasons.\nThis town is primarily home to the Dagaaba and Brifor people, who heavily rely on agriculture and livestock farming.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom Wa to Lawra covers about 85 to 89 km along the main Wa-Lawra road. The trip takes 2-2\u00bd hours by car or shared transport. You can catch commercial minibuses, shared taxis, or private cars from the main Wa station. Vehicles leave regularly once filled. The paved road passes through towns like Kaleo and Charia, making for a straightforward drive.\nBy bus.\nDirect long-distance buses from Accra and Kumasi are provided by Royal VVIP / VIP Jeoun, Intercity STC and Metro Mass Transit.\nBy tro-tro/shared taxi.\nHead to the Wa Main Transport Yard and ask for the \"Lawra line\". Shared sedan cars and minibuses fill up and depart constantly throughout the day. From the north or near the Burkina Faso border, hopped-on tro-tros head south down the main road right into the Lawra town center market hub.\nGet around.\nBy motorized tricycle (pragya / mahama camboo).\nThese drive along the main roads picking up multiple passengers. You pay a very cheap, fixed local fare per drop.\nOn foot.\nLawra's main landmarks, such as the Lawra Market, the Lawra Naa\u2019s Palace, and local guesthouses, are clustered closely together. Walking is completely safe during the day, and it gives you the best chance to see local weavers and interact with community members.\nBy tro-tro and shared taxi.\nIf you want to leave Lawra town to visit nearby spots like Babile (for the Mushroom Rocks) or Nandom, head to the central station or wait along the main Lawra-Hamile Road. Minibuses (tro-tros) and old shared sedan cars run fixed routes and leave only when they are completely full.\nSee.\nSome places in Lawra that are worth seeing are the Lawra Naa Palace that has intricate carvings, the Erimon Crocodile Pond and the Kobine Festival grounds.\nDo.\nWalking along with the crocodiles, visiting the chief's palace, taking part in the Kobine celebration.\nBuy.\nThe Lawra market and shopping malls at the road side are places to get most provisions to buy.\nConnect.\nMTN Ghana is the best and most reliable telecommunications network for mobile data and phone calls in Lawra. You can get a basic signal from other carriers, though MTN Ghana has the widest infrastructure presence in this part of the Upper West Region.MTN Provides the most stable 3G and 4G internet coverage across Lawra town. It is highly reliable for checking emails, streaming, and using social media. MTN also sets up customer service \"Experiential Centres\" during major events like the local Kobine Festival.\nTelecel (formerly Vodafone),offers an adequate signal for basic voice calling and texting in the main town area. However, the internet data connection is often weak or non-existent once you step out into the surrounding rural farming communities.\nAT (formerly AirtelTigo), has a much weaker network footprint here. It is generally not recommended for travelers relying on consistent data in Lawra.\nIf mobile data is running slow or costs too much, the non-profit organization Action Through Enterprise (ATE) runs an Inclusion Centre in Lawra. They have a dedicated Technology and Learning Zone that provides free Wi-Fi and computers to the local community.Because MTN has the strongest signal, MTN Mobile Money (MoMo) is widely used for business transactions and buying items in the local Lawra markets.You may notice slower internet speeds during peak hours (like late evenings) when many locals log online simultaneously.\nGo next.\nFrom Lawra, you can easily continue your journey to Jirapa, Nandom, or back down toward Wa to explore the Wechiau Hippo Sanctuary, Gwollu Slave Defense Wall, Sankana and Bulenga caves and the Babile Mushroom Rocks. These nearby destinations offer a mix of high-end resorts, historic cathedrals, and exciting wildlife safaris."}
{"id": "219656", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219656", "title": "La Sepultura Biosphere Reserve", "text": "La Sepultura Biosphere Reserve is a protected natural area in the state of Chiapas in southern Mexico. It covers an area of forested mountain slopes in the Sierra Madre del Sur mountain range known locally as the \"Sierra Madre de Chiapas\". It is an area of rugged mountain landscapes that provides habitat to numerous plant and animal species including hundreds of birds and more than 50 types of mammals, from ferocious big cats to small forest creatures.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a large park, covering an area of about 167,000 hectares.\nHistory.\nThe Mexican government began setting aside conservation zones in the area in 1995. The reserve was formally adopted by the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves in 2006. It is managed under a cooperative agreement between local communities and the federal government. It is administered by CONANP (\"Comisi\u00f3n Nacional de \u00c1reas Naturales Protegidas\"), the federal agency that manages most federal parks and natural areas.\nLandscape.\nThe reserve consists of steep mountain slopes covered in varying types of forest depending on the elevation and other factors. At lower elevations are deciduous forests and tropical dry forests. At mid elevations, the forests become more tropical and remain green year-round. Mid-level forests include areas of cloud forest. \nFlora and fauna.\nTrees include pines and oaks with native cedars and other trees. \nSix of the seven feline species native to Mexico live in La Sepultura. These include the jaguar, the puma, and the ocelot as well as smaller cats like jaguarundi, margay, and ocilla. Quite a few large to mid-size mammals constitute prey for these predators. Tapir, red brocket deer, white tailed deer, and peccary are all hunted by predators, as are smaller mammals like the cacomistle, kinkajou, river otter, tamandua, anteater, and tayra. Several kinds of monkeys can be found swinging from the canopy branches including spider monkeys and howler monkeys.\nAn estimated 300 kinds of birds can be spotted, some of which are year-round residents, while others are migratory and only spotted during a few months of the year. Resident birds include colorful tropical species like parrots, toucans, trogons, and quetzals. Some are carnivorous predatory birds like vultures, hawks, owls, and raptors. Many of the estimated 90 species of migratory birds are northern woodland songbirds.\nClimate.\nThe area has a constant warm, temperate climate year round but conditions vary considerably by elevation with low valleys being hotter and more humid while mountain tops remain cool and often breezy. There is a clear dry and a wet season. The months of November to April are generally dry with pleasant temperatures. The months of May to October are more likely to see heavy rainfalls and hot, humid conditions.\nGet in.\nThe nearest airport with scheduled commercial flights is in Tuxtla Guti\u00e9rrez. From Tuxtla Gutierrez, you can rent a car (4WD truck is highly recommended), or you can take a combi or bus to a nearby town and hire a guide to take you into the reserve.\nBy car.\nPick up a 4WD vehicle directly from the international airport in Tuxtla Gutierrez. Take a highway south (either Chiapas state route 133 or state route 133) through Suchiapa to the town of Villaflores. From Villaflores, the route is on a remote, unpaved dirt mountain track called \"Camino a Francisco Villa\". It will take at least an hour to drive from Villaflores to any of the ecotourist lodges. Arrange lodging ahead of time or hire local guides to take you into the reserve and show you where the hiking trails are. \nBy combi.\nShared vans (called \"combis\" or \"ecolectivos\") go to Villaflores from the bus station or the ercado de los Ancianos in Tuxtla Gutierrez. This option is only good for travelers with very good Spanish skills as you will need to interact with locals to find the right van and connections or transfers along the way are likely.\nFees and permits.\nCONANP charges a daily admission fee of M$125.\nSleep.\nLodging.\nThere are other tour guides from local indigenous communities (ejidos) and there are two other Centros Ecoturisticos that offer a full range of accommodations, backcountry guides, and other traveler services. They are in other parts of the reserve and offer a different natural experience:\nCamping.\nCamping is permitted only at the designated ecotourism sites. No backcountry camping is allowed within the reserve."}
{"id": "219672", "revid": "2331447", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219672", "title": "Yongjing", "text": "Yongjing (\u6c38\u9756) is a rural township in Changhua County. It is the second most densely populated township in Taiwan."}
{"id": "219681", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219681", "title": "Jirapa", "text": "Jirapa is the capital of the Jirapa Municipal District in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. It is around the border of Lawra and Nadoli districts. It has two main seasons; the dry season and the rainy season. The people are farmers and the farmer products like millet, maize rice and many more. Apart from farm products the they also rarely animals. Such as goats, sheep hens.\nGet in.\nFrom Wa to Jirapa covers about 68 to 70 km along the main Wa-Jirapa road. It can take you 1 to 2 hours depending on the kind of car ones takes. You go with a commercial transport or a private one. You can also ride on a bike which can also take you roughly 2 hours to get there. On your way to Jirapa you passed town like Kaleo, Sombo, Nadoli before entering Jirapa. "}
{"id": "219693", "revid": "2245569", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219693", "title": "Kusaal phrasebook", "text": "Kusaal is spoken by the people of the Kusasi tribe in the Upper East region of Ghana. It is spoken in Bawku, Zebilla, and Binduri districts), as well as some parts of Burkina Faso and Togo. The singular: Kusaa, plural: Kusaas (often referred to as the Kusasi people) are the common names in Ghana. There are main two dialects of the language: the Agole and the Toende. There is a complete Bible translation in Kusaal."}
{"id": "219697", "revid": "2439030", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219697", "title": "Waali phrasebook", "text": "Waali, Wali, Waale, Walee or Waalii is spoken by the Waala tribe in the Upper West Region of Ghana. It is widely spoken in Wa and its environs. \nPronunciation guide.\nAlphabets (uppercase and lowercase).\nA a\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 B b \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u0186 \u0254 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 CH ch \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0D d \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 E e\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u0190 \u025b \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0F f\u00a0 \u00a0 G g \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 GB \ngb \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0H h\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 I i \u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u0269 \u0269 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0J j \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 K k\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0KP kp \u00a0 \u00a0 L l \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0M m \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0N n\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 NY ny \u00a0\n\u014a \u014b \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 \u014aM \u014bm \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 O o \u00a0 \u00a0 P p \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 R r \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 S s \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 T t\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 U u\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0V v \u00a0 \u00a0\n\u01b2 \u028b \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 W w \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0Y y\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\nVowels.\nWaali has 9 phonemic vowels. These are divided into two distinct sets based on ATR harmony (whether the root of the tongue is pushed forward or retracted). Long vowels are written by doubling the letter (e.g., aa, ee). Waali vowelsa, e, i, o, u, \u028b, \u025b, \u0254, \u0269\nShort vowels\nLong Vowels\nConsonants.\nSingle Consonants (Saakinuunih\u0129).\nb \u00a0 \u00a0d \u00a0 \u00a0 k \u00a0 \u00a0 g \u00a0 \u00a0 t \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 p \u00a0 \u00a0 s \u00a0 \u00a0 j \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 v \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 f \u00a0 \u00a0 h \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 y \u00a0 \u00a0 w \u00a0 \u00a0 l \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 n \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 m \u00a0 \u00a0\u014b \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 r\nB b, as in ba \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 father / to plant\nD d, as in da \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 buy\nK k, as in ku \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 give\nG g, as in godo \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 bed\nT t, as in t\u0269 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 we/our/us\nP p, as in po \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 divide\nS s, as in so \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 bath\nJ j, as in ju \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 head\nV v, as in vori \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 alive\nF f, as in fa \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 to cheat\nH h, as in ha \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 hate\nY y, as in yiri \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 home\nW w, as in wa \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 come/Wa (upper West capital)\nL l, as in la \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 laugh\nN n, as in naa \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 king/chief\nM n, as in ma \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 mother\n\u014a \u014b, as in \u014ba \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 this\nR r, as in s\u025b\u028br\u0269 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0\u00a0 dance/writings \nPhrase list.\nTime.\nDuration.\nMintipunii (mintipurimo) _ Second(s)\nDays.\nBeri"}
{"id": "219698", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219698", "title": "Gushegu", "text": "Gushegu is a bustling town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. Embedded deeply within the Kingdom of Dagbon (one of West Africa's oldest traditional kingdoms), it operates under the customary leadership of the paramount chief, Naa Yab' Gushe-Naa.\nUnderstand.\nGushegu is the administrative capital of the Gushegu Municipal District in the northeast quadrant of Ghana's Northern Region.\nThe town serves as a crucial rural-urban hub, connecting smaller surrounding agricultural villages like Kpatinga and Zinindo to broader regional trade routes. While traditional architecture, local markets, and farming define daily life, it is also recognized across northern Ghana as an educational center due to its prominent nursing and midwifery college.\nGet in.\nBy car or taxi route.\nThe primary route out of Tamale heads north through Savelugu, branching northeast through Karaga before arriving in Gushegu. The journey spans roughly 164 km (102 miles). A private car or hired taxi takes approximately 2 hours depending on seasonal road conditions. Be prepared for bumpy stretches. Local farmers and motorists frequently navigate changing conditions along the Tamale-Gushegu and Yendi-Gushegu corridors.\nBy trotro or shared taxi.\nLocal minibuses (trotros) and shared taxis head toward Gushegu daily from the Tamale Central Market or Aboabo Market transit areas. Buses leave most often in the morning, afternoon, or on busy market days. Big public buses (Metro Mass Transit) also run this route, but they do not always have a strict schedule.\nAsk transport operators specifically for vehicles loading for the Gushegu or Yendi-Gushegu lines.\nEat.\nFood in Gushegu is simple, local, and filling. Most meals are based on staples grown in the area.\nDrink.\nDrinks in Gushegu are mostly non-alcoholic and locally made. Alcohol is available but less common and usually consumed privately.\nSleep.\nAccommodation in Gushegu is basic. Don\u2019t expect luxury hotels, but there are a few guesthouses suitable for travelers and NGO workers.\nConnect.\nStaying connected in Gushegu is possible but basic.\nNetwork. MTN and Telecel have the best coverage in town. Airtel Tigo works in some areas but can be unstable\nData. Buy data bundles or airtime from vendors in the market. 4G exists in central Gushegu but often drops to 3G/2G at the edges of town.\nWi-Fi. Not widely available. A few guesthouses and offices may have Wi-Fi, but it\u2019s unreliable. Plan to use mobile data.\nElectricity. Power is on the national grid but outages happen. Guesthouses may not have generators.\nPost. Ghana Post has a branch in Gushegu for mail and parcels.\nMobile Money. MTN MoMo and Telecel Cash agents are common around the market and lorry station. Useful for paying for taxis and small purchases."}
{"id": "219700", "revid": "1812", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219700", "title": "Osun Osogbo Sacred Grove", "text": "Osun Osogbo Sacred Grove in Nigeria is a sacred forest and cultural heritage site on the outskirts of Osogbo, Osun State, Nigeria. It lies along the Osun River and is regarded as the sacred abode of Osun, the Yoruba river goddess associated with fertility. The grove contains shrines, sculptures, sacred places and artworks dedicated to Osun and other Yoruba deities.\nUnderstand.\nThe Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove is a 75-hectare, UNESCO-listed primary forest serving as the spiritual home of the Yoruba goddess of fertility. It features active shrines, medicinal plants, and monumental, 20th-century sculptures, acting as an active, sacred cultural landscape that hosts a major annual festival. \nIt is a living religious and cultural landscape rather than simply a conventional tourist attraction. Traditional worship and ceremonies continue to take place in the grove, and visitors are expected to respect its religious significance.\nThe grove is one of the last surviving examples of the sacred forests that historically existed around many Yoruba settlements. The Osun River passes through the forest, while shrines, sculptures and sacred sites are distributed along its banks. UNESCO describes the grove as an important expression of Yoruba cosmology and cultural identity.\nThe grove also became notable for its distinctive modern sculptures. Many of the works were created during the 20th century as part of efforts to restore the spiritual and artistic character of the site. Austrian-born artist Suzanne Wenger played an important role in the revival and conservation of the grove and became integrated into the local Yoruba community.\nThe annual Osun-Osogbo Festival is held in and around the grove, normally during August. The festival brings together traditional worshippers, local residents, visitors and tourists for a programme of religious and cultural activities.\nThe festival includes traditional rites, processions and ceremonies connected with Osun and the cultural heritage of Osogboland. It is an important living tradition and contributes to the continuing significance of the grove.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nThe grove is located in Osogbo, the capital of Osun State. Osogbo can be reached by road from other major cities in southwestern Nigeria, including Ibadan, Ile-Ife, Akure and Lagos.\nFrom central Osogbo, the grove can be reached by taxi, tricycle or other local transport.\nBy train.\nOsogbo is served by Nigeria's railway network. Visitors arriving by train can continue to the grove using local taxis or other road transport.\nFees and permits.\nAn entrance fee is charged to visitors, but current admission and photography fees are not consistently published online. Visitors should confirm the current charges with the site management before visiting.\nGuided visits are available. Visitors intending to undertake commercial photography, filming or other special activities should obtain permission from the relevant authorities before doing so.\nThe site is managed by the National Commission for Museums and Monuments (NCMM).\nThe published opening hours are Monday to Friday, 8:00\u201318:00; Saturday, 9:00\u201318:00; and Sunday, 11:00\u201318:00.\nGet around.\nThe main attraction is explored on foot. Visitors should follow designated paths and comply with instructions from site staff and traditional custodians.\nThe forest environment can be uneven in places, so comfortable footwear is recommended. Visitors should avoid entering restricted or sacred areas without permission.\nSee.\nSacred shrines.\nThe grove contains numerous shrines dedicated to Osun and other Yoruba deities. UNESCO records around 40 shrines and associated sculptures and artworks within the World Heritage property.\nOsun River.\nThe Osun River flows through the grove and is central to the religious significance of the site. It is traditionally associated with Osun, the river deity to whom the grove is dedicated.\nSculptures and artworks.\nLarge sculptures and other artworks can be seen throughout the grove. Many were created or restored during the 20th century as part of the artistic and cultural revival of the site.\nSacred forest.\nThe grove preserves an important area of forest and provides habitat for a variety of plant species. UNESCO describes it as one of the remaining areas of primary high forest in southern Nigeria and notes the presence of more than 400 plant species.\nMuseum and visitor facilities.\nVisitors should check locally for the availability of guides, interpretation facilities and other visitor services, as facilities and arrangements may change.\nBuy.\nWhile there are no large commercial shopping complexes inside the protected forest, the entrance area and the nearby road leading to the palace feature several excellent shopping opportunities for traditional items.\nEat.\nOsogbo has numerous restaurants and local food vendors where visitors can try Yoruba and other Nigerian dishes. There are limited food options within the sacred grove, so visitors may prefer to eat in Osogbo before or after visiting.\nDrink.\nBottled water and soft drinks are readily available in Osogbo. Visitors should carry drinking water when walking around the grove, particularly during hot weather.\nSleep.\nThere are hotels and guesthouses in Osogbo, but none within the sacred grove.\nStay safe.\nThe grove is a protected cultural and religious site. Visitors should:\nTraditional rules and conservation measures are important to the protection of the grove. UNESCO notes that traditional customs prohibit activities such as hunting, fishing, poaching, tree felling and farming within the protected area."}
{"id": "219710", "revid": "2436120", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219710", "title": "Dagaare phrasebook", "text": "Dagaare (also known as Dagara or Dagari) is a Gur language spoken by the Dagaaba people in the Upper West Region of Ghana, southern Burkina Faso, and northern Ivory Coast. It is closely related to Waale and Dagbani.\nPronunciation guide.\nDagaare uses Advanced Tongue Root (ATR) vowel harmony. This means vowels within a word are pronounced with either an advanced or retracted tongue position to stay harmonized.\nVowels.\nDagaare has 9 phonemic vowels. Long vowels are written by doubling the letter.\nConsonants.\nMost consonants sound like English, but watch out for these double-articulated African sounds:\nPhrase list.\nTime.\nWagere\nClock time.\nWagere/gogori\nMonths.\nKyue\nMoney.\nLibiri\nEating.\nDiibu\nBars.\nD\u00e3\u00e3 dire\nShopping.\nDaa diibu/ boma daabo\nDriving.\nM\u0254mmo"}
{"id": "219734", "revid": "1812", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219734", "title": "Nekede", "text": "Nekede is a town in Owerri West Local Government Area of Imo State, southeastern Nigeria. It forms part of the wider Owerri urban area and is known for the Federal Polytechnic.\nUnderstand.\nNekede shares its sourthern border with Ihiagwa, the well-known host community of the Federal University of Technology, Owerri (FUTO).\nHistory.\nNekede is one of the fifteen communities that form the Owerri West Local Government Area. The town is composed of three villages, which are Umualum, Umudibia, and Umuoma. These three villages are traditionally bound together by shared history and native customs.\nNekede changed forever in 1978 when a technical college opened on its land. In 1993, this school became the Federal Polytechnic, Nekede, bringing in thousands of students and turning the town into a busy university hub. The town's history is also tied to its water, as Nekede sits where the Nworie River meets the Otamiri River. In Igbo tradition, the Otamiri River is named after a powerful water deity believed to protect the town and punish evil-doers. These rivers served as natural borders, trade routes, and fishing grounds for early settlers, and they remain the most important geographic landmarks in Nekede today.\nClimate.\nNekede features a tropical savanna climate, characterized by high humidity, a prolonged wet season, and a short but distinct dry season. Temperatures remain uniformly warm to hot throughout the year, with a mean annual temperature hovering around 26.4\u00b0C. The rainy season brings heavy downpours, while the dry season are accompanied by the dusty harmattan wind, making the period between late November and early February the most comfortable time for visitors.\nGet in.\nNekede is highly accessible by air and road.\nBy road.\nTravellers coming from Owerri Municipal can easily take a local commercial tricycle (popularly called Keke) or a mini-bus from the major Douglas Road axis directly to Nekede.\nBy plane.\nTravellers coming from outside the state, the nearest flight hub is the Sam Mbakwe International Cargo Airport in Owerri, from where you can charter a taxi straight to Nekede.\nGet around.\nTaxis, tricycles and motorcycles are commonly used to travel around Nekede.\nStay safe.\nTake normal precautions with personal belongings, especially in crowded areas and around transport stops. At night, use established transport services where possible."}
{"id": "219735", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219735", "title": "Conca de Barber\u00e0", "text": "Conca de Barber\u00e0 is a county in inland Catalonia, in the province of Tarragona, known for being part of the Cistercian Route and for housing the Royal Monastery of Santa Maria de Poblet, a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nUnderstand.\nConca de Barber\u00e0 is a little over an hour by car from Barcelona, and close to Tarragona, Lleida and the Costa Daurada. The county combines Cistercian monastic heritage, medieval town centres, a wine-growing tradition (Conca de Barber\u00e0 Denomination of Origin) and nature, with protected areas such as the Poblet Natural Site and the Prades Mountains.\nGet in.\nBy car: via the AP-2, with exits towards Montblanc and l'Espluga de Francol\u00ed. By train: Rodalies stations at l'Espluga de Francol\u00ed and Vimbod\u00ed (lines R13 and R14), connecting to Barcelona, Lleida and Tarragona.\nGet around.\nThe most convenient way to get around the county is by car, although the GR175 trail (Cistercian Route) allows several municipalities to be explored on foot or by bicycle.\nSleep.\nThere are rural guesthouses, hotels and hospeder\u00edas in several municipalities across the county, including the Hostatgeria de Poblet, next to the monastery itself."}
{"id": "219738", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219738", "title": "Nadowli", "text": "Nadowli is a town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nNadowli is a town and the capital of the Nadowli-Kaleo District in the Upper West Region of Ghana. The district is centred around subsistence mixed farming, local community markets, and rural administrative governance. It is bordered by Jirapa Municipal to the north, Wa West and Wa Municipal to the south, Daffiama-Bussie-Issa to the east, and the international border with C\u00f4te d'Ivoire to the west.\nGo next.\nWa Naa Palace, Wechiau Community Hippo Sanctuary,Mole National Park &amp; Larabanga,Royal Cosy Hills Safari Resort (locally dubbed \"Jirapa Dubai\"),Mushroom Rocks in nearby Wulling,Nandom Cathedral and Gwollu Slave Defense Wall."}
