{"id": "219740", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219740", "title": "Andoni", "text": "Andoni is a coastal Local Government Area in Rivers State, Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nIts headquarters is at Ngo. The area lies along the Atlantic coast and contains islands, beaches, mangrove wetlands, creeks, fishing settlements and forest habitats. Andoni is particularly notable for its coastal scenery, fishing culture, wildlife and cultural festivals. A study of tourism potential in Andoni identified festivals, islands, fishing and wild animals among the area's major tourism attractions.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nAndoni can be reached by road from other parts of Rivers State. The LGA headquarters, Ngo, is the main administrative centre and a useful starting point for exploring the area.\nA new road project linking Ngo with the Atlantic coast and Oyorokoto is intended to improve access to the coastal tourism areas. As construction and road conditions may change, visitors should check the current condition of the route before travelling.\nBy boat.\nSeveral communities and attractions in Andoni are connected by creeks and waterways. Boat transport is used locally, particularly for reaching island and coastal communities. Visitors unfamiliar with the waterways should arrange transport with a local boat operator or resident familiar with the route.\nFrom Port Harcourt.\nPort Harcourt is the main major-city gateway to Rivers State. From Port Harcourt, travellers can continue by road towards Andoni and then use local road or water transport depending on their destination.\nGet around.\nRoad transport is suitable for the mainland communities, while boats are important for reaching some island and creek-side communities.\nBy boat.\nBoat transport is particularly useful for exploring the coastal and island areas. Use experienced local boat operators and wear a life jacket where available.\nBy road.\nMotorcycles, cars and other local transport are used within communities. Road conditions vary, particularly away from the main routes.\nWith a local guide.\nA local guide is strongly recommended for visitors interested in wildlife, beaches, fishing communities or the mangrove and creek environment. Local guides can help with navigation, community introductions and understanding local customs.\nSee.\nAndoni Island.\nAndoni Island is a coastal barrier island in Rivers State known for its combination of Atlantic beaches, forest, wetlands and marine environments. The island has been associated with African forest elephants, sea turtles and other wildlife. Migratory dolphins have also been reported along its Atlantic shores.\nThe island is particularly suitable for visitors interested in nature, wildlife, coastal scenery and fishing communities.\nOyorokoto.\nOyorokoto is a major fishing settlement on Andoni Island. The community is known for its fishing activity, fish landing areas and connection to the Atlantic coast. Visitors can observe fishing boats, nets, fish processing and the trading of seafood, subject to local permission and customs.\nOyorokoto is also associated with an extensive Atlantic beach and has been identified as an emerging tourism destination.\nOyorokoto Atlantic Beach.\nOyorokoto Atlantic Beach is a long sandy beach facing the Atlantic Ocean. The beach and surrounding coastal environment provide opportunities for sightseeing, photography and nature-based tourism. Facilities are limited, so visitors should come prepared with drinking water, sun protection and other necessities.\nAndoni Beach.\nAndoni Beach stretches along the Atlantic coast near communities including Ikuru. The beach combines sandy shoreline with mangrove and forest environments and is being promoted for ecotourism and beach recreation.\nIkuru Town and Beach.\nIkuru is a coastal community on Andoni Island associated with rainforest, beaches and wildlife. The Rivers State Tourism Development Agency has highlighted Ikuru Town and its beach as part of the area's tourism resources.\nMangroves and creeks.\nThe waterways and mangrove environments of Andoni form part of the wider Niger Delta ecosystem. Exploring these areas by boat can provide views of mangrove vegetation, tidal channels, fishing activity and coastal wildlife.\nWildlife.\nAndoni's forests, wetlands and coastal waters support a variety of wildlife. African forest elephants have been reported on Andoni Island, while dolphins and sea turtles are associated with the coastal environment.\nWildlife sightings are not guaranteed. Visitors should not approach, feed or disturb wild animals.\nFishing communities.\nFishing is one of the area's most visible traditional economic activities. In Oyorokoto, visitors can observe boats returning with catches, fish landing activities and the use of different fishing nets and techniques. A tourism study identified fishing as a major attraction in the community.\nDo.\nWildlife watching.\nExplore the forests, wetlands and coastal areas with a local guide and look for birds and other wildlife. African forest elephants have been reported on Andoni Island, although sightings are unpredictable.\nBirdwatching.\nThe combination of forest, mangrove, wetlands and coastal habitats makes Andoni suitable for birdwatching. Bring binoculars and avoid disturbing nesting or feeding birds.\nBoat trips.\nHire a local boat and explore the creeks, mangroves and waterways around the coastal communities. Use an experienced operator and check weather and water conditions before setting out.\nFishing experience.\nVisitors can observe traditional fishing activities in communities such as Oyorokoto. Ask permission before photographing fishermen, their families, boats or catches.\nBeach activities.\nThe Atlantic beaches provide opportunities for walking, photography, sightseeing and relaxing. Visitors should be cautious around the sea and avoid entering the water unless local conditions and safety arrangements are suitable.\nPhotography.\nAndoni offers opportunities for landscape, wildlife, fishing and cultural photography. Always seek permission before photographing people or private activities.\nCultural festivals.\nAndoni has a variety of traditional festivals. A 2020 tourism study identified Nwamtam as a widely practised cultural festival in Andoni, alongside other festivals including Ekekpe, Egwu-eni and Ekparu.\nBuy.\nSeafood.\nFresh fish and other seafood can be purchased in fishing communities such as Oyorokoto, subject to availability and local trading practices.\nLocal crafts.\nTraditional crafts and products made from locally available materials may be available in some communities. Availability varies, so ask locally rather than expecting dedicated souvenir shops.\nEat.\nLocal communities offer opportunities to eat freshly caught fish and other seafood. In fishing settlements such as Oyorokoto, visitors may find locally prepared fish and seafood depending on the season and availability.\nVisitors heading into remote coastal or island areas should carry sufficient food and drinking water, as restaurant facilities may be limited.\nDrink.\nBottled water and other drinks may be available in larger communities, but supplies can be limited in remote coastal areas. Carry sufficient drinking water when travelling to beaches, islands and wildlife areas.\nSleep.\nAccommodation options within Andoni are limited compared with Port Harcourt. Visitors planning to explore the coastal and island areas should arrange accommodation in advance and confirm availability before travelling.\nFor a wider range of hotels and other accommodation, Port Harcourt provides more options.\nStay safe.\nAndoni contains coastal waters, creeks, wetlands and forest areas. Visitors should use local guides when travelling into unfamiliar areas and should not enter waterways alone.\nWear a life jacket when travelling by boat where one is available. Check weather and sea conditions before travelling to Atlantic beaches.\nDo not approach or feed wildlife. African forest elephants and other wild animals should be observed from a safe distance and only with experienced local guidance.\nKeep valuables secure and follow local advice about areas that may be unsafe or difficult to access."}
{"id": "219742", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219742", "title": "Ogoni", "text": "Ogoni is a region in Rivers State, Nigeria, in the eastern Niger Delta. It is home to the Ogoni people and comprises four Local Government Areas: Khana, Gokana, Tai and Eleme.\nUnderstand.\nThe region has a distinctive cultural heritage expressed through its languages, traditional institutions, festivals, music, dance, food and community traditions. Much of Ogoni is characterised by rivers, creeks, wetlands, mangrove vegetation and lowland forest.\nOgoni is also internationally known for its environmental history, particularly the effects of petroleum production and the long-running campaign for environmental restoration and community rights. Visitors interested in the region should approach this aspect of Ogoni's history with sensitivity and respect.\nThe traditional and cultural centre of the region is Bori, the headquarters of Khana Local Government Area and one of the major urban centres of Ogoni.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nOgoni is primarily accessed by road from Port Harcourt and other parts of Rivers State. Bori, the headquarters of Khana Local Government Area and a major commercial centre of Ogoni, is a useful base for exploring the region.\nFrom Port Harcourt, travellers can reach Bori through the Port Harcourt\u2013Eleme\u2013Bori road network. Road conditions and travel times can vary, so it is advisable to check current conditions before travelling.\nFrom Port Harcourt.\nPort Harcourt is the main urban gateway to Ogoni. Shared taxis, buses and other commercial vehicles operate along the major routes connecting Port Harcourt with communities in Ogoni.\nBy public transport.\nCommercial buses, taxis and shared vehicles are available from Port Harcourt and surrounding communities to Bori and other major towns. Public transport within the region is generally more readily available along the main roads than in smaller communities.\nBy air.\nThe nearest major airport is Port Harcourt International Airport at Omagwa. From the airport, travellers can continue by road towards Ogoni.\nBy boat.\nAlthough much of Ogoni is accessible by road, some communities and natural areas are connected by rivers, creeks and waterways. Boat transport may be useful for specific excursions, but arrangements should be made with local operators familiar with the waterways.\nGet around.\nBy road.\nRoad transport is the main way to travel between the four Local Government Areas that make up Ogoni: Khana, Gokana, Tai and Eleme. Bori is a useful starting point for exploring the central and eastern parts of the region.\nCars, taxis, motorcycles and commercial buses are used for movement between towns and communities. Transport availability is generally better on major roads than on smaller community roads.\nBy taxi and motorcycle.\nTaxis and motorcycles are commonly used for shorter journeys within and between communities. Agree on the fare before starting a journey when using an informal taxi or motorcycle.\nBy boat.\nSome parts of Ogoni contain rivers, creeks and wetlands. Boat transport can be arranged for selected waterways and community visits. Visitors should use experienced local boat operators and wear a life jacket where available.\nWith a local guide.\nA local guide is recommended for visitors exploring cultural sites, traditional communities, waterways or less-developed natural areas. A local guide can also help visitors understand local customs and obtain permission before entering community or culturally sensitive locations.\nSee.\nBori.\nBori is the traditional and commercial centre of Ogoni and the headquarters of Khana Local Government Area. It is one of the best places to begin exploring Ogoni's culture, history and contemporary life. Bori is also associated with Ken Saro-Wiwa and hosts the Ken Saro-Wiwa Polytechnic.\nKen Saro-Wiwa Memorial.\nThe Ken Saro-Wiwa Memorial in Bori commemorates the Ogoni writer, environmental campaigner and activist Ken Saro-Wiwa. The site provides an opportunity to learn about his life and the history of the Ogoni environmental movement. Visitors interested in the modern history of the Niger Delta should approach the site as a place of remembrance and reflection.\nEleme.\nEleme forms the western part of Ogoni and is one of its four Local Government Areas. The area combines traditional communities and agricultural landscapes with the industrial development associated with the wider Port Harcourt area.\nVisitors interested in the history and changing landscape of the Niger Delta can explore Eleme with local guidance.\nGokana.\nGokana is one of the four Local Government Areas of Ogoni. The area contains numerous traditional communities and waterways and is known for its distinctive cultural heritage. Visitors interested in Ogoni's traditional institutions and festivals can explore communities in Gokana with local guidance.\nThe traditional headquarters of Gokana is Giokoo, which is associated with the traditional administration of the Gokana Kingdom.\nTai.\nTai is one of the four Local Government Areas of Ogoni. The area is predominantly rural and contains agricultural communities, waterways and traditional settlements. Visitors can explore the landscape and learn about local farming and community life with the assistance of a local guide.\nOgoni Cultural Sites.\nOgoni has a rich cultural heritage expressed through its traditional institutions, festivals, music, dance, language and community customs. Cultural sites vary from one community to another, and visitors should arrange local guidance when visiting traditional or sacred places.\nRivers, creeks and wetlands.\nThe landscape of Ogoni includes rivers, streams, creeks and wetlands. These waterways have traditionally supported fishing, transportation and agriculture and form an important part of the region's natural environment.\nSome waterways can be explored by boat, although visitors should arrange trips with experienced local operators.\nDo.\nTraditional communities.\nThe villages and towns of Ogoni retain strong traditional institutions and community structures. Visitors may encounter traditional rulers, community festivals, cultural ceremonies and local markets.\nAccess to some traditional or sacred sites may require permission from community leaders or custodians. Visitors should make arrangements in advance rather than arriving unannounced.\nCultural festivals.\nOgoni communities celebrate a variety of traditional festivals and ceremonies. These events provide opportunities to experience local music, dance, dress, food and traditional practices.\nFestival dates and access arrangements can vary by community. Visitors should confirm dates locally and obtain permission before photographing ceremonies or sacred activities."}
{"id": "219744", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219744", "title": "Nandom", "text": "Nandom is a historic border town in the Northern Plains of Ghana. It is celebrated for its deep Catholic missionary history, master musical craftsmen, and rich Dagara cultural heritage.\nUnderstand.\nNandom is the capital of the Nandom Municipality in the extreme northwestern corner of the Upper West Region of Ghana, close to the international border with Burkina Faso.\nGet in.\nYou can fly into Wa from other parts of the country.\nFrom Wa or Nadowli:\nBy trotro (commercial minibus).\nHead to the Wa Main Station or the Lawra/Nandom terminal in Wa. Look for a commercial minibus heading directly to Nandom or Hamile. If you are starting from Nadowli, you can wait by the main highway junction to flag down these same northern-bound buses.\nBy shared taxi.\nYou can find shared station taxis in Wa or Lawra that fill up with passengers and head straight into Nandom town.\nBy car.\nIf you are driving or hiring a private taxi, take the highway north out of Wa. You will pass through Kaleo, Nadowli, Babile, and Lawra before arriving directly in Nandom.\nGet around.\nBy motorbike taxi.\nMotorbikes are the absolute fastest and most common way to zip across town. You can easily flag a rider down anywhere on the streets to take you to landmarks like the St. Theresa's Basilica, the Nandom Hospital, or local schools.\nBy tricycle (mahama camboo).\nThese three-wheeled passenger trikes are incredibly popular for cheap, short-distance trips within the town centre.\nOn foot.\nBecause the main commercial hub, the stone cathedral, and the local transport station are clustered relatively close together, you can comfortably walk to many inner-town destinations.\nBy shared taxi and trotro.\nThese are best saved for moving out of Nandom toward surrounding rural communities or neighboring towns like Lawra, Babile, and the Hamile border post. They depart from the main Nandom lorry station once they are fully loaded with passengers.\nGo next.\nCross north into Burkina Faso (the Border Route), explore east toward the Slave Defense Walls, and journey south along the Black Volta River."}
{"id": "219748", "revid": "1540201", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219748", "title": "Beijing/Fangshan", "text": "Fangshan District (\u623f\u5c71\u533a; \"F\u00e1ngsh\u0101nq\u016b\") is in the south-west of Beijing.\nSee.\nThese are ruins rather than restored monuments, and are of more interest to specialists than to general visitors."}
{"id": "219749", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219749", "title": "Beijing/Daxing", "text": "Daxing District (\u5927\u5174\u533a; \"D\u00e0x\u012bngq\u016b\") is in the south of Beijing.\nUnderstand.\nDaxing is a large, flat district of farmland and industrial parks stretching from the 4th Ring Road down to the border with Hebei. It has few of the historic sights that draw visitors to other parts of Beijing, and most people who come here are passing through on the way to the airport or attending a trade event.\nTwo places account for nearly all visitor interest. Beijing Daxing International Airport (PKX) opened in 2019 at the far southern edge of the district, and its Zaha Hadid-designed terminal is a sight in its own right. In the opposite corner, Beijing E-Town (\u5317\u4eac\u4ea6\u5e84; \"B\u011bij\u012bng Y\u00eczhu\u0101ng\"), formally the Beijing Economic-Technological Development Area (BDA) is a state-level development zone built around robotics, biotechnology and vehicle manufacturing. In the summer, Daxing hosts the annual World Robot Conference, which is barely promoted outside China but draws an international crowd anyway of people who have sought it out on their own curiosity.\nThe district is also known within Beijing for its watermelons, celebrated at a festival each spring.\nGet in.\nBy subway.\nFor E-Town, take Line 17. You can board at Workers' Stadium in central Beijing and stay on all the way to the southern end. Change at Ciqu for the Yizhuang line if you are heading for the convention centre or anywhere in the core of the development zone.\nThe rest of the district is served piecemeal, and there is no line running its length: getting between the airport, Daxing New Town and E-Town by rail means going back towards the centre to change.\nBy plane.\nBeijing Daxing International Airport (PKX) is at the southern edge of the district, about 46 km from the city centre.\nGet around.\nWithin E-Town, the Yizhuang T1 tram links the industrial parks and the convention centre with the Yizhuang line at Rongchang Dongjie. It is a local circulator rather than a way in or out."}
{"id": "219755", "revid": "1812", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219755", "title": "Brechfa Forest", "text": "Brechfa Forest is a rural area in Carmarthenshire, South Wales. It is a vast 6,500-hectare woodland, known for walking trails, mountain biking, and wildlife.\nUnderstand.\nBrechfa Forest is managed by Natural Resources Wales. It sits roughly 17 miles northeast of Carmarthen. Further information can be found at Visit Brechfa Forestand Roam Brechfa Forest\nGet in.\nTo reach Brechfa Forest from Carmarthen, take the 282 bus from Carmarthen to Brechfa or travel by car via the A40 and then the B4310.\nGet around.\nA car or bike is recommended to travel round the area due to the limited public transport.\nDo.\nActivities.\nMountain biking\nThere are a number of free access MTB trails in the forest. The following are the start point for the trails. Please check the Natural Resources Wales website for up to date information about which trails are open and details of any diversions.\nCommercial MTB trails:\nHorse Riding \nWalking\nThere are a wide range of walks throughout the area. The following are the starting points for popular walks:\nParking.\nThe following are the main car parks in the forest and starting points for the various trails:\nSleep.\nA wide range of accommodation is available within the area: "}
{"id": "219756", "revid": "79587", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219756", "title": "Vy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod", "text": "Vy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod is a town of 2,600 inhabitants (2026) in South Bohemia, in the Czech Republic, on the Vltava River. The town is a popular river tourism spot. The main landmark of the town is the Vy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod Monastery, which is protected as a national cultural monument. The historic town centre with the monastery complex is well preserved and is protected as an urban monument zone. It is also a popular starting point for river tourism.\nUnderstand.\nVy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod was founded in the 13th century at the latest and became a town in 1870. \nAbout two thirds of the municipal territory lie in the Bohemian Forest Foothills, only the western part lies in the Bohemian Forest. The highest point is the mountain Jezev\u010d\u00ed vrch at 984 m (3,228 ft) above sea level. The municipal territory borders Austria in the south and the Vy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod Pass is on this state border. Vy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod is the southernmost municipality of the Czech Republic.\nThe Vltava River flows through the town. The streams Men\u0161\u00ed Vltavice and V\u011bt\u0161\u00ed Vltavice flow into the Vltava in Vy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod. The Lipno II Reservoir, built on the Vltava, is on the northern outskirts of the town.\nGet in.\nVy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod is about 21 km (13 mi) south of \u010cesk\u00fd Krumlov and 40 km (25 mi) south of \u010cesk\u00e9 Bud\u011bjovice. \nBy train.\nVy\u0161\u0161\u00ed Brod lies on regional railway line from Rybn\u00edk to Lipno nad Vltavou.\nYou can use connections from \u010cesk\u00e9 Bud\u011bjovice, there are direct local train connections and the trip takes about 1 hr 20 min; from Prague, you can go by an EC train to Rybn\u00edk and transfer here, this trip takes about 3 hours. For more details look at IDOS.\nThe train station is north-east of the monastery and north of the town centre; the walk takes about 10 minutes to the monastery or 20 minutes to the town square.\nBy bus.\nThere are direct bus connections for \u010cesk\u00e9 Bud\u011bjovice (1 hr 20 min to 1 hr 40 min).\nSee.\nIf you like hiking, you can follow a yellow hiking path from townsquare and go about 10km to the southernmost point of the Czech republic."}
{"id": "219766", "revid": "2197649", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219766", "title": "Miranda de Ebro", "text": "Miranda de Ebro is in Castile and Le\u00f3n. Although it is an industrial city, it has a rich cultural history that undoubtedly surprises the tourist who arrives. Its old town has a certain medieval touch and is being renovated. The gastronomy of Mirandesa is very rich and varied thanks to the privileged geographical situation that has united the Spanish, Rioja and Basque cuisine. It stands out above all the delicious pintxos offered by most of the bars.\nUnderstand.\nMiranda de Ebro is located in the province of Burgos, although it borders the province of \u00c1lava and the autonomous community of La Rioja. In just a radius of 80 km are the cities of Bilbao, Burgos, Logro\u00f1o and Vitoria-Gasteiz (just 33 km). It is the second most important city in the province after the capital and the railway is its hallmark.\nThe city is very close to the Basque County but only Spanish is spoken here.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nMiranda de Ebro is well connected by road. The city can be reached via the A-1 or AP-1 if we depart from Madrid or San Sebasti\u00e1n or the A-68 if we depart from Barcelona or Bilbao. All these highways have direct access to the city.\nGet around.\nThe city centre is reasonably compact but there are a few urban bus lines provided by Bus Miranda. Price in 2026 for a single journey is \u20ac1.25. There is a BusK Bus Miranda de Ebro smartphone app should you want real-time information but may not be available on phones registered outside Spain.\nThe is a useful point of reference if you are on foot as roads to the two bridges and the main highway passing the town originate from here as well as two different roads to the railway station,\nDrink.\nTry around Plaza de Cervantes.\nConnect.\nYou should get 5G in the city centre."}
{"id": "219767", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219767", "title": "Picos de Europa", "text": "Picos de Europa (\"Peaks of Europe\") National Park is in located in the mountainous part of the northern Spain regions - Asturias, Cantabria and Castile and Le\u00f3n. This wooded limestone mountain range is particularly popular with hikers and climbers in the summer due to its scenic beauty.\nUnderstand.\nLandscape.\nThere are three parts of the masif:\nThe rivers Deva (with tributaries Quiviesa and Bull\u00f3n), Duje, Cares and Sella flow through the area to their mouths near. Here you can find sea lampreys, salmon, white-clawed crayfish (Austropotamobius pallipes), otters, and the nocturnal Pyrenean desman (Galemys pyrenaicus).\nGet in.\nWhen driving in by the car, some information can be retrieved in or."}
{"id": "219769", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219769", "title": "Aguleri", "text": "Aguleri is an ancient, culturally significant, and highly fertile agrarian town in Anambra East Local Government Area of Anambra State, Nigeria. It is nestled along the banks of the Omambala River basin.\nUnderstand.\nAguleri is bordered by Umueri, Anam, and Nando to the west; Anaku, Omor, Ifite Ogwari, Igbakwu, and Umueje to the east; Iggah, Ojjor, Asaba, and Ogwurugwu communities of Uzo Uwani LGA in Enugu State to the south; and Obele, Odeke, and Eshonwa communities of Ibaji LGA in Kogi State to the north. \nHistory and mythological origins.\nAguleri is traditionally regarded as the ancestral cradle of the Igbo race, founded around 4500 BC by the patriarch Eri. He settled at the Ezu and Omambala river confluence. Due to persistent annual flooding, Eri moved inland to \"Eri-aka\" near the Odanduli stream, eventually establishing Obu Uga (Obu-Gad), the ancestral temple. Eri\u2019s children included Agulu (Aguleri), Attah, Oba, Menri, and his only daughter, Adamgbo (Iguedo). Following his death, his children dispersed to found prominent kingdoms across Nigeria, including the Benin, Igala, and Nri kingdoms. Eri's eldest son, Agulu, remained behind to inherit his father's land, expanding the settlement with his three wives. Their descendants formed the core quarters and villages that structure the town today:\nToday, these lineages populate both the historic homeland (Enuobodo) and the highly fertile riverine farmlands of Aguleri-Otu, while Otuocha (Amaeze) serves as the bustling urban and commercial center.\nClimate.\nAguleri experiences a classic tropical climate characterized by two distinct seasons: a hot, intensely humid rainy season and a hot, dry season. Temperatures remain warm year-round, making lightweight clothing essential for travelers.\nEat.\nThe local diet is heavily focused on fresh river seafood and yam-based dishes. Try Anambra-style seasonal fresh fish pepper soup caught directly from the Omambala, paired with pounded yam.\nDrink.\nFresh Palm Wine (Upwine) sourced from the local riverine palm trees is highly sought after here. For modern amenities, the town relies on local bars and hotel lounges.\nStay safe.\nAguleri is generally welcoming to visitors, particularly those showing deep respect for traditional customs and sacred ancestral institutions like the Obu Uga grove. The town is fortified by community-maintained security infrastructure, including the 54 Squadron Mobile Police Force Barracks. Standard travel precautions apply: avoid travelling alone along isolated farm roads or river paths late at night."}
{"id": "219770", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219770", "title": "Ido-Osun", "text": "Ido-Osun is in Osun State, Nigeria.\nUnderstand.\nIdo-Osun is a town in Egbedore Local Government Area of Osun State, southwestern Nigeria. It is situated in the Osun West area of the state and is within easy reach of Osogbo. The town is surrounded by farmland, hills and valleys, with the Erinle River and its associated water resources in the wider area.\nIdo-Osun is a Yoruba community. Yoruba is the main language used in everyday communication, while English is also used in schools, government offices and other formal settings. The town has its own traditional institution, headed by the Olojudo-Alayemore, alongside the local government administration.\nGet in.\nIdo-Osun is accessible by road from Osogbo and Ede. The town is connected to the surrounding communities by the Ede\u2013Ido-Osun\u2013Osogbo road network.\nBy road.\nFrom Osogbo, travel towards Ido-Osun using the road through the Ofatedo/Ido-Osun area. Local transport is available from Osogbo for travellers heading towards Ido-Osun,, especially at Oke Fia bus stop.\nFrom Ede, Ido-Osun can be reached by road via the Ede\u2013Ido-Osun\u2013Osogbo route.\nBy public transport.\nThere is no need to travel to Ido-Osun by a long-distance bus. Travellers coming from outside Osun State can first travel to Osogbo, if they are entering Osogbo from Ikire, Ilesa, Ikirun and continue to Ido-Osun by local transport.\nLocal buses, taxis and motorcycles operate around Osogbo and the surrounding communities. Travellers should confirm the destination with the driver before boarding, as some vehicles may terminate at nearby communities rather than in Ido-Osun itself.\nBy taxi.\nA taxi or hired car from Osogbo is a convenient option for travellers who want to go directly to Ido-Osun. This can be particularly useful when travelling with luggage or when visiting places outside the town centre."}
{"id": "219771", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219771", "title": "Kpandai", "text": "Kpandai is the capital of Kpandai District in the eastern Oti River plain in the Volta Basin. It is a large farming and market town known as one of Ghana's biggest yam markets and for its mix of Konkomba, Nawuri, Bassari and Gonja communities.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nKpandai was historically a Nawuri settlement with Gonja influence, later heavily settled by Konkomba farmers from the north. It has seen Nawuri-Gonja-Konkomba land disputes in the past but is now a busy agricultural hub. It became a district capital in 2008.\nPeople.\nMixed \u2014 Nawuri (native), Konkomba (majority), Gonja, Bassari, Kotokoli, Ewe and Akan traders. Likpakpaal (Konkomba), Nawuri, Twi, Ewe, Hausa spoken. Christianity dominant, Islam and traditional religion present. Very traditional area.\nClimate.\nGuinea savanna, humid eastern side near Oti River.\nLandscape.\nOti River floodplain \u2014 savanna, yam farms, small hills, near Lake Volta tributaries.\nOrientation.\nLarge spread-out town. Central market and lorry station, district assembly on edge, roads to Nkanchina, Buya, and Ekumdipe to Lake Volta side.\nDo.\nWith regards to tradition, the people of Kpandai celebrate:"}
{"id": "219772", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219772", "title": "Zabzugu", "text": "Zabzugu is the capital of the Zabzugu District in the Volta Basin of Ghana, Zabzugu\u2019s history is rooted in the expansion and administrative organization of the Dagbon state. Historically, it grew as a strategic settlement overseeing surrounding agrarian and trading communities.\nUnderstand.\nZabzugu is 140 km from Tamale, with a population of 82,846 people.\nThe local economy relies heavily on farming, growing crops like yam, maize, and rice.\nIt shares borders with Yendi and Tatale.\nGet in.\nTo travel to Zabzugu, you primarily use road transport via cars, minibuses (tro-tros), commercial taxis, or motorbikes connecting through major regional hubs like Yendi and Tamale. One may use Shared taxis, minibuses (tro-tros), private cars, or commercial motorbikes (okadas) when moving from Yendi or Tamale. However, Long-distances like from Accra or Kumasi involves the Vip buses.\nGet around.\nPeople in Zabzugu use motorbikes, motorized tricycles (locally called \"yellow yellow\"), and cars or taxis to travel around the town and nearby areas.\nMotorbikes: Very common for personal travel and quick trips.\nTricycles (\"Yellow Yellow\"): Used as taxis for short distances and carrying goods on rough roads.\nCars and taxis: Used for local trips and traveling to nearby towns like Yendi.\nCargo trucks: Used to carry heavy loads and farm crops when roads are too rough for standard cars.\nDo.\nThe people of Zabzugu celebrate the following festivals:\nBuy.\nAt the main weekly market and local shops in Zabzugu, traders primarily sell food crops like yams, maize, rice, sorghum, and soybeans, alongside livestock such as goats and sheep.\nEat.\nZabzugu people primarily eat Tuo Zaafi (TZ) made from maize or millet, served with slimy green leaf or okro soups, alongside abundant local yam dishes, shea butter-based accompaniments, and local watering spots serving bottled beer or traditional pito brew.\nSleep.\nAccommodations in Zabzugu include local spots like ABM Hotel on the Zabzugu-Tatale Road, and regional roadside lodgings such as K.M Guest House on the Yendi-Zabzugu Road.\nConnect.\nStaying connected in Zabzugu is possible, but expect basic services.\nMobile and Internet:\nNetwork: MTN has the strongest coverage in Zabzugu town and surrounding villages. Telecel is usable but slower. Other networks are unreliable. If you have two SIMs, make MTN your primary.\nData: Buy airtime and data bundles from vendors around Zabzugu Market and the lorry station. You'll get 4G in the center of town on MTN, but it often drops to 3G or Edge when you move to villages like Kpalagun or Ojoja.\nWi-Fi: No public Wi-Fi spots. Some guesthouses or NGO offices may have a router, but speeds are very slow and not dependable. Plan to use mobile data.\nOther services\nMobile Money: MTN MoMo and Telecel Cash agents are everywhere around the market. This is the easiest way to pay for taxis, food, and accommodations \u2014 most places are cash-only.\nElectricity: Zabzugu is on the national grid, but outages are frequent, especially during the rainy season. Most guesthouses do not have backup generators. Bring a power bank and charge devices whenever you have light.\nPhone Charging: Small shops around the market offer phone charging for 2-3 GHS if there is a blackout.\nPost and Courier: Ghana Post has a small branch in town, but for faster services, people use bus stations to send packages via buses to Tamale or Yendi."}
{"id": "219777", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219777", "title": "Wulensi", "text": "Wulensi is the capital of Nanumba South District in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. It's a small market town on the road between Bimbilla and Nkwanta/Kpandai, serving as a trading centre for Nanumba, Konkomba, and Bassari farming communities. The town is known for its busy market, the nearby Wulensi Waterfalls, and as a stopover for travellers heading south into the Oti Region.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nWulensi is a historic town in the old Nanun kingdom. Traditionally a Nanumba town, it now has a mixed population of Nanumba, Konkomba, Bassari (Chamba), and other groups, which has sometimes led to land tensions in the past. Today it is a growing administrative town and important rural market.\nPeople.\nMain groups are Nanumba and Konkomba, plus Bassari. Nanunli and Likpakpaln (Konkomba language) are widely spoken, along with Hausa in the market and Twi among traders. English is spoken by officials and teachers. Both Islam and Christianity are present, with a strong traditional belief presence in villages.\nClimate.\nHot savanna climate.\nLandscape.\nFlat to gently rolling Guinea savanna. The surrounding area is farmland, small teak plantations, and scattered shea and baobab trees. Small streams feed into the Oti River system.\nOrientation.\nThe town is linear along the main Bimbilla road. The market, lorry station, and guesthouses are all along or just off the main road, within 10 minutes walk.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major airport is Tamale Airport.\nDo.\nFestivals: Wulensi celebrates vibrant cultural events including the traditional festivals such as Bugum chuhu, Damba, featuring community durbars, drumming, and musical concerts.\nSleep.\nBecause Wulensi is a smaller rural district capital, formal accommodation options are very limited.\nMost visitors, government workers, and festival tourists secure lodging in nearby municipal towns or look for informal local homestays."}
{"id": "219779", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219779", "title": "Damongo", "text": "Damongo is a town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains near Mole National Park, Ghana's largest and most famous wildlife refuge.\nUnderstand.\nDamongo is a busy town and the administrative capital of the West Gonja Municipal District, serving as the capital of the Savannah Region of Ghana. The total population for the West Gonja Municipality stands at 63,449. Damongo Town is estimated at 18,209. The town experiences a single, unpredictable rainy season and prolonged heat.\nGet in.\nYou can reach Damongo by road from anywhere in Ghana, most commonly using Tamale as your primary transit hub.Because Damongo has no commercial airport, travelers from distant areas usually fly or take a long-distance bus to Tamale, and then complete the final part of the journey by vehicle.\nGo next.\nFrom Damongo, you are perfectly positioned in northern Ghana to explore places like Kintampo Waterfalls, Bui National Park,Tamale, Wa Bole Ancient Mosque and the Banda Nkwanta Mosque."}
{"id": "219791", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219791", "title": "Hamile", "text": "Hamile is a town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains on the international border with Burkina Faso.\nUnderstand.\nHamile is a border town in the Lambussie District of the Upper West Region of Ghana. It serves as a key transit and trading post on the country's northwestern corridor.\nDo.\nVisiting Hamile gives a special look into local West African border life, where the cultures of Ghana and Burkina Faso naturally blend. Because it is a small, remote trading community, activities here center on cultural immersion, exploring local trade, and taking short excursions into the surrounding Upper West Region.\nBuy.\nShopping in Hamile offers a unique opportunity to buy original West African goods directly from local artisans, farmers, and traders. Because Hamile is a busy international trading hub, the markets feature an assorted blend of goods from northern Ghana, Burkina Faso, and the wider Sahel region.The best place to buy these items is the vibrant, open-air Hamile Market, which comes alive with extra vendors on its designated market days.\nSleep.\nHamile itself has very limited, basic lodging options, primarily consisting of small, unlisted local rest houses or basic rooms geared toward cross-border traders and truck drivers.Because of this, most travelers choose to stay in nearby towns within the Lambussie, Nandom, or Jirapa districts, which offer far more comfortable and reliable accommodations.\nConnect.\nIf your phone is set to automatically select networks, it may switch to a Burkinabe carrier. However, the National Communications Authority (NCA) of Ghana and Burkina Faso's ARCEP signed a Free Roaming agreement, eliminating massive roaming fee surprises for travelers moving between the two countries.\nTo avoid your phone constantly jumping between Ghana and Burkina Faso signals (which drains your battery and disrupts steady internet sessions), go into your phone\u2019s network settings and manually lock your carrier to MTN GH. Expect basic internet speeds suitable for WhatsApp, browsing, and emails. High-definition streaming or heavy file downloads will be slow and inconsistent.\nGo next.\nBecause Hamile sits at the northwestern tip of Ghana along the Western Corridor route, it serves as a natural jumping-off point for several exciting travel routes. Your next destination depends entirely on whether you want to continue exploring Ghana or venture internationally.\nContinue south through the Upper West Region, head east into the Savanna &amp; Wildlife Zones (Ghana) or cross the border into Francophone West Africa."}
{"id": "219792", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219792", "title": "Rawson", "text": "Rawson is the capital city of Chubut Province in Patagonia (Argentina). It is known for its Welsh heritage, and its seaside location with beaches, rocky shoals populated by sea lions, and a fishing fleet known as the \"flota amarilla\". \nUnderstand.\nRawson has a population of just under 25,000 residents (2010). It was founded by immigrants from Wales in 1865. \nGet in.\nThe closest city with an airport is Trelew, though flights are infrequent. Buses from Trelew to Rawson are available, but it is close enough to use local taxis or shared vans.\nBuses from Puerto Madryn to Rawson are operated by various bus lines on an infrequent schedule (typically once or twice daily). The trip takes about 1 hour and 15 minutes and costs about US$5. Buses are operated by Andesmar, Viatac, and Tramar.\nGet around.\nWalking is possible within the core downtown area but you'll want a taxi to get to the La Union beaches (about 7 km from downtown). Private taxis called \"remises\" operate like an Uber but at a fixed rate for a given route. The way these work is to call a central dispatch number and they send a car for you. Local hotels can call a car for you, or you can usually flag down a taxi in the city center.\nSleep.\nThere are several Airbnb-type apartments available in the beach areas of Playa la Union. "}
{"id": "219793", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219793", "title": "Be\u010dej", "text": "Be\u010dej (Serbian: \u0411\u0435\u0447\u0435\u0458; Hungarian: \u00d3becse, pronounced roughly \"BEH-chay\") is a small town on the Tisa River in Vojvodina, northern Serbia. Its main draws are a compact 19th-century centre, a relaxed riverbank and swimming pools fed by thermal water. Be\u010dej makes an easy day trip from Novi Sad, although an overnight stay leaves time for the river and the countryside.\nUnderstand.\nBe\u010dej lies on the Ba\u010dka, or western, bank of the Tisa. Serbian and Hungarian are both in official use, and bilingual names are part of everyday life. Serbian appears in both Cyrillic and Latin script. English is spoken in the main hotel and by some younger residents, but a few words of Serbian or Hungarian are useful outside the centre.\nThe town is small enough to explore without a plan. The main square, locally known as \"Poga\u010da\" (\"the flatbread\"), contains the town hall and the principal Orthodox and Roman Catholic churches. The museum, Than Memorial House, sports centre and riverbank are all within walking distance.\nDo not confuse Be\u010dej with Novi Be\u010dej. Despite the name, Novi Be\u010dej is a separate town on the Banat side of the Tisa, about 8 km away by road.\nSpring and early autumn are the most comfortable seasons for walking and cycling. Summer is the liveliest time along the river and at the outdoor pools, but afternoons can be hot and mosquitoes appear around dusk.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nBuses from Novi Sad run several times a day and normally take about an hour. From Belgrade, it is usually simplest to travel to Novi Sad by train or bus and change there. A few through or seasonal services may also operate, so check the timetable before setting out.\nBy car.\nBe\u010dej is about 50 km from Novi Sad and 120 km from Belgrade. Allow roughly one hour from Novi Sad and 1\u00bd\u20132 hours from Belgrade. Roads across Ba\u010dka are mostly two-lane and pass through several villages. Parking is usually easier a block or two away from the main square.\nBy train.\nThere is no useful passenger rail service to Be\u010dej. The nearest practical rail connection is Novi Sad, with an onward bus to Be\u010dej.\nGet around.\nWalking is enough for the centre. From the main square, the museum is a few minutes away, the sports centre about 10 minutes and the Tisa riverbank about 15 minutes. The flat terrain also suits bicycles, although cycle lanes are patchy.\nA taxi is useful for the bus station with luggage and for places outside town, especially Fantast Castle, Ribnjak and the Old Tisa. Agree on a fare before a longer trip.\nBuy.\nThe green market sells fruit, vegetables, cheese, honey and household goods and is busiest in the morning. Bring cash in Serbian dinars.\nSeveral times a year, the Flower Market (\"Cvetna pijaca\") brings plant growers, food producers and craft stalls to the main square. Dates vary from year to year.\nEat.\nLocal menus mix Serbian and Hungarian influences. River fish, fish soup (\"riblja \u010dorba\"), paprika-flavoured stews and grilled meat are common. Restaurant kitchens sometimes close before the bar.\nDrink.\nCaf\u00e9s around the main square are the usual choice for coffee or an evening drink. Friday and Saturday are noticeably livelier than weeknights.\nSleep.\nBook ahead on summer weekends. A few private apartments operate around the riverbank, but their names and availability change often.\nConnect.\nMobile coverage is reliable, and hotels and many caf\u00e9s have Wi-Fi. Serbia is outside the EU roaming area, so check roaming charges before using a foreign SIM card. ATMs are easy to find around the main square, but cash is useful at the market and in taxis.\nStay safe.\nBe\u010dej is generally quiet, and normal precautions are enough. Mosquitoes can be troublesome beside the Tisa from late spring to early autumn. Swim only in established areas: river currents and water levels change. The general emergency number is 112."}
{"id": "219796", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219796", "title": "El Impenetrable National Park", "text": "El Impenetrable National Park is the largest national park in the Chaco region of northern Argentina. The Chaco is a vast, hot, semi-arid lowland plain. It maintains a good state of conservation and pristine characteristics in many sectors, leading to the survival of abundant large fauna, such as jaguars, tapirs, and anteaters.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a large park covering an area of 128,000 hectares.\nHistory.\nThe park has a grisly history behind it. The park was once part of the vast land holdings in the Chaco owned by Manuel Roseo and operated as \"Estancia La Fidelidad\". Early in 2011, a nefarious con man and serial killer named Luis Ra\u00fal \"El Gusano\" Menocchio plotted to murder Roseo so he could get control of his land. Menocchio forged a fake bill of sale and hired hit men to kill Roseo in his home. Authorities saw through his plot and instead of giving him title to Roseo's lands, they gave him and his accomplices life prison sentences. The lands were then expropriated by the government. The lands are being used as a protected natural area. In 2017, about 128,000 hectares of this land were decreed as the \"Parque Nacional El Impenetrable\". \nThe park is managed for the federal government by the \"Administraci\u00f3n de Parques Nacionales\" \u0308(National Parks Administration) in cooperation with regional conservation organizations including Fundaci\u00f3n Rewilding Argentina and the Tompkins Conservancy. \nLandscape.\nThe landscape is flat with vast grassland savannas, flat alluvial plains with low thorn forests, and scrub woodlands.\nFlora and fauna.\nThe Chaco is a biodiversity hotspot with more than 3,000 cataloged plant species along nearly 500 birds, 150 mammals, and more than 100 reptiles. The types and concentrations of plants and animals changes across the region as the climate transitions from the moist and humid \"wet Chaco\" in the east to the drier, more arid areas of the \"dry Chaco\" in the west. \nEl Impenetrable National Park is in the \"dry Chaco\" and the predominate landscape is flat, semi-dry grasslands and rocky plains. There are, however, seasonal wetlands that are wet only during a few months of the year, and there are aluvial woodlands along the Rio Bermejo and Rio Bermejito. \nPlants include the \"Quebracho\" tree, which comes in red and white varieties and was once harvested for tannins; the \"Carob\" tree, which is an important food source for both wildlife and local human communities; and \"Palo Santo\", an aromatic tree that is a protected species. Probably the most widespread plant is the \"vinal\", a thorny shrub that aggressively expands its growing area and is regarded as a scourge in most of the country. There are also areas where various cacti and palms can be found.\nThree mammals dominate conservation discussions in the park\u02d0 jaguars, tapirs, and anteaters. The jaguar is not widely seen due to its secretive habits but is being studied and introduced to the park as part of a rewilding project. The South American tapir is a threatened species that is related to the horse and the rhinoceros. Tapirs are herbivorous but are an important part of the local ecosystem because they spread seeds throughout the region. Jaguars also find them to be quite tasty, as do pumas and the occasional maned wolf, which also thrive in the park. \nThe park's rewilding projects are also reintroducing species such as guanacos, giant river otters, marsh deer, and pampas deer.\nThe park's varied landscapes, with grasslands, wetlands, and aluvial forests draws a mix of bird species, including raptors, ground dwellers like the giant rhea, and tree dwellers like songbirds and the red ovenbird (\"Rufous Hornero\"), which is Argentina's national bird. \nReptiles include iguanas, anoles, skinks and several kinds of grassland and woodland snakes, including the boa constrictor. \nClimate.\nThe park has a hot, humid wet season and a mild dry season. The wet season is October to March and has blisteringly hot temperatures (daytime highs of 40\u00b0C to 50\u00b0C). The winter months of May through September are mild with temperatures of 20\u00b0C to 30\u00b0C, but this is the driest time of the year so carry plenty of water with you. May to August are the most pleasant times of year to visit. \nGet in.\nThe closest cities with commercial airports are Corrientes or Resistencia. Both cities are about 5 hours by car from El Impenetrable National Park. The best way to get to the park is by renting a 4WD truck. \nBy car.\nIf you start in Corrientes, cross the river to Resistencia. Depart Resistencia by driving northwest on National Route RN16 for about 2 hours (177 km) to Presidencia Roque S\u00e1enz Pe\u00f1a, Turn north on RN95 towards Juan Jos\u00e9 Castelli for 1-1/2 hours (116 km). Note\u02d0 Google Maps sometimes tells you to take Provincial Route 9 instead of RN16 and RN95. Doing so can save you a few miles but the road is not as well maintained as the federal highways and service stations are more infrequent. Most foreign travelers would be well advised to use the larger, better roads.\nJuan Jose Castelli is your last chance to buy gas and supplies. It is also wise to inquire about road conditions into the park as the road is sometimes washed out or impassible even with 4WD.\nOnce you leave Juan Jose Castelli, you will travel Provincial Road 9 about 40 km to La Armonia where you will turn onto unpaved back roads that are either muddy and washed out by summer rains or dry, dusty and full of potholes by the winter dry season. Either way, you'll be glad you rented the 4WD truck. It is about 60 km to the park ranger station, on the banks of the Rio Bermejo. \nFees and permits.\nThere is no entry fee to go into the park, but registration at the park office is mandatory and a pre-advisement (\"Intendencia\") is recommended. There are times when the park is unreachable or conditions make travel ill-advised. Using a local guide is not required, but is highly recommended.\nBuy, eat, and drink.\nAll park visitors must be completely self-sufficient. There is no park store and the nearest source of anything is about 3 hours away by car.\nSleep.\nThere are some established camping areas in the park as well as local operators offering \"glamping\" experiences.\nCamping.\nThere are established campsites with varying amenities and services\u02d0\nBackcountry.\nDispersed backcountry camping is prohibited."}
{"id": "219799", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219799", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Windtoy Reef", "text": "The dive site Windtoy Reef is an offshore rocky reef in the Off-Whittle Eastern Reefs area of False Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\n S34\u00b014.423', E018\u00b035.191', northeast of Whittle Rock.\nUnderstand.\nPosition.\nThe name \"Windtoy Reef\" refers to the large shoals of juvenile windtoy fish seen over the higher rocks of the reef during the first recorded exploration dive in August 2026.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 40\u00a0m on the surrounding sand. The top of the shallowest point is about 24\u00a0m. Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 35\u00a0m. The site has been surveyed by multibeam sonar to a resolution of about half a metre.\nTopography.\nThe reef comprises a relatively small area of high profile outcrops and large boulders surrounded by lower profile reef and relatively small boulders and rubble. \nGeology:\nPre-Cambrian granite, probably of the \"Peninsula\" pluton. Jointing appears to be fairly closely spaced.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to wind from all directions, and swell from both southwest and less often, from southeast, so should be dived when the forecast is for low swell in the bay, and light to moderate winds Good conditions may occur at any time of the year, as may bad conditions.\nThis is an area which sometimes has red tides, or lesser algal blooms following a period of strong sunshine, resulting in poor visibility in the surface waters. The depth of the murky water will increase over time and strength of sunshine. There may be clearer water at depth, but it is not reliably predictable.\nGet in.\nThe sites are only accessible by boat, usually from Simon's Tow,Gordon's Bay Old Harbour or Harbour Island marina.\nAnchoring is not recommended. The site is on unpredictable holding ground, and there is a good chance that the anchor will either drag or foul, possibly both, and a rope rode may chafe through during a dive. This is a site where only the suicidally foolhardy would dive from an unattended boat, as it may not be possible to swim back to the boat on the surface due to current, and besides dragging or fouling, the anchor tackle will do undesirable and unnecessary ecological damage. Leave the boat in the charge of a competent person who will be able to pick up stray divers and call for help in an emergency. If for some reason you really must dive from an anchored boat, a distance line to either the anchor cable or directly to the boat will improve the chances of getting back safely.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nNo known site-specific hazargs.\nSkills.\nNo special skills recommended\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nOther offshore dive sites of False Bay:\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of reef dive sites in the Whittle Rock and surrounds offshore area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "219800", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219800", "title": "Tatale", "text": "Tatale is the capital of Tatale-Sanguli District in the Volta Basin, on the border with Togo. It is a quiet farming and border market town in the eastern Oti River area in the Volta Basin, known for yams, border trade, and as the Ghanaian gateway to Koutammakou in Togo. \nUnderstand.\nHistory\nTatale grew as a Konkomba and Basare farming settlement and border crossing. It became a district capital in 2012 after splitting from Zabzugu.\nPeople\nPredominantly Konkomba (Bikpakpaam), Basare, and Kotokoli. Likpakpaal (Konkomba), French and Ewe spoken at border. Christianity and traditional religion strong, Islam present. Very traditional.\nClimate\nHot Guinea savanna, more humid than central north due to eastern hills.\nLandscape\nLow hills, savanna woodland, Oti River plains. Near Togo's Koutammakou hills.\nOrientation\nSmall town, one main road from Yendi to border. Border post 1km east of town. Market center, lorry station near market.\nGet in.\nYou can drive or fly into Tamale from other parts of the country.\nFrom Tamale:"}
{"id": "219806", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219806", "title": "Junnar", "text": "Junnar in western Maharashtra is best known for the Shivneri Fort, the birthplace of Shivaji, the founder of the Maratha Empire. Other than that, the town is home to various cave temples.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThere are ST buses from Pune (Shivaji Nagar Bus Stand) every hour from 6:30AM, and from Kalyan (near Mumbai) every 10\u201330 minutes from 5:20AM. In addition, there are buses from Ahmednagar (Ahilyanagar) and Nashik."}
