{"id": "219817", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219817", "title": "Tumu", "text": "Tumu is a bustling town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains on the border with Burkina Faso. The town serves as a vital cross-border transit hub.\nUnderstand.\nTumu is the capital of the Sissala East Municipal District in the Upper West Region of northern Ghana. It is an economic engine for the region.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nDriving from Wa to Tumu by car is a journey of 138 kilometres and typically takes about 2 hours and 15 minutes, depending on road conditions.\nGet around.\nBy motorized tricycle (pragya / mahama camboo).\nThese drive along the main roads picking up multiple passengers to any part of the town. You pay a very cheap, fixed local fare per drop depending on the distance. If you're new to the system, confirm the price before getting in.\nOn foot.\nWalking is completely safe during the day, and it gives you the best chance to see local weavers and interact with community members.\nBuy.\nVisit the local markets to experience the lively community trading and pick up local crafts, grains, and daily goods as traders gather across the township.\nSleep.\nThere is no shortage of accommodation options in Tumu. Because it is easy to get around with yellow-yellows, even accommodation options far away from the centre of town should be considered. That said, it does help to choose one located close to a main road."}
{"id": "219822", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219822", "title": "Iluka", "text": "Iluka is a small, sleepy beachside coastal village of 1,800 (2021) in the Clarence Valley region of New South Wales' Northern Rivers. The town's nature reserve, the Iluka Nature Reserve, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, as part of the Gondwana Rainforests of Australia.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nIluka is around 17 km from the Iluka Road Exit on the M1 Pacific Motorway, the primary road between Sydney and Brisbane. The feeder road is reasonably maintained, with an 80 km/h speed limit. \nNote there's no direct road access from Yamba without using the motorway.\nBy coach.\nNSW Trainlink operates 1 daily coach in each direction from Grafton and Ballina, connecting to the Casino/Brisbane XPT (the daytime North Coast service). The timetable is as follows.\nBy bus.\nThe 386 bus route (timetable) from Maclean operates thrice a day. Make sure to have some cash on hand with you, as some buses may not have EFTPOS terminals.\nBy ferry.\nAs of August 2026, Clarence River Ferries operates 4 daily shuttle ferries from Yamba to (leaving at 9:30AM, 11AM, 3:15PM and 4:45PM \u2013 note the 4:45PM service does not return to Yamba for the day), with a 5th service at 1:45PM on M, Tu, Th and Sa. Services do not run on Christmas Day or Australia Day.\nOne-way tickets cost $11.90 one way and half that ($5.95) for pensioners and children under 16. If you're travelling as a family, a pass (including return) for 2 adults and up to 4 children costs $80.\nBicycles are permitted on the ferry.\nGet around.\nThe town centre is small enough to be traversed on foot. However, the furthest you can likely go without a car is up to Iluka Bluff via the rainforest trail. Anything beyond that and you'll be significantly pressed.\nGo next.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" group=\"route1\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n \"type\": \"Feature\",\n \"properties\": {\n \"title\": \"Iluka Rainforest Walking Track\",\n \"stro