{"id": "219824", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219824", "title": "Karaga", "text": "Karaga is the capital of Karaga District in the Volta Basin region of Ghana. It\u2019s a small, predominantly Dagomba farming town about 60km northeast of Tamale. It\u2019s known as the gateway to eastern Dagbon, with a large traditional palace, a busy periodic market, and easy access to rural villages.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nKaraga is one of the traditional paramount chieftaincies of the Dagbon Kingdom. The town has historically been a seat of the Karaga chief, who is a senior chief under the Ya-Na, the King of Dagbon in Yendi. Like much of eastern Dagbon, its history is tied to agriculture, trade routes to Tamale and Yendi, and Dagomba warrior traditions. The Karaga Naa (chief of Karaga) holds a high rank within the Dagbon traditional hierarchy. Traditionally, the karaga skin is one of the gate-ways to the Yaa Naa (paramount king of Dagbon).\nPeople.\nThe town is almost entirely Dagomba. Dagbani is the main local language, with Hausa widely used in the market. English is spoken by some teachers, officials, and younger people. Islam is the dominant religion, which shapes festivals and daily life.\nClimate.\nKaraga has a hot semi-arid climate.\nLandscape.\nThe area is flat savanna with scattered baobab and shea trees, small farms of yams, maize, millet, groundnuts, and soybeans, and traditional round mud-brick compounds where extended families live together. \nOrientation.\nKaraga is compact. The market, lorry station, chief\u2019s palace, and main guesthouses are all within 10-15 minutes walk of each other. Everything radiates out from the market road. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe main way in is by shared taxi or tro-tro from Tamale Station. The road is partly paved, partly laterite. In the dry season, it takes 1.5 - 2.5 hours. In rainy season, allow 3-4 hours. Cost is 100-115 GH\u20b5 (Aug 2026). \nYou can also get a shared taxi from Yendi or Gushegu via feeder roads. There is no formal bus service \u2014 ask at the lorry station.\nThe nearest airport is Tamale Airport.\nGet around.\nThe town is small enough to walk. For longer distances:\nEat.\nAround Karaga Market a simple chop bars serving Tuo Zaafi (TZ) \u2014 maize or millet dough with okra, groundnut, or ayoyo soup."}
{"id": "219827", "revid": "155858", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219827", "title": "Baldock", "text": "Baldock is a town with a population of 10,615 (in 2021) in Hertfordshire. The River Ivel rises north of the town from springs.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe A1(M) is an accessible motorway to reach Baldock. Alternatively, you can use the A505, A507 or the B656 if you\u2019re not using the motorway.\nBy train.\nFrom London, use the Great Northern or Thameslink departing from London Kings Cross Station which takes 30-45 minutes. \nGet around.\nBy bus.\nIt can be traversed by car and is not too large to traverse by walking. However, there are bus services operated mostly by Arriva, that often go to Hitchin.\nBy bike.\nThere are pedestrian paths to bike, especially near Avenue Park.\nDrink.\nThere are 8 pubs in Baldock, these include:"}
{"id": "219828", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219828", "title": "Green Velo", "text": "The Green Velo Eastern Cycle Trail is a long-distance cycle route running from Elbl\u0105g through Warmia-Masuria, Podlaskie, Lubelskie, Roztocze, Podkarpackie and the \u015awi\u0119tokrzyskie region to Ko\u0144skie in Poland. Riding the entire route involves about 2,100 km and usually takes from a couple of weeks to several weeks, but Green Velo is also well suited to shorter trips thanks to the many towns along the route with railway connections.\nGreen Velo is not a single, continuously separated cycleway. It uses cycle paths, quiet paved local roads, forest roads, gravel tracks and unpaved roads. Most of the route is technically easy, although sections in the Suwa\u0142ki region, Roztocze and especially between Przemy\u015bl, Dyn\u00f3w and Rzesz\u00f3w are more demanding.\nUnderstand.\nKey facts.\nPublished route lengths differ slightly between maps and publications. When planning a particular section, use the current track in the official Green Velo planner rather than relying solely on distances given in older guidebooks.\nWaymarking and navigation.\nThe trail is marked with distinctive orange signs from the Polish R-4 family. They indicate the course of the route, turns, destinations, distances and access to Cyclist Service Areas and selected attractions.\nThe waymarking is generally easy to recognise, but additional navigation is advisable on a multi-day journey. Inspections have found local gaps in the signage in the past, and signs may temporarily be removed or obscured during roadworks.\nUseful equipment includes:\nMOR and MPR.\nThere are 228 Cyclist Service Areas (\"Miejsca Obs\u0142ugi Rowerzyst\u00f3w\", MOR) along the route. A typical MOR includes a shelter, benches, bicycle stands, litter bins and an information board. Facilities vary from site to site.\nMORs should not be confused with Cyclist-Friendly Places (\"Miejsca Przyjazne Rowerzystom\", MPR). MPRs are businesses and attractions participating in the Green Velo scheme, including:\nFor current participating establishments, use the Green Velo database. Individual accommodation and restaurant recommendations are also covered in the relevant Wikivoyage destination guides.\nPrepare.\nChoosing a bicycle.\nThe most versatile choices for the complete route are a trekking or touring bicycle, a cross bike or a gravel bike. A mountain bike is not necessary for most of Green Velo, but performs well on forest and unpaved sections.\nA road bike with very narrow tyres is an uncomfortable choice for the whole route. Even where most of a stage is paved, several-kilometre stretches of gravel, earth or forest road may occur.\nFor a multi-day trip, consider carrying:\nWhen to go.\nThe most predictable conditions are from late spring to early autumn. Each region has its own characteristics:\nSupplies.\nThere is no need to carry several days' worth of food, but some parts of the trail pass through very small settlements where shops may have limited opening hours or operate only seasonally.\nCarry additional water and food especially when crossing:\nDo not rely on MOR rest areas as your only source of water.\nAccommodation and camping.\nAccommodation is very unevenly distributed. Larger towns have hotels, guesthouses, apartments and private rooms, while in sparsely populated areas it is advisable to plan overnight stops in advance.\nUseful natural stage bases include Elbl\u0105g, Braniewo, Lidzbark Warmi\u0144ski, Bartoszyce, W\u0119gorzewo, Go\u0142dap, Suwa\u0142ki, August\u00f3w, Goni\u0105dz, Tykocin, Bia\u0142ystok, Supra\u015bl, Bia\u0142owie\u017ca, W\u0142odawa, Che\u0142m, Zwierzyniec, Horyniec-Zdr\u00f3j, Przemy\u015bl, Rzesz\u00f3w, Le\u017cajsk, Sandomierz, Kielce and Ko\u0144skie.\nIn forests administered by the Polish State Forests, camping is possible in designated areas of the Zanocuj w lesie (\"Spend the night in the forest\") programme. Always check the current programme map and the rules of the relevant forest district. Restrictions on fires and camping stoves may change according to wildfire risk.\nDesignated campsites in Biebrza National Park include:\nCheck current rules and availability on the Biebrza National Park website[dead link].\nGet in.\nGreen Velo can be started at either Elbl\u0105g or Ko\u0144skie, but there are many places along the route where shorter trips can begin or end.\nThe most useful railway stations include:\nAs of August 2026, additional seasonal trains useful for cyclists may operate in summer, including services towards W\u0142odawa. Do not include a seasonal service in your itinerary without checking the current timetable.\nWhen planning to take a bicycle on a train, check:\nRoute.\nGreen Velo can be divided in several ways. A practical overview uses 12 large regions corresponding approximately to the official cycling \"kingdoms\". Distances below are approximate planning values; the current GPS track may differ slightly.\nDo not treat the 12 sections as one-day stages. Most require two or three days of relaxed cycling.\nVistula Lagoon.\nRoute: Elbl\u0105g \u2013 Elbl\u0105g Upland \u2013 Frombork \u2013 Braniewo.\nThe beginning of the route combines two very different landscapes: the flat Vistula delta and the hillier, forested Elbl\u0105g Upland. Near Frombork, Green Velo passes close to the Vistula Lagoon.\nStage bases: Elbl\u0105g, Frombork, Braniewo.\nHighlights:\nConnections: EuroVelo 10, EuroVelo 13, R1 and the Elbl\u0105g Canal Cycle Route.\nWarmia and surroundings.\nRoute: Braniewo area \u2013 Pieni\u0119\u017cno \u2013 Lidzbark Warmi\u0144ski \u2013 Bartoszyce.\nThis section crosses the sparsely populated countryside of Warmia. The landscape is more rolling than the Vistula delta, but is not technically difficult. Distances between sizeable towns are greater than near the beginning of the route.\nStage bases: Lidzbark Warmi\u0144ski, Bartoszyce.\nHighlights:\nConnections:\nNorthern Masuria.\nRoute: Bartoszyce area \u2013 W\u0119gorzewo \u2013 Go\u0142dap \u2013 Sta\u0144czyki.\nGreen Velo crosses the quieter northern part of Masuria. Do not expect the busy resort atmosphere of Gi\u017cycko or Miko\u0142ajki: parts of this section are sparsely populated and noticeably hilly.\nStage bases: W\u0119gorzewo, Go\u0142dap.\nHighlights:\nConnection: at W\u0119gorzewo you can join the Masurian Cycle Loop and continue around the Great Masurian Lakes.\nSuwa\u0142ki region and August\u00f3w Forest.\nRoute: Sta\u0144czyki area \u2013 Suwa\u0142ki \u2013 Wigry National Park \u2013 August\u00f3w Forest \u2013 August\u00f3w.\nThis is one of the more varied northern parts of Green Velo. There are climbs as well as forest and unpaved roads. Around Wigry and August\u00f3w Forest, do not expect continuous asphalt.\nStage bases: Suwa\u0142ki, August\u00f3w.\nHighlights:\nPractical: refill water and food before entering longer sections of August\u00f3w Forest.\nConnection: the Suwa\u0142ki and August\u00f3w region has connections with EuroVelo 11/R11.\nBiebrza and Narew valleys.\nRoute: August\u00f3w \u2013 Osowiec-Twierdza \u2013 Goni\u0105dz \u2013 Tsar's Road \u2013 Narew area \u2013 Tykocin.\nOne of the flattest and most scenic sections of Green Velo, passing through extensive marshlands and river valleys.\nStage bases: Goni\u0105dz/Osowiec-Twierdza and Tykocin; Bia\u0142ystok is the nearest major service centre.\nHighlights:\nOsowiec-Twierdza is particularly convenient logistically: it has a railway station, the headquarters of Biebrza National Park and a campsite.\nBia\u0142owie\u017ca and Knyszyn forests.\nRoute: Bia\u0142ystok \u2013 Supra\u015bl \u2013 Knyszyn Forest \u2013 Narew area \u2013 Hajn\u00f3wka \u2013 Bia\u0142owie\u017ca / Narewka.\nThis is one of the most heavily forested parts of the entire journey. It includes both good asphalt roads and stretches of earth and forest track.\nStage bases: Bia\u0142ystok, Supra\u015bl, Hajn\u00f3wka, Bia\u0142owie\u017ca or Narewka.\nHighlights:\nBug valley.\nRoute: southern Podlaskie \u2013 Kleszczele \u2013 Mielnik area \u2013 Jan\u00f3w Podlaski \u2013 Terespol \u2013 S\u0142awatycze.\nThis section has a quiet, rural character. Settlements are scattered, so supplies should be planned in advance on longer stretches.\nStage bases: Mielnik area, Jan\u00f3w Podlaski, Terespol.\nHighlights:\nCrossing the Bug.\nAs of August 2026:\nPolesie.\nRoute: S\u0142awatycze \u2013 W\u0142odawa \u2013 Sobib\u00f3r area \u2013 Wola Uhruska \u2013 Che\u0142m \u2013 Krasnystaw area.\nThe area around W\u0142odawa is almost completely flat. Further south-west, the landscape gradually becomes more varied. Unpaved surfaces occur in forest sections, including the Sobib\u00f3r forests.\nStage bases: W\u0142odawa/Okuninka, Che\u0142m.\nHighlights:\nSeasonal summer trains may operate to W\u0142odawa. Check the current timetable before relying on them.\nRoztocze.\nRoute: Krasnystaw area \u2013 Szczebrzeszyn \u2013 Zwierzyniec \u2013 J\u00f3zef\u00f3w \u2013 Narol \u2013 Horyniec-Zdr\u00f3j.\nRoztocze is one of the most varied parts of Green Velo, with forests, river valleys, short climbs and unpaved stretches.\nStage bases: Zwierzyniec, J\u00f3zef\u00f3w/Narol, Horyniec-Zdr\u00f3j.\nHighlights:\nConnection: around Zwierzyniec and J\u00f3zef\u00f3w, Green Velo connects with the Central Roztocze Cycle Route; the two routes share some stretches.\nCarpathian foothills.\nRoute: Przemy\u015bl \u2013 Przemy\u015bl Foothills \u2013 Dyn\u00f3w \u2013 Dyn\u00f3w Foothills \u2013 Rzesz\u00f3w.\nStage bases: Przemy\u015bl, Dyn\u00f3w area, Rzesz\u00f3w.\nHighlights:\nA gravel, trekking or mountain bike with sufficiently wide tyres is particularly useful on this section.\nSandomierz region and lower San valley.\nRoute: Rzesz\u00f3w / \u0141a\u0144cut \u2013 Le\u017cajsk \u2013 San valley \u2013 Sandomierz.\nThe Podkarpackie part of this section is generally easier than the Carpathian foothills. The terrain becomes hillier towards Sandomierz.\nStage bases: Rzesz\u00f3w/\u0141a\u0144cut, Le\u017cajsk, Sandomierz.\nHighlights:\n\u015awi\u0119tokrzyskie Mountains and surroundings.\nRoute: Sandomierz area \u2013 Rak\u00f3w area \u2013 Kielce \u2013 Obl\u0119gorek \u2013 Sielpia Wielka \u2013 Ko\u0144skie.\nThe final part of Green Velo is hilly but generally less demanding than the Dyn\u00f3w and Przemy\u015bl foothills.\nStage bases: Sandomierz, Kielce, Ko\u0144skie.\nHighlights:\nKo\u0144skie is the natural western end of the journey and has a railway connection, so there is no need to arrange a car transfer after completing the trail.\nBicycle repair.\nThe network of repair shops is adequate when a breakdown occurs near a larger town, but distances between full-service workshops can be substantial.\nPlaces where you are most likely to find a full bicycle repair service include:\nSome towns have self-service bicycle repair stations, but they should not be treated as a continuous network available every few kilometres.\nConnections to other routes.\nGreen Velo forms a backbone for a substantial network of regional and international cycle routes.\nThe former R64 route around the Vistula Lagoon may still appear on older maps and signs, but it has formally been discontinued and should not be treated as a current maintained cycle route.\nStay safe.\nRoad traffic.\nA substantial part of Green Velo uses existing public roads. These are generally quiet local roads, but conditions vary. Take particular care:\nUnpaved and forest roads.\nConditions on unpaved sections can change significantly after:\nA bicycle capable of handling both asphalt and gravel is the most useful choice.\nNational parks.\nGreen Velo passes through or close to five national parks:\nAlways follow the local park regulations. Do not assume that every forest road shown on a map is open to cyclists.\nPolish\u2013Belarusian border.\nAs of August 2026, Green Velo runs through areas directly adjoining the Belarusian border. Restricted-access zones and other measures related to border security may periodically be introduced in the region.\nCurrent border information is available from granica.gov.pl.\nEmergency number.\nIn an emergency, call 112. On remote sections, give the operator your GPS coordinates or the nearest road number, settlement or identifiable landmark.\nPractical maps, notices and timetables.\nUseful sources to check shortly before departure:\nGo next.\nGreen Velo can form part of a much longer cycling journey."}
{"id": "219829", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219829", "title": "Piat", "text": "Piat is a town of Cagayan province in the Philippines. It is the pilgrimage center of the province, hosting a venerated image of the Virgin Mary dating from the 17th century.\nUnderstand.\nPiat was established by the Spanish in the early 17th century along the Chico River. It is part of the Itawis-speaking region of southwestern Cagayan.\nPiat is primarily an agricultural town, with additional income coming from Roman Catholic pilgrims who visit the town's Basilica Minore, hosting the venerated Marian image of Our Lady of the Visitation of Piat.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nPiat is along the Cagayan-Apayao Highway from Tuguegarao, and there are passenger vans going there from the latter city, although this entails waiting for an indefinite period for the vehicle to fill up. As the highway bypasses downtown Piat, motorists take a junction in Barangay Maguilling, where the highway turns left on its way to the neighboring municipality of Tuao. \nFive Star operates a nightly bus service to Piat that departs from Cubao station in Metro Manila every 9PM. The return journey leaves Piat at 3PM. Commuters taking the Victory Liner and GV Florida buses that run to neighboring Tuao can also disembark at Maguilling Junction. Most travelers instead switch vehicles in Tuguegarao, where there are more frequent services.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation within town. As the town center sits on a plateau on a road terminus, it is easy to get lost along the winding streets heading to locations beside the Basilica.\nBuy.\nThe area around Piat Basilica is packed with makeshift stalls and traders selling all kinds of products and souvenirs including religious items. The basilica also operates its own souvenir stores.\nEat.\nA local delicacy commonly sold around the Basilica is pawa, a glutinous rice cake bun filled with ground peanuts.\nSleep.\nMost standard accommodation is in Tuguegarao.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3527."}
{"id": "219926", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219926", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Stef's Reef", "text": "The dive site Stef's Reef or Stef's Ridge is an offshore rocky reef in the Off-Whittle Eastern Reefs region of False Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nStef's Reef was found during the multibeam sonar survey of False Bay during 2022 by Wreckless Marine. Its presence was previously not recorded, probably unknown. It is one of several relatively deep granite reefs in the region of False Bay known as Off-Whittle Eastern Reefs. It was first dived in August 2026, as part of a program of visiting the reefs of False Bay by SURGmap and Wreckless Divers.\n S34\u00b014.977' E018\u00b035.441', a short distance east of Bruce's Mark.\nPosition.\nThis site is not in a Marine Protected Area. A permit is not required.\nName.\nThe name \"Stef's Reef\" was given in recognition of one of the divers on the first recorded dive at the site.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth by multibeam sonar is about 40\u00a0m. and the top of the ridge is about 32\u00a0m. Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 36\u00a0m.\nVisibility.\nVisibility is likely to be similar to other offshore sites in False Bay with similar depth, distance offshore and recent weather history. Visibility may be better than on the inshore reefs, but as with any False Bay reef, it is not very predictable, and there may be better visibility at the bottom below a dirty surface layer of plankton, which can follow a period of strong sunshine. Visibility can exceed 20\u00a0m on rare and unpredictable occasions. The colour of the surface water is not a reliable indicator of the visibility at depth.\nTopography.\nThe site is an isolated granite outcrop in a group of granite outcrops known as the Off-Whittle Eastern Reefs This reef is distinguished by at least one long, high, steep sided, sharp peaked ridge. It appears to run roughly north-south, and there is a second ridge probably parallel to it near the mark.\nGeology: Pre-Cambrian granite, probably of the Peninsula pluton.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to wind and waves from all directions, and swells from the southwest, however it is quite deep, so short period waves will not affect conditions on the bottom greatly. Low short swell and light wind is best. The prevailing long period swell is from the southwest, and if long enough can cause surge even on the deep reef. Other waves are generally short period wind waves and chop, but the reef is far enough offshore for a vicious chop to develop in a strong wind, which can be very uncomfortable on the ride back to shore. The area is protected from north-westerly swell, and to a lesser extent south-westerly swell by the Cape Peninsula, but longer period south-westerly swell will refract round Cape Point, and though attenuated, will reach this area.\nGet in.\nThis site is only accessible by boat. It is about ?\u00a0km from Simon's Town Jetty, ?\u00a0km from Miller's Point slipway and ?\u00a0km from Gordon's Bay Old Harbour. On a good day in a fast boat it is about a half hour run from Simon's Town Jetty.\nAnchoring is not recommended. The holding ground is unreliable, and there is a good chance that the anchor will either drag or foul, possibly both, and a rope rode may chafe through during a dive. This is a site where only the suicidally foolhardy would dive from an unattended boat, and besides dragging or fouling, the anchor tackle will do undesirable and unnecessary ecological damage. Leave the boat in the charge of a competent person who will be able to pick up stray divers and call for help in an emergency.\nDo.\nExplore the reef and let us know what you see.\nRoutes.\nNo known routes recommended. The site needs more exploration and feedback.\nSee.\nPhotography.\nVisibility is unpredictable, so macro is most likely to produce good results. Artificial lighting will be necessary. A video light or modeling light will usually be useful, possibly with fill-in flash.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nNo known site-specific hazards, but most of the site is deeper than generally accepted for recreational diving.\nSkills.\nThe skills and knowledge to do safe decompression ascents is strongly recommended.\nEquipment.\nBailout gas, nitrox, DSMB and a good dive light are recommended.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nOther offshore dive sites of False Bay:\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of reef dive sites in the Whittle Rock and surrounds offshore area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
