{"id": "219954", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219954", "title": "Gambaga", "text": "Gambaga is the historic capital of East Mamprusi Municipal in the North East Region of Ghana located in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. Perched on the Gambaga Escarpment above the White Volta valley, it offers dramatic views, cooler breezes, and deep Mamprusi history. It was once the capital of the entire Northern Territories during British rule and is home to one of Ghana's oldest mosques and the famous Gambaga Witch Camp.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nGambaga is an ancient centre of the Mamprusi Kingdom, founded in the 15th century as part of the Kingdom of Naa Gbewaa the ancestral kingdom of the Mole-Dagbani groups (Mamprusi, Dagomba, Nanumba). From 1897 to 1907, the British made Gambaga the capital of the Northern Territories because of its strategic, healthy highland location and its proximity to French Upper Volta (Burkina Faso). The remains of old British colonial buildings can still be seen.\nPeople.\nPredominantly Mamprusi. Mampruli is the main language, with Hausa and English widely spoken in town. Islam is the dominant religion.\nClimate.\nHotter and drier than Tamale, but slightly cooler at night due to the elevation (~400 m).\nLandscape.\nTown sits on the edge of the Gambaga Escarpment a long cliff that marks the northern edge of Ghana's savanna plateau. From the scarp you get sweeping views north over the White Volta River and into Burkina Faso. Below is dry savanna with baobabs, shea trees, and farmland.\nOrientation.\nSmall town. Market, lorry station, chief\u2019s palace, old British cemetery, and mosque are all within a 10- to 15-min walk. The escarpment viewpoint is on the northern edge of town.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest airport is Tamale Airport.\nBy car.\nFrom Tamale: Take a tro-tro or shared taxi to Nalerigu (2.5-3.5 hr via Walewale), then a taxi/motorbike to Gambaga (10-15 min). Road Tamale-Walewale is paved, Walewale-Nalerigu-Gambaga is partly paved. Total GH\u20b560-100 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Bolgatanga: Via Walewale, 3-4 hours by shared car.\nFrom Walewale: Direct taxis to Gambaga/Nalerigu run frequently, 1-1.5 hr.\nEat.\nAlways drink bottled or sachet water."}
{"id": "219956", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219956", "title": "Chereponi", "text": "Chereponi is the capital of Chereponi District in the North East Region of the Ghanaian Northern Plains. It sits on the Ghana-Togo border in the far northeast corner of the country, serving as a remote border market town for Chokosi (Anufo), Konkomba, and Togolese traders. It\u2019s known for its international border market, traditional Chokosi chieftaincy, and as a gateway to Togo\u2019s Savanes Region.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nChereponi was a centre of the Chokosi (Anufo) people who migrated from Ivory Coast in the 18th century. The area sits at the intersection of Ghana and Togo, and was divided by colonial borders \u2014 Chereponi on the Ghana side, Mango and other Chokosi towns on the Togo side. It became a district capital in 2008.\nPeople.\nMain groups are Chokosi (Anufo) who are the traditional owners, plus Konkomba, Dagomba, and Bimoba. Anufo, Konkomba, and Hausa are widely spoken in the market. English is spoken by officials and teachers. Islam and Christianity are both present, with strong traditional beliefs.\nClimate.\nHot, semi-arid Guinea savanna.\nLandscape.\nFlat to gently rolling savanna, scattered shea and baobab, farmland of yams, maize, and soybeans. The border is marked by small streams and farmland \u2014 no fence.\nOrientation.\nIt is a very small town. Everything is along the main road to the border market, lorry station, district assembly, and guesthouses within 10 min walk. The Togo border is 1-2 km east of town centre.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom Tamale: Tamale \u2192 Yendi \u2192 Saboba \u2192 Chereponi. About 7-9 hours in dry season, can be 10-12+ in rainy season. Last stretch Saboba-Chereponi is unpaved and rough. Shared taxis run from Saboba.\nFrom Nakpanduri: Via Wenchiki, about 2-3 hours by motorbike/taxi in dry season, difficult in rains.\nFrom Togo: Foot crossing to villages near Mango. You must clear Ghana Immigration in Chereponi and Togo immigration on other side. Bring passport / ECOWAS ID.\nNo airport. Closest is Tamale Airport.\nEat.\nUse only bottled/sachet water."}
{"id": "219957", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219957", "title": "Nakpanduri", "text": "Nakpanduri is a dramatic escarpment town in East Mamprusi Municipal in the Ghanaian Northern Plains of North East Region of Ghana. Perched on the Gambaga Escarpment close to the Togo border, it is famous for breathtaking cliff views, cool breezes, and its old German-British border history. The town sits above the Oti River plain and offers some of the best sunset views in northern Ghana. \nUnderstand.\nHistory\nNakpanduri was an important village on the edge of the Mamprusi Kingdom and later a colonial border post. When Togo was German Togoland, the escarpment marked the boundary between British Gold Coast and German territory. Old border pillars and stories of colonial patrols remain.\nPeople\nMainly Mamprusi and Konkomba, plus Bimoba. Mampruli, Konkomba, and Hausa spoken. English spoken by teachers and officials. Predominantly Muslim and traditional beliefs, with Christians as well.\nClimate\nHot but slightly cooler than lowlands due to elevation (~450m) and constant wind on the scarp.\nLandscape\nSteep escarpment dropping 200-300m to the plain below. On top: dry savanna plateau. Below: flat Oti River plain with farms extending into Togo. Baobab and shea trees cling to cliff edges.\nOrientation\nTown straddles the escarpment edge. Market and lorry station on plateau side, viewpoint and road down the cliff on the eastern edge. Small enough to walk in 15 min.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Tamale Airport.\nBy car.\nFrom Gambaga / Nalerigu: Shared taxi/motorbike, 1.5-2 hr east along dramatic escarpment road. Road is unpaved but graded, very scenic.\nFrom Bunkpurugu: 45 min - 1 hr west.\nFrom Chereponi: Via Wenchiki dirt road, 2-3 hr in dry season, difficult in rains.\nFrom Togo: Small foot paths down escarpment into Togo. Formal border crossing is further south. Carry your passport.\nSleep.\nBetter lodging in Bunkpurugu (30-45 min) or Nalerigu (1.5-2 hrs)."}
{"id": "219960", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219960", "title": "Abucay", "text": "Abucay is a town in Bataan, Central Luzon, the Philippines. Situated at the eastern side of the Bataan peninsula, it has natural and historic attractions, including the oldest Roman Catholic church in Bataan. \nUnderstand.\nAbucay was founded by the Spanish in 1588, and hosted the country's first printing press. In 1647, the town was briefly seized by the Dutch following a bloody battle, with much of its population either massacred or deported to the Dutch East Indies. The town was devastated again during World War II in the Battle of Bataan in 1942. The Bataan Death March also passed through the town.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nAbucay lies along Roman Superhighway (Route 301), the main six-lane transport artery through eastern Bataan which bypasses downtown Abucay. Bataan National Road, a narrow two-lane thoroughfare, passes through the town center. The two highways are connected to each other by capillary roads.\nAbucay is connected by minibuses to the provincial capital Balanga. From Metro Manila, Genesis and Victory Liner operate daily bus trips on their way to Balanga and Mariveles.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays. \nStay safe.\nThe lowland areas of Abucay are prone to flooding during the rainy season.\nConnect.\nAbucay's postal code is 2114."}
{"id": "219961", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219961", "title": "Bunkpurugu", "text": "Bunkpurugu is the capital of Bunkpurugu-Nakpanduri District in the Ghanaian Northern Plains region of Ghana. It's a busy market town just west of the Togo border and just below the Gambaga Escarpment, serving Bimoba, Mamprusi, and Konkomba communities. It functions as the commercial hub for this far corner of the North East Region, with a large market, lorry station, and better guesthouses than surrounding villages.\nUnderstand.\nHistory. \nBunkpurugu grew as a market center for the Bimoba people and surrounding Mamprusi communities. It became a district capital in 2004. Its location near the Togo border and below the Nakpanduri escarpment made it a natural collection point for yams, livestock, and imported goods.\nPeople. \nMainly Bimoba (the traditional owners), plus Mamprusi and Konkomba. Moar (Bimoba language), Mampruli, and Hausa are widely spoken in the market. English spoken by officials, teachers, health workers. Mix of Christianity, Islam, and traditional beliefs.\nClimate. \nHot semi-arid savanna.\nLandscape\nFlat to gently rolling savanna at the foot of the Gambaga Escarpment. Scattered baobab and shea, farmland of yams, millet, groundnuts, and soybeans. Nakpanduri escarpment rises sharply to the west.\nOrientation. \nLinear town along the main road from Nalerigu to the Togo border. Market, lorry station, district assembly, and guesthouses all along or just off main road, walkable in 15-20 min.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Tamale Airport.\nBy car.\nFrom Nalerigu / Gambaga: Shared taxi/motorbike, 1.5-2 hrs northeast via Nakpanduri. Road is unpaved but graded and scenic.\nFrom Tamale: Tamale \u2192 Walewale \u2192 Nalerigu \u2192 Nakpanduri \u2192 Bunkpurugu. Total 5-7 hr in dry season.\nFrom Togo: Small border crossing east of town to Togo villages. Formal immigration in Bunkpurugu. Bring passport/ECOWAS ID.\nSleep.\nThe best hotel options in the region remain to be the Baptist Medical Center area guesthouses in Nalerigu."}
{"id": "219985", "revid": "2363356", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219985", "title": "Gyirong", "text": "Gyirong (also Kyirong; Chinese: \u5409\u9686 \"J\u00edl\u00f3ng\") is a town and county in Xigaze prefecture, at the border of Tibet and Nepal.\nUnderstand.\nGyirong is one of China's main border crossings with Nepal. The border crossing itself is known as (\u5409\u9686\u53e3\u5cb8 \"J\u00edl\u00f3ng k\u01d2u'\u00e0n\") or Resuo (\u70ed\u7d22 \"R\u00e8su\u01d2\") to differentiate it from the town of, which lies some 25 km north of the border.\nThe Nepalese side is known as Rasuwa Gadhi (\u0930\u0938\u0941\u0935\u093e\u0917\u0922\u0940), located in Rasuwa District across the Trishuli River from Gyirong Port. The nearest towns are (3 km south) and (also Syafru, Syabru, Syabrubesi etc; 15 km south).\nHistorically, Dram (Zhangmu)-Kodari was the main crossing between Nepal and China, but the Friendship Bridge there was destroyed by earthquakes in 2015, closing the border for four years. This made the Rasuwa Gadhi\u2013Gyirong corridor the primary overland hub for travelers, trade, and pilgrims heading between Kathmandu and Lhasa or Mount Kailash. However, devastating floods and a massive landslide hit Gyirong in 2026, which will likely reverse these fates yet again.\nGet in.\nForeigners cannot use a standard Chinese tourist visa issued at an embassy to enter China here, you must hold a Chinese Group Visa (arranged through a Kathmandu travel agency) and a Tibet Travel Permit. See Tibet#Get in for details. \nGoing in the other direction, Nepal Visa-on-Arrival is available at the Rasuwa Gadhi immigration post for eligible nationalities. Bring passport photos and USD cash for the visa fee.\nThe border operates during daytime hours (typically 09:30\u201315:30 Nepal Time / 11:45\u201317:45 China Standard Time). You will need to walk across the bridge on foot.\nFrom Kathmandu.\nDaily shared jeeps and public buses depart early in the morning from Machha Pokhari (Bus Park) in Kathmandu toward Syabrubesi or Timure. The drive takes 7 to 10 hours covering roughly 175 km, though road conditions can be rough and prone to delays during the monsoon.\nFrom Tibet.\nForeign passport holders crossing from Tibet into Nepal must travel in vehicles arranged by a licensed Chinese/Tibetan tour agency, as independent travel in Tibet is prohibited. Vehicles drop travelers at the Chinese immigration control at Gyirong Port. A massive landslide in 2026 buried the road and destroyed the border crossing facilities, so this crossing is closed until further notice.\nGet around.\nThe border area between the customs posts and the nearby staging towns is best traversed on foot or via local shuttles/taxis:\nSleep.\nAccommodation on the Nepalese side is basic. Most travelers stay overnight in Syabrubesi or Timure before crossing the border early the next morning."}
{"id": "219990", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219990", "title": "Mauritian Creole phrasebook", "text": "Mauritian Creole (\"Kreol\" or \"Morisyen\") is one of the national languages of Mauritius. The orthography used in this phrasebook is renewed by the Mauritian government in 2011. In this orthography, the word \"Morisyen\" is spelled \"morisien\".\n\"Some in this still need to be translated. If you know anything about this language, you can by and.\"\nPronunciation guide.\nVowels.\nA a (pronounced like the a in cat) \nE e (pronounced like the ay in day yet shorter, same as \u00e9 in French)\nI i (pronounced like the ee in see)\nO o (pronounced like the o in box)\nU u (only used in loan words from English, pronounced like the u in cut) \nou (pronounced like oo in too yet shorter, same as in French)\nNasal vowels include an, en, in, on and oun:\nConsonants.\nB = b as in bait;\nD = d as in date;\nF = f as in fate;\nG = g as in gate; \nJ = j as in jade;\nK = k as in kite;\nL = l as in late; \nN = n as in navy;\nM = m as in mate;\nP = p as in papa; \nR = French guttural 'r';\nS = s as in sage;\nT = t as in tape;\nV = v as in vain;\nW = w as in wait;\nX = gz in French-based loanwords, ks elsewhere;\nY = y as in yeah; \nZ = z as in zero;\nch = ch as in chat;\nsh = sh as in shy;\nng = ng as in wing;\ngn is the same as that in French, which may be compared to gn in lasagna.\nNote that when a vowel is followed by n, it always becomes nasal, but the n itself is usually unpronounced. Only when another n follows the n that nasalises the preceding vowel, it is pronounced as normal.\nPhrase list.\n\"Some in this still need to be translated. If you know anything about this language, you can by and.\"\nTime.\nClock time.\none o'clock - enn-er (inn-er also common)\ntwo o'clock - di-z-er\nthree o'clock - trwa-z-er\nfour o'clock - kat-r-er\nfive o'clock - sink-er\nsix o'clock - si-z-er\nseven o'clock - set-er\neight o'clock - wit-er\nnine o'clock - nef-er\nten o'clock - di-z-er\neleven o'clock - onz-er\nmorning - gramatin\nnoon / midday - midi\nafternoon - apremidi / tanto\nnight - lanwit\nmidnight - minwi\nnow - aster(la)\ntoday - zordi\ntomorrow - demin\nMonths.\nJanuary - Zanvie\nFebruary - Fevriye\nMarch - Mars\nApril - Avril\nMay - Me\nJune - Zi(e)n\nJuly - Zilie\nAugust - Out\nSeptember - Septam\nOctober - Oktob\nNovember - Novam\nDecember - Desam\nColours.\nrouz - red\nblan - white\nver - green\nble - blue\nzonn - yellow\nnwar - black\ngri - grey\nroz - pink\nzoranz - orange\nTransportation.\nBus and train.\n'Where does this bus go to? ': 'Sa bis-la kot ale?'\n'How many trains are there? ': 'Komie trin ena?'\nTaxi.\n'Please take me to this address. ': 'Amenn mwa sa ladres-la, silvouple.'\n'I want to get off here. ': 'Mo anvi desann isi.'\n'How much is the taxi fare? ': 'Enn traze par taxi komie pou koute?'\nLodging.\n'I'm calling to enquire about flats available for rent. ': 'Mo pe telefone par rapor a apartman ki ou pe Iwe.'\n'Can I view the flat today? ': 'Eski mo kapav vizit lakaz-la zordi?'\n'How much is the rent? ': 'Lwaye komie?'\nEating.\n'Could you show me the menu please? ': 'Ou kapav donn mwa meni, silvouple?'\n'Do you have any vegetarian/kosher/halal diet? ': 'Eski ou ena manze vezetarien/kasher/halal?'\n'Can I pay with credit card? ': 'Eski ou aksepte kart kredi?'\nBars.\n'How many kinds of beer do you have?': 'Ki labier zot ena?'\n'May I have a look at your wine list?': 'Eski mo kapav get zot seleksion divin?'\n'Cheers!': 'Chinn-chinn!'"}
{"id": "219991", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219991", "title": "Bole", "text": "Bole is a town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nBole is a major town located in the Savannah Region of Ghana, situated along the main highway connecting the southern parts of the country to the Upper West Region.\nGet in.\nFrom Kumasi: Drive or take a vehicle north along the main road for about 316 kilometers (roughly 4.5 to 5 hours).\nFrom Accra: Drive west/north through Kumasi or take a long-distance bus service toward the northern regions, which takes around 8 to 9 hours.\nFrom Tamale: Travel west via the major connecting regional highways toward the Savannah Region\nGet around.\nShared Taxis: These operate on fixed routes throughout the town and nearby settlements. They are the most affordable option, and you can flag them down along any main road.\n\"Drop-In\" (Private) Taxis: If you need to go to a specific destination quickly or have heavy luggage, you can negotiate a private fare (a \"drop-in\") with a local taxi driver before starting the trip.\nTricycles (Pragya / Mahama Can Do): These three-wheeled passenger tricycles are common for short trips around the inner streets and market squares. They offer a very cheap and breezy way to travel short distances.\nWalking: Since central Bole is relatively small, most essential spots, including local businesses, local markets, and the Bole District Hospital, are easily reachable on foot if you are staying in the heart of the town.\nBuy.\nShopping in Bole offers a fantastic opportunity to buy authentic northern Ghanaian agricultural products and traditional crafts. Because it is a major farming hub in the Savannah Region, you will get the freshest items directly from local producers.\nTraditional Northern Clothing &amp; Crafts Fugu (Traditional Smocks) also known as Batakari,Bolga Baskets,Pure Shea Butter, a traditional African locust bean seasoning used to flavor local soups and stews called Dawadawa and fresh yams straight from the local farmers at the market are products to be bought here.\nConnect.\nMTN Ghana offers the most reliable mobile network connection and widest 4G data coverage in Bole. While Bole is a major town in the Savannah Region, its remote location means connectivity can fluctuate compared to southern urban hubs\nGo next.\nFrom Bole, you are strategically positioned to explore some of the most famous wildlife, cultural landmarks, and cities in Northern Ghana. Depending on the direction you choose to travel, your best next destinations are Mole National Park (East), Wa (North), or Tamale (Northeast)."}
{"id": "219996", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219996", "title": "Sajek Valley", "text": "Sajek Valley (\u09ac\u09be\u0982\u09b2\u09be: \u09b8\u09be\u099c\u09c7\u0995 \u09ad\u09cd\u09af\u09be\u09b2\u09bf) is a hill station in Sajek Union of Baghaichhari Upazila, Rangamati District, Bangladesh. Situated among the hills of the Kasalong Range in the northern Chittagong Hill Tracts, it is widely known as the \"Queen of Hills\" and the \"Roof of Rangamati\".\nUnderstand.\nSajek Valley lies approximately 67 km northeast of Khagrachhari and about 95 km north of Rangamati town. Although it is part of Rangamati District, almost all visitors travel through Khagrachhari because of easier road access.\nThe valley is located about 1,800\u20132,000 ft (550\u2013610 m) above sea level and is surrounded by mountains, dense forests, grasslands and deep valleys. The Sajek River forms part of the Bangladesh\u2013India border and gives the valley its name.\nSajek is inhabited by several indigenous communities including Chakma, Marma, Tripura, Pankho and Lushai peoples. Their culture, traditional lifestyle and hospitality are among the main attractions for visitors.\nHistory.\nFor much of its history, Sajek remained one of the most remote regions of Bangladesh due to steep terrain and poor transportation. Tourism began to expand rapidly after a road network linking the valley with Khagrachhari was completed in the early 2010s. The road was constructed over roughly a decade and transformed the area's economy through tourism.\nTourism facilities grew rapidly, with the number of resorts increasing significantly after 2014.\nThe valley remained closed for several months during the COVID-19 pandemic before reopening in September 2020.\nIn February 2025, a major fire destroyed numerous resorts, cottages, houses and shops in Sajek, affecting the tourism industry.\nLandscape.\nThe valley is famous for its rolling hills, cloud-covered horizons, forests and panoramic viewpoints. During the monsoon and post-monsoon seasons, clouds often drift through the valleys below the hilltops, creating the famous \"sea of clouds\" effect.\nThe road to Sajek crosses the scenic Mayni Range and offers dramatic views of mountains and valleys.\nClimate.\nSajek can be visited throughout the year.\nThe peak tourist season is generally from September to March.\nWhen to go.\nSajek can be visited throughout the year, but the experience varies considerably by season.\nSeptember to March generally offers cooler temperatures and better visibility. The rainy season can bring mist, clouds and lush green scenery, but roads may become slippery and travel conditions can change quickly.\nWeekends and public holidays are usually much busier than weekdays. Travellers looking for a quieter experience should consider visiting during weekdays or outside major holidays.\nGet in.\nFrom Dhaka.\nMost visitors travel to Sajek via Khagrachhari.\nSecurity convoy.\nVehicles travelling to Sajek often move in scheduled convoys accompanied by security personnel, particularly on the Dighinala\u2013Baghaihat\u2013Sajek route.\nGet around.\nMost attractions can be explored on foot. Motorcycles are available for local transportation between viewpoints and villages.\nSee.\nRuilui Para.\nThe main settlement and tourism center of Sajek. Most resorts, restaurants and viewpoints are located here.\nKonglak Hill.\nOne of the highest accessible points in the valley. It offers panoramic views of surrounding hills and nearby indigenous villages. Konglak Para is among the last settlements before the Indian border.\nHelipad.\nThe most famous viewpoint in Sajek. Sunrise, sunset and sea-of-cloud views attract photographers from across Bangladesh.\nIndigenous Villages.\nVillages inhabited by Chakma, Tripura, Pankho and Lushai communities provide opportunities to learn about local culture and traditions.\nBuy.\nSmall local markets sell handicrafts, woven products, bamboo items and seasonal fruits.\nEat.\nRestaurants serve Bengali and indigenous dishes. Local vegetables, bamboo shoots and hill-grown produce are commonly available.\nSleep.\nAccommodation ranges from basic cottages to modern resorts. Advance reservations are highly recommended during weekends, holidays and the peak season.\nRespect.\nSajek is home to several indigenous communities. Visitors should respect local customs and ask permission before photographing people.\nConnect.\nMobile phone coverage is available in parts of Sajek, although signal strength can vary depending on location and weather. Internet access may be slower or less reliable than in major cities.\nWi-Fi is available at some hotels and resorts, but availability and speed vary. Travellers who need reliable internet should download maps and important information before travelling to Sajek.\nMobile network coverage may be limited in remote villages and on some sections of the mountain road."}
{"id": "219998", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=219998", "title": "Salaga", "text": "Salaga is the capital of East Gonja Municipal in the Volta Basin region of Ghana. Once one of the most important market towns in West Africa, it was the largest slave market in the region in the 18th and 19th centuries. Today it is a historic, predominantly Gonja town with a large market, old slave wells and cemeteries, and serves as a gateway between Tamale and the southern Volta Lake area.\nUnderstand.\nHistory \nSalaga's name comes from \"Salgi\" meaning \"To get used to a place\". In the 1700s-1800s it was a cosmopolitan trading center linking the Asante Kingdom in the south with Hausaland, Sokoto, and Timbuktu in the north. Kola nuts, gold, salt, cloth and slaves were traded here. The town was sacked in 1892 during the Gonja civil war (Kpembewura war) and never regained its former size, but its historic relics remain.\nPeople\nPredominantly Gonja, with Hausa, Dagomba, Konkomba, and Zongo communities. Gonja, Hausa, and Twi are widely spoken. English is spoken by officials. Islam is the dominant religion.\nClimate \nHot Guinea savanna.\nLandscape \nLow-lying savanna, scattered baobabs and shea trees. The town is flat, surrounded by farmland.\nOrientation \nSmall but spread out. The main market, lorry station, and historic sites (Salaga Slave Market, wells) are all within 10- to 15-min walk or a short motorbike ride in the old town.\nGet in.\nThe plane.\nThe closest major airport is Tamale Airport."}
{"id": "220003", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220003", "title": "Sawla", "text": "Sawla is the capital of Sawla-Tuna-Kalba District in Ghanaian Northern Plains in the Savannah Region of Ghana. It is a busy road junction town where the Wa - Damongo - Tamale highway meets, making it the main transit hub for western Savannah. Most travellers pass through Sawla when going to Mole National Park, Wa, Bole, or Buipe.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nSawla grew as a market settlement of Gonja, Vagla, and Dagaaba farmers and traders. Its importance rose sharply when the Wa-Damongo road was paved, turning it from a village into the district capital and commercial hub for the Tuna-Kalba area. It remains a farming and trading town.\nPeople.\nMixed population: Gonja, Vagla, Dagaaba, Brifor, and Fulani. Vagla and Gonja are widely spoken, and Waale/Dagaare and Hausa are spoken in the market. English is spoken by officials and teachers. Islam and Christianity are present.\nClimate.\nHot Guinea savanna.\nLandscape.\nFlat to gently rolling savanna with shea, baobab, and farms. The town is linear along the main highway.\nOrientation.\nVery easy \u2014 everything is on or just off the main Wa-Damongo road. Market, lorry station, guesthouses, fuel stations all within a 10-min walk.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest airports are Wa Airport ' and Tamale Airport '.\nBy car.\nFrom Wa: Tro-tro or shared taxi, 2-2.5 hr, paved road. GH\u20b550-80 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Damongo or Mole National Park: 1.5-2 hr, paved road. GH\u20b540-70 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Tamale: Via Damongo or Buipe, 4-5 hr. GH\u20b5100-150 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Bole: 30-45 min south, GH\u20b520-40 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Buipe: 2-2.5 hr, GH\u20b560-90 (Aug 2026).\nStay safe.\nSawla is a highway town \u2014 watch for fast traffic when crossing the road. It is generally safe, but keep valuables close to you in the market or lorry station.\nThere is a risk of malaria in Sawla so it is advised to use nets and mosquito repellent. The nearest good hospital is Sawla District Hospital; for serious cases go to Damongo or Wa."}
{"id": "220005", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220005", "title": "Buipe", "text": "Buipe is a river port and junction town in Central Gonja District in the Savannah Region of the Volta Basin of Ghana. Sitting on the Black Volta River and on the main Tamale - Kumasi highway, it is famous for the Buipe Bridge, its inland port, shea butter factories, and as the gateway between northern and southern Ghana.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBuipe has long been a Gonja trading settlement on the Black Volta. Its modern importance comes from the bridge that carries the north-south highway over the river, and from its designation as an inland port with a shea processing plant and storage facilities for goods moving to Burkina Faso and beyond.\nPeople.\nPredominantly Gonja, with Hausa, Dagomba, Konkomba, and Dagaaba traders. Gonja, Hausa, and Twi are widely spoken. English is used in schools and offices. Islam is the dominant religion.\nClimate.\nHot Guinea savanna, slightly more humid because of the river.\nLandscape.\nFlat lowland with the Black Volta River cutting through. Floodplains, shea trees, and farmland.\nOrientation.\nLinear town along the highway. The bridge and river are at the southern edge, market and lorry station central, fuel stations and warehouses north.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom Tamale: Direct tro-tro or bus, 2-2.5 hr, paved. GH\u20b550-80 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Kumasi: VIP or tro-tro to Tamale stops at Buipe, 6-7 hr.\nFrom Damongo: 1-1.5 hr via Busunu, paved. GH\u20b530-50 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Sawla: 2-2.5 hr, GH\u20b560-90 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Salaga: 1-1.5 hr, GH\u20b530-50 (Aug 2026).\nBy plane.\nThe closest airport is Tamale Airport."}
{"id": "220006", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220006", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/T.S. McEwan", "text": "The dive site T.S McEwan or Smoky Sue is an offshore recent wreck in Table Bay area on the west coast of Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nThe steam tug \"T.S. McEwan\" was well known in Table Bay harbour for many years. At the time of its construction in 1925 at Paisley, Renfrewshire, Scotland, it was said to be the most powerful tug in the world, and it provided a long service to shipping in Cape Towm from 1925 of some 50 years, including salvage operations. Length 48.72 m, beam 10.55 m, 793 tons, powered by two triple expansion steam engines driving two shafts, each with two water tube boilers, giving 2800 hp, for a maximum speed of 13 knots and a bollard thrust of 30 tons.\nAfter 50 years of service it was scuttled in Table Bay. \n S33\u00b051.764' E018\u00b016.166', 11.1km SW of Robben Island lighthouse. \nPosition.\nThis site is in the Robben Island Marine Protected Area since 2019. A permit is required.\nName.\nThe tug was named for T.S. McEwan, who was the general manager of Cape Railways from 1902 to 1920. The nickname Smoky Sue referred to the large amounts of black smoke it tended to emit.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth of the site is about 82\u00a0m. and the top of the shallowest point on the wreckage is about 75\u00a0m. \nVisibility.\nVisibility at the wreck is not very predictable based on surface conditions, but turbid surface waters predict very low light levels at depth. \nTopography.\nThe wreck lies roughly upright on a rocky bottom, with a slight list to starboard, and has broken up considerably. The centreline lies at 133\u00b0T, with the bow to the southeast. The superstructure and deck have mostly wasted away, but the boilers and engines are fairly intact.\nGeology:\nPrecambrian sedimentary rock of the \"Tygerberg\" formation.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to all winds and swells with a westerly component, so should be dived in calm or light offshore winds and low swell, and may be good after a southeaster. The depth limits the effect of surface waves on the wreck, but conditions during the ascent and visibility of divers at the surface should be taken into account. South-easterly winds may be strong on the south peninsula but light over Table Bay.\nThe site is likely to be at its best in summer but there may be opportunities at other times of the year. This is an area which sometimes has upwellings caused by south-easterly winds, but it is not as strongly affected as some other parts of the Cape Peninsula. Sunny days in summer will generally cause plankton bloom to close down surface visibility, but under the bloom it may be relatively clean but can be very dark.\nThe site is exposed to wind and wavaes fron the northeast and southeast, so should be dived in calm or moderate southeasterly weather and sea conditions, but if a strong southeaster is forecast it would be prudent to avoid diving as considerable decompression would be needed from the depth of the wreck.\nGet in.\nBoat access only. The site is a long way offshore. Boats will usually leave from the Offshore Powerboat Club at Granger Bay, or from the V&amp;A Waterfront. \nDo.\nThe only known reason to dive at this site is to see the wreckage of a well-known feature of mid 20th century Cape Town harbour.\nSee.\nFeatures.\nWreckage of a large twin screw steam powered tug of the early 20th century, somewhat broken up.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThe site is deep by technical diving standards, and requires bottom gas mixture which will not support consciousness et the surface, and multiple decompression mixtures. It is also in the approached to a major international port, so large ship traffic ma be expected. Long decompression times will be complicated by low water temperatures. Strong offshore winds may develop during the periods when best underwater conditions are likely, but forecasts are usually reliable.\nSkills.\nCompetence at advanced trimix diver level is necessary as a minimum.\nEquipment.\nThe depth of the site makes hypoxic trimix necessary for the bottom sector, putting this squarely in the realm of advanced trimix technical diving. Either open circuit with multiple decompression gases, or rebreather equipment is necessary.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites of Table Bay and approaches\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220007", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220007", "title": "Daboya", "text": "Daboya is the capital of North Gonja District in the Savannah Region of Ghana which is located in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. It is an ancient Gonja town on the White Volta River, historically famous across Ghana for its salt, handmade smocks (fugu), and as the site of the Daboya Weavers. It is a quiet, traditional town off the main highway, ideal for culture and river scenery.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nDaboya is one of the oldest towns in Gonja, founded as part of the Gonja Kingdom of Naa Ndewura Jakpa. For centuries it was the main source of salt for northern Ghana \u2014 extracted from saline soils along the White Volta. It is also a historic center for fugu weaving and dyeing, supplying chiefs across Gonja and Dagbon.\nPeople.\nPredominantly Gonja. Gonja (Ngbanye) is main language, with Hausa and English spoken in schools and offices. Islam is dominant. The locals are very traditional and welcoming.\nClimate.\nHot Guinea savanna.\nLandscape.\nLow-lying floodplain of the White Volta River. Savanna with baobabs, dawadawa, and salt flats near river.\nOrientation.\nSmall town. Chief's palace, mosque, market, and river are all within a 15-min walk. Town is reached via a 25km feeder road off the Tamale-Buipe highway at Busunu junction.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe closest airport is Tamale Airport.\nBy car.\nFrom Tamale: Shared taxi to Busunu junction (1-1.5 hr), then taxi/motorbike to Daboya (30-45 min on feeder). Total 1.5-2.5 hr. Road is unpaved after Busunu \u2014 rough in rainy season.\nFrom Damongo: Via Busunu junction, 1.5-2 hr.\nFrom Buipe: Via Busunu, 1-1.5 hr.\nA 4WD recommended in rainy season. No direct tro-tro to Daboya \u2014 you will change at Busunu.\nEat.\nBring bottled/sachet water and extra snacks."}
{"id": "220011", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220011", "title": "Hampton (Georgia)", "text": "Hampton is a suburban town in the US state of Georgia.\nGet in.\nHampton has easy access to Atlanta via Interstate 75 and US 19/41. It is around a 30-minute drive from the city. If you want to fly, the closest airport is Hartsfield Jackson Atlanta International Airport. \nConnect.\n&lt;mapframe latitude=\"33.385156\" longitude=\"-84.299812\" zoom=\"14\" width=\"686\" height=\"436\" align=\"right\" /&gt;"}
{"id": "220017", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220017", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Reigersdaal", "text": "The dive site Reigersdaal is an inshore historical wreck in the Silwerstroomstrand area of Table Bay near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nOn 25 October 1747, the Dutch East Indiaman \"Reigersdaal\" was wrecked at Silwerstroomstrand. The ship was built for the Amsterdam chamber of the Dutch East India Company (VOC) in 1738 in Amsterdam. It was wrecked in shallow water on 25 October 1747 at Silwerstroomsrand when the anchor cable parted in bad weather. Very little remains of the wreck besides heavily encrusted anchors and cannon in the surf zone.\n S33\u00b035.800' E018\u00b021.200', 560\u00a0m NW of Springfontein Point.\nPosition.\nThis site is not in a Marine Protected Area. A permit is not required.\nDepth.\nAbout 5\u00a0m.\nVisibility.\nThree main influences affect visibility: Turbulence from breaking waves, algal blooms boosted by insolation, and wind effects. An offshore wind or a wind blowing along the coast from the south may drive an upwelling of bottom water, which may be relatively clear.\nTopography.\nThe bottom is reportedly covered by black mussels, red bait and kelp. No reports on the reef structure are available.\nConditions.\nDiving is restricted to when the swell is low, as the site is within the surf zone for some conditions. An offshore wind (southeaster) and high tide are likely to improve conditions, but the site is exposed to both the prevailing southwesterly swell, and the common winter northwesterly seas.\nGet in.\nThe site is accessible by boat, and from the shore.\nSee.\nMarine life.\nThe site is reputed to have heavy growths of kelp, red baid and black mussels.\nFeatures.\nBroken up and scattered remnants of an 18th century wooden wreck, which was salvaged in the 20th century. Heavily encrusted fragments including cannon and anchors remain.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThe site may be in or near the surf zone.\nSkills.\nNo special skills recommended.\nEquipment.\nNo special equipment recommended.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in Table Bay approaches\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220019", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220019", "title": "Yunyoo", "text": "Yunyoo is the capital of Yunyoo-Nasuan District in the North East Region of Ghana. It is a remote, quiet farming town northeast of Nalerigu, home to Konkomba communities and serving as a new district capital. Few travellers come here, which makes it one of the most off-the-beaten-path district capitals in Ghana. \nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nYunyoo is traditionally Konkomba land. It was part of Bunkpurugu-Yunyoo District until 2018 when Yunyoo-Nasuan was created as a separate district, with Yunyoo as capital. The area is predominantly agricultural and has seen gradual development of district assembly infrastructure.\nPeople.\nAlmost entirely Konkomba. Likpakpaln (Konkomba language) is main language, with Mampruli and Hausa in market, English spoken by teachers/officials. Mix of Christianity, traditional beliefs, and Islam.\nClimate.\nHot savanna, similar to Bunkpurugu.\nLandscape.\nFlat to gently undulating Guinea savanna, with farmland, fallow bush, and scattered shea and dawadawa trees. Very rural.\nOrientation.\nIt is a tiny town. The district assembly, market, and lorry station are all within a 10-min walk along main dirt road.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Tamale Airport.\nBy car.\nFrom Nalerigu: Shared taxi/motorbike via Bunkpurugu road turnoff. 2-3 hr in dry season. Road is unpaved, rough, sandy.\nFrom Bunkpurugu: 1.5-2.5 hr southwest.\nFrom Tamale: Tamale \u2192 Nalerigu \u2192 Yunyoo, 6-8 hr dry season.\nNo public bus \u2014 early morning lorry station departure only.\nSleep.\nMost travellers base thenselves in Nalerigu or Bunkpurugu and visit as day trip if they have motorbike/4x4."}
{"id": "220021", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220021", "title": "Bamboi", "text": "Bamboi is a town in Bole District in the Savannah Region of Ghana, on the Volta Basin region of the Black Volta River. It marks the boundary between Savannah and Bono Region and is the main crossing point for traffic between Kumasi and Wa. Known for its long bridge, river fishing, and as a truck stop town. \nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBamboi grew as a Gonja and Mo fishing settlement and ferry crossing. When the Bamboi Bridge was built over the Black Volta, it became a key north-south transport link and market town.\nPeople.\nMixed Gonja, Mo, Brifor, Dagaaba, and Akan traders. Gonja, Mo, Twi, and Hausa spoken. English in schools. Islam and Christianity both present.\nClimate.\nHot Guinea savanna, slightly more humid due to river.\nThe best time to visit is from November to February.\nLandscape.\nBlack Volta floodplain \u2014 river, gallery forest, and savanna.\nOrientation.\nLinear town along Kumasi-Wa highway. Bridge at south end, market and lorry station in the centre.\nGet in.\nBy car\nFrom Kumasi: 5-6 hr via Wenchi. GH\u20b580-130 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Wa: 4-5 hr via Sawla and Bole. GH\u20b580-120 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Bole: 1-1.5 hr.\nFrom Techiman: 3-4 hr.\nNearest airports: Kumasi Airport ' and Wa Airport '."}
{"id": "220022", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220022", "title": "Yapei", "text": "Yapei is a small town in Central Gonja District of the Savannah Region located in Volta Basin region of Ghana. It sits at the confluence of the Black Volta and White Volta Rivers which form the Volta River. Known for its historic ferry, fishing port, and strategic location on the Tamale-Kumasi highway.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nYapei was traditionally an important river crossing for Gonja. Before bridges were built at Buipe, the Yapei ferry carried vehicles across the White Volta. It remains a historic river town and local fishing center.\nPeople.\nIn terms of ethnicity, Yapei is predominantly Gonja, with Hausa and Dagomba minorities. Gonja, Hausa, and English are the main languages spoken in the town. Islam is the dominant religion.\nClimate.\nHot savanna, riverine humidity.\nLandscape.\nYapei is at the confluence of two Voltas \u2014 wide rivers, floodplains, savanna.\nOrientation.\nIt is a tiny town just off the Tamale-Buipe highway. Turn-off near the Yapei bridge area. Market near lorry station.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Tamale Airport.\nBy car.\nFrom Tamale: 1.5-2 hr south. GH\u20b540-70 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Buipe: 15-20 min north.\nFrom Kumasi: 6-7 hr, via Buipe.\nTro-tros between Tamale and Buipe stop at Yapei junction \u2014 then motorbike 5 min into town."}
{"id": "220024", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220024", "title": "Bawku", "text": "Bawku is a major border town and capital of Bawku Municipal in the Upper East Region of Ghana, on the border with Burkina Faso and near Togo. It is a busy cross-border trading town in the White Volta lowlands of the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBawku developed as a major market centre linking Kusasi, Mamprusi, Bissa and Mossi traders between Ghana and Burkina Faso. It is known for smock weaving and livestock trade.\nPeople.\nKusasi, Mamprusi, Bissa and Mossi. Kusaal, Mampruli and Hausa are widely spoken. Muslim majority with Christian minority. Very traditional.\nClimate.\nHot semi-arid savanna.\nOrientation.\nMarket in centre, main road to Bolgatanga west, borders to Burkina Faso north at Pusiga and Kulungugu.\nSleep.\nIf tense, stay in Bolgatanga."}
{"id": "220025", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220025", "title": "Awhum", "text": "Awhum (also known as Ohum) is a town in Udi Local Government Area of Enugu State, southeastern Nigeria. It is known for its natural landscape of waterfalls, caves, streams and lakes, as well as the Our Lady of Mount Calvary Cistercian Monastery. The community has become an important destination for religious pilgrimage and nature tourism.\nUnderstand.\nAwhum is a rural community about 24 km from Enugu, the capital of Enugu State. Neighbouring communities including Okpatu, Nike, Ukana and Egede surround it.\nThe town consists of several villages and has traditionally depended on farming, hunting and trading. The community also has a local market, Nkwo Market, which serves residents and visitors.\nAwhum's natural environment includes waterfalls, caves, streams and lakes. One of the attractions is Awhum Waterfall, located in the Amaugwe/Amu-Ugwu area of the community.\nThe establishment of the Cistercian monastery has made Awhum a destination for Christian pilgrimage. The monastery was established in 1970 and later developed at its present location in Awhum.\nClimate.\nAwhum has a tropical climate, with a rainy season and a dry season. Visitors should expect humid conditions and wet, sometimes muddy paths around the waterfall during the rainy season.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nAwhum can be reached by road from Enugu and other communities in Udi Local Government Area.\nFrom Enugu, travel towards the Udi/Awhum area and continue by local road to Awhum. The waterfall and monastery are within the Awhum community.\nBy public transport.\nLocal buses, taxis, motorcycles and tricycles can be used to reach Awhum from surrounding towns. Visitors going specifically to the waterfall should confirm the local route to the monastery or waterfall entrance before travelling.\nGet around.\nGetting around Awhum is mainly by motorcycle, tricycle and private vehicle. Some attractions, particularly the waterfall, require walking along natural paths.\nThe walk from the parking area near the waterfall to the waterfall has been reported to take approximately 45\u201350 minutes.\nEat.\nLocal food is available in homes, small eateries and food sellers in the community. Visitors may find traditional Igbo dishes and locally produced agricultural foods.\nDrink.\nPalm wine has historically been associated with Awhum and the surrounding communities. Visitors should ask locally where it is available and consume responsibly.\nSleep.\nBasic accommodation may be available around the monastery and in nearby communities. Visitors planning an overnight stay should confirm availability before travelling.\nFor a wider range of accommodation, Enugu is the nearest major city.\nRespect.\nAwhum includes sites of religious importance. Visitors should dress appropriately and behave respectfully around the monastery, caves and other places regarded as sacred.\nAsk for permission before photographing people, religious activities or restricted areas.\nConnect.\nMobile phone coverage may vary in different parts of the community. Visitors should download important information and maps before travelling to remote parts of Awhum."}
{"id": "220026", "revid": "2442477", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220026", "title": "Navrongo", "text": "Navrongo is the capital of Kassena-Nankana Municipal in the Upper East Region of Ghana, 30 km from the Burkina Faso border. It is known for its historic mud-built cathedral and is a main town in the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nNavrongo became the first Catholic mission in northern Ghana in 1906. The mud cathedral built 1906-1920 remains the landmark. Town grew as an administrative and farming center.\nPeople.\nKassena and Nankana mainly. Kasem, Nankani and English spoken. Friendly.\nClimate.\nHot semi-arid. Dry October-April hot, rainy May-September. Best November-February.\nOrientation.\nOne main road Bolgatanga to Paga. Cathedral south of town centre, market centre.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is Tamale.\nSleep.\nOther basic guesthouses GH\u20b580-150 (Aug 2026) on main road."}
{"id": "220030", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220030", "title": "Bongo", "text": "Bongo is the capital of Bongo District in the Upper East Region of Ghana, 15 km northeast of Bolgatanga. It is a small farming town famous for the Bongo Rocks and as the heart of the Bongo-Sirigu pottery and art area in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. \nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBongo is a Boosi (Bosi) traditional area, part of the Gurune-speaking zone. Known for rock outcrops used historically as refuge and viewpoint.\nPeople.\nBoosi and Gurune (Frafra). Boosi, Gurune and English spoken. Christian majority, traditional and Muslim.\nClimate.\nHot semi-arid. Dry Oct-Apr 38-42 \u00b0C, rainy May-Sep. Best Nov-Feb.\nOrientation.\nSmall town off Bolga-Bawku road. Market centre, lorry station, rocks to west."}
{"id": "220031", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220031", "title": "Zebilla", "text": "Zebilla is the capital of Bawku West District in the Upper East Region of Ghana, on the main Bolgatanga-Bawku road. It is a market and farming town in the White Volta lowlands of the Ghanaian Northern Plains, serving Kusaal communities.\nUnderstand.\nHistory\nZebilla is a Kusasi market town that grew along the Bolga-Bawku highway. The name Zebilla is from Kusaal.\nPeople\nMainly Kusasi, some Mossi and Bissa traders. Kusaal and Hausa spoken.\nClimate\nHot. Dry October-March, rainy May-September. Dusty harmattan Dec-Feb. Best Nov-Jan.\nOrientation\nLinear town on highway. Market central, lorry station on main road.\nGet in.\nBy car\nFrom Bolgatanga 45min-1 hr east. Tro-tro 20-30 GHS.\nFrom Bawku 30-40 min west.\nFrom Tamale 4 hrs via Bolga."}
{"id": "220032", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220032", "title": "Sandema", "text": "Sandema is the capital of Builsa North Municipal in the Upper East Region of Ghana. It is the heart of Builsa land in the Ghanaian Northern Plains, known for Builsa culture and as gateway to the Tono area.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nSandema is the traditional capital of the Builsa (Builsa) people. Known for resistance history against slave raiders \u2014 historic war dance.\nPeople.\nBuilsa (Buli) mainly. Buli language, English. Very traditional, friendly.\nClimate.\nHot savanna. Dry October-April very hot, rainy May-September. Best November-February.\nOrientation.\nSmall town north of Tono Dam, west of Navrongo road.\nGet in.\nFrom Navrongo 20-30 min northwest. Tro-tro GH\u20b510-15 (Aug 2026).\nFrom Bolgatanga 1-1.5 hr northwest.\nFrom Tamale 4 hr."}
{"id": "220037", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220037", "title": "Batuan", "text": "Batuan is a municipality of Masbate in Ticao island.\nUnderstand.\nThe municipality of Batuan is one of the four towns comprising the Ticao Island within the Masbate province. It is located on the southern end of Ticao, it serves as a serene eco-tourism escape. It is home to the oldest and largest mangrove tree in the Philippines.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe province's only airport is in Masbate City (), served by a daily Cebgo flight every morning.\nFrom Manila (MNL) to Daraga, (DRP), Cebu Pacific &amp; Philippine Airlines is 1 hour by air and 3 hours land travel from Legazpi to Pilar or Bulan, Sorsogon, and a 1-hour ferry to San Jacinto, Ticao Island. \nFrom Clark International Airport (CRK) to Masbate City (MBT), the travel time is approximately 1 hour for Cebu Pacific. From Masbate Airport, ride a tricycle to Masbate Port, then take a public fast craft or Roro to Lagundi, Ticao Island. From Lagundi Port, ride a motorcycle. It would take 1 hour to reach the destination.\nGet around.\nMotorcycles or habal-habal and tricycles are the main modes of transportation for going around the Island.\nGo next.\nMasbate City - the capital city of Masbate province"}
{"id": "220039", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220039", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/King Emperor", "text": "The dive site King Emperor is an offshore recent wreck in Table Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nThe site is the wreck of a steel steam trawler in reasonably good structural condition. The vessel was built by the Dundee Shipbuilding Company, in Scotland in 1914 for the Imperial Fishing Company. It served as a minelayer and minesweeper in the First World War, and did similar service in WW II. Eventually it was sold to East Fisheries in Cape Town. In July 1948 it was damaged in heavy seas near Duiker Point and foundered in Table Bay while attempting to return to harbour with no loss of life. \n S33\u00b053.267' E018\u00b021.949', 3.5\u00a0km NW from Green Point lighthouse\nPosition.\nThis site is in a Marine Protected Area (2019). A permit is required.\nName.\nThe name \"King Emperor\" is the name of the vessel wrecked at the site.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 43\u00a0m. and the shallowest point is about 38\u00a0m. (Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 41\u00a0m.)\nVisibility.\nVisibility can be good, and natural illumination adequate, but this is generally unpredictable, although it is more likely immediately after strong southeasterly winds'\nTopography.\nThe wreck lies upright on a rocky bottom with the centreline at 85\u00b0T. It is 39\u00a0m long, 7\u00a0m wide, and about 5\u00a0high. The Scotch boiler and triple expansion steam engine are still in place.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to wind and seas from the southwest and northwest, so should be dived after southeasterly winds, and is more likely to be good in summer.\nGet in.\nAccess is only by boat, usually from the V&amp;A Waterfront, or the Offshore Powerboat Club at Granger Bay. \nStay safe.\nHazards.\nCold water. Nitrogen narcosis. Decompression sickness, Breathing gas emergencies. Sea and wind conditions changing during a dive. The site is in the approaches to a mojor international port, and ship traffic may be expected.\nSkills.\nCompetence in planning and performing technical dives to depths beyond the range of most recreational diving. Skill in the use of appropriate equipment and gases for the planned dive. Dives on this site will be decompression dives\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in Table Bay and approaches\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220042", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220042", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Disa", "text": "The dive site Disa is an offshore recent wreck in Table Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nhe \"Disa\" was built for Irvin and Johnson in 1959 in Yorkshire, England. It was a 453 ton steel side trawler of 43\u00a0m length,8.3\u00a0m beam, and 4.3\u00a0m depth, powered by a single coal fired triple expansion engine capable of a top speed of 10 knots.\nIt was wrecked in a collision with another fishing boat, the \"Lupin\", on a clear day in a heavy sea. The vessel sank rapidly, and ten men were lost.\n S33\u00b052.398' E018.18.981', about 8km north of the Green Point lighthouse.\nPosition.\nThis site is in the Robben Island Marine Protected Area since 2019. A permit is required.\nName.\n\"Disa\" is the name of the wreck at this site\nDepth.\nDepth on the sand is about 67\u00a0m. and the shallowest part is about 60\u00a0m. (Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 64\u00a0m.)\nVisibility.\nVisibility is unpredictable. The site has not been dived often. It is likely to be similar to other sites in the area of similar depth. Surface visibility is not a reliable indicator of visibility at depth, and a heavy layer of plankton bloom may overlay a dark but relatively clear deeper layer. Or maybe not.\nTopography.\nThe wreck lies on its starboard side, partly embeddedin the sand bottom, on an axis of about 66\u00b0T, with the bow to the northwest. The hull is nearly intact, and is 46.6\u00a0m long, with intact tripod mast, trawl winch and and boat davit. The superstructure is mostly intact, but missing the stack. Many of these details are recognisable on the sonar images. The collision damage was on the starboard side, which is on the sand.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to all winds and swells with a westerly component, so should be dived in calm or light offshore winds and low swell, and may be good after a southeaster. The depth limits the effect of surface waves on the wreck, but conditions during the ascent and visibility of divers at the surface should be taken into account. South-easterly winds may be strong on the south peninsula but light over Table Bay.\nThe site is likely to be at its best in summer but there may be opportunities at other times of the year. This is an area which sometimes has upwellings caused by south-easterly winds, but it is not as strongly affected as some other parts of the Cape Peninsula. Sunny days in summer will generally cause plankton bloom to close down surface visibility, but under the bloom it may be relatively clean but can be very dark.\nGet in.\nBoat access only, usually from V&amp;A Waterfront or Oceana Powerboat Club at Granger Bay. \nDo.\nDive and explore the wreck.\nRoutes.\nNo routes have been recommended.\nSee.\nFeatures.\nWreck of a steam powered steel fishing vessel in fairly good structural condition.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThe wreck is deeper than the generally accepted limits for recreational diving on air and nitrox.\nSkills.\nSkills should be appropriate to the equipment used.\nEquipment.\nDue to the depth, breathing mixtures using helium to limit narcosis and work of breathing are strongly recommended. Decompression will be accelerated by use of suitable gases, and a DSMB will allow safer decompression if the shotline is not used for the ascent. Use of a rebreather can limit consumption of helium.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Table bay and approaches\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220045", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220045", "title": "Imsouane", "text": "Imsouane is a fishing village on the South Atlantic Coast of Morocco, roughly halfway between Agadir and Essaouira. Surfers know it for the long right-hand point break in its bay.\nUnderstand.\nImsouane sits in a wide bay where the western end of the High Atlas comes down to the ocean. It is still a working fishing port, and the harbour is the centre of village life. Most visitors come for the surf, and the village stays quiet outside the swell season.\nThe village has two ends: the harbour and the bay, and the headland to the south known as Cathedral Point.\nGet in.\nImsouane is about 80 km north of Agadir. Take the N1 coastal road north through Taghazout and Tamri, then the signposted turning west; the last stretch is a narrow road down to the coast. Allow around two hours from Agadir.\nGrands taxis run from Agadir and from Tamri. Surf camps in Taghazout and Tamraght run day trips here when the swell suits the bay.\nGet around.\nThe village is small enough to walk. The bay and Cathedral Point are a couple of kilometres apart.\nDo.\nThe surf season runs roughly from September to April, when north-west Atlantic swells reach this coast. Summer is smaller and quieter.\nConditions in the bay vary a lot with swell direction, and it is worth checking a forecast before making the drive.\nEat.\nCaf\u00e9s and grills around the harbour serve the day's catch.\nSleep.\nGuesthouses and surf accommodation operate in the village. The choice is small, so book ahead during the autumn and winter surf season."}
{"id": "220047", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220047", "title": "Samal (Bataan)", "text": "\"This article is about the municipality in Bataan. For the city in Davao Region, see Samal\"\nSamal is a town in Bataan, Central Luzon, the Philippines.\nUnderstand.\nSamal was founded by the Spanish in 1641. The town was devastated during World War II in the Battle of Bataan in 1942. The Bataan Death March also passed through the town.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nSamal lies along Roman Superhighway (Route 301), the main six-lane transport artery through eastern Bataan which bypasses downtown Samal. Bataan National Road, a narrow two-lane thoroughfare, passes through the town center. The two highways are connected to each other by capillary roads.\nSamal is connected by minibuses to the provincial capital Balanga. From Metro Manila, Genesis and Victory Liner operate daily bus trips on their way to Balanga and Mariveles.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays.\nStay safe.\nThe lowland areas of Samal are prone to flooding during the rainy season.\nConnect.\nSamal's postal code is 2113."}
{"id": "220048", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220048", "title": "Sierra la Laguna Biosphere Reserve", "text": "Sierra la Laguna Biosphere Reserve is a protected natural area in Baja California Sur in western Mexico. The Sierra la Laguna is a small mountain range with thick green forests at its higher elevations. It is an oasis of green in a region dominated by sandy, rocky dry deserts. The Sierra la Laguna is a critical watershed for the city of La Paz and for the resort areas of Cabo San Lucas and San Jos\u00e9 del Cabo.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a large area covering more than 112,000 hectares.\nHistory.\nBefore the conquest of Mexico, the mountains were inhabited by an indigenous people known as the \"Peric\u00fa\". The land was lightly exploited from the 17th through the mid 20th century, but large-scale industrial corporation wanted to destroy the environment to build gold mining operations. Scientists raised concerns about the fact that the watershed supplies the vast majority of drinking water to southern Baja. In 1994 a presidential decree signed by Carlos Salinas Gotari established protections as a biosphere reserve. This was augmented by the UNESCO Biosphere designation in 2003.\nLandscape.\nThe landscape of the Sierra la Laguna contrasts sharply with the dry lowlands from which it rises up sharply with sheer cliffs, huge granite boulders, and deep canyons like \"Ca\u00f1\u00f3n de la Zorra\" with a narrow river that forms calm swimming pools and even a 12-meter high waterfall. \"Picacho\" is the highest peak in Baja California Sur with an elevation of 2,090 meters. \nFlora and fauna.\nThe reserve is often described as an \"island in the sky\" due to its high-elevation forests contrasting with the very close-by flat desert landscape that covers most of southern Baja California. More than 2,000 species have been identified in the reserve (most of them plants) and 23% of the species are endemic. The high proportion of endemic species is attributed to the geological separation of the Baja peninsula from mainland Mexico which gave nature millions of years to evolve distinct species. \nThe types of plants and animals that you are likely to see depends on whether you are in the desert-like lowlands, or higher up the mountains in the forests. The mountain-top forests are dominated by pine, oak and similar trees. In the desert lowlands, vegetation includes massive cacti (including giant cardones), saguaros, pitayas, and twisted elephant trees. \nThe biggest and most dramatic of the park's mammals are the pumas. Aside from being apex predators who find plenty of mule deer and other animals to hunt, the puma are protected in the reserve and many are tracked by biologists. Other mammals in the park include large numbers of coyotes, gray foxes, and black-tail hares. \nThere are about 60 bird species living in the park including eagles, hawks, vultures and hummingbirds. Reptiles include lizards, tree frogs and a number of snakes. \nClimate.\nThe climate varies dramatically by elevation with hot, arid desert conditions in the lowlands and a cool, temperate climate in the forested highlands. In the lowlands, daytime high temperatures regularly exceed 40\u00b0C while winter nights in the highland forests can drop below freezing (0\u00b0C). The reserve gets little rain, but what moisture it does get tends to fall between the months of August and October when tropical storms drive the local \"rainy season\" (which isn't particularly rainy compared to other coastal areas).\nGet in.\nThere's basically two ways to get to the Sierra la Laguna from the Los Cabos resort areas\u02d0 1) book a day trip from one of the tour operators, it will cost a little more but removes logistical problems. 2) rent a 4WD truck in Los Cabos and drive there yourself. You will want the 4WD because access roads are unpaved dirt back roads.\nBy car.\nBefore leaving, download maps because you will lose cell signal in the remote mountainous area.\nFrom San Jose del Cabo, drive north on highway MEX-1 (towards La Paz) for about 1 hour. Look for the turnoffs to either Santiago or Miraflores. Which turnoff you use depends on what you want to see and do\u02d0 the two roads go through enitrely different canyons. Use the Santiago turnoff if you want to see the Ca\u00f1\u00f3n de la Zorra (Fox Canyon Waterfall) or the El Chorro Hot Springs. Use the Miraflores turnoff if you want to go to the Santa Rita Hot Springs or to Boca de la Sierra/\nFees and permits.\nThe reserve is managed for the federal government by the National Commission of Protected Natural Areas (CONANP). CONANP charges a daily admission fee of M$125. The fee can be paid in cash at major trailheads into the park or at either of two range stations at El Port\u00f3n or La Burrera. Be aware that trailheads or access to some of the park's features (including Canon de Zorra) are via privately owned or ejido lands. These landowners usually allow public access upon payment of a modest fee (usually M$100 to M$200). Carry modest amounts of cash with you to handle such payments."}
{"id": "220049", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220049", "title": "Ciudad Constituci\u00f3n", "text": "Ciudad Constituci\u00f3n is a small city in Baja California Sur. It is a common stop for travelers driving the length of the Baja peninsula and is a good place to stay for nature travelers wanting to experience the whale sanctuaries of Magdalena Bay (see Baja Whale Trail). It is also a popular stop for off-road race fans as it lies along the route of the Baja 1000.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a young city whose roots stretch back only to 1940 when it was a crossroads known as \"El Crucero\". It grew into a city due to agriculture being developed in the area starting in the late 1940s. Wheat was the dominant crop. Today it is a small city with a population just under 44,000 as of 2020.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe Ciudad Constituci\u00f3n National Airport is a small airfield served only by small regional carriers, particularly Aereo Servicio Guerrero (ASG). \nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses run frequently along the entire length of the Baja California peninsula. Services are provided by two major bus lines\u02d0 \"Aguila\" and \"ABC\". A bus from La Paz takes about 3\u00bd hours. It is about 2 hours from Loreto and about 7 hours from Los Cabos. See also \"Bus travel in Mexico\".\nGet around.\nThe city is small and compact so walking is often the best way to get to local businesses, plazas and restaurants. Taxis are readily available but they don't use meters, so ask about fares before getting in and negotiate if the quoted price seems high."}
{"id": "220054", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220054", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Hermes", "text": "The dive site Hermes is an inshore historical wreck Table Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nA shallow wreck site on a sand bottom, within swimming distance of the beach. SS \"Hermes\" was built in 1899 in Sunderland, England, for the Houston Line Ltd. It was powered by a single triple expansion steam engine with a top speed of 12.5 knots. The ship was driven aground by a northwest wind and large seas while at anchor in Table Bay. The wreck has broken up almost completely as it is in the surf zone in rough weather.\n S33\u00b052.210' E018\u00b029.255'. About 170\u00a0m off Milnerton beach. This site is not in a Marine Protected Area. A permit is not required.\nName.\n\"Hermes\" was the name of the ship wrecked at this site.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 4(?)\u00a0m. and some of the wreckage is exposed at low tide. \nVisibility.\nThe wreck is close inshore on a fine sand bottom, and visibility is seldom more than 3 to 5 m, as there is almost always some surge, even on very flat days.\nTopography.\nThe site can be within the surf line. There are scattered pieces of wreckage in a north-south line on a relatively flat fine silica sand bottom. Some of the wreckage can be exposed by breaking waves at low tide.\nGeology: The immediate substrate is relatively fine silica sand.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to northwesterly winds and waves, and is sufficiently shallow that the prevailing southwesterly swells will often refract around Green Point and break on the shore The site is welly protected from southeasterly waves, as the fetch is extremely short, but it is exposed to southeasterly wind.\nThe site is likely to be at it's best in summer after a southeasterly wind, but there are also occasional opportunities at other times of the year.\nGet in.\nUsual access: Boat dive from Oceana Power Boat Club slipway or the V&amp;A Waterfront, or shore dive from Milnerton beach.\nDo.\nDive on the site and explore the wreckage. The site is small and shallow enough to visit comprehensively on a single dive. The biggest difficulty is finding all the scattered pieces of wreckage, but the direction of surge is across the wreck, and sand ripples are roughly parallel to the main axis of wreckage, which is helpful for navigation.\nSee.\nMarine life.\nThe wreck is in quite a high energy zone in winter, and will be pounded by winter storms. That does not stop considerable biodiversity developing on the equivalent rocky reefs, but for some reason the wreckage has low biodiversity. \nFeatures.\nScattered fragments of the wreckage of a steel ship.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThe site is often in the surf zone, and even when there is no surf, there may be strong surge. Some of the wreckage has sharp edges and points, which present the risk of injury, equipment damage, or fouling the diver.\nSkills.\nNo special skills required. The depth is suitable for entry level divers.\nEquipment.\nNo special equipment required.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Table Bay area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220057", "revid": "35982", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220057", "title": "Sharks", "text": "Sharks are a group of cartilaginous fish characterised by placoid scales, five to seven gill slits on each side of the body, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. In addition to seeing them in aquariums, there are many places where travellers can observe sharks in the wild.\nUnderstand.\nThere are over 500 extant species of sharks, and many more extinct ones. Despite their popular reputation as ferocious man-eating monsters, the vast majority of shark species, including the whale shark, the largest shark species, are harmless to humans. Only about 30 species have been known to attack humans unprovoked, and about 10 species are known to have caused human fatalities. For comparison, you are more likely to be struck by lightning or be killed in a car accident than to be attacked by a shark.\nNevertheless, sharks are a common threat in adventure and horror fiction, notably the \"Jaws\" franchise.\nEat.\nShark meat is eaten in many parts of the world, and goes by different names (e.g. flake in Australia). Sharksfin is a delicacy in Chinese cuisine, but controversial because many fishermen cut off the fin and throw the shark back into the sea to die a slow and painful death.\nStay safe.\nAs always, be sure to maintain a safe distance when viewing wild animals and do not attempt to touch them. Most shark species are harmless to humans so long as they are not provoked. \nThe rarity of shark attacks does not mean they cannot be fatal. Nor does the seemingly random nature of shark attacks help to allay fears about being bitten. Sharks usually attack because they think you are a seal, a turtle, a fish, or various other types of prey. Sometimes they test bite if they are curious, but sharks do not intentionally bite, attack, or kill humans.\nHere are a few common sense tips to reduce the risk of encountering sharks while swimming or diving:\nWhen diving in waters where the great white shark is endemic, you may need to wear a shark shield, which keeps the sharks away by sending out electrical pulses that cause them immense discomfort."}
{"id": "220063", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220063", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Blomvlei", "text": "The dive site Blomvlei is an offshore recent wreck in the Table Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\n S33\u00b052.484' E018\u00b020.729'. This site is the Robben Island Marine Protected Area since 2019. A permit is required.\nUnderstand.\nName.\n\"Blomvlei\" is thought to be the name if the vessel wrecked at this site, but it may be its sister ship, the \"Mooivlei\".\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 53\u00a0m. and the top of the shallowest point of the wreckis is at about 47\u00a0m. (Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 50 \u00a0m.)\nVisibility.\nVisibility is unpredictable. The site has not been dived often. It is likely to be similar to other sites in the area of similar depth. Surface visibility is not a reliable indicator of visibility at depth, and a heavy layer of plankton bloom may overlay a dark but relatively clear deeper layer. Or maybe not.\nTopography.\nThe substantially intact hull lies upright on a flat sand bottom with the centreline at 202\u00b0T. The forecaste still carries the anchor winch and two anchors in the hawses. The big trawl winch is in the main deck forward of where the superstructure used to be, and there is a rectangular structure with skylights on the quarterdeck. The boiler and propeller are still present but not the rudded.\nGeology: The wreck lies on a flat sand bottom.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to all winds and swells with a westerly component, so should be dived in calm or light offshore winds and low swell, and may be good after a southeaster. The depth limits the effect of surface waves on the wreck, but conditions during the ascent and visibility of divers at the surface should be taken into account. South-easterly winds may be strong on the south peninsula but light over Table Bay.\nThe site is likely to be at its best in summer but there may be opportunities at other times of the year. This is an area which sometimes has upwellings caused by south-easterly winds, but it is not as strongly affected as some other parts of the Cape Peninsula. Sunny days in summer will generally cause plankton bloom to close down surface visibility, but under the bloom it may be relatively clean but can be very dark.\nGet in.\nAccess is by boat. The site is about (distance)\u00a0km from (the V&amp;A Waterfront, or ?\u00a0km from the Offshore Powerboat Club at Granger Bay.\nDo.\nExplore the wreck, and let us know if you find anything that could help with a positive identification.\nSee.\nFeatures.\nWreck of a steam powered steel fishing vessel in moderately intact condition.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Table bay and approaches\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220067", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220067", "title": "Kabayan", "text": "Kabayan is a municipality in the province of Benguet, Philippines.\nUnderstand.\nLocated at the triboundary region between Benguet, Nueva Vizcaya and Ifugao provinces, Kabayan is known for having the only-known mummies in the Philippines, which were created by the indigenous Ibaloi inhabitants using a lengthy and complicated process that involved smoking the corpses over certain types of leaves for months before depositing them in burial caves, hence their moniker, the Fire Mummies. Sadly, several of the mummies were stolen by grave robbers since the 20th century, with some of them still being sold on the black market. This has led to restrictions on access to the burial caves.\nIt is also the main base camp for hikers heading to Mount Pulag, the highest mountain in Luzon.\nGet in.\nThere are two main roads leading to Kabayan from Baguio. One is through a junction leading south from Halsema Highway in Buguias town, while another is a junction from the Benguet-Nueva Vizcaya Road in Bokod leading north. There are bus, van and jeepney connections to the town from Baguio.\nGet around.\nThere are tricycles in town, but a 4WD or any vehicle suitable for mountain roads is best used. Most attractions in town are accessible following extended hikes.\nBuy.\nAll transactions are in cash. There are no banks or ATMs in town.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition.\nRegistration is mandatory for would-be trail hikers. For the Tabeyo Trail, this can be done at the ranger station in Ballay. The hiking season runs during the dry season from November to May. While Benguet is often known for its cool weather, do not underestimate the effects of heat and exhaustion while climbing, especially as many of the slopes are directly exposed to sunlight.\nRespect.\nMany of the descendants of the deceased who were mummified and interred in the burial caves are still around today, and still visit the caves to offer their respects to their ancestors. Refrain from making actions that show disrespect in these locations the same way one avoids doing so in other religious sites.\nConnect.\nThe town\u2019s postal code is 2606."}
{"id": "220068", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220068", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Mooivlei", "text": "The dive site Mooivlei is an offshore recent wreck in the Table Bay, near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nThe site is at the wreck of a steam powered steel side trawler, scuttled at the end of its useful service life. The \"Mooivlei\" was a 252 ton steel steam trawler built in 1935 in Yorkshire for the National Trawling and Fishing Co. Ltd of Cape Town. The ship was powered by a single triple expansion steam engine with a coal fired scotch boiler. It was requisitioned to serve as a minesweeper in WW II, but was converted for antisubmarine work by 1940. In 1944 it was returned to the owners and worked as a fishing vessel again until it was stripped and scuttled in Table Bay in February 1964.\n S33\u00b052.386' E018\u00b019.495', 7.7\u00a0km northwest of Green Point lighthouse. This site is in the Robben Island Marine Protected Area since 2019. A permit is required.\nName.\n\"Mooivlei\" is thought to be the name if the vessel wrecked at this site, but it may be its sister ship, the \"Blomvlei\". Both ships were scuttled in the same general vicinity.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 62\u00a0m. and the top of the shallowest point is about 53\u00a0m. (Average depth of a dive is likely to be about 60\u00a0m.\nVisibility.\nVisibility is unpredictable. The site has not been dived often. It is likely to be similar to other sites in the area of similar depth. Surface visibility is not a reliable indicator of visibility at depth, and a heavy layer of plankton bloom may overlay a dark but relatively clear deeper layer. Or maybe not.\nTopography.\nThe wreck lies on a flat sand bottom with the centreline at about 200\u00b0T. The vessel is roughly upright, but inclined to port, and the hull is structurally fairly intact except for damage to the stern, which includes damage to the propeller. The forecastle still carries the anchor winch. The midships superstructure and trawl winch are gone. The aft superstructure with skylights is largely intact. There are scour pits in the sand at bow and stern.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to all winds and swells with a westerly component, so should be dived in calm or light offshore winds and low swell, and may be good after a southeaster. The depth limits the effect of surface waves on the wreck, but conditions during the ascent and visibility of divers at the surface should be taken into account. South-easterly winds may be strong on the south peninsula but light over Table Bay.\nThe site is likely to be at its best in summer but there may be opportunities at other times of the year. This is an area which sometimes has upwellings caused by south-easterly winds, but it is not as strongly affected as some other parts of the Cape Peninsula. Sunny days in summer will generally cause plankton bloom to close down surface visibility, but under the bloom it may be relatively clean but can be very dark.\nGet in.\nAccess is by boat. The site is about (distance)\u00a0km from the V&amp;A Waterfront, or ?\u00a0km from the Offshore Powerboat Club at Granger Bay.\nDo.\nExplore the wreck, and let us know if you find anything that could help with a positive identification.\nSee.\nFeatures.\nWreck of a steam powered steel fishing vessel in moderately intact condition.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nThe site is deep and in the approaches to a large international port, where shipping traffic can be expected.\nSkills.\nAppropriate skills are needed for the depth and associated equipment. Training in technical diving skills with Trimix is recommended as a minimum.\nEquipment.\nThe site is at a depth below that generally accepted as safe for air and nitrox breathing gases. Trimix is recommended to limit narcosis and work of breathing. Decompression may be assumed necessary from this depth, and appropriate gas mixtures are recommended to reduce decompression time in the cold water. DSMBs are also recommended if ascent occurs away from the shotline. A rebreater can limit the consumption of helium.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Table bay and approaches\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220071", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220071", "title": "Black sand beaches", "text": "Black sand beaches are much rarer than their gold and white counterparts. This rarity along with their contrasting and otherworldly aesthetic have made them popular as tourist attractions. \nUnderstand.\nGeologically, there are two types of black sand beaches:\nBlack sand beaches are often surrounded by dramatic and rugged landscapes. As such, rather than swimming or surfing, the most popular activity at black sand beaches is usually photography.\nStay safe.\nBlack sand heats up quickly. Under a scorching sun, it can become hot enough to burn your feet. Wear footwear if you are allowed to or otherwise avoid visiting when it is very warm. "}
{"id": "220075", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220075", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/Sao Jose Paquete d' Africa", "text": "The dive site S\u00e3o Jos\u00e9 Paquete d' Africa is an inshore historical wreck in the Clifton area on the Cape Peninsula west coast, near Cape Town in the Western Cape of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\nThe \"S\u00e3o Jos\u00e9 Paquete d' Africa\" is the wreck of a Portuguese slave ship of historical and cultural interest, but there is little wreckage remaining.\n S33\u00b056.22' E018\u00b022.543', about 150\u00a0m west of Second Beach, Clifton.\nPosition.\nThis site is in Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area since 2004. A permit is required.\nName.\n\"\"S\u00e3o Jos\u00e9 Paquete d' Africa\" is the name of the vessel wrecked at the site.\nDepth.\nDepthis about 8\u00a0m.\nSee.\nFeatures.\nScattered remains of a historically important wreck of an 18th century Portuguese slave ship, in very broken up condition,\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nNo known site-specific hazards reported.\nSkills.\nNo special skills recommended.\nEquipment.\nNo special equipment needed.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Clifton area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220078", "revid": "79572", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220078", "title": "Canal du Midi", "text": "The Canal du Midi is a historic 17th-century canal in southern France, linking Toulouse with the Mediterranean. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nUnderstand.\nThe Canal du Midi is a historic 17th-century canal in southern France, in the Occitanie region, in the departments of H\u00e9rault, Aude, and Haute-Garonne, as well as Tarn for a section of its water supply system.\nThe canal links Toulouse with the Mediterranean near S\u00e8te, forming part of the Canal des Deux Mers, which connects the Atlantic and Mediterranean waterways. Built between 1666 and 1681 under Pierre-Paul Riquet, it is widely regarded as one of the great engineering achievements of its era and was originally known as the Canal Royal en Languedoc. \nThe canal is about 240 km long and includes a complex system of locks, aqueducts, bridges, reservoirs, and other structures designed to solve the challenge of supplying water across its summit level. Its water supply depends on works associated with the Montagne Noire, including the reservoir at Bassin de Saint-Ferr\u00e9ol, which made the canal\u2019s construction possible. \nToday, the Canal du Midi is valued less for commercial transport than for leisure boating, cycling, and riverside travel, while also serving as a major heritage route through Occitania. In 1996, UNESCO recognised it as a World Heritage Site for its outstanding engineering and artistic design.\nWhile the itinerary is mainly for boats, a bike path runs parallel to the canal, and there are bike rental services in many villages.\nStay safe.\nMost of the boats you can rent do not require a boat license, so anybody can drive it with a half an hour training, mostly teaching some basic driving rules and the lock crossing procedure.\nBeware of cargo boats. They are heavy and can't turn easily, so get out of his way.\nGo next.\nSee the articles of the endpoint of your journey.\nOther waterway itineraries include:\nOr if you want to follow a waterway by land away from the tow paths:"}
{"id": "220079", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220079", "title": "Interstate 5/Alternatives", "text": "Highway 99.\nSeveral segments of Interstate 5 directly replaced U.S. Route 99, which is now decommissioned.\nFrom its southern end near Wheeler Ridge (Exit 221 of I-5) to its northern end at SR 36 in Red Bluff, State Route (SR) 99 follows the Golden State Highway through the densely populated eastern parts of the valley. Cities served include Bakersfield, Delano, Tulare, Visalia, Kingsburg, Selma, Fresno, Madera, Yosemite National Park, Merced, Turlock, Modesto, Manteca, Stockton, Sacramento, Yuba City, and Chico. SR 99 replaces US Route 99 between Wheeler Ridge and Sacramento and US Route 99E from north of Sacramento to Red Bluff (as the Golden State Freeway). Interstate 5 bypasses the Central Valley along its western edge between Wheeler Ridge and Sacramento and parallels the old US Route 99W between Sacramento and Red Bluff as the Westside Freeway.\n Historically US Route 99 splits in Sacramento at the present junction of State Route (SR) 99 and US Route 50. US Route 99W goes west from Sacramento to Davis along I-80 (formerly US Hwy 40), from Davis to Woodland on Road 99, and alongside I-5 from Woodland to Red Bluff. The old US Route 99E goes east along I-80/US Route 50 to Roseville. SR-65 from Roseville to Olivehurst; SR-70 from Olivehurst to Yuba City and on the Golden State Freeway (present day CA-99) from Yuba City to Red Bluff. The remaining segments in northern California, to Oregon, have been decommissioned as a US or state highway except SR 273 through Anderson and Redding; SR 263 and a section of SR 96 between Holbrook and Yreka; or as Business Loop (BL) 5 through different towns along the way in northern California. In southern California the old Highway 99 continues east along the present Interstate 10 from Los Angeles to Indio and then SR 86 from Indio to Brawley along the western shores of the Salton Sea. From Brawley to the Mexican border in Calexico the Old Hwy 99 follows SR-111. In everywhere else the Old Hwy 99 exists as local roads/streets named \"Old Highway 99\", \"State Highway 99W\", \"Golden State\", \"Road 99\" or by a different name from a time before the Golden State Freeway (US Route 99/99E, Los Angeles to Red Bluff) and the Cascade Wonderland Freeway (from Red Bluff to the Oregon border) were built in the 1940s through the 1950s which are described throughout the Interstate 5 articles.\nBetween Eugene and Grants Pass, I-5 runs concurrent on OR-99. OR-99 runs as separate spur roads through Sutherlin, Roseburg and Winston in separate sections and continuously from Grants Pass (exit 58) to Ashland (exit 14). OR-99 (Rogue Valley Hwy) passes through Grants Pass, Gold Hill, Central Point, Medford, Phoenix, Talent and Ashland as a local surface street. From Ashland to the border with California the Old Hwy 99 follows OR-66 to the south of Emigrant Lake and on OR-273 from Emigrant Lake to the California border. Going north from Cottage Grove, OR-99 goes through Eugene as a series of local streets and north to Junction City where it splits between OR 99E &amp; OR 99W (formerly US Hwy 99E &amp; 99W), through the Willamette Valley, to Portland where they rejoin before crossing the Columbia River into Washington:\n Junction City, Halsey, Albany, Salem, Woodburn, Canby, Oregon City, Gladstone, Milwaukie, Portland\n Eugene, Albany, Salem, Wilsonville, Portland. I-5 runs concurrent with OR-99E from Albany to Exit 258 in Keizer, north of Salem\n Junction City, Corvallis, Amity, Monmouth, McMinnville, Newberg, Tigard, Portland\n Unlike California and Oregon only a short segment of Washington's Old Hwy 99 ((47 mi long) between Fife and Everett have been commissioned as State Route (SR) 99. The remaining segments of Old Highway 99 run continuously alongside I-5, as a series of separate roads, between Olympia and Vancouver, Washington; Tacoma through Fife; and from Everett to Bellingham as \"Old Pacific Highway\", \"Old Hwy 99\", \"Pacific Hwy\" or by a local name. Some sections have been re-numbered as a different state highway described throughout the article in the below. There have been different alignments and re-alignments throughout its existence before 1940 which are included in some parts of the I-5 articles but not all. Most of the segments described in this article are what is/was there as US Highway 99 when Interstate 5 was built in 1964-1969:\nOther highways.\nAlternatives for traveling north/south through California include US-Hwy 101, El Camino Real and the Pacific Coast Highway in the west, or US-Hwy 395 along the Eastern Sierra and east of the Cascades in Oregon and Washington. All of these are more scenic and interesting routes but are much longer and slower and are more of a journey to experience than just for getting from A to B. SR-99, US-395, US-Hwy 101 and Old Hwy 99/Pacific Hwy are also local streets/roads at slower speeds through some of the cities and towns they pass through. In some places the de-commissioned \"Old Hwy 99\" can have a different local street name rather than \"Pacific Hwy\" or \"Old Highway 99\". They are all connected to I-5 and each other via various east-west roads in all three states:\n* Amtrak Coast Starlight runs once daily between Los Angeles and Seattle via Portland, Klamath Falls, Redding, Sacramento, San Francisco Bay Area, Salinas, Paso Robles, San Luis Obispo, Santa Barbara, Oxnard, Simi Valley and Van Nuys. It shares the same route with the \"Cascades\" between Seattle and Eugene, with the same stops (except Tukwila, Vancouver, Washington, and Oregon City); and with the \"Pacific Surfliner\" between San Luis Obispo and Los Angeles (see below).\n* Amtrak Cascades runs between Seattle and Portland four times daily serving Tukwila, Tacoma, Lacey (Olympia), Centralia and Kelso in Washington state. They run from Seattle to Vancouver via Everett, Stanwood, Mt Vernon &amp; Bellingham twice daily and from Portland to Eugene via Oregon City, Salem &amp; Albany twice daily. Additional service from Seattle to Vancouver and from Portland to Eugene are provided on the Amtrak Thruway Bus.\n* Pacific Surfliner San Luis Obispo, Santa Barbara, Oxnard, Los Angeles &amp; San Diego. The segment of this route closest or parallel to the I-5 corridor is between Simi Valley and San Diego. Passengers continue south towards the Mexican border in San Ysidro on the SDMTS 'UC San Diego Blue Line' Trolley from the American Plaza Station, across the street the Santa Fe Station.\n* Gold Runner Sacramento, Merced, Stockton, Fresno and Bakersfield. Some trains on this route will go west towards Emeryville and Oakland from Stockton instead of continuing north to Sacramento. This route is then connected to Los Angeles from Bakersfield and from Stockton &amp; Sacramento to Redding by Thruway buses via I-5.\nWithout a car.\nBy bus.\nThere are several bus companies operating buses along the I-5/CA-Hwy 99 corridor with Greyhound, Flixbus and Fronteras del Norte serving the entire length while other companies operate buses along certain sections of the I-5 corridor in the Pacific Northwest (Oregon or Washington) or California to make the trip possible without a car:\nThere are additional express and local buses operating as local public transportation along the I-5 corridor at varying distances in different places. These options are generally cheaper than Greyhound or other intercity carriers for the same distances. See \"By bus\" under \"Get in\" and \"Get around\" in the article of a particular city or locale and under \"Get around\" in a state article for an additional list of other bus companies serving the west coast."}
{"id": "220081", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220081", "title": "Interstate 5/Washington", "text": "The maximum speed limit is 70 MPH (112 km/h) for cars &amp; 60 MPH (100 km/h) for large trucks on rural stretches and 60 MPH (100 km/h) in urban areas or lower if posted otherwise. Some of the highway rest stops in Washington are also staffed by volunteers from different local religious and fraternal organizations such as the Lions Club or Elk's Club to offer free coffee, tea, water and cookies to travelers. Some even offer home baked goods such as brownies too. A donation of any amount is appreciated in the can provided on the counter. Rest stops in Washington state do not have exit numbers but rather marked with a blue sign rather than the green sign.\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \nSeattle Metropolitan Area in The Puget Sound Region.\nNorth of Olympia the interstate enters the large conurbation of cities in Thurston, Pierce, King and Snohomish counties. This section is multi-lane (3 to 5) in both directions and is busy with traffic at nearly all times of the day. If at all possible avoid driving at peak commuter times. Some drivers may be tempted to take Interstate 405 through Bellevue as an alternate route, but check traffic conditions before doing so as it is often even worse for traffic congestion than Interstate 5.\n* \n* \n* \n* \nDue to long term construction activities along the freeway, through Joint Base Lewis-McChord (JBLM), from Center Dr (Exit #118) to Gravelly Lake #134 the speed limit is reduced to 50 mph. Exits into local side streets may have changed so follow signs closely and prepare for heavy traffic of less than 50 mph going through this area. There are limited or no access through JBLM to reach other surface roads in adjacent civilian areas.\n* \n* \n* \nDue to long term construction activities along the freeway from S 38th through Fife the I-5/SR-16 interchange, the adjacent exit configurations and the freeway lane alignments between Exits 137A-B through 132 are subject to change. Speed limit through this segment is 50 mph. Follow signs closely and prepare for heavy traffic of less than 50 mph through this area.\n* \n* \n* \n* Serves as the major east-west arterial route through Federal Way.\n* Accessible only to carpools (of 2 or more); buses and mopeds. Exit and merging ramps are on left side in both directions. Yes they do check for violators of the carpool only rule.\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* "}
{"id": "220082", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220082", "title": "Interstate 5/Oregon", "text": "The maximum rural speed limit is 70 mph (112 km/h) &amp; 65 mph for large trucks which is more relevant along I-84 between The Dalles &amp; the Snake River (Idaho border). The I-5 corridor remains at 65 mph (104km/h) in rural areas and 55-60 mph in urban areas or lower if posted otherwise. The stretch of I-5 between Portland &amp; Eugene remains at 55-60mph (88-100km/h). The state fully lifted its decades-long ban on self-service gas on August 4, 2023. However, the law does not require any station to offer self-service, and stations in counties along the I-5 corridor must reserve at least half of their pumps for full-service. When you arrive at a filling station that doesn't offer self-service, wait for an attendant to come to your car rather than attempting to get out and fill up your car on your own.\n In Oregon, Highways 99 and 99E serves as a business routes to Interstate 5, and are which are described throughout I-5 articles. Highway 99W, formerly U.S. 99W, runs several miles west of I-5 and serves some communities I-5 does not. For more information on Highway 99 in Oregon, see Interstate 5/Alternatives#Highway 99.\nSouthern Oregon.\nSouthern Oregon has become a popular retirement community over the past few decades; what was once a lumber-oriented economy has gradually adapted to service and tourism-based businesses. Like much of Oregon, the outdoors are one of the region's most valued assets, but more visible products include pears, wine, and timber products. The region is primarily composed of mountains and high desert, while the Rogue Valley, Grants Pass, and Klamath regions are home to agricultural, commercial, and industrial enterprises.\n* \n* Accessible from the northbound lanes only.\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \nWillamette Valley.\nThe Willamette Valley is centered around the Willamette River, and home to the state's three largest cities and much of the state's agriculture industry and the Oregon Wine Country:\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* There are multiple roads/bridges crossing over I-5 between Hwy 22 at mm 253 and Hwy 99E at mm 258 that are not accessible. This is the only exit to take between Hwy 22 and 99E.\n* \nPortland Metropolitan Area.\nThe Portland Metropolitan area is a large conurbation of cities (municipalities) surrounding Portland, Oregon in the northern end of the Willamette Valley and Southwest Washington (Clark County, WA) at the confluence of the Willamette and Columbia Rivers.\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* "}
{"id": "220083", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220083", "title": "Interstate 5/Central and Northern California", "text": "The maximum speed limit is 70 mph (112 km/h) for cars on rural stretches and 65 mph (104 km/h) in urban areas or lower if posted otherwise. Speed limit for large trucks is 55 mph (88 km/h) in both rural and urban areas.\nCentral Valley.\nMost of this section is two-lane carriageway in both directions, running straight with only small rises through sparsely populated areas. Most of the route here is uninteresting, though in some places small detours reveal points of interest.\nSacramento Valley.\nThe Sacramento Valley is the area of the Central Valley of California that lies north of the Sacramento\u2013San Joaquin River Delta and is drained by the Sacramento River. It encompasses all or parts of ten Northern California counties. Although many areas of the Sacramento Valley are rural, it contains several urban areas, including the state capital, Sacramento.\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \nShasta Cascades Region.\nThe Shasta Cascades region is home to volcanoes, lush forests, high mountains, and clear lakes. California is the most populous state in the US, but you wouldn't suspect it from traveling through most of this region, which is generally wild and remote.\n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* \n* "}
{"id": "220084", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220084", "title": "Interstate 5/Southern California", "text": "* \n* \n* \n* \n* \nOrange County and Los Angeles.\nPast the Camp Pendleton Marine Base the interstate enters the large conurbation of cities in Orange and Los Angeles Counties. This section is multi-lane (3 to 8) in both directions and is busy with traffic at nearly all times of the day. If at all possible avoid driving at peak commuter times. Some drivers may be tempted to take Interstate 405 as an alternate route, but check traffic conditions before doing so as it is often even worse for traffic congestion.\nGrapevine.\nThe road climbs out of the San Fernando Valley, which although multi-lane will have slow-moving traffic before entering the Santa Clarita Valley. Oddly, at one point the north and south bound carriageways cross and run for a number of miles separate from one another. This is often closed during snow storms when driving conditions become treacherous. Due to steep grades, it is common for big rig trucks to \"jack-knife\" on this stretch. The highway descends at a sharp six percent grade through a curving path within Grapevine Canyon from Lebec down to the Central Valley. The original route was infamous for its accidents until being straightened and widened between 1933-34. "}
{"id": "220088", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220088", "title": "Mulgimaa", "text": "Mulgimaa is a distinct cultural and historical region in South Estonia, encompassing parts of Viljandi and Valga counties. Famous for its rich heritage, rolling hills, ancient hill forts, manor estates, and iconic black longcoats (Mulgi kuub), Mulgimaa offers a peaceful escape into Estonian nature and traditional rural culture.\nUnderstand.\nMulgimaa consists of five historical parishes: Halliste, Karksi, Helme, Paistu, and Tarvastu.\nIn the mid-19th century, Mulgimaa became the birthplace of the Estonian national awakening when local farmers were among the first in Estonia to buy out their farmsteads from Baltic-German estate owners, primarily through profitable flax cultivation. This wealth created a proud, distinct identity characterized by:"}
{"id": "220089", "revid": "2441142", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220089", "title": "M\u00f5isak\u00fcla", "text": "M\u00f5isak\u00fcla is officially the smallest town in Estonia, located in Mulgimaa (Viljandi County) right on the border with Latvia. Once a thriving railway hub, M\u00f5isak\u00fcla is famous for its industrial heritage, quiet tree-lined streets, traditional wooden architecture, and its location along the green railway hiking routes (Green Railways / Rohelised R\u00f6\u00f6pad).\nUnderstand.\nFounded in 1895 around the Valga\u2013R\u016bjiena\u2013P\u00e4rnu narrow-gauge railway and major railway maintenance workshops, M\u00f5isak\u00fcla grew rapidly into an industrial railway settlement. At its peak, the town housed over 3,000 residents, train depots, and heavy workshops.\nAfter the closure of the railway network in the late 20th century, M\u00f5isak\u00fcla transformed into a peaceful town with roughly 750 inhabitants. The former track beds have since been revitalized into non-motorized recreational trails (Green Railways / Rohelised R\u00f6\u00f6pad), attracting hikers, cyclists, and railway history enthusiasts."}
{"id": "220096", "revid": "2360618", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220096", "title": "Lancieux", "text": "Lancieux is a small town in the C\u00f4tes-d'Armor.\nDo.\nWalk \"Autour de Lancieux\", this is a pleasant circuit walk that takes you along the western border of Lancieux (following the GR34 long-distance hiking trail), past a string of bays, through some peaceful countryside and a few quiet roads, then in the estuary of the river Fr\u00e9mur and back to the town centre. An easy walk that should take between two and three hours. The route is signposted in places (on yellow fence posts), but there are reasonably long stretches without sign posting, so it may be wise to use the GPX route.\nBuy.\nThere's a weekly market that runs on Tuesday mornings on Rue de Poncel, it can get quite busy. Items sold include clothes, cookware, souvenirs, rotisserie chicken, Japanese food from stalls, fruit and vegetables, cheese, saucisson, etc. Ideally, bring cash."}
{"id": "220124", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220124", "title": "Libmanan", "text": "Libmanan is a municipality in northwestern part of Camarines Sur.\nUnderstand.\nLibmanan is the largest municipality in Camarines Sur in terms of population, and the second largest in terms of land area. It stretched across nearly the whole width of the Bicol peninsula, from where it borders Cabusao Municipality on the San Miguel Bay all the way out to Ragay Gulf, including three islands known locally as the 'tres Marias', including one island known as 'puro island'. The main town, or 'poblacion', is located along the Libmanan River on the lowland alluvial plain adjacent to Cabusao.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Pili (') or take a flight to Daraga (').\nBy land.\nThere are many bus terminal in Metro Manila that are bound to Bicol. Libmanan is just along the AH26.\nGet around.\nPublic transportation is provided by tricycles and pedicabs.\nGo next.\nNaga - a vibrant city in Camarines Sur."}
{"id": "220130", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220130", "title": "Matnog", "text": "Matnog is the southernmost municipality of the province of Sorsogon and all of Luzon. It is at the tip of Bicol Peninsula. The town has a port going to Samar Island and vice versa.\nUnderstand.\nIn 1785, Matnog became a barrio of Bulusan, with Juan Evangelista serving as its first head. Fifteen years later, it was designated as Puerto Civil. It later became a separate parish on November 21, 1963.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Daraga () because the province lacks that infrastructure.\nBy land.\nThere are many bus terminals in Metro Manila that are bound to Bicol especially to Sorsogon City. From there, you can ride a bus heading to Matnog.\nGet around.\nPedicabs and tricycles are available in downtown.\nGo next.\nSorsogon City - the vibrant capital city of Sorsogon."}
{"id": "220134", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220134", "title": "Buhi", "text": "Buhi is a municipality in Camarines Sur which contains the eponymous lake. The town borders the province of Albay in the south. It is the only town in Rinconada District that does not speak Rinconada but instead speaks the variety of Bikol language called Buhinon\nUnderstand.\nThe town is known for its Lake Buhi where sinarapan (\"Mystychtis luzonensis\") is located, the world\u2019s smallest edible fish. This tiny fish lives in the lake and is a well-known local species. The town is recognized for being the home of this unique freshwater fish.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Daraga ().\nBy land.\nPITX in Para\u00f1aque has a bus heading directly to Buhi.\nGet around.\nYou will see lots of public utility vehicles like jeepneys and tricycles in downtown Buhi."}
{"id": "220137", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220137", "title": "Sag\u00f1ay", "text": "Sag\u00f1ay is a municipality in Partido District of Camarines Sur that borders the town of Tiwi of Albay province. Its coastline is facing Lagonoy Gulf.\nUnderstand.\nSag\u00f1ay was established in the late 17th century by the Spanish friars. Like the town of Lagonoy, Sag\u00f1ay also formed part of the province of Albay but it was separated in 1846 by order of Governor-General Narciso Claver\u00eda.\nSaint Andrew\u2019s Parish was founded the same year Sag\u00f1ay became a separate town. The town's annual fiesta is held every November 30, Saint Andrew\u2019s Day.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is either in Pili (') or in Daraga (').\nBy land.\nPara\u00f1aque Integrated Terminal Exchange in Para\u00f1aque has buses bound to Bicol especially to Sag\u00f1ay.\nGet around.\nJeepneys and tricycles are available in downtown Sag\u00f1ay."}
{"id": "220139", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220139", "title": "Dadra", "text": "Dadra (Gujarati: \u0aa6\u0abe\u0aa6\u0ab0\u0abe \"D\u0101dr\u0101\") is an enclave within Gujarat in Western India. It is a part of the union territory of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu.\nGet in.\nVapi is the nearest railway station to Dadra."}
{"id": "220140", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220140", "title": "Rural Nagar Haveli", "text": "Rural Nagar Haveli is one of the less-explored parts of Western India, and part of the union territory of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu."}
{"id": "220141", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220141", "title": "Vapi", "text": "Vapi (\u0ab5\u0abe\u0aaa\u0ac0 \"V\u0101p\u012b\"), an industrial town in South Gujarat, is a major transport hub serving the union territory of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nVapi is well-connected to Mumbai, Ahmedabad and Delhi by the Golden Quadrilateral (NH 48).\nDrink.\nAlcohol is available at the Daman side of the Daman-Vapi border."}
{"id": "220146", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220146", "title": "Rajasthani phrasebook", "text": "Rajasthani (\u0930\u093e\u091c\u0938\u094d\u0925\u093e\u0928\u0940 \"R\u0101jasth\u0101n\u012b\") is the everyday tongue of Rajasthan with over 50 million speakers. Although Hindi is commonly used in markets, hotels and transport hubs, people in rural areas may speak mostly in Rajasthani.\nRajasthani is actually not a single language but a group of related dialects, with Marwari being the most common. This phrasebook focuses on Standard Rajasthani, which is based on literary Marwari.\nPronunciation guides.\nRajasthani is more challenging than Hindi due to the presence of implosives, which is rather uncommon in South Asian languages except Sindhi.\nConsonants.\nImplosives.\nImplosives are pronounced by inhaling instead of exhaling."}
{"id": "220151", "revid": "2441594", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220151", "title": "Wirye", "text": "Wirye New Town is a planned city developed across Jangji-dong and Geoyeo-dong in Songpa-gu, Seoul; Changgok-dong in Sujeong-gu, Seongnam-si; and Hagam-dong in Hanam-si, Gyeonggi-do. It is named after Wiryeseong.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe name Wirye New Town originates from Wiryeseong, the capital of Baekje, one of the three kingdoms during the Three Kingdoms period of Korea. The area was subsequently merged and split into various administrative divisions, eventually taking on its current administrative names through multiple boundary reorganizations. It was previously a greenbelt zone where development was restricted, but the restrictions were lifted in 2005, and it came to be called Wirye New Town following the 2008 master plan. The initial move-ins began in December 2013, starting with the two complexes: Wirye Complex 22 and Complex 24."}
{"id": "220154", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220154", "title": "Chandaneswar", "text": "Chandaneswar (Odia: \u0b1a\u0b28\u0b4d\u0b26\u0b28\u0b47\u0b36\u0b4d\u0b71\u0b30 \"Chondoneshworo\", Bengali: \u099a\u09a8\u09cd\u09a6\u09a8\u09c7\u09b6\u09cd\u09ac\u09b0 \"Chondoneshwor\") is a popular pilgrimage site in northeastern Odisha, near the border to West Bengal. It is often called the \"Second Puri\" due to the popular Shiva temple."}
{"id": "220165", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220165", "title": "Bato (Camarines Sur)", "text": "\"This article is about the municipality in Camarines Sur. For the municipality in Catanduanes, see Bato\"\nBato is a municipality of Camarines Sur that is home to a lake teeming with various kinds of fishes like tilapia housed in fish cages. Sinarapan, the world's smallest commercially harvested fish, occupies the waters of Lake Bato.\nUnderstand.\nThis town used to be called \"Kaliligno\" or \"Caliligno\" by the natives that settled along the river area.\nThis small village was elevated into a status of a town under the decree of Spanish Superior Government on February 15, 1753. Years back, a parish was already existing which was also made under the same decree. This parish adopted \"The Most Holy Trinity\" as its patron saint. Its feast day is celebrated every Sunday after the Pentecost. The municipality is also known for its best noodles called \"Pansit Bato\".\nBato is 26 kilometres (16 mi) from the capital town of Pili and 476 kilometres (296 mi) from the country's capital city of Manila.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is either in Pili (') or in Daraga (').\nBy land.\nBus terminals in Metro Manila has a direct point-to-point route to Bato. The town sits along the Asian Highway 26.\nGet around.\nPublic Utility Vehicles like jeepneys and tricycles are available in downtown Bato."}
{"id": "220166", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220166", "title": "Labo", "text": "Labo is a municipality in Camarines Norte, Philippines, known for its rich agricultural heritage, scenic mountains, waterfalls, and the Busig-on River. Its culture and natural resources are celebrated through local festivals, particularly the Busig-on Festival, reflecting the creativity, traditions, and warm hospitality of the Laboe\u00f1os.\nUnderstand.\nThe town traces its roots to an early settlement traditionally known as Busog-on, which flourished around its rivers and fertile lands. Local history tells of the community being led by Tarik-Kudook, a local chieftain.\nThe municipality's name, Labo, is traditionally associated with a story from the Spanish period involving a missionary who asked about the name of the place. A local boatman reportedly answered, \"\u201cLabo po ng tubig,\u201d\" referring to the unclear or muddy river water. The name eventually became associated with the settlement.\nLabo was formally established as a municipality on September 8, 1800. Over the years, it developed as an agricultural and resource-rich community, benefiting from its extensive forests, rivers, fertile lands, and mineral deposits.\nToday, Labo is one of the largest municipalities in Camarines Norte, known for its rich natural resources, majestic mountains, rivers, waterfalls, agricultural landscapes, and vibrant cultural traditions. Its history reflects the enduring connection between its people, land, water, and community\u2014heritage that continues to shape Labo's identity and future.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThe town is accessible by bus from Manila, Daet, and other major cities via the Maharlika Highway, with buses bound for Daet stopping in Labo.\nBy car.\nLabo, Camarines Norte is accessible via the Maharlika Highway (AH26), approximately 16 kilometers from Daet and about 326 kilometers from Manila.\nBy plane.\nBy plane, fly to Naga Airport () in Pili, Camarines Sur, the nearest airport with scheduled flights, then continue by land to Labo, Camarines Norte.\nGet around.\nJeepneys and tricycles are abundant in downtown. They can take you to the outlying barangays."}
{"id": "220168", "revid": "50919", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220168", "title": "Dholka", "text": "Dholka is a historic city and municipality in Central Gujarat, India. Located 50 km southwest of Ahmedabad, Dholka offers a quiet escape into medieval Indo-Islamic architecture, Solanki-era stepped tanks, and rich mythological lore.\nUnderstand.\nLocal tradition links the city to the ancient town of Viratnagar from the Mahabharata. Dholka grew into an influential administrative and commercial centre during the Chaulukya (Solanki) and Vaghela dynasties (10th\u201313th centuries) when it was known as Dhavallak. During the Tughluq and Gujarat Sultanate period, several architecturally beautiful mosques were built by the generals. Later it fell under the Maratha rulers for a brief period. During the British period, it was under the Bombay Presidency.\nThe zenith of the city is long gone but the monuments of the glorious past still remain.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Ahmedabad, 50 km away.\nBy bus.\nFrequent state transport (GSRTC) buses connect to all major cities in Gujarat from and. Ahmedabad is reached in 1 to 1.5 hours.\nBy car.\nFrom Ahmedabad, the drive takes 45\u201360 minutes via NH 8A or the Bagodara highway.\nGet around.\nAutorickshaws are the other main local mode of public transport. The old town is best explored on foot due to narrow lanes."}
{"id": "220171", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220171", "title": "Tajpur", "text": "Tajpur (\u09a4\u09be\u099c\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0 \"T\u0101jpur\") is a beach spot in Purba Medinipur, West Bengal.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nThe nearest railway station is in Contai, although many Digha tourists also come to Tajpur.\nBy car.\nFor the last 6 km into Tajpur, there is a narrow but all-weather road, which takes you directly to the hotels and resorts, provided you travel in a hired car. You can still drive along the beachside road, but beach developments has a seriously adverse effect on the local ecology, not to mention the atmosphere of the beaches. While driving on the beachside road, stick to the side far from the sea or keep to the established track or you may risk getting caught in mud or sand. The Mandarmani\u2013Tajpur Road connects Jalda bride. After reaching Digha, take or hire a car and stuff which would take one to Tajpur. It takes around 45 minutes to reach there. While the journey takes place, you can feel little unsafe looking at the road side darkness, but no need to worry; actually the locality hardly have electricity so they avoid going out after it gets dark."}
{"id": "220173", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220173", "title": "Udaypur (Odisha)", "text": "Udaypur (Odia: \u0b09\u0b26\u0b5f\u0b2a\u0b41\u0b30 \"Udoyopuro\", Bengali: \u0989\u09a6\u09af\u09bc\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0 \"Udoypur\") is a beach town in northeastern Odisha, near Digha in West Bengal, India.\nGet in.\nUdaypur is 3 km from Digha."}
{"id": "220181", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220181", "title": "Mexico City/Tl\u00e1huac", "text": "Tl\u00e1huac is a borough of Mexico City. It is very different from the traditional tourist areas of the city and is regarded as an Off-the-Beaten-Path part of the city where only naturalists and those studying the Iidigenous practices of pre-Hispanic cultures come to visit. It is one of the 16 \"alcaldias\" (boroughs) that make up CDMX. A poor choice for a first-time visitor, Tlahuac has some hidden gems that can be rewarding for a tenth trip to Mexico City as it reveals some of the city's hidden dimensions.\nGet in.\nBy Metro.\nTlahuac is served by Line 12 of the Mexico City Metro system. There are five Metro stations in Tlahuac\u02d0\nBy bus.\nTl\u00e1huac is served by several routes of the RTP bus service. All RTP routes through Tl\u00e1huac terminate at Metro Tl\u00e1huac, which is a transportation center for the area. No Metrobus routes serve Tl\u00e1huac.\nSee.\nSeven Native Towns.\nThe Siete Pueblos Originarios de Tl\u00e1huac refers to seven indigenous villages making up Tlahuac that significantly pre-date the Spanish conquest. These villages are\u02d0\nConnect.\n5G cell service is common throughout Tlahuac. There are no dead zones."}
{"id": "220183", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220183", "title": "Baao", "text": "Baao is a municipality in Rinconada District of Camarines Sur.\nUnderstand.\nBaao is 15 kilometres (9.3 mi) from Pili and 465 kilometres (289 mi) from Manila.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nCommuter rail services are provided by PNR serving from Naga City as its terminus and stops at Baao Station up to Ligao City in Albay then back again northward to Naga. As of 2026, rail service to Manila remains suspended due severe damages brought by Typhoon Reming in 2006. \nBy bus.\nFrom Naga City, the easiest way to go to Baao is to ride a bus going to Iriga City from the Naga City Central Bus Terminal and disembark at Baao bus stop.\nGet around.\nJeepneys and tricycles are available in downtown."}
{"id": "220184", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220184", "title": "Sipocot", "text": "Sipocot is a municipality of Camarines Sur. It is one of the most populous municipalities in the province and plays a significant role in the socio-economic development of northern Camarines Sur.\nUnderstand.\nStrategically located at the northern part of Camarines Sur, Sipocot serves as a vital gateway between the provinces of Camarines Norte and Camarines Sur. It lies along the Maharlika Highway (Asian Highway 26), a major arterial road that connects the Bicol Region to Metro Manila and other parts of Luzon, making Sipocot a central transit point for trade, travel, and commerce. The municipality is approximately 38 kilometers from Naga City, the region\u2019s commercial and cultural center, and about 280 kilometers from Metro Manila.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nCatch a Bicol-bound bus from major terminals in Cubao (Quezon City) or Pasay/Buendia (Manila) operated by companies like Bicol Isarog or Jam Liner.\nGet around.\nPublic Utility Vehicles are plenty in downtown."}
{"id": "220185", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220185", "title": "Upper West Region", "text": "The Upper West Region is a region in the Ghanaian Northern Plains. It is one of Ghana's northernmost regions and shares an international border with Burkina Faso.\nUnderstand.\nThe three main ethnic groups of the Upper West Region are the Wala, Dagaba and Sisaala. \nEat.\nThe staple food of the Upper West Region is \"tuo zaafi\", a swallow made from maize or millet, which is usually consumed with a soup or stew."}
{"id": "220186", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220186", "title": "Savannah Region", "text": "Savannah Region is a region in the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nTrue to its name, the vegetation in the region is characterised by savannah. "}
{"id": "220187", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220187", "title": "Upper East Region", "text": "The Upper East Region is in the Ghanaian Northern Plains."}
{"id": "220189", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220189", "title": "North East Region", "text": "The North East Region is in the Ghanaian Northern Plains.\nUnderstand.\nThe dry season is between December and April."}
{"id": "220198", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220198", "title": "Bus travel in India", "text": "While you cannot take a bus journey across India, buses are the second most popular means of travelling across states after railways. India's bus network is wide, and it's the only cheap way of reaching many places not on the rail network, such as Imphal, Leh or Gangtok. Although the journey can be rough on Indian roads, travelling on them can add a new dimension to a visitor's experience.\nPublic buses.\nEvery Indian state and union territory has its own transport companies that operate public buses within the state and also in neighbouring states. These are often called state transport buses or ST buses for short. Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu, Ladakh, Manipur, Meghalaya and Mizoram don't have their own transport companies.\nMany public bus companies have their own websites to find the schedule and prices, but also to book tickets. If searching for connections, the website will sometimes not accept smaller villages along the way, and you may have to select or search for the next larger town or city.\nPrivate buses.\nPrivate buses may or may not be available in the area you are travelling to, and even if they are, the quality could vary a lot. Be warned that many of the private buses, especially long-distance lines, play music or videos at ear-splitting volume. Even with earplugs, it can be nerve-wracking. Restrooms are available in large bus stations but are crowded. The bus industry is extremely fragmented and there are few operators who offer services in more than 2 or 3 neighbouring states. Travel agents usually \"only\" offer seats on private buses. As of 2024, FlixBus offers rides in the north and north-west of India and intends to serve the whole country.\nFor major private bus connections, schedules, prices and tickets, try one of the following websites:\nFares.\nPrices between public and private buses do not differ much. The advantage of public buses is that you will get a proper ticket indicating the origin and destination of your journey. Private buses will only give you a paper ticket with a price written on it by the conductor. For buses serving smaller villages there might not even be any ticket at all for private buses \u2014 if the other Indian passengers also don't get a ticket, then that's just the way. In general, you can expect to pay as little as \u20b91/km in the plains (Punjab) and \u20b92\u20132.30/km in more mountainous areas (Himachal Pradesh) with public and private buses. After a short while you will figure out what is the going local rate. Anything way beyond that rate, especially in private buses, is an indication that the conductor is trying to rip you off. Stand your ground and don't give in, potentially demand your money back and leave the bus.\nCity buses.\nDespite the uber-popularity of metro railways, buses are still one of the many sustainable ways to explore cities like Kolkata and Mumbai. Many large cities have their own transport companies operating on intracity and intercity routes.\nSome cities have bus rapid transit (BRT) systems, which have additional features like higher capacity, reliability and separate busways. As of 2026, Ahmedabad, Amritsar, Hubli-Dharwad, Indore, Jaipur, Pune, Raipur, Rajkot, Surat, Vijayawada and Visakhapatnam are served by BRT systems.\nStay safe.\nAll buses have to contend with the poor state of Indian highways and the havoc of Indian traffic which usually makes them slower, less comfortable and less safe than trains. Night buses are particularly hazardous, and for long-distance travel it is wise to opt for sleeper train services instead."}
{"id": "220215", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220215", "title": "Pamplona (Camarines Sur)", "text": "Pamplona is a municipality in Camarines Sur.\nUnderstand.\nThe beginnings of this town can be traced back from the date it was made a visita of Milaor. The town of Pamplona was founded on May 8, 1817. It first started as a settlement west of the Bicol River, then it grew to become a Sitio called Patong. The name was changed to Pamplona by a retired Spanish military officer who resided in the place and named it after his native city in Spain - Pamplona, capital of the province of Navarre.\nPamplona officially became a parish on May 8, 1885, which coincided with the date of the town's celebration honoring their patron saint, Saint Michael the Archangel.\nGet in.\nBy land.\nBus lines like Isarog Line and Pe\u00f1afrancia Tours offer direct trip from EDSA Cubao to Pamplona, Camarines Sur. Travel time is about 9 hours approximately \u20b1950 to \u20b11,100 depending on the bus tier (regular or elite). You can also take a bus bound for Naga City (Bicol Central Station) and ride a local jeepney or taxi going toward Pamplona (about 14 km away from Naga).\nGet around.\nIn downtown you'll find plenty of public utility vehicles like jeepneys and tricycles."}
{"id": "220230", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220230", "title": "Espinavell", "text": "Espinavell is in Girona Pyrenees. \nUnderstand.\nEspinavell is a hamlet belonging to Moll\u00f3. located at the East entrance to Cap\u00e7aleres del Ter i del Freser Natural Park\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe village is at the end of \u00a0GIV-5225\u00a0, which you can get north of Moll\u00f3 from the \u00a0C-38\u00a0, the road from Camprodon to the French border. Crossing the border becomes de \u00a0D115\u00a0 that goes from the border until Le Boulou.\nIn the city starts the unpaved road Cam\u00ed de Setcases a Espinavell, which ends near Setcases."}
{"id": "220235", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220235", "title": "Pilar (Sorsogon)", "text": "\"For other places by the name, see Pilar (disambiguation).\"\nPilar is a municipality in the province of Sorsogon. The town is accessible via Asian Highway 26 coming from Daraga town.\nUnderstand.\nThe town got its name in honor of a newborn Spanish princess named Pilar, the daughter of King Philip and Queen Isabela.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest airport is in Daraga ().\nHead to the van or jeepney terminals in Legazpi City or Daraga (such as near Ba\u00f1ag). Take a UV Express van or jeepney directly bound for Pilar or Pilar Port. Travel time from Legazpi/Daraga to Pilar is around 45 to 60 minutes.\nGet around.\nJeepneys and tricycles are abundant in downtown connecting the town proper to the outlying barangays."}
