{"id": "220239", "revid": "2361892", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220239", "title": "Magallanes (Sorsogon)", "text": "\"For other places by the name, see Magallanes (disambiguation).\"\nMagallanes is a municipality of the province of Sorsogon.\nUnderstand.\nAccording to renowned historians and anthropologists such as Domingo Abella, Luis Camara Dery, Merito Espinas, F. Mallari, Norman Owen, Mariano Goyena del Prado, et al., Magallanes was the location of the ancient settlement of Ibalong.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nBook a flight from Manila to Bicol International Airport. From Legazpi, ride a van or bus to Sorsogon City (approx. 1.5 to 2 hours).\nBy plane.\nTake a provincial bus (such as Philtranco, DLTB, or Cagsawa Travel) bound for Sorsogon City from terminals in Metro Manila (like Pasay, Cubao, or Alabang). The trip takes about 12 to 14 hours.\nGet around.\nJeepneys and tricycles are available in downtown."}
{"id": "220244", "revid": "794879", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220244", "title": "Byala (Ruse Province)", "text": "Byala (Bulgarian: \u0411\u044f\u043b\u0430) is a small town in North Bulgaria, halfway between Ruse and Veliko Tarnovo. Its modest main attractions are an old stone bridge and a museum dedicated to the Russo-Turkish War of 1877.\nUnderstand.\nByala is 45 km south-west from Ruse, and the same distance north-east from Veliko Tarnovo, on the west bank of the river Yantra, the same one that flows through Tarnovo. The town's population of about 6000 people (2021) makes it the second-largest urban settlement in Ruse Province. The name \"Byala\" is simply the Bulgarian adjective for \"white\" in feminine form; it's assumed that the town was named after the white clay soil of the nearby heights.\nThe convenient river crossing has attracted people for millennia. In the 4th century CE, a Roman fortress was erected on the hill to guard a road connecting Iatrus and Nicopolis-ad-Istrum. The earliest records of the modern settlement date to the 16th century, under Ottoman rule, when it was a small village. Over time, the road between the river port of Ruse and the mountain passes by Veliko Tarnovo grew in importance, and so did also Byala.\nIn the mid-19th century, the Ottoman authorities commissioned a stone bridge over the river. The task was taken up by the famed Bulgarian master-stonemason Kolyo Ficheto (Nikola Fichev). Reportedly, he was illiterate; he made a model of the bridge out of wax candles and recorded key dimensions as notches on his measuring stick. The bridge was completed in 1867. It was 267 m long, 9 m wide, and decorated with stone carvings. Sadly, only four masonry arches on the east side survive out of the original fourteen; the rest were washed out by a flood in 1897 and have been replaced with a series of concrete structures over the years.\nByala was also the scene of two minor episodes in Bulgaria's liberation struggle. In the aftermath of the failed April Uprising of 1876, two of its leaders - Panayot Volov and Georgi Ikonomov - attempted to escape to Romania and hid in Byala on their way. They were discovered, the bridge was under guard, and in the ensuing firefight they drowned when they tried to swim across the Yantra, which was swollen by spring flood.\nDuring the Russo-Turkish War of 1877 (a.k.a. the Liberation War in Bulgaria), Byala was the second settlement liberated by Russian troops after they crossed the Danube at Svishtov. Holding Byala was of strategic importance to cut off Turkish forces coming from the \"quadrangle of fortresses\" Ruse\u2013Silistra\u2013Varna\u2013Shumen to the east. Emperor Alexander II himself visited the village. (Byala was given official status of \"grad\" (town/city) after the liberation, in 1891.)\nGet in.\nByala is a minor station on the railway line Ruse-Gorna Oryahovitsa, which gives it a surprising amount of connectivity by rail (including the international trains from Romania via Ruse). There are multiple daily direct trains from Ruse (80-90 min) and Gorna Oryahovitsa (45-60 min, junction with the Sofia-Varna line), and one direct train from Veliko Tarnovo (70 min) that actually starts in Plovdiv (5 hr 40 min), otherwise from Tarnovo you need to change at nearby Gorna Oryahovitsa (total 80-130 min). The line passes far from Byala proper due to the river, and the railway station has become the seed of a small suburb, creatively named \"Gara Byala\" (\"\u0413\u0430\u0440\u0430 \u0411\u044f\u043b\u0430\", Station Byala). \nLocal bus lines connect Byala to Ruse and Svishtov, as well as neighbouring villages.\nByala is still a crossroads town. European Route E85/National Road 5 passes through the north outskirts of the city, connecting Ruse and Veliko Tarnovo. Secondary roads provide access from Pleven, Svishtov (and its ferry to Romania), and Targovishte (via Popovo).\nSee.\nByala's few sights make it suitable mainly for a stop during a longer journey, or the target of a day trip (e.g. from Ruse).\nBuy.\nThere are two bank offices with ATMs close to each other on the short street on the east side of the main square, across from the church: UBB (\u041e\u0411\u0411) at the south corner, DSKBank (\u0411\u0430\u043d\u043a\u0430 \u0414\u0421\u041a) at the north corner.\nEat.\nThere are several restaurants around town, and the bridge's visitor centre has a cafe.\nConnect.\nThe area code for landline numbers is 817 (0817 if you are dialling from another area in the country). Byala is covered by the networks of all three mobile operators in Bulgaria, which also means that there's also at least 4G coverage. As of September 2026, there are offices of A1 and Vivacom in town, but not Yettel.\nByala's post code is 7100. The post office is in the north-western corner of the main square, next to the \"chitalishte\" building."}
{"id": "220245", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220245", "title": "Opobo-Nkoro", "text": " Opobo-Nkoro is a Local Government Area in Rivers State, Nigeria. It is located in the Niger Delta and consists of riverine communities surrounded by creeks, mangroves and waterways.\nUnderstand.\nOpobo-Nkoro is one of the riverine areas of Rivers State. Much of the area is made up of islands, creeks and wetlands, making waterways an important part of everyday life and transportation.\nThe area is closely associated with the historic town of Opobo, which developed as an important trading centre in the Niger Delta. Fishing, trading and other water-based activities are important to local communities. The landscape is predominantly low-lying and characterized by mangrove vegetation, tidal creeks and rivers.\nHistory.\nThe history of Opobo-Nkoro is closely connected with the history of the Opobo Kingdom and the development of trade in the eastern Niger Delta.\nOpobo became an important centre of commerce during the nineteenth century, particularly through the palm-oil trade. King Jaja of Opobo played an important role in the history of the area and in resistance to British attempts to control the palm-oil trade.\nThe waterways around Opobo-Nkoro historically provided important transportation routes between communities and trading centres.\nClimate.\nOpobo-Nkoro has a tropical rainforest climate with high temperatures and substantial rainfall for much of the year.\nThe rainy season can bring heavy rainfall and flooding in some low-lying communities. The dry season is generally more suitable for travelling by road and water, although temperatures remain warm.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nSome parts of Opobo-Nkoro can be reached by road from other parts of Rivers State, although the riverine geography means that road access is limited in many communities.\nTravellers should check local road conditions before making the journey.\nBy boat.\nBoat transport is an important way of reaching many communities in Opobo-Nkoro.\nPassenger boats and locally operated water transport connect settlements through the extensive network of rivers and creeks.\nGet around.\nBoats and water taxis are among the most practical means of transportation between riverine communities.\nWalking is possible within individual settlements, while motorcycles and other local transport may be available where roads exist."}
{"id": "220246", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220246", "title": "Bonny", "text": "Bonny is a historic island town in Rivers State, Nigeria. It is located in the Niger Delta, on Bonny Island near the Atlantic coast. The town is known for its history, traditional culture, waterways and importance to Nigeria's oil and gas industry.\nUnderstand.\nBonny is the traditional capital of the Bonny Kingdom, one of the historic kingdoms of the eastern Niger Delta.\nThe town is situated on Bonny Island and is surrounded by rivers, creeks, mangrove forests and coastal wetlands. Water transport is therefore an important part of life in the area.\nBonny has a mixture of traditional Niger Delta culture and modern industrial development. The island is also associated with major oil and gas facilities and has become an important centre for the energy industry.\nBonny Local Government Area had a population of 214,983 according to the 2006 census. The town and surrounding communities are predominantly associated with the Ibani people.\nIbani is the main indigenous language spoken in Bonny. English is used for official communication, while Nigerian Pidgin is also widely used in everyday communication.\nHistory.\nBonny has a long history as a trading centre in the Niger Delta. The Bonny Kingdom became particularly important during the nineteenth century through its involvement in regional and overseas trade.\nThe kingdom later became an important centre of the palm-oil trade. Its rulers and merchants maintained trading relationships with European merchants operating along the West African coast.\nThe historic importance of the Bonny Kingdom can still be seen in the town's traditional institutions, cultural festivals and historic buildings.\nClimate.\nBonny has a hot and humid tropical climate with substantial rainfall throughout much of the year.\nThe rainy season can bring heavy rainfall and flooding, while the drier period generally provides more favourable conditions for travelling and outdoor activities.\nGet in.\nBy boat.\nBoat transport is one of the main ways of reaching Bonny Island.\nPassenger boats and water taxis connect Bonny with Port Harcourt and other communities in the Niger Delta.\nTravel times depend on the departure point, boat type and weather conditions.\nBy road.\nBonny Island has road connections within the island, but travellers arriving from mainland Rivers State generally need to use water transport unless travelling via specialised industrial access routes.\nGet around.\nBonny town can be explored on foot, while taxis and motorcycles may be available for local journeys.\nBoats are useful for travelling between Bonny and nearby riverine communities.\nVisitors travelling for business or industrial purposes may need to use authorised transport arranged by their host organisation."}
{"id": "220250", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220250", "title": "Nembe", "text": "Nembe is a historic town in Bayelsa State, Nigeria, and the headquarters of Nembe Local Government Area. It is the traditional centre of the Nembe Kingdom and lies in the riverine environment of the Niger Delta, surrounded by creeks, rivers, mangrove forests and wetlands.\nUnderstand.\nNembe is in the eastern part of Bayelsa State in the Niger Delta. The town is the traditional centre of the Nembe Kingdom and the administrative headquarters of Nembe Local Government Area.\nThe Nembe people are an Ijaw/Izon people whose traditional livelihoods have included fishing, farming, trading and other activities connected with the waterways. Today, petroleum-related activities, government employment, commerce, fishing and small businesses are also important to the local economy.\nNembe is made up historically of important communities including Ogbolomabiri and Bassambiri. The surrounding area contains numerous creeks and waterways, and some communities outside the main town are much easier to reach by boat than by road.\nNembe is the main indigenous language spoken by the people of Nembe. It belongs to the Ijaw (Izon) language group and is therefore commonly described as an Ijaw language. English is used for official communication, education and government business, while Nigerian Pidgin is also widely spoken.\nHistory.\nNembe has a long history as a political and trading centre in the eastern Niger Delta. The Nembe Kingdom developed as one of the traditional Ijaw states of the region and became involved in trade with European merchants during the nineteenth century.\nThe kingdom became particularly important in the palm-oil trade. Nembe traders used the waterways connecting the inland settlements with the coast to transport palm produce, fish and other goods.\nThe history of Nembe is also strongly associated with King Frederick William Koko, commonly known as King Koko. In January 1895, Koko led a major attack against the Royal Niger Company's headquarters at Akassa. The action became one of the most significant episodes of resistance to British commercial and colonial expansion in the Niger Delta.\nBritish forces subsequently carried out a retaliatory expedition against Nembe. The conflict caused extensive destruction and became an important part of the history of the Nembe Kingdom.\nToday, the history of the kingdom, King Koko and the 1895 conflict remains an important part of Nembe's cultural identity.\nClimate.\nNembe has a hot and humid tropical climate typical of the Niger Delta. Rainfall is high for much of the year and the surrounding environment consists largely of freshwater swamp, mangrove and riverine vegetation.\nThe wet conditions can affect road and water transportation, particularly during periods of heavy rainfall.\nGet in.\nNembe can be reached by road and by water. Because the area lies within the Niger Delta, boats remain an important means of transportation, especially for journeys to surrounding riverine communities.\nBy road.\nRoad travel is the main option for reaching the town itself from inland parts of Bayelsa State.\nTravellers coming from Yenagoa can travel towards Nembe by road. The exact journey time depends on the starting point in Yenagoa, traffic, road conditions and the type of vehicle, so travellers should allow several hours rather than relying on a fixed timetable.\nFrom other parts of Nigeria, travellers generally travel first towards Yenagoa or another road-connected part of Bayelsa State before continuing to Nembe.\nRoad conditions can change, particularly during the rainy season. Travellers should confirm current road conditions before making a long journey.\nBy boat.\nWater transport is an important way of travelling around Nembe and the surrounding Niger Delta.\nBoat routes connect Nembe with other riverine settlements and provide an important means of transporting passengers, food, fish and other goods.\nA traveller intending to use a boat should confirm the departure location, destination, fare, loading time and return arrangements before boarding.\nBoat travel time depends on the type and speed of the boat, weather, water conditions, the number of stops and the time required to load passengers.\nNembe to Brass.\nThe Nembe-Brass route is one of the important water transportation routes in the area. A boat journey between Nembe and Brass can take roughly about 1 hour under favourable conditions, although actual travel time varies.\nThe Nembe-Brass road is also being developed to improve land transportation between the two areas. The surrounding terrain has historically made road construction difficult, leaving waterways as a major means of transportation.\nFrom Yenagoa.\nYenagoa is the capital of Bayelsa State and is one of the most useful starting points for travellers going to Nembe.\nTravellers can arrange road transportation from Yenagoa towards Nembe. Depending on the final destination, travellers may also use a combination of road and boat transportation.\nFrom Port Harcourt.\nTravellers coming from Port Harcourt can travel through the road network towards Bayelsa State and continue to Nembe.\nAnother option is to travel towards Yenagoa and then continue to Nembe. Water transportation can also be used for some Niger Delta routes, but travellers should confirm the current route and departure point before travelling.\nGet around.\nBy motorcycle.\nMotorcycles are useful for short-distance journeys around Nembe. They can reach smaller streets and areas where larger vehicles may have difficulty.\nTravellers should agree on the fare before starting a journey and use appropriate safety precautions.\nBy boat.\nBoats are important for travelling beyond the main town into the surrounding creeks and riverine communities.\nVisitors should use established operators, confirm the destination and fare before departure and wear a life jacket throughout the journey.\nOn foot.\nParts of central Nembe can be explored on foot. Walking is particularly practical around markets, shops, public buildings and nearby streets.\nConnect.\nMobile telephone services are available in Nembe, although coverage may be weaker in remote riverine communities. Mobile data is the main means of accessing the internet."}
{"id": "220255", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220255", "title": "Ogbia", "text": "Ogbia is a town in Bayelsa State, Nigeria, and the headquarters of Ogbia Local Government Area. It is also the traditional headquarters of Ogbia Kingdom. The town lies in the Niger Delta and is surrounded by the rivers, creeks, wetlands and tropical vegetation characteristic of the region.\nUnderstand.\nOgbia is an important traditional and administrative centre in Bayelsa State. The wider Ogbia area contains numerous communities, including Oloibiri, Otuaka, Elebele, Imiringi and other settlements.\nThe area is historically important because of its connection with the early petroleum industry in Nigeria. Oloibiri, in present-day Ogbia Local Government Area, is widely associated with the first commercial crude-oil discovery in Nigeria in 1956. The economy of the area includes fishing, farming, trading, public service and petroleum-related activities.\nOgbia Local Government Area has a large and growing population spread across numerous towns and villages. Population figures vary according to the year and whether the figure refers to the town or the entire local government area.\nThe people of Ogbia speak languages belonging to the Ijaw/Izon linguistic environment. English is used for official communication, education and government business, while Nigerian Pidgin is also widely used.\nHistory.\nOgbia has a long history as a traditional community in the Niger Delta. The Ogbia Kingdom developed as an important traditional institution in the area, with the Obanobhan serving as the traditional ruler.\nThe waterways of the region played an important role in communication, fishing, farming and trade.\nThe modern history of Ogbia is strongly connected with the development of the petroleum industry. Oloibiri, a community within the wider Ogbia area, became famous after crude oil was discovered there in commercial quantities in 1956.\nThe discovery at Oloibiri marked a major turning point in Nigeria's economic history and eventually contributed to the development of the country's petroleum industry.\nClimate.\nOgbia has a hot and humid tropical climate. Rainfall is heavy for much of the year, while the surrounding landscape consists of freshwater swamp, wetlands, forests and river systems.\nThe rainy season can affect road conditions and travel to remote communities.\nGet in.\nOgbia is connected to other parts of Bayelsa State by road. Waterways are also important in the wider local government area, particularly for communities located along rivers and creeks.\nBy road.\nThe easiest way to reach Ogbia Town is by road.\nTravellers coming from Yenagoa can travel eastward towards Ogbia. Ogbia is roughly 27 km from Yenagoa by straight-line distance, although the actual road distance is longer.\nThe journey from Yenagoa generally takes less than an hour under favourable road and traffic conditions, but travellers should allow additional time during heavy traffic or bad weather.\nTravellers coming from Port Harcourt can travel through the road network towards Bayelsa State and continue through the Yenagoa/Ogbia corridor.\nBy boat.\nWater transport is important in the wider Ogbia area because many communities are located around rivers and creeks.\nBoat travel can be arranged for journeys to riverine communities, fishing areas and nearby settlements. Departure points and schedules can vary, so travellers should confirm arrangements locally.\nGet around.\nBy taxi.\nLocal taxis and private vehicles can be used for movement around Ogbia Town and nearby communities.\nBy motorcycle.\nMotorcycles are commonly useful for short-distance journeys and for reaching smaller roads.\nBy boat.\nBoats are useful for reaching riverine communities outside the main road network. Visitors should use established operators and wear life jackets.\nOn foot.\nCentral parts of Ogbia can be explored on foot, particularly around markets, shops and public buildings.\nSleep.\nAccommodation in Ogbia is more limited than in Yenagoa. Visitors who require a wider choice of hotels may prefer to stay in Yenagoa and travel to Ogbia during the day.\nStay safe.\nTravellers should obtain current local security information before travelling to remote parts of Ogbia Local Government Area.\nVisitors travelling into remote communities should arrange transportation and return plans in advance.\nStay healthy.\nCarry drinking water when travelling outside the main town.\nThe hot and humid climate means visitors should remain hydrated and protect themselves from mosquitoes and other insects.\nTravellers going into remote areas should carry any personal medicines they require.\nConnect.\nMobile telephone networks are available in Ogbia, although coverage can become weaker in remote riverine communities. Mobile data is the main means of internet access."}
{"id": "220258", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220258", "title": "Mukungarh", "text": "Mukundgarh is one of several villages in the Shekhawati region, once known for the merchant families who left for larger cities in search of business, leaving many settlements sparsely populated. These merchants rarely forgot their hometowns, however, and sent back substantial wealth to build ornately painted havelis (mansions), along with schools and colleges. Many of these havelis still stand today, their frescoed walls retaining much of their original charm.\nUnderstand.\nLike the rest of Shekhawati, Mukundgarh is best visited between October and March, when daytime temperatures are mild. Summers (April-June) are extremely hot and best avoided, and the monsoon (July-September) brings humidity and occasional flooding on rural roads.\nTalk.\nWhile Hindi and the local Shekhawati dialect are most commonly spoken, many locals especially those involved in tourism - know basic English. Visitors are generally welcomed warmly, and people are often happy to help travellers with directions or recommendations, even across a language gap.\nGet in.\nMukundgarh lies roughly 150 km northwest of Jaipur, between the towns of Nawalgarh and Jhunjhunu. There is no direct rail link from major cities, but a branch line connects Mukundgarh to Jaipur via Sikar, with a small station (Dundlod-Mukundgarh) served by a few daily passenger trains.\nBy road.\nOver 45-54 daily bus services run between Jaipur and the Shekhawati region via RSRTC (state transport) and private operators, with fares starting around \u20b9202. Options include AC and non-AC seater, sleeper, semi-sleeper, and Volvo buses. Major boarding points in Jaipur include Sindhi Camp, Polo Victory, Narayan Singh Circle, and Chumu Puliya. Private taxis from Jaipur are also a convenient option for travellers short on time."}
{"id": "220260", "revid": "1789", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220260", "title": "Diving the Cape Peninsula and False Bay/MV Seli 1", "text": "The dive site MV \"Seli 1\" or just the Seli is an inshore recent wreck in Table Bay near Cape Town in the Western Cape province of South Africa.\nUnderstand.\n\"Seli 1\" was a bilk carrier of 18534 tons, built by Hyundai Heavy Industries, and originally named \"Asia Sun\". It was powered by a low speed diesel engine and had a top speed of 16 knots. The vessel was driven agroun by wing in September 2009. Efforts to refloat were unsuccessful, and salvage was done at SA taxpayer's expense as it was not properly insured. The cargo of coal was mostly recovered, an most of the fuel oil removed. An explosion on board while wrecking the hull on site was followed by bad weather which did further damage, and some of the fuel leaked out ad fouled seabirds.\n S33\u00b049.473' E18\u00b028.261', 530\u00a0m off the beach at Bloubergstrand.\nPosition.\nThis site is not in a Marine Protected Area. A permit is not required.\nName.\n\"Seli 1\" is the name of the ship that was wrecked at the site.\nDepth.\nMaximum depth is about 10\u00a0m, and the top of the stern is awash at low tide\nTopography.\nThe wreckage is considerably broken up. The wreck lies upright on the sand, roughly parallel to the shoreline, with the bow to the south. There is a lot of scour all round the wreck, and sand has been deposited ofer the depper wreckage. The hull is 171\u00a0m long on a debris field of about 200\u00a0mmlong and 95\u00a0m wide, as much of the hull plating has collapsed outwards. On the port side roughly amidships there is wreckage of one of the ship's cranes.\nConditions.\nThe site is exposed to winds from any direction, and waves from the west and north-west, so should be dived in easterly winds or a calm, with no westerly swell, and is most likely to be diveable in summer, during or after south easterly winds. The site is reasonably protected from south-westerly swell. A long swell will produce powerful surge, as can be seen from the extensive scour pits.\nGet in.\nBoat dive from Oceana Power Boat Club slipway or the V&amp;A Waterfront, or from Melkbosstrand.\nIt can also be done as a shore dive from the beach but if a southeasterly (offshore) wind develops the swim back should be underwater as far as reasonably practicable.\nDo.\nDive and explore the wreck, and let us know what you find.\nSee.\nFeatures.\nExtensive wreckage of a large cargo vessel of the late 20th century.\nStay safe.\nHazards.\nA long swell will cause strong surge across the wreck, lifting sand and reducing visibility, and potentially knocking a diver against hard and sharp wreckage, which could cause injury or damage to equipment.\nSkills.\nNo special skills required.\nEquipment.\nNo special equipment needed.\nNearby.\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"TMNPMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.470833,-34.109833],[18.556883,-34.109833],\n[18.556883,-34.407400],[18.250000,-34.407400],\n[18.250000,-33.901250],[18.400617,-33.901250,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Table Mountain National Park Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"KRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.364117,-33.979283],[18.296617,-33.979283],\n[18.296617,-34.061000],[18.337533,-34.061000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Karbonkelberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"COGHRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.369900,-34.209517],[18.369900,-34.294833],\n[18.391667,-34.306650],[18.404300,-34.306650,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Cape of Good Hope Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"PRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.474400,-34.275817],[18.483333,-34.275817],\n[18.483333,-34.295733],[18.467000,-34.295733,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Paulsberg Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"CRRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.475133,-34.235000],[18.488333,-34.235000],\n[18.488333,-34.258000],[18.472400,-34.258000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Castle Rock Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"BRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.446033,-34.192783],[18.453267,-34.176350],\n[18.463017,-34.176350],[18.463017,-34.211750,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Boulders Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt; \n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"SJRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.459467,-34.118717],[18.459467,-34.126117],[18.450833,-34.126117,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"St James Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIMPA\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.000000,-33.750000],[18.400000,-33.750000],\n[18.401000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.900000],[18.000000,-33.750000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Marine Protected Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ffff00\",\n \"stroke-width\":3\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\n&lt;maplink text=\"\" latitude=\"-34.0\" longitude=\"18.6\" zoom=\"9\" group=\"RIRZ\" class=\"no-icon\"&gt;\n {\"coordinates\":\n18.202000,-33.793000],[18.362000,-33.793000],\n[18.362000,-33.900000],[18.202000,-33.900000],[18.20200,-33.793000,\n \"type\":\"LineString\"},\n \"properties\":{\n \"title\": \"Robben Island Restricted Area\",\n \"description\": \"Seaward boundary\",\n \"stroke\":\"#ff9900\",\n \"stroke-width\":2\n&lt;/maplink&gt;\nBack to the Alphabetical list of sites, or list of dive sites in the Robben Island area\nOther regional dive sites:"}
{"id": "220264", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220264", "title": "Sagbama", "text": "Sagbama is a town and the headquarters of Sagbama Local Government Area in Bayelsa State, Nigeria. It is located in the western part of Bayelsa State in the freshwater-swamp region of the Niger Delta.\nUnderstand.\nSagbama is an important administrative and community centre in western Bayelsa State. The town serves as the headquarters of Sagbama Local Government Area and is surrounded by a network of rivers, creeks, wetlands and freshwater swamp forest.\nThe wider Sagbama area is strongly associated with Ijaw culture and the waterways of the Niger Delta. The local economy is based largely on farming, fishing, trading, public services and petroleum-related activities.\nIjaw languages are widely spoken in the area. English is the official language and Nigerian Pidgin is also commonly used in everyday communication.\nHistory.\nSagbama is one of the established communities of the Niger Delta and has historically depended on the surrounding waterways for transportation, fishing and trade.\nThe wider area has strong Ijaw cultural connections. Bayelsa State sources identify Ijaw languages as widely spoken across the state, while the Sagbama area also has communities associated with other ethnic groups, including Urhobo communities.\nSagbama developed as an administrative centre and became the headquarters of Sagbama Local Government Area.\nClimate.\nSagbama has a tropical Niger Delta climate, with high humidity, heavy rainfall and a long wet season. The surrounding landscape consists largely of low-lying freshwater swamp forest, creeks and wetlands.\nVisitors should expect frequent rainfall and muddy or flooded roads during particularly wet periods.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nSagbama can be reached by road from Yenagoa and other communities in Bayelsa State.\nFrom Yenagoa, travellers generally use the road network connecting the state capital with western Bayelsa and the Sagbama area. Allow additional travel time during periods of heavy rainfall because flooding and poor road conditions can affect parts of the journey.\nBy bus.\nCommercial buses, minibuses and shared vehicles operate between Sagbama and other communities in Bayelsa State. The main local transport hub is Sagbama Motor Park.\nBy boat.\nBecause Sagbama is located within the Niger Delta's extensive river and creek system, boats are important for reaching surrounding communities that are difficult to access by road.\nBoat journeys should be arranged locally and visitors should confirm departure times before travelling.\nGet around.\nBy taxi.\nLocal taxis and shared vehicles can be used for movement around Sagbama and neighbouring communities.\nBy motorcycle.\nMotorcycles are commonly used for short-distance journeys, particularly where roads are narrow or difficult for larger vehicles.\nBy boat.\nBoats are useful for reaching riverine communities and locations away from the main road network. Travellers should use experienced local boat operators and wear a life jacket.\nOn foot.\nThe central parts of Sagbama can be explored on foot, although visitors should be prepared for muddy conditions during the rainy season.\nConnect.\nMobile-phone coverage is available in populated parts of Sagbama, although signal strength can vary in remote riverine communities.\nStay safe.\nTake normal precautions with valuables and avoid travelling alone into unfamiliar remote areas.\nWhen travelling by boat, use a life jacket and confirm the condition of the boat and weather before departure.\nDuring heavy rainfall, ask locally about flooding and road conditions before travelling.\nStay healthy.\nUse safe drinking water and protect yourself from mosquito bites. Carry basic medicines and personal supplies when travelling to remote communities because access to larger pharmacies and medical facilities may be limited."}
{"id": "220266", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220266", "title": "Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary", "text": "Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary is a protected wildlife area in Boki Local Government Area of Cross River State, Nigeria. It protects approximately 100 km\u00b2 of lowland and submontane forest around the Afi Mountains and is an important refuge for endangered wildlife, including the Cross River gorilla, Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzee, and drill.\nThe sanctuary is also known for its birdlife, mountain scenery, hiking opportunities and the nearby Drill Ranch, a wildlife rehabilitation and conservation project. Afi Mountain is listed as an Important Bird Area in Nigeria, and the area contains a major roost for migratory European barn swallows.\nUnderstand.\nAfi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary was established in May 2000 from part of the former Afi River Forest Reserve. It is managed by the Cross River State Forestry Commission, with conservation and research support from organisations including the Wildlife Conservation Society and Pandrillus.\nThe sanctuary covers lowland and submontane rainforest, with rocky mountain peaks reaching approximately 1,300 metres. It forms part of a wider forest landscape that connects with the Mbe Mountains and other important wildlife habitats in the Cross River region.\nAfi is particularly important for the conservation of the Cross River gorilla, one of the world's most endangered great apes. Other important species include the Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzee, drill and grey-necked rockfowl.\nAfi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary consists mainly of tropical lowland and sub-montane forest, with rocky peaks rising to about 1,300 m. The sanctuary is an important habitat for several endangered species, including the critically endangered Cross River gorilla, Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzee, drill and grey-necked rockfowl.\nThe forest contains a wide variety of tree species, including \"Parkia bicolor\", \"Pycnanthus angolensis\", \"Albizia zygia\", \"Monodora myristica\" and \"Irvingia gabonensis\". Open areas created by dry-season fires are quickly colonised by herbaceous plants, which provide an important food source for gorillas.\nOther wildlife found in the sanctuary includes red river hogs, bushbucks and several species of monkeys. The western side of the mountain also contains an important roost for migratory European barn swallows, which can number in the millions during the wintering season.\nAfi Mountain has a humid tropical climate, with a distinct rainy season and a drier period during the year. The area receives heavy rainfall, with annual rainfall generally ranging from about 3,000 to 3,800 mm. The rainy season usually lasts from around March or April to September, while the drier season generally occurs from October to March.\nTemperatures are generally warm throughout the year, although the mountainous terrain produces cooler conditions at higher elevations. During the rainy season, heavy rainfall can make roads and hiking trails difficult to use. Visitors planning to hike should be prepared for wet conditions and bring suitable rain gear and sturdy footwear.\nGet in.\nAfi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary is in Boki Local Government Area of Cross River State. The sanctuary is reached through the surrounding communities, with access to the mountain and conservation facilities arranged locally.\nVisitors planning to hike the mountain should make arrangements in advance with the conservation project or local guides. Pandrillus advises visitors to contact Drill Ranch before travelling because road conditions and access can change.\nFees and permits.\nFees for visiting Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary may vary. Drill Ranch's visitor information page lists fees from November 2019, but these may no longer be current.\nA guide is required for hikes into Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary. Day hikes and multi-day treks can be arranged through Drill Ranch, with additional fees collected on behalf of the Cross River State Forestry Commission.\nVisitors should contact Drill Ranch in advance to confirm the current fees, guide charges and any required permissions before travelling. Fees may vary for student groups and other organised groups.\nThe sanctuary is a protected area, so visitors must follow the instructions of the Forestry Commission, guides and conservation staff. Birdwatching is permitted, but mist-netting is not allowed except by special arrangement with the state wildlife authority.\nGet around.\nAfi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary is a rugged mountainous area with steep canyons, ridges and forest trails. There are no regular public transport services within the sanctuary.\nVisitors exploring the sanctuary must be accompanied by a guide. Day hikes and multi-day treks can be arranged through Drill Ranch at Afi Mountain. For longer hikes, visitors should be physically fit and carry suitable hiking equipment, food and drinking water.\nWithin the Drill Ranch area, the animal enclosures and forest attractions are reached on foot along winding paths. Motorcycles can be used to reach the area from nearby villages, while some sections of the access road may require a four-wheel-drive vehicle, particularly during the rainy season.\nVisitors should contact Drill Ranch in advance before travelling, as road conditions can change and access arrangements may vary.\nSee.\nWildlife.\nThe sanctuary is home to populations of Cross River gorillas, Nigeria-Cameroon chimpanzees, drills and other forest wildlife. Wildlife sightings are not guaranteed, as many of the animals are shy and live in dense forest.\nDrill Ranch.\nDrill Ranch is a conservation and rehabilitation project associated with Afi Mountain. It cares for endangered drill monkeys and orphaned chimpanzees. At the Afi site, visitors can see drills in large forest enclosures and learn about the conservation programme.\nMigratory swallows.\nThe western side of Afi Mountain contains an important roost for migratory European barn swallows. The roost has been estimated to contain millions of birds and is one of the notable birdwatching features of the area.\nDo.\nHiking.\nDay hikes and multi-day treks can be arranged at Afi Mountain. The terrain is mountainous and can be steep and rugged, so visitors should be physically prepared and use a guide.\nBirdwatching.\nAfi Mountain is an Important Bird Area and is suitable for birdwatching. Visitors may see forest birds as well as migratory species, particularly around the swallow roost.\nVisit the conservation facilities.\nVisitors can arrange a visit to Drill Ranch to see the rehabilitation facilities and learn about the conservation of drills and chimpanzees.\nBuy.\nThere are no regular shops at Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary. Basic supplies such as soap, batteries, kerosene and other necessities may be available in nearby villages. Onions, beans, rice, salt and cooking oil may also be available locally. The nearest reliable fuel supplies are in Ikom or Obudu, so visitors travelling by car should stock up before going to the mountain.\nEat.\nAfi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary is a self-catering destination. Visitors should bring their own food and prepare their own meals during their stay. Cooking facilities, including basic cooking utensils, pots and pans, are available for visitors.\nSome basic food supplies may be available in nearby villages, but visitors should not rely on local supplies and are advised to bring enough food for their stay.\nDrink.\nVisitors to Afi Mountain Wildlife Sanctuary should bring their own drinking water. Borehole water in the area is only semi-potable, so visitors are advised to bring bottled water or a suitable water-purification method.\nSoft drinks and beer may be available in nearby villages, although refrigeration facilities are limited.\nSleep.\nOvernight accommodation and camping have been available at Drill Ranch Afi Mountain. Visitors should contact the project in advance to confirm current accommodation arrangements and prices, as the facilities and fees may change.\nCamping.\nCamping is possible in the Afi Mountain area. Visitors can camp at the designated camping area, where basic facilities such as water, a shower and a latrine are available.\nFor camping elsewhere on the mountain, visitors should arrange their trip with a guide and carry suitable camping equipment, food and drinking water. A tent may be useful, although a mosquito net is also recommended.\nVisitors should contact the local conservation project in advance to confirm camping arrangements and current conditions.\nBackcountry.\nAfi Mountain is a rugged massif of steep canyons, ridges and forested slopes. Day hikes and multi-day treks can be arranged for visitors who want to explore deeper into the sanctuary.\nA guide is compulsory for hikes, and a porter is recommended for longer trips. Visitors should be physically fit and carry hiking boots, a backpack, food, drinking water, a canteen and something to sleep on. A compass and mosquito net are also recommended.\nSeveral camps and caves on the mountain can be used for overnight stays. Visitors planning multi-day treks should make arrangements in advance and be prepared for changing weather and difficult terrain.\nStay safe.\nAfi Mountain is a working conservation area rather than a conventional tourist park. Visitors should follow the instructions of guides and conservation staff.\nThe mountain terrain is steep and rugged, and visitors should wear suitable hiking footwear and carry sufficient water and food. Pandrillus recommends long trousers, long-sleeved clothing and appropriate footwear.\nDo not approach, feed or attempt to touch wild animals. Visitors should also respect surrounding communities and local customs.\nDuring the rainy season, roads and trails may become difficult to use, so check conditions before travelling."}
{"id": "220270", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220270", "title": "Nov\u00e1 Byst\u0159ice", "text": "Nov\u00e1 Byst\u0159ice is a historic town of roughly 3200 inhabitants in 2025 in South Bohemia, near the border with Austria, about 15 km southeast from Jind\u0159ich\u016fv Hradec. It is a charming town and definitely an off-the-beaten-path destination.\nUnderstand.\nNov\u00e1 Byst\u0159ice was first mentioned in written texts in 1175, making it the oldest settlement in today's Jind\u0159ich\u016fv Hradec district.\nIn 1897, a narrow gauge line from Jind\u0159ich\u016fv Hradec to Nov\u00e1 Byst\u0159ice was opened.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nNov\u00e1 Byst\u0159ice is on the southern edge of the Czech Republic and it takes approximately 2 hr 20 min by car to get there from Prague.\nBy bus.\nThere is no direct connection from Prague, but you can take a bus from Roztyly bus station (near metro station on red C line) to Jind\u0159ich\u016fv Hradec and transfer here to a local bus, the ride takes between 3 hr and 3 hr 50 min.\nBy train.\nNov\u00e1 Byst\u0159ice is an endpoint of a narrow gauge line from Jind\u0159ich\u016fv Hradec; trains on narrow gauge railway are slow, but the rides are scenic. Buy tickets from the conductor on the train.\nGet around.\nThe whole place is rather small and you should have no major problems getting around on foot."}
{"id": "220272", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220272", "title": "Gbele Resource Reserve", "text": "Gbele Resource Reserve is a protected wildlife area in the Upper West Region of Ghana. Covering about 565 km\u00b2, it is one of Ghana's larger wildlife reserves and protects an extensive area of Guinea savanna woodland. The reserve is known for its antelopes, monkeys, birds and other savanna wildlife, as well as opportunities for game viewing, birdwatching and hiking..\nUnderstand.\nGbele Resource Reserve was established and gazetted in 1975 to protect wildlife and vegetation in the area. It is managed by the Wildlife Division of Ghana's Forestry Commission.\nThe reserve lies between several districts in the Upper West Region, including Sissala East, Sissala West and Daffiama-Bussie-Issa. Tumu is the nearest major town and an important base for visiting the reserve.\nGbele is a relatively remote destination and receives fewer visitors than Ghana's better-known wildlife destinations. Visitors should make arrangements before travelling, particularly for transport, guides and accommodation.\nGbele is mainly made up of open Guinea savanna woodland, with grassland, thickets and patches of woodland. The terrain is generally flat and low-lying, with some rocky outcrops. The seasonal Kulpawn River passes through the reserve and forms pools and areas of denser vegetation in parts of the reserve.\nThe reserve supports a variety of savanna wildlife. Animals recorded in Gbele include roan antelope, hartebeest, bushbuck, waterbuck, savanna duiker, warthog, patas monkeys, green monkeys and olive baboons.\nGbele is also important for birdwatching. About 194 bird species have been recorded in the reserve, including species associated with the Sudanian savanna. Birds of particular interest include Willcocks's honeyguide, Gambaga flycatcher, black-headed weaver, northern carmine bee-eater, spotted thick-knee and yellow-billed oxpecker.\nThe vegetation includes species such as shea trees, African locust bean and shea woodland trees, as well as baobab and other savanna species.\nGet in.\nGbele Resource Reserve is best reached by road from Tumu or Wa. Tumu is the nearest major town and is a useful place to arrange transport, food and other supplies before entering the reserve.\nRoads and tracks within and around the reserve can be rough and unpaved. Recent visitors have reported poor road access to the reserve's visitor facilities, so a suitable vehicle and local guidance may be necessary.\nVisitors should contact the Wildlife Division or the reserve management before travelling to confirm the current route, road conditions, entrance arrangements and availability of guides.\nFees and permits.\nVisitors need permission to enter Gbele Resource Reserve. Entry and other visitor fees may apply, but current charges are not reliably published online.\nVisitors should contact the Wildlife Division of the Forestry Commission before travelling to confirm the current entrance fee, guide charges and any permits or other requirements.\nGet around.\nTravel within Gbele is mainly by vehicle and on foot. Game viewing and exploration of the reserve should be done with local guides or rangers.\nWalking trails and nature walks are available, but visitors should not walk into remote areas alone. The reserve is large and wildlife may be spread over considerable distances.\nSee.\nWildlife.\nGbele's main attraction is its savanna wildlife. Early morning and late afternoon are generally the best times for observing animals, although sightings are never guaranteed.\nRock outcrops.\nRocky outcrops occur in parts of the reserve and provide scenic features within the otherwise relatively flat savanna landscape.\nKulpawn River.\nThe seasonal Kulpawn River passes through the reserve. Areas around the river support denser vegetation and provide habitat for wildlife and birds.\nDo.\nGame viewing.\nGame viewing is one of the main activities at Gbele. Visitors may encounter antelopes, monkeys, warthogs and other savanna wildlife while travelling through the reserve.\nBirdwatching.\nGbele is a notable birdwatching destination, with about 194 recorded species and a strong representation of birds associated with the Sudanian biome. Visitors interested in birds should consider hiring a local guide who knows the reserve.\nHiking.\nNature walks and hiking are possible in the reserve. Some routes can involve long walks, so visitors should carry drinking water, suitable footwear and protection from the sun.\nBuy.\nThere are no regular tourist shops within the reserve. Visitors should buy food, water, fuel and other necessities in Tumu or another nearby town before travelling into the reserve.\nLocally made crafts and other supplies may be available in nearby communities.\nEat.\nThere are no regular restaurants inside the reserve. Visitors staying at the reserve should make arrangements for food before travelling.\nFor a wider choice of food, restaurants and local dishes, Tumu is the most practical base.\nDrink.\nVisitors should carry enough drinking water for activities inside the reserve. Water and other drinks should be purchased before entering remote areas.\nSleep.\nTented camp.\nGbele has a tented camp for visitors. The facility is in a remote part of the reserve and access can involve walking from the entrance or along rough internal roads.\nThe availability and operating arrangements of the camp may change, so visitors should contact the Wildlife Division or reserve management before travelling to confirm whether accommodation is available.\nTumu.\nTumu has guesthouses and hotels and is a practical alternative for visitors who prefer to stay outside the reserve.\nCamping.\nGbele Resource Reserve has a tented camp for visitors. The camp provides a way to stay within the reserve and experience the surrounding savanna environment.\nVisitors planning to camp or stay at the tented camp should make arrangements in advance with the Wildlife Division of the Forestry Commission, as facilities and operating arrangements may change.\nVisitors should carry drinking water, suitable clothing, insect repellent and other personal supplies. Access to the camp can involve walking, and roads and walkways in the reserve may be rough.\nStay safe.\nGbele is a large and remote wildlife reserve. Visitors should follow the instructions of rangers and guides and should not approach wild animals.\nCarry sufficient drinking water, sun protection, suitable footwear and basic first-aid supplies. Mobile-phone coverage inside the reserve can be limited or unavailable.\nRoad conditions can deteriorate during the rainy season. Check local conditions before travelling and use a suitable vehicle for rough roads.\nVisitors should also check with reserve management about current wildlife protection measures and access arrangements before entering the reserve."}
{"id": "220276", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220276", "title": "Bay Harbor Islands", "text": "Bay Harbor Islands is a town in Miami-Dade County with a population of about 6,000 (2020). It consists of two principle islands where homes and businesses exist, with a long island to facilitate the crossing over Biscayne Bay."}
{"id": "220281", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220281", "title": "Goa (Camarines Sur)", "text": "Goa is a municipality in Partido area of Camarines Sur. It is the educational center of Partido area as it hosts the Partido State University.\nUnderstand.\nThe history of Goa, Camarines Sur is closely linked to the eruptive activity of Isarog Volcano and its associated hazards. Geological evidence suggests that around 2,000 years ago, a breach in the volcano\u2019s crater led to the sudden drainage of its crater lake, generating a lake breakout flood that affected downstream communities. In the 12th century, an eruption of Isarog Volcano emplaced block and ash flow that buried settlements near Culasi River, significantly altering the landscape and impacting early inhabitants.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nBicol International Airport has direct flights from Manila, and then go Legazpi Grand Central Terminal.\nBy land.\nFrom Manila, take a direct provincial bus or catch a flight to the Bicol Region followed by land transport.\nGet around.\nJeepneys and tricycles are available in downtown."}
{"id": "220283", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220283", "title": "Kyabobo National Park", "text": "Kyabobo National Park is a protected mountainous area in the Volta Basin of Ghana, near Nkwanta and the border with Togo. The park lies in the transition between forest and savanna and is known for its rugged hills, waterfalls, wildlife and hiking opportunities.\nUnderstand.\nKyabobo National Park lies in the Atwode traditional area of eastern Ghana. The park is adjacent to Fazao-Malfakassa National Park in Togo and forms part of a wider ecological area along the Ghana-Togo border.\nThe park is managed as a protected area by Ghana's Wildlife Division under the Forestry Commission.\nKyabobo is characterised by steep hills, forest, transition woodland and savanna. Elevations in the protected area range from about 150 m to 850 m, with Mount Kyabobo reaching about 873 m according to Ghana's Forestry Commission.\nThe park includes the Breast Mountains and other scenic mountain areas. The Koue River forms part of the international boundary with Togo.\nKyabobo supports both forest and savanna wildlife. The Forestry Commission records 51 mammal species, 235 bird species, 33 reptiles and 26 amphibians.\nAnimals recorded in the park include buffalo, kob, warthog, aardvark and baboons. Threatened species reported from the park include elephant, bongo, reedbuck, hartebeest and lion.\nThe park's forests also contain the nationally endangered tree Talbotiella gentii.\nThe Nkwanta area has a pronounced dry season from about November to March. Temperatures are highest during the first part of the year, while rainfall and vegetation conditions vary seasonally.\nThe waterfalls and Koue River are particularly impressive after the rains, with Visit Ghana recommending the period from August to December for these attractions.\nGet in.\nKyabobo National Park is reached from Nkwanta in the Oti Region.From the main Ho-Tamale road at Nkwanta, turn at the Kyabobo National Park sign and follow the road for about 5 km to the park headquarters.\nTrotros operate to Nkwanta from Accra, Hohoe and Tamale, although travellers coming from Tamale may need to change at Bimbila. Local taxis may be available for the final journey to the visitor centre.\nVisitors should contact the park authorities before travelling to confirm current access arrangements, guides and transport.\nFees and permits.\nVisitors should obtain permission before entering the national park and pay the applicable entrance and activity fees.\nCurrent fees are not reliably published online. Visitors should contact the park authorities before travelling to confirm entrance fees, guide charges and any other applicable charges\nGet around.\nThe park is explored mainly on foot, by bicycle and with arranged transport. Guides are available for visitors and are recommended for hikes and other activities.\nThe terrain is mountainous in places, with steep hills and uneven trails. Visitors should follow the instructions of park guides when exploring the area.\nDo.\nBackcountry.\nKyabobo National Park is a remote and mountainous protected area. Visitors wishing to explore areas away from the main visitor routes should arrange the trip with the park authorities and use a park guide.\nThere are steep and uneven sections of terrain, so backcountry walks require suitable footwear, sufficient drinking water and protection from the sun. Visitors should not enter remote areas alone.\nBecause access and trail conditions can vary, check with the park authorities before undertaking a backcountry trip.\nBuy.\nThere are no shops inside the park. Visitors should buy food, drinking water and other necessities in Nkwanta before travelling to the park.\nEat.\nMeals are available at chop bars and restaurants in Nkwanta. Visit Ghana also reports that meals can be arranged with a community cook at the picnic site.\nDrink.\nVisitors should carry drinking water when hiking or spending time in the park.\nDrinks and refreshments are available in nearby villages and in Nkwanta.\nSleep.\nCamping.\nA campsite next to the park headquarters has showers and flush toilets. Another camping facility, Laboum Camp, has showers and pit toilets. Visitors should confirm current availability before travelling.\nLodging.\nNkwanta has hotels and other accommodation and is the main town for visitors travelling to Kyabobo.\nStay safe.\nKyabobo is a remote and mountainous protected area. Visitors should follow the instructions of park guides and avoid exploring unfamiliar areas alone.\nCarry sufficient drinking water, sun protection and suitable footwear when hiking.\nThe park borders Togo. Visitors should not cross the international boundary unless they have the necessary travel documents and permission.\nAs conditions in the area can change, visitors should check current local conditions and contact the park authorities before travelling. In December 2025, park management reported that the park was open to visitors and that arrangements for accommodation and transport could be made with the authorities."}
{"id": "220284", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220284", "title": "Bui National Park", "text": "Bui National Park is a protected area in the Ashanti-Kwahu region of Ghana, located in the transition between woodland savanna and the forest zone. The park is bisected by the Black Volta River and is particularly known for its hippopotamus population, river scenery, mountains, islands and birdlife. The Bui Dam and its reservoir are located at the southern end of the park.\nUnderstand.\nBui National Park was established in 1971 and covers about 1,821 km\u00b2. It extends across parts of the Bono and Savannah regions of Ghana. The Black Volta River passes through the park and forms an important part of its landscape.\nThe park is managed as part of Ghana's protected-area system by the Wildlife Division of the Forestry Commission.\nThe construction of the Bui Dam and reservoir changed part of the landscape of the park, creating extensive areas of open water and islands.\nBui lies in a woodland-savanna transition zone and contains savanna woodland, riverine vegetation and gallery forests along watercourses. The Black Volta is one of the defining features of the park.\nThe reservoir created by the Bui Dam has added large areas of open water and islands to the landscape. Mountains and rocky areas provide additional scenic features.\nBui is particularly known for its population of hippopotamuses in the Black Volta and the reservoir. The park also supports elephants, waterbuck, bushbuck, baboons, patas monkeys, green monkeys and several species of antelope.\nMore than 250 bird species have been recorded in the park. The birdlife includes species associated with the woodland and riverine habitats.\nOther wildlife recorded in the park includes reptiles, including African pythons and turtles, as well as other mammals.\nGet in.\nBui National Park can be approached from towns in the Bono and Savannah regions. Wenchi, Techiman and [Nsawkaw are among the towns associated with the wider Bui area.\nThe park is remote in places and visitors should arrange transport and confirm access with the park authorities before travelling.\nThe Bui area is connected by road to other parts of Ghana, but some roads approaching the park may be unpaved or in variable condition.\nFees and permits.\nVisitors should obtain permission to enter the national park and pay the applicable entrance and activity fees.\nCurrent Bui National Park fees are not reliably published online. \nVisitors should contact the Wildlife Division or park management before travelling to confirm entrance fees, guide charges, boat-trip costs and any other applicable charges.\nGet around.\nExploring Bui National Park is mainly done with arranged transport, on foot and by boat on the Black Volta and Bui reservoir.\nVisitors should follow the instructions of park guides and avoid entering unfamiliar areas of the park alone.\nDo.\nBackcountry.\nBui is a large protected area with remote sections away from the main visitor facilities. Visitors wishing to explore less-developed areas should arrange the trip with the park authorities and use an appropriate guide.\nCarry sufficient drinking water, sun protection and suitable footwear. Do not enter remote areas alone or approach wild animals.\nBuy.\nThere are no shopping facilities inside the park. Visitors should purchase food, drinking water and other necessities before travelling into remote areas of the park.Local supplies may be available in nearby communities.\nEat.\nVisitors should make arrangements for food before entering remote parts of the park.\nFor a wider selection of restaurants and local food, use one of the nearby towns as a base.\nDrink.\nCarry sufficient drinking water when visiting the park, particularly during hiking and other outdoor activities.\nWater and other refreshments should be purchased before travelling into remote areas.\nSleep.\nAccommodation within the park is limited. Visitors should confirm current accommodation and camping arrangements with the park authorities before travelling.\nVisitors who prefer to stay outside the park can use accommodation in nearby towns.\nCamping.\nCamping facilities and arrangements can change. Visitors wishing to camp should contact the park authorities in advance to confirm whether camping is available and what facilities are provided.\nStay safe.\nBui is a large wildlife area and visitors should follow the instructions of park rangers and guides.\nDo not approach hippopotamuses or other wild animals. Hippos can be dangerous, particularly when approached too closely or when people come between them and the water.\nVisitors undertaking boat trips should follow the instructions of the boat operator and wear appropriate safety equipment.\nCarry drinking water and protection from the sun when spending time outdoors."}
{"id": "220285", "revid": "2441213", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220285", "title": "Uiseong", "text": "Uiseong (\uc758\uc131) is a county in North Gyeongsang, South Korea. It is known across the nation as the capital of garlic. While it offers a quiet escape from bustling metropolitan areas, it is distinguished by its rich agricultural heritage, fertile lands, and vibrant local culinary culture.\nUnderstand.\nLocated in the central inland region of Gyeongsangbuk-do, Uiseong is a peaceful rural county.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nUiseong can be reached by train from Cheongnyangni Station in Seoul. Travelers can take either the KTX-Eum or ITX-Maum to Uiseong Station.\nGet around.\nLocal buses and taxis are available in Uiseong. As many attractions are located outside the main town area, travelling by car can be convenient.\nStay safe.\nUiseong is generally a quiet and safe rural area. Visitors should take normal precautions when travelling and check transportation schedules in advance, especially when travelling to rural areas.\nGo next.\nAndong \u2014 a historic city known for traditional Korean culture."}
{"id": "220287", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220287", "title": "Ezinihitte Mbaise", "text": "Ezinihitte Mbaise is a Local Government Area (LGA) in Imo State, southeastern Nigeria. Its headquarters is in Itu, Imo State. The area is part of the wider Mbaise cultural region and is made up of several towns and communities with strong Igbo cultural traditions.\nEzinihitte Mbaise is known for its agricultural activities, local markets, traditional music and dance, and cultural celebrations, particularly the annual Oji Ezinihitte festival, which highlights the cultural importance of the kola nut.\nUnderstand.\nEzinihitte Mbaise is a Local Government Area in Imo State, southeastern Nigeria. Its headquarters is at Itu. It forms part of the wider Mbaise cultural area and is predominantly inhabited by Igbo people.\nThe area consists of several traditional communities and is known for its agricultural activities, local markets, traditional music and dance, and cultural festivals.\nEzinihitte Mbaise is one of the three local government areas that make up the wider Mbaise area, together with Aboh Mbaise and Ahiazu Mbaise.\nEzinihitte Mbaise has a strong Igbo cultural identity. Traditional music, dance, community festivals and ceremonies remain important parts of local life.\nThe kola nut, known as \"oji\", has an important cultural role in Igbo society. The presentation and sharing of kola nut are associated with hospitality, traditional ceremonies and important community gatherings.\nTraditional music and dance are also important parts of cultural celebrations in the area. Abigbo is a traditional Igbo performance associated particularly with the Mbaise area.\nThe Orji Ezinihitte festival is one of the best-known cultural celebrations associated with the area. The festival is centred on the cultural importance of the kola nut and involves traditional ceremonies, music, dance and community gatherings.\nThe wider Mbaise region is also known for the Iri-ji Mbaise (New Yam) festival, which celebrates the yam harvest and Mbaise cultural heritage.\nEzinihitte Mbaise has a tropical climate with a rainy season and a drier season. The area is generally warm and humid, with heavier rainfall during the wet season.\nVisitors travelling during the rainy season should be prepared for heavy rain and changing road conditions.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThe nearest major airport is Sam Mbakwe International Cargo Airport, also known as Owerri Airport, in Imo State.\nFrom the airport, travellers can continue by road towards Owerri and then travel onward to Ezinihitte Mbaise.\nBy road.\nEzinihitte Mbaise is primarily reached by road. Travellers coming from Owerri can use roads connecting the city with the wider Mbaise area.\nVisitors arriving from neighbouring parts of Imo State, Abia State and other parts of southeastern Nigeria can travel by commercial bus, taxi or private vehicle.\nBecause Ezinihitte Mbaise contains several communities, travellers should confirm their exact destination before starting the journey.\nBy bus.\nCommercial buses and minibuses operate between Owerri and communities in the wider Mbaise area.\nFor smaller communities, travellers may need to change from an intercity bus to a taxi, tricycle or motorcycle.\nGet around.\nTravel within Ezinihitte Mbaise is mainly by road.\nTaxis, tricycles (keke) and motorcycles (okada) are commonly used for shorter journeys between communities and markets. Private vehicles are more convenient for visitors who want to visit several communities.\nVisitors travelling to smaller communities should ask locally about current road conditions and available transport.\nSee.\nItu.\nItu is the administrative headquarters of Ezinihitte Mbaise and one of its main administrative and commercial centres.\nThe town is also home to Nkwo Mbaise Market, an important local market serving Itu and surrounding communities.\nOboama.\nOboama is one of the historic communities of Ezinihitte Mbaise and has an important place in the cultural traditions of the area.\nThe community is associated with traditional music and the Abigbo dance tradition.\nUmunama.\nUmunama is a community in Ezinihitte Mbaise with an important place in the traditional history and cultural identity of Ezinihitte.\nThe community is closely associated with the Oji Ezinihitte tradition.\nOkpofe.\nOkpofe is a community in Ezinihitte Mbaise and is home to Orie Ihitte Market, which serves the surrounding communities.\nLocal markets.\nThe traditional markets of Ezinihitte Mbaise are useful places for visitors who want to experience everyday local commerce and see agricultural produce and other goods.\nDo.\nAttend the Oji Ezinihitte festival.\nThe Oji Ezinihitte festival is the area's most distinctive cultural attraction. It centres on the traditional importance of the kola nut and includes ceremonies, music, dance and community gatherings.\nVisitors attending cultural events should follow the instructions of local organisers and respect traditional customs.\nExperience traditional music and dance.\nTraditional music and dance are important parts of community celebrations in Ezinihitte Mbaise.\nAbigbo is a traditional performance associated with the Mbaise area and particularly with Oboama.\nVisitors may encounter traditional performances during festivals, weddings, community celebrations and other major events.\nVisit local markets.\nVisitors can explore local markets to experience everyday trading and see locally produced agricultural goods, foodstuffs and household products.\nExperience Mbaise food culture.\nVisitors can try food associated with southeastern Nigerian cuisine, including dishes prepared with yam, cassava, vegetables, palm products and local soups.\nBuy.\nLocal markets are the main places to buy fresh agricultural produce, foodstuffs, clothing, household goods and other everyday products.\nEat.\nLocal food is predominantly based on southeastern Nigerian cuisine. Visitors can find dishes prepared with yam, cassava, vegetables, palm products and local soups.\nFood choices within Ezinihitte Mbaise are mainly local restaurants, food vendors and small eateries. Visitors looking for a wider range of restaurants may prefer to travel to nearby Owerri.\nDrink.\nPalm wine is traditionally associated with the agricultural culture of the wider Mbaise area.\nVisitors may encounter locally produced palm wine in communities and during cultural celebrations. Ask locally about appropriate places to buy traditional drinks and respect local customs.\nSleep.\nAccommodation within Ezinihitte Mbaise is more limited than in nearby Owerri, but there are hotels and apartments in several communities.\nTravellers who require a wider selection of accommodation may prefer to stay in Owerri and make day trips into Ezinihitte Mbaise.\nStay safe.\nVisitors should take normal precautions when travelling in Nigeria.\nKeep valuable belongings secure, avoid displaying large amounts of cash and avoid travelling alone at night in unfamiliar areas.\nVisitors travelling to smaller communities should ask locally about current road conditions and transport options.\nDuring the rainy season, some roads may become difficult to travel on after heavy rainfall."}
{"id": "220296", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220296", "title": "Iguig", "text": "Iguig (pronounced \u201cEe-geeg\u201d) is a town of Cagayan. A suburb of the provincial capital Tuguegarao, it is is known for its multiple pilgrimage spots.\nUnderstand.\nIguig was established by the Spanish in 1610 along the banks of the Cagayan River. It is primarily an agricultural town, with additional income coming from Roman Catholic pilgrims who visit the town's multiple religious sites, namely Saint James parish, a Spanish era church; Calvary Hills, a replica of the crucifixion site of Jesus Christ; and Poor Clare Monastery. The town is slowly being subjected to urbanization from neighboring Tuguegarao.\nGet in.\nIguig is along the Maharlika Highway running from Tuguegarao to Ilocos, and there are buses, minibuses and passenger vans passing here generally every hour all day from Tuguegarao to other towns of eastern and northern Cagayan.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation within town. \nBuy.\nIguig is also the main pottery-producing town of Cagayan, with roadside stalls selling wares derived from the alluvial red clay along the Cagayan River.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in Tuguegarao.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3504."}
