{"id": "220437", "revid": "2350368", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220437", "title": "Belonia", "text": "Belonia is a town in southern Tripura, northeastern India, close to the international border with Bangladesh. It is the administrative headquarters of South Tripura district and an important road and rail centre for reaching Trishna Wildlife Sanctuary, the archaeological remains at Pilak and the smaller towns and border landscapes of southern Tripura.\nBelonia itself is a modest district town rather than a conventional tourist resort. Its principal value to travellers is as a practical base with shops, markets, banks, government services, local transport and accommodation before heading into the surrounding countryside.\nUnderstand.\nBelonia is the headquarters of South Tripura district and one of the principal towns of the southern part of the state.\nThe present South Tripura district was created after the former district was divided, with Belonia becoming the headquarters of the reorganized district in 2012.\nBelonia Municipal Council was established in 1978 and administers a compact urban area surrounded by villages, agricultural land and low forested hills.\nThe town lies close to the Muhuri River and the India\u2013Bangladesh international boundary. Cross-border geography has strongly influenced the area's trade, roads and settlement patterns.\nBengali is the language most commonly heard in town, while Kokborok and other Tripuri languages are spoken in the wider district.\nBelonia is not generally a destination requiring several days. One or two nights are useful for travellers using it as a base for:\nHistory.\nBelonia developed as an administrative and trading centre in southern Tripura.\nThe surrounding Muhuri valley appears in older Tripura historical traditions and has long provided an important route through the southern part of the kingdom and later state.\nModern municipal administration began when the Belonia Municipal Council was established on 11 August 1978.\nSouth Tripura district originally had its headquarters at Udaipur. Following administrative reorganization, the former district was divided and the present South Tripura district was formally inaugurated in January 2012, with Belonia as its headquarters.\nThe town's position immediately beside Bangladesh has also made it important for cross-border trade. The Muhurighat Land Customs Station handles trade with Bangladesh, although facilities remain much more limited than at major passenger land ports such as Agartala\u2013Akhaura.\nClimate.\nBelonia has a humid subtropical to tropical monsoon climate.\nNovember through February is usually the most comfortable period for travel, with relatively mild mornings and less rainfall.\nTemperatures and humidity rise during March and April.\nThe main monsoon generally extends from roughly May through September or October. Heavy rain can cause:\nAn umbrella or light rain jacket is useful during much of the year.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nBelonia railway station (station code BENA) provides rail access to the town.\nPassenger trains connect the southern Tripura railway corridor with Agartala and other parts of the state.\nThe station lies outside some parts of the central commercial area, so an auto-rickshaw, e-rickshaw or local vehicle may be useful for the final journey.\nTrain timetables can change, so check current Indian Railways information shortly before travelling.\nBy bus.\nTripura Road Transport Corporation and private operators run buses and smaller passenger vehicles between Agartala and Belonia.\nThe South Tripura district administration gives the road distance from Agartala as approximately 85 km.\nServices also connect Belonia with:\nShared vehicles can be quicker than large buses but may wait until enough passengers have boarded.\nBy car or taxi.\nBelonia is connected to Agartala and the rest of Tripura by the state's principal southern road network.\nAllow roughly 2\u00bd\u20133\u00bd hours from Agartala depending on traffic, weather, roadworks and stops.\nRoads to larger towns are generally paved, but local roads approaching wildlife, archaeological and rural attractions may be narrower or rougher.\nTaxis can be hired in Agartala, Udaipur or Belonia.\nFor a full-day excursion to Trishna or Pilak, agree on:\nbefore departure.\nBy air.\nThe nearest commercial airport is Maharaja Bir Bikram Airport () at Agartala.\nThe South Tripura district administration places Belonia roughly 100 km from the airport.\nContinue by taxi, hired car, bus or train.\nBelonia has several designated helipad locations, but these are not ordinary scheduled passenger-air services.\nFrom Bangladesh.\nBelonia sits immediately beside the India\u2013Bangladesh frontier and has the Muhurighat Land Customs Station.\nHowever, travellers should not assume that Muhurighat functions as a normal international passenger crossing simply because customs and trade facilities exist there.\nInfrastructure at Muhurighat remains under development, and official government material in 2025\u201326 continued to discuss upgrading the customs station.\nFor ordinary international travel between Tripura and Bangladesh, use a recognized passenger immigration crossing and confirm current rules before departure.\nNever attempt to cross the international boundary informally through fields, riverbanks or minor roads.\nGet around.\nCentral Belonia is compact enough for short journeys by walking.\nFor longer journeys, common local transport includes:\nNegotiate the fare before entering an auto or taxi if no fixed fare is displayed.\nThe railway station, district offices, markets and residential neighbourhoods are spread over a wider area than the small commercial centre, so local vehicles are useful.\nFor attractions outside Belonia, a hired car or taxi provides much more flexibility than trying to coordinate multiple local buses.\nDistances to nearby attractions include approximately:\nRoads become quieter outside the town but can also become narrower.\nAvoid unnecessary rural travel after dark.\nBuy.\nBelonia is the principal shopping centre for this part of South Tripura.\nThe Belonia Sub-Divisional Market and surrounding commercial streets sell:\nThe town has banks and ATMs, although carrying some cash is useful for rural excursions.\nFor trips to Trishna or Pilak, buy in Belonia:\nbefore leaving.\nLocal markets are much more useful for ordinary Tripuri and Bengali products than for purpose-made tourist souvenirs.\nEat.\nBelonia has numerous simple restaurants, sweet shops and tea stalls serving mostly Bengali and Indian food.\nTypical choices include:\nFish is particularly common in local Bengali cooking.\nVisitors interested in regional Tripuri cuisine may find dishes using bamboo shoot, pork, dried fish and local vegetables, although specialist Tripuri restaurants are less common than ordinary Bengali eateries.\nRestaurants are concentrated around the market, transport areas and central commercial streets.\nFor excursions into Trishna or rural South Tripura, carry snacks or arrange lunch beforehand rather than assuming a restaurant will be available near every attraction.\nDrink.\nBottled drinking water is widely sold in town.\nTea is ubiquitous, particularly around markets and transport stands.\nFresh juices and soft drinks are also easy to find.\nDo not drink untreated water from the Muhuri River, ponds or rural streams.\nAlcohol is available through licensed shops and establishments, but nightlife in Belonia is limited.\nThis is primarily a district town rather than an entertainment destination.\nSleep.\nBelonia has a modest range of government and private accommodation.\nIt is a useful overnight base for travellers visiting Trishna or continuing farther south.\nLodging.\nPrivate guesthouses and small hotels also operate in town, but standards can vary.\nFor more choice, Udaipur and especially Agartala have substantially larger accommodation markets.\nConnect.\nMobile service is generally good within Belonia.\nMajor Indian mobile networks operate in the town, although signals can weaken in rural and forested areas around Trishna.\nBecause Bangladesh is very close, a phone near the frontier may occasionally detect a Bangladeshi network. Check the network name before using mobile data if international roaming is enabled.\nWi-Fi is available at some accommodation but should not be assumed.\nATMs and banking facilities are available in town.\nSouth Tripura District Administration\nBelonia Information Centre\nFor emergencies in India:\nThe district administration also publishes a disaster-management helpline for South Tripura.\nStay safe.\nBelonia is a generally straightforward small Indian town, but normal precautions are appropriate.\nRoad traffic is the most obvious everyday risk.\nWatch for:\nFootpaths are inconsistent, so walk facing traffic where necessary.\nThe monsoon can bring intense rainfall and temporary waterlogging.\nDo not walk or drive through fast-moving floodwater.\nMosquitoes are common. Use repellent, particularly around dusk and during the rainy season.\nThe international border requires additional care.\nBelonia lies immediately beside Bangladesh, and some roads, fields and riverbanks approach the frontier very closely.\nDo not:\nFollow Border Security Force instructions where signs or checkpoints are present.\nThe Muhurighat customs area is a working trade and border facility, not a sightseeing attraction.\nTravellers visiting Trishna Wildlife Sanctuary should follow wildlife precautions there, particularly around gaur.\nAvoid isolated forest roads after dark."}
{"id": "220439", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220439", "title": "Yinan", "text": "Yinan (\u6c82\u5357; Y\u00edn\u00e1n) is a county in the Linyi area of Shandong, China. This guide covers the county seat and surrounding towns and countryside. Visitors can explore the bamboo groves and spring-fed village landscape at Zhuquan Village, visit sites commemorating Zhuge Liang, and walk on Wolong Hill.\nRegions.\nThis guide covers Yinan County as a whole; the county seat and rural attractions are described together below.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThe area is associated with ancient Yangdu and with Zhuge Liang, the statesman and strategist of the Three Kingdoms period (220-280 CE); the memorial hall in Zhuanbu and the cultural park on Wolong Hill commemorate him. Han-dynasty (206 BCE-220 CE) remains at Beizhai reflect an older layer of local history.\nDuring the war against Japan, Yinan was an important center of the Yimeng Communist base area. In August 1940, a provincial wartime administrative body was established at Qingtuo Temple. This wartime history is another theme of local museums and memorial sites.\nOrientation.\nThe county seat is centered on Jiehu. Wolong Hill and its Zhuge Liang cultural park are on the western side of the built-up area. Zhuquan Village is farther north, in Tongjing Town; the Zhuge Liang Hometown Memorial Hall is south of the county seat, in Zhuanbu Town. The two Zhuge Liang attractions are separate places: show the Chinese name and address when arranging transport. The three attractions are spread across the county and cannot all be covered as a walk around the town center.\nClimate.\nLike the rest of inland Shandong, Yinan has four distinct seasons. Summers are hot and rainy, while winters are cold and dry. Spring can be windy and changeable; autumn is generally milder. Bring rain protection for summer visits and warm clothing for winter walks.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nUse the attraction's Chinese name and town in navigation. Zhuquan Village, Wolong Hill and the memorial hall in Zhuanbu are separate destinations; navigating only to \u201cYinan\u201d will not take you to all three.\nGet around.\nThe attractions below are spread across the county. The memorial hall in Zhuanbu is south of the county town, while Zhuquan Village is to the north; allow time to travel between them.\nBy public transport.\nYinan Shengtong Bus Company (\u6c82\u5357\u53bf\u76db\u901a\u516c\u4ea4\u6709\u9650\u8d23\u4efb\u516c\u53f8), based at the bus station, is the local bus operator; its published contact number is \u260f. Ask which stop serves your destination and confirm the last return departure. Do not assume a town bus continues to a rural attraction.\nBy car or taxi.\nFor a trip combining the northern and southern attractions, arrange the itinerary and return pickup in advance. Show the driver the Chinese attraction names, particularly for the two different Zhuge Liang sites.\nOn foot and by bicycle.\nWalking is useful within the county seat and individual attractions. The northern village and southern memorial hall are too spread out to treat as one town-center walk. For cycling, check the full route and daylight available; a line between map pins is not a verified cycle route.\nDrink.\nThe Zhisheng Tangquan Hotel, Building 3 (see Sleep), lists a lobby bar among its facilities. Ask the hotel about its drinks menu and whether it is serving non-residents before making a special trip.\nStay safe.\nOn walks around Wolong Hill and the village waterways, wear shoes with a good grip and take extra care on wet steps. Check the weather before leaving for rural attractions and arrange the return journey before dark. Carry the Chinese address of your accommodation and a charged phone."}
{"id": "220440", "revid": "43226", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220440", "title": "Rote", "text": "Rote (or Roti) is an island in Nusa Tenggara, Indonesia. It is an off-the-beaten-path destination mainly popular with Australian surfers for its world-class surf breaks. But there's no reason Rote should be restricted to surfers (nor Australians): there is plenty to see and do for the active traveller, and no shortage of secluded beaches and sleepy villages for those seeking peace and tranquillity. You just have to figure out how to get there.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBefore Europeans arrived, Rote was divided into a patchwork of small kingdoms or domains, each with its own rulers and local identity. The island remains surprisingly diverse for its size: several closely related languages are spoken here, including Bilba, Dengka, Lole, Ringgou, Dela-Oenale, Termanu and Tii. Life was shaped by Rote's dry climate, and especially by the lontar palm, which supplied food, drink, sugar, building materials and a source of income alongside fishing, farming and jewellery-making.\nThe Portuguese arrived in the 16th century. According to local legend, Rote got its name when a lost Portuguese sailor asked a farmer where he was. Unable to understand the question, the farmer assumed he was being asked his name and replied, \"Rote\". The Dutch later became the dominant power in the region, and Christianity spread in Rote under their influence. Today, about 80% of the population is Christian. \nAustralian surfers began making their way to Nembrala in the 1980s, and word spread about long, powerful reef breaks off the island's west coast. Surf camps and small resorts followed, with Nembrala becoming Rote's main tourist centre. Although a few resorts have popped up, tourism is still heavily concentrated around the surf scene. Much of the island still feels rural and lightly visited, with lontar palms, limestone hills, fishing villages and long stretches of beach.\nAlong with nearby small islands, such as Nuse Island and Ndao Island, Rote forms the Rote Ndao Regency, which in 2022 was estimated to have a population of nearly 150,000.\nClimate.\nRote has a tropical savanna climate with dry months for much of the year due to dry winds that blow from mainland Australia. Between 80% and 95% of all rain falls between November and March, while little rain falls between July and October. During the dry season, many streams and rivers run dry and local inhabitants must rely only on wells for their water supplies.\nGet in.\nRote is not especially easy to reach. This keeps tourism to a minimum. Almost all routes to the island go through Kupang, West Timor, other than the occasional large PELNI ferry from the larger islands (although these do stop at Kupang as well) and flights from the small island of Savu (where you almost certainly will not be).\nA common itinerary is to fly to Kupang, stay in a hotel overnight, and then catch the fast ferry from Kupang's Port Tenau early the next morning. Flights between Kupang and Rote are expensive relative to their short distance. During the windy months of July and August, ferry services can experience a lot of disruptions, so it may be worth booking a flight during these months unless you're particularly flexible and happy to spend some time in Kupang.\nIf you're coming to Rote from overseas, fly to Bali first and then fly to Kupang. If you fly with Batik Air or a Lion Air-affiliated airline, you should be able to do this on a single ticket. Then follow the itinerary above.\nBy plane.\nThe flight from Kupang takes 20-30 minutes.\nA taxi from the airport to Nembrala costs around Rp350,000-400,000 as of 2026. It takes about an hour \u2013 longer than the flight!\nBy ferry.\nThere are three ports on the island, although only two are regularly used by passenger services.\nGetting tickets for the ferries to Rote in Kupang is confusing. You may be advised to book online or with an app \u2013 assuming you're foreign, don't bother with this. Instead, get to the correct port an hour before the ferry's departure (as of September 2026, that's 06:00 for the fast ferry and 08:00 for the slow ferry). For the fast ferry at Port Tenau, look for the ticket window at the front of the terminal building and buy your ticket there. (They tried to make everyone buy online but relented after a flood of complaints.) For the slow ferry at Port Bolok, ignore the main ticket window in the terminal building, as they will demand you at least register your details online, and if you don't know Indonesian then this will be difficult. Instead, look like a tourist (i.e. confused) and start asking around for help in English. Someone will approach you and offer to buy your ticket on their phone. Once that's done, tip them Rp10,000-20,000 for the service. At Bolok, this process has been confusing enough that they actually set up a small tent with semi-official staff who will buy people's tickets using their phones, but given that they usually don't speak English, you're better off going with the English-speaking unofficial helpers.\nBy sailboat.\nIf arriving from outside of Indonesia, immigration and customs formalities must be completed in Kupang, West Timor, before heading to Rote. Beware of the notoriously strong currents of the Puku Afu Strait (\"Selat Puku Afu\") between Rote and Timor \u2013 this is one of the most dangerous stretches of water in the world!\nGet around.\nThe short answer: rent a car or a scooter. Rote has almost no public transport and taxis and even \"ojeks\" (motorcycle taxis) are hard to come by on the street. If you can't ride a scooter, then Rote, with its long quiet highways, is a fantastic place to learn.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis generally require that you pre-arrange pickup. When you find a taxi driver you trust (such as the one who took you to your accommodation when you first arrived), save their WhatsApp number in your phone in case you need a car to go somewhere. You can also always have your hotel arrange car transport for you.\nTaxis on Rote are expensive compared to much of the rest of Indonesia. As of 2026, there are no ride-hailing services available on the island.\nBy motorbike.\nYour hotel will be able to arrange motorcycle or scooter rental. Prices tend to be reasonable on Rote, even more reasonable than the rest of Indonesia in a lot of cases. Be aware that off the main highways, roads on Rote tend to be in horrific condition, their only saving grace being that they lack the steep gradients found on some other Indonesian islands.\nNeither locals nor tourists tend to wear helmets within the tourist triangle between Nembrala, Bo'a and Oeseli. If travelling towards Ba'a and beyond, you should don a helmet, if not for your own safety then because the police will be motivated to stop you.\nAs of September 2026, a bottle of petrol costs Rp20,000-25,000. If you'll be riding around a lot over an extended period of time, it pays to find the cheaper sellers and fill up with them. Sometimes even sellers within sight of one another can sell fuel at different prices!\nBy car.\nThere are plenty of car rental agencies on Rote, particularly in Ba'a. Popular ones include:\nBy bicycle.\nMany hotels offer bicycles for rent, which is a great way to get around Nembrala given that it is oriented along one long paved road. You should be able to rent a mountain bike to explore some of the backcountry tracks, presuming you want to brave the heat.\nOn foot.\nRote is hot and its towns and villages tend to stretch out along a single main road, making walking undesirable for anything but short distances.\nSee.\nKeep in mind that, due to the state of the roads or the need to organise boats, etc., most of these attractions will take significant time to reach. It will be difficult to visit more than one or two in a day.\nDo.\nBeaches.\nAll the beaches on the west coast (including Nembrala and Bo'a) are great sunset-watching spots.\nSurfing.\nRote offers world-class surfing. The main wave at Nembrala Beach, has several sections, which can link up on bigger days.\nThe wave tends to be bigger and better when the swell is more west, and suffers from lengthy flat spells. It can hold virtually any size swell, the main problem being the very strong winds in season, which make getting into bigger waves more difficult (over 25 knots at times). The wave is unusual in that it often gets bigger further down the wave, due to focusing from refraction.\nThere are numerous other waves in the area, of low to excellent quality, although access is sometimes only by boat, and some of these won't work at different times of the very large tidal range of more than 6m. These include Bo'a, Sucky Mamas, Dana, Do'a, the Bommie, and several other offshore island waves, as well as waves further around to the east of the island sometimes without a name, although these need very large swells.\nFor those learning to surf there is a surfing school:\nScuba diving.\nAlthough far more famous as a surf island, Rote is surrounded by coral reefs and has numerous dive sites. \nSnorkelling.\nWith snorkelling equipment (ask at your hotel), you can snorkel at most beaches. Loedi Beach is a particularly popular beach for snorkelling. The area around Oeseli Beach is even better with its sheltered coves. The reefs around Ndao Island and Nuse Island are also superb. Other spots dot the island, although almost all of them are completely undeveloped.\nKayaking.\nYou can rent kayaks at Oeseli Beach to explore the bay and Lake Nirvana. Most hotels there will have them, or just ask around. (There will be people offering you boat trips to Lake Nirvana \u2013 ask them.) A single-person kayak will cost between Rp100,000 and Rp300,000 for a day (2026), depending on who you rent from.\nBuy.\nMoney.\nTo get cash, look for BNI ATMs. Ignore Bank NTT ATMs \u2013\u00a0they don't work with foreign cards. The on the main road in Nembrala is where most tourists get their cash, but it often runs out, so if you find that it's working pull as much out as you think you'll need in anticipation of it being out of cash for several days. If you're in desperate need of cash, you'll need to trek to the, pay a 5% commission, and then wait 1-2 days to receive it.\nSupermarkets.\nThere are no true supermarkets on the island. There is nothing even close to a supermarket in Nembrala. Instead you'll have to trek to Ba'a.\nHandicrafts and fabrics.\nLeaves from the lontar palm are used to craft \"sasando\", a traditional music string instrument native to Rote. A stringed bamboo tube is surrounded by a bag-like fan of dried lontar leaves, which serves as the resonator for the instrument. Miniature \"sasando\" instruments are available as souvenirs and are far easier to transport.\nHandwoven \"ikat\" fabrics, often with floral patterns, are mainly sold in Nembrala. The traditional garment is the \"kain\", a 2.5-metre-long cloth that is wrapped around the hips and reaches to the knees or ankles. Designs and colours vary across the island. More elaborate pieces can take considerable time to weave.\nNembrala has the widest selection aimed at visitors, with \"ikat\" sold through small shops, guesthouses and individual vendors. Elsewhere, fabrics can sometimes be bought directly from weavers or at local markets, particularly around Ba'a. Ndao Island is especially noted for its weaving and traditional jewellery. Buying directly from the maker generally offers a much wider choice than the souvenir selection found in tourist areas.\nEat.\nIt can be a struggle to find restaurants open on Sunday, particularly on Sunday morning.\nDrink.\nAs Rote is a majority-Christian island, and due to Nembrala's tourist-orientation, it's quite common to find alcohol on restaurant menus. It is far from party-central, however. Expect a few laid-back beachfront resto-bars and drinks provided by your hotel. The restaurant at the 81 Palms resort probably has the best cocktails on the island.\nYou can find good coffee at Indika and The Pasar restaurants.\nSleep.\nAccommodation on Rote is expensive for Indonesia and particularly poor value at the budget end. Most accommodation is in the Nembrala-Bo'a-Oeseli triangle, although there are a few hotels and guesthouses in Ba'a. The high season is the dry season from May to October, and the \"really high\" season is June through August. Don't expect long-term-stay discounts from most hotels outside of the low season.\nThe hotels near the port in Ba'a are not great. If you must stay in Ba'a, Homestay JavaRote (located away from the port) is likely your best option.\nBudget.\nThere are cheaper stays than these on the island, but expect very low standards of accommodation.\nStay safe.\nThere is virtually no crime on Rote. Your only real risk is from saltwater crocodiles, but even those are quite rare compared to islands such as Timor.\nConnect.\nWifi is available on Rote, but it is about as good as you might expect and very much depends on where you stay. Generally, it's serviceable before 17:00 and then pretty much useless after 17:00.\nTelkomsel provides good mobile phone coverage on the island, but the signal isn't particularly strong. For example, you might lose reception upon entering your hotel room.\nRote Guide has a good guide on staying connected while on Rote."}
{"id": "220468", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220468", "title": "Babuyan and Calayan Islands", "text": "The Babuyan and Calayan Islands are a volcanic archipelago in the Babuyan Channel between northern Luzon and Batanes that belong to the province of Cagayan in northern Philippines. It it is comprised of five major islands, four of which (Babuyan, Calayan, Dalupiri, and Camiguin) are administered by the municipality of Calayan, while the island of Fuga is administratively part of Aparri.\nUnderstand.\nThe islands are mostly of volcanic origin, and contain some of the country's active volcanoes. They have a rich history of human settlement even before the Spanish conquest, as seen by multiple archaeological excavations across the islands. Its inhabitants were repeatedly relocated to mainland Luzon due to difficulties in accessing them on account of the rough waters of the Babuyan Channel and its position along the Philippines' typhoon belt, only to return when the opportunity came. Even today, the islands are incredibly difficult to access, and facilities remain rudimentary compared to the mainland or even in the Batanes archipelago further north, but the islands' natural wonders are appreciated by those who prefer to see a minimal amount of fellow tourists.\nThe islands' residents are a mix of Ibanags, Ilocanos and Ivatans. They subsist on agriculture, with Calayan's name originating from a Ibanag term for a place to grow ginger while Babuyan literally means piggery, as well as fishing and catering to the occasional tourist brave enough to endure frequent power outages, expensive fuel imported from the mainland, and getting stranded when the seas are too rough seas for sailing. The area has also seen a lot of emigration, especially as there are no universities or college and mundane health conditions frequently become medical emergencies due to the lack of resources.\nGet in.\nTransportation to the islands is incredibly disorganized. While there are airfields and airstrips on Calayan and Fuga Islands, due to low population and demand, these are restricted to military and charter flights, with the one in Fuga exclusively used by the company which bought the island in the 1970s. There are seasonal flights from Tuguegarao Airport to Calayan Airport operated by SkyPasada.\nThe only viable option is to join the residents in taking boats known as \"lampitaw\" in the mainland Cagayan port towns of Santa Ana (for Camiguin and Babuyan Islands), Aparri, and Claveria (the last two ports for the other main islands). Travel times take a minimum of 3 hours in fair weather, but can reach 12 hours in rough waters. Note that this journey is not for the faint-hearted; expect to see a rickety wooden boat rather than a leisurely cruise and share space with bulky cargo and even livestock. Be sure to take enough safety gear and identification with you in the event of an emergency. Always notify authorities in Calayan as well as in your port of entry of your travel plans.\nGet around.\nThere is no public transport in the islands and very little to no cars or any larger vehicle. Expect to ride motorcycles, tuktuks or the buggy known locally as \"kuliglig\" instead. To travel between islands, you will need to hire boats again; there is no organized boat transport. Expect the same conditions as getting into the islands. Even some destinations in the same island are only accessible by boat due to the lack of roads. Expect to pay thousands of pesos when renting a boat.\nSee.\nBabuyan Island.\nThe furthest among the islands from mainland Luzon. It is a quiet place mostly subsisting on agriculture and fishing, and dominated by the Babuyan Claro Volcano at its center, with black sand beaches. \nCalayan Island.\nThis is the islands' most populated and is also the political and economic hub. The most developed tourist attractions and infrastructure can be found here.\nCamiguin Island.\nThe nearest island from Santa Ana, and the most visible from mainland Luzon. This should not be confused with the equally volcanic island province of Camiguin in the southern Philippines.\nDalupiri Island.\nProbably the least known of the islands, it is an elongated stretch of land with white-sand beaches on its eastern side and rocky coastlines to the west.\nFuga Island.\nAdministratively part of Aparri, it is visible from the coast of northwestern Cagayan. Since the 1970s, it has been the property of Fuga Island Holdings Inc. and is also part of the Cagayan Economic Zone Authority (CEZA) based in Santa Ana, which has been trying to develop the island into a luxury holiday destination without much success, with the resident population trying to get by in between.\nDo.\nWhile on a boat, expect to see the islands' vibrant marine life, including dolphins and humpback whales. On land, try to find endemic birds such as the Calayan rail (\"Garillalus calayanensis\", locally known as \"piding\") as well as migratory ones while hiking. Dalupiri has some rare reptiles such as the Philippine crocodile and the endemic Ross's wolf snake.\nBuy.\nPrices of most goods tend to be higher as they have to be imported from the mainland, especially fuel. These can skyrocket when bad weather prevents deliveries from the mainland. Due to erratic phone signals and the absence of banks and ATMs, always pay in cash. As there are no malls, grocery stores or markets to speak of, make sure to bring enough food, water, batteries and other necessary supplies whenever you travel in the area.\nEat.\nApart from the usual seafood, a popular souvenir from the islands is the honey gathered from the forests and banayuyu wine, which is made from an endemic species of berry.\nSleep.\nThere are no large-scale hotels to speak of, just some homestays and inns.\nStay safe.\nCrime is minimal, as the few residents seem to know each other and are enthusiastic to learn about new visitors. There are also no insurgents waging armed rebellions in the hinterlands, although there is an active presence by the Philippine military and Coast Guard across the islands due to its strategic position near Taiwan and mainland China. \nAvoid visiting during the typhoon season from June to October, when the islands frequently suffer direct hits from storms and the risk of getting stranded for weeks is a real possibility. The cold season from November to February is also a no-go season due to squalls generated by the northeast monsoon.\nThe islands' volcanoes, despite being active, are not closely monitored by the government compared to the more famous volcanoes on the mainland such as Mayon or Taal, leaving you at the mercy of residents' guesswork. Climb these peaks at your own risk.\nFuga Island has the peculiar distinction of operating under two sets of laws, the first being that of the government, and the second from the Metro Manila-based company that bought the island in the 1970s as part of a plan to turn the island into a luxury tourism hub, although the island is still nowhere near the Caribbean paradise one expects.\nStay healthy.\nAvoid getting into a major medical emergency during your stay at all costs, as there are no hospitals while the clinics have limited medicines and staff. You will need to take the dreaded boat journey back to the mainland, or if you're lucky, get on a rare outbound charter flight. \nConnect.\nCalayan Municipality's postal code is 3520, while Fuga Island, being part of Aparri, shares 3515 with the mainland. \nCellphone and internet signals are historically abysmal in the islands. Sometimes the reception from radio stations in Taiwan can be clearer than the domestic ones. Electricity is on a rotational basis, with generators and solar panels being the norm."}
{"id": "220472", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220472", "title": "Do\u00f1a Remedios Trinidad", "text": "Do\u00f1a Remedios Trinidad (commonly abbreviated to DRT) is a municipality in the province of Bulacan in the Philippines. One of the geographically largest yet least populated towns in the country, it is home to less known natural attractions along the Sierra Madre mountains.\nUnderstand.\nDRT is the youngest municipality in Bulacan, having been created in 1977 by Philippine dictator Ferdinand Marcos and named after his mother-in-law, the mother of first lady Imelda Marcos. Cobbled together from parts of the neighboring towns of Norzagaray, Angat, and San Miguel, it is the largest municipality in Bulacan by land area, with 932.96 km2, but is mostly comprised of forests and a portion of the Sierra Madre mountains that run vertically through the town. It forms Bulacan's boundary with the provinces of Quezon, Aurora and Nueva Ecija. It is also the least populated town in Bulacan, with around 30,000 residents composed of Tagalogs and members of the indigenous Aeta communities that continue to make a living from the hunting, fishing, and gathering opportunities offered by the forests. \nThroughout its history, DRT was racked with political violence brought about by the New People's Army insurgency and local rivalries among politicians. Apart from this, DRT is not well-known even among other residents of Bulacan, making the town a subject of jokes about its existence. Despite this, DRT is slowly becoming a tourist destination in its own right, as the largest green space in a province that is increasingly being submerged by Manila Bay and consumed by the expanding urbanization and commercialization from Metro Manila.\nGet in.\nDRT is the most difficult town to travel to in Bulacan, being outside major highways and connected to neighboring municipalities by narrow two-lane roads. Most visitors will enter the town through M. Valte Road originating from Angat, which is accessible from Metro Manila via the Bocaue exit of the North Luzon Expressway (NLEX) and then Halili Highway through Santa Maria and Norzagaray before reaching Angat. An alternate toll-free route to Angat is through Quirino Highway from Quezon City, passing through North Caloocan, San Jose del Monte before merging with Halili Highway in Norzagaray onwards to Angat. There are no road connections to Nueva Ecija, Aurora and Quezon provinces.\nVisitors heading to Biak-na-Bato National Park enter DRT via San Miguel, which is along the lies along Maharlika Highway, also known as Asian Highway 26 (AH 26) and Cagayan Valley Road, which connects with the North Luzon Expressway (NLEX) at Santa Rita Exit in Guiguinto, Bulacan. From Metro Manila, it is served by buses heading to the cities of Gapan and Cabanatuan in Nueva Ecija.\nPublic transportation is scarce, apart from a jeepney from Angat or spending hundreds of pesos to hire a tricycle in the same town going to DRT. It is more preferable to bring your own vehicle, preferably a 4WD, as DRT is not the stereotypical flat Central Luzon town.\nGet around.\nTricycles are available in the downtown, though given the extensive land area and resulting wide distances, a private 4WD vehicle is better suited to get around. Many natural attractions are only accessible through hiking.\nDrink.\nThere are coffeeshops serving locally-produced coffee in Barangay Talbak, which is along the town's major hiking trails.\nStay safe.\nLandslides and flashfloods are common during the rainy season. \nThere may be a few NPA rebels hiding in the mountains. \nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3009."}
{"id": "220475", "revid": "2169258", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220475", "title": "Bridal Veil and Corbett", "text": "Bridal Veil and Corbett are communities in Multnomah County, Oregon, at the west end of the Columbia River Gorge, about 25 miles east of Portland. \nGet in.\nInterstate 84, exit 22 for Corbett and 28 for Bridal Veil. Alternately use the Historic Columbia River Highway; most of the destinations are best reached from it.\nGet around.\nThe Gray Line operates the Waterfall Trolley between many destinations in this area.\nSleep.\nAdditional accommodations can be found in Cascade Locks, Troutdale and near the Portland Airport."}
