{"id": "220495", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220495", "title": "Sarteneja", "text": "Sarteneja is a small coastal village in Northern Belize.\nUnderstand.\nSarteneja is at the mouth of the bay of Chetumal; the quiet little village lives mainly from fishing. Here you can enjoy nature away from the main tourist crowds.\nThere were likely Maya settlements here as early as the first millennium, and numerous structures still bear witness to this. The original Maya name for the town was Tza Ten-A-Ha, which means \"water from the rock\" and refers to the many wells found throughout the area. This Maya settlement lasted until the 18th century before it was abandoned.\nIn the mid-19th century, refugees from the Caste War resettled the area, and Tza Ten-A-Ha quickly became Sarteneja. In 1956, it was destroyed by a hurricane; only the church was spared, as the previous lightweight huts were no easy match for the storm. The village was subsequently rebuilt with sturdier, permanent buildings. Running water and electricity have been available here since the 1970s, and Sarteneja received telephone service in 1990.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nSarteneja recently got a small airport, or rather an airstrip. Tropic Air serves the airstrip on request along the Corozal / San Pedro route.\nBy boat.\nThe Thunderbolt water taxi runs daily to Corozal and San Pedro (Belize).\nGet around.\nThe centre is compact and easily explored on foot. To explore the surrounding area, you can rent bicycles from Fernando's Seaside Guesthouse (see Sleep).\nSee.\nSarteneja is primarily a small, tranquil fishing village in a paradisiacal setting. Rainforest, lagoons, and the Caribbean Sea are the natural backdrop. Within the town, there are also a few other things to see.\nEat.\nThese guesthouses feature restaurants offering local cuisine:\nGo next.\nThe surrounding Shipstern Nature Reserve, Bacalar Chico Marine and Cerros are not far from Sarteneja. "}
{"id": "220500", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220500", "title": "Palanan", "text": "Palanan is a municipality on the Pacific coast of Isabela province in the northern part of the island of Luzon in the Philippines. Cut off from the rest of the country by the Sierra Madre mountains, it is one of the least explored areas of the province, but is being geared as its future beach tourism destination.\nUnderstand.\nPalanan sits on the extensive valley of the Palanan River leading northeast to Palanan Bay, an extension of the Pacific Ocean, from the Sierra Madre, which separates it from the rest of the Cagayan Valley. It was founded during the Spanish era in 1625 by Franciscan missionaries who sailed from Tayabas (now Quezon Province in southern Luzon to convert the indigenous Dumagat tribes and outlaws from the rest of Luzon. As access to the town was easier by boat than overland as it is even today, Palanan was initially administered under Tayabas and then Nueva Ecija provinces before it finally became part of Isabela in 1856. \nDuring the Philippine-American War from 1900 to 1901, the small, quiet town was the last capital of the First Philippine Republic headed by President Emilio Aguinaldo, who directed resistance to the American occupiers for six months until he was captured in a daring raid by American forces and their Filipino auxiliaries under Col. Frederick Funston and brought back to Manila, where he officially surrendered. After this, Palanan creeped back into obscurity, interrupted partially by World War II, the presence of New People's Army insurgents in the 1970s and the entry of commercial logging operations that continues to be an issue despite the area being declared a protected area in 1978 that is now known as the Northern Sierra Madre National Park (NSMNP), encompassing the entirety of the mountain range within Isabela. \nDespite its isolation, Palanan is the most populated and economically advanced among the four municipalities of Isabela that are situated on the Pacific coast, although problems remain such as erratic electricity, high fuel prices, limited government and medical services, and the absence of roads connecting it to the rest of the country. The few tourists who visit will be rewarded with hospitable residents, lush mountains and jungles, pristine beaches and some of the best seafood in the country.\nThe people of Palanan are primarily engaged in farming, fishing and tourism. They speak a variety of languages including Tagalog, Ilocano and Ibanag. There is also an endemic and endangered language called Paranan. The Dumagats also subsist on hunting, fishing and gathering from the forests.\nGet in.\nPalanan has its own airport, which accommodates commercial flights using light, turboprop aircraft originating from Cauayan and Tuguegarao. Flight time is between 45 minutes to 1 hour. The town center is clustered around the airport.\nAnother option is to take an eight-hour boat journey from Santa Ana in Cagayan to the north or from the southern town of Dinapigue, which is accessible by road from Aurora province. There are also ferries in the towns of Dilasag, Casiguran and Baler in Aurora. Travel time is roughly six hours coming from these directions. Another boating option is to take a vehicle on the unpaved road connecting the provincial capital Ilagan with the town of Divilacan and then take a boat heading south towards neighboring Palanan, although access to the road is sometimes restricted due to bad weather.\nIn any case, travel by boat is no leisurely cruise; expect to see a rickety wooden boat and share space with bulky cargo and even livestock. Be sure to take enough safety gear and identification with you in the event of an emergency.\nGet around.\nTricycles and motorcycles are the main form of transport in the downtown. Apart from that and some heavy-duty trucks, there are very few vehicles due to its isolation from the rest of Luzon's road network and high fuel costs. Hiring a boat is necessary to reach some beaches.\nDo.\nBeing in the middle of the NSMNP, expect to see rare marine species on the coast and even rarer plant and animal species such as the pitcher plant while hiking inland.\nBuy.\nPrices of most goods tend to be higher as they have to be imported from the mainland, especially fuel. These can skyrocket when bad weather prevents deliveries from the mainland. Due to erratic phone signals and the absence of banks and ATMs, always pay in cash. While there is a market and small-scale stores, make sure to bring enough food, water, batteries and other necessary supplies whenever you travel in the area.\nEat.\nFreshly-bought seafood can be bought on the beaches. A highlight is the lobsters which, by directly buying from the fishermen, avoid the overcharging that accompanies their transport towards the mainland and its restaurants.\nSleep.\nThere are no large-scale hotels to speak of, just some homestays and inns.\nStay safe.\nCrime is minimal in town, as the few residents seem to know each other and there are no escape routes for offenders to escape apart from taking the dangerous hike across the uncharted Sierra Madre or getting on a boat. \nThere may be a few NPA rebels in the mountains and saltwater crocodiles swimming along the beaches.\nStay healthy.\nAvoid getting into a major medical emergency during your stay at all costs, as facilities and resources at the town's Station Hospital are limited. You will need to take a boat journey back to the mainland, or get on an outbound flight.\nRespect.\nThe indigenous Dumagat communities still adhere to their precolonial customs and belief systems and are widely respected even by the non-Dumagat resident population. As they are the most experienced in dealing with the natural settings of Palanan, you are expected to exercise maximum respect for their traditions and when visiting them.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3334.\nCellphone and internet signals are often weak despite the presence of celltowers in the area. Electricity is on a rotational basis, with generators and solar panels being the norm."}
{"id": "220971", "revid": "155858", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220971", "title": "Liaocheng", "text": "Liaocheng (\u804a\u57ce) is a prefecture level city in western Shandong Province, China, located at the junction of Shandong, Hebei, and Henan provinces. This city has a history of over 5,000 years and is a national historical and cultural city in China.\nUnderstand.\nHistorically, Liaocheng was an important commercial port along the Beijing-Hangzhou Grand Canal. During the Ming and Qing dynasties, it was one of the nine major commercial ports along the Grand Canal, known as the \"throat of the canal and the elbow of the heavenly capital\". This city is also famous for many stories from classical novels such as Water Margin and Strange Tales from a Chinese Studio that take place here.\nLiaocheng is known for its unique water city style. The city is surrounded by Dongchang Lake (\u4e1c\u660c\u6e56), the largest urban lake in northern China, forming a unique landscape of \"water in the city, city in the water\". Therefore, it is often referred to as the \"Jiangbei Water City\" and the \"Venice of Northern China\".\nThe main attractions include the Guangyue Tower (\u5149\u5cb3\u697c), a 600-year-old wooden structure building and one of the largest surviving Ming Dynasty pavilions in China; Shanshan Guild Hall (\u5c71\u9655\u4f1a\u9986), a magnificent Qing Dynasty merchant guild hall; And Jingyang Hillock (\u666f\u9633\u5188), the legendary location where Wu Song fought the tiger in \"Water Margin\". Liaocheng is also the place of origin of the famous medicinal material Donkey-Hide Gelatin (\u963f\u80f6).\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nLiaocheng does not have its own commercial airport. The nearest major hub is Jinan Yaoqiang International Airport (TNA). From the airport, you can take a high-speed train or long-distance bus to Liaocheng (approximately 1.5 to 2 hours).\nBy train.\nLiaocheng is well connected by rail. There are two main stations:\nBy car.\nLiaocheng is located at the intersection of several major highways, including the Qinglan Expressway (G22) and the Jinan-Liaocheng Expressway (S1). About 1.5 hours' drive from Jinan and about 3 hours' drive from Zhengzhou.\nGet around.\nThe scenic spots in Liaocheng are scattered throughout the city. There are also some scenic spots distributed in various counties of Liaocheng. The core area of the ancient city Dongchang Lake (\u4e1c\u660c\u6e56) restricts motor vehicles from entering the city walls, so walking, sharing electric bicycle, or dedicated sightseeing buses are the most suitable for sightseeing. There is no subway system in the city.\nBy bus\nBuses cover most urban destinations. The standard ticket price is 2 yuan per trip. The seasonal free ride policy applies during the winter heating period.\nTake bus K1 or K2 to reach Guangyue Tower\uff08\u5149\u5cb3\u697c\uff09 in the ancient city. The H1 line is a tourist loop that surrounds Dongchang Lake (\u4e1c\u660c\u6e56). Both Liaocheng Railway Station and Liaocheng West High speed Railway Station have bus routes.\nMost of the signs and announcements are in Chinese. Use Amap or Baidu Maps for reliable route selection; The local application, Shuichengtong e-xing, provides real-time bus tracking.\nBy taxi or ride-hailing\nThere are numerous taxis available at affordable prices, starting at around 6-8 yuan. Didi and other ride hailing apps are widely used. A taxi from the train station to the city center usually takes 15-20 minutes.\nBy bicycle or walking\nDue to the concentration of major attractions around Dongchang Lake (\u4e1c\u660c\u6e56), walking is a great way of transportation. Shared electric bicycle such as Hellobike and Meituan are spread throughout the city. Renting a electric bicycle to ride around the lake is highly recommended for a scenic experience.\nEat.\nThe culinary culture of Liaocheng is deeply rooted in Shandong (Lu) Cuisine, characterized by its delicious, fresh, and rich taste. Historically, the Grand Canal brought merchants from all over China, blending northern and southern culinary traditions and enriching local diet.\nBecause of its location in the north, the city's cuisine emphasizes wheat based staple foods, such as noodles, pancakes and Mantou. The local breakfast culture is full of vitality, with distinctive breakfast dishes such as broth and aged tofu, as well as Shazhen Guada. The freshwater fish in Dongchang Lake also play an important role in the local diet. In addition, Liaocheng is the birthplace of the famous traditional medicine and cooking material Dong'e Ejiao (donkey-hide gelatin). ass hide glue can be eaten directly or used to boil health drinks."}
{"id": "220975", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220975", "title": "Anenii noi", "text": "Anenii Noi is a district in central Moldova, with the city of Anenii Noi as its administrative centre. It has a predominantly rural character, with numerous villages and communities. The district is known for its agricultural activities and its location near the B\u00eec River, relatively close to Chi\u0219in\u0103u.\nGet in.\nThe main gateway to the area is Chi\u0219in\u0103u, primarily departing from the Central Bus Station (Gara Central\u0103). You can easily reach the region using regular minibuses (\"rutiere\") operating along the Chi\u0219in\u0103u\u2013Cobusca and Chi\u0219in\u0103u\u2013Varni\u021ba routes. Additionally, frequent intercity buses run throughout the day on the Chi\u0219in\u0103u\u2013Anenii Noi route, providing reliable and continuous local connections."}
{"id": "220978", "revid": "2440920", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220978", "title": "Osan", "text": "Osan (\uc624\uc0b0, \u70cf\u5c71) is in Gyeonggi province in South Korea.\nUnderstand.\nOsan is located in the southern part of Gyeonggi-do, bordering Hwaseong to the west and Pyeongtaek to the south. The city is predominantly urban, with a mix of residential, commercial, and military areas due to the presence of Osan Air Base. Osan City Hall is located in Sinjang-dong.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nOsan Intercity Bus Terminal is located adjacent to Osan Station at 59 Yeokgwangjang-ro, making transfers between bus and subway convenient.\nBy train.\nOsan is well-connected by rail via the Gyeongbu Line and Seoul Metropolitan Subway Line 1. The main station is Osan Station (\uc624\uc0b0\uc5ed), located at 59 Yeokgwangjang-ro in central Osan."}
{"id": "220986", "revid": "2440929", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220986", "title": "Tongjing", "text": "\"Classroom sandbox draft for the \u201cStarting a new page\u201d activity. This guide covers Tongjing in Yinan County, Shandong.\"\nTongjing (\u94dc\u4e95\u9547, T\u00f3ngj\u01d0ng) is a town in Yinan County, in the Linyi area of Shandong, China. Its rural attractions include the spring-fed lanes of Zhuquan Village, the lakeside Hongshizhai resort and the orchards of Maquan. The sights are spread across several villages, so plan transport between them rather than treating the town as one compact attraction.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nZhuquan Village was formerly called Quanshangzhuang and acquired its present name during the Qing dynasty. Its springs shaped the settlement: houses, stone paths and bamboo groves grew around the water. Tourism development preserved the old village while residents moved to newly built homes. The historic village opened to visitors as a scenic area in 2009.\nOrientation.\nThe town center is south of the main sightseeing cluster. Zhuquan, Hongshizhai and Maquan are separate destinations: use the attraction's Chinese name when arranging a ride. This is Tongjing in Yinan County (\u6c82\u5357\u53bf); do not confuse it with the place of the same name near Nanjing.\nClimate.\nTongjing has a temperate monsoon climate with four distinct seasons; much of the rain falls in the warmer part of the year. The village lanes and outdoor attractions make the day's weather relevant when planning a visit.\nGet in.\nFor intercity rail and air connections, see Linyi#Get in. Continue to Yinan County and arrange onward transport to Tongjing or your chosen attraction.\nBy bus.\nYinan bus route 3 is listed as serving Tongjing town government and Zhuquan Village Scenic Area (\u7af9\u6cc9\u6751\u666f\u533a). Confirm the boarding point, direction and last return service locally; a bus to the town center does not necessarily stop at every resort.\nBy car or taxi.\nFor Zhuquan, search for \u6c82\u5357\u53bf\u94dc\u4e95\u9547\u7af9\u6cc9\u6751 in a Chinese navigation app. For Maquan, use \u6c82\u8499\u9a6c\u6cc9\u4f11\u95f2\u56ed or \u9a6c\u6cc9\u521b\u610f\u519c\u4e1a\u4f11\u95f2\u56ed. Arrange a pickup location and return journey when traveling from Yinan by taxi.\nGet around.\nWalk within the village attractions. For travel between resorts, a car or prearranged ride allows more flexibility. The map marks two sightseeing areas and Maquan's southeast entrance; the entrance marker is a meeting point, not the center of the entire park.\nDo.\nA suggested sequence is Zhuquan's village lanes, Hongshizhai's waterfront, then Maquan if time permits. For a slower visit, choose two attractions and stay locally. Confirm current admission arrangements and opening hours with each operator before setting out.\nBuy.\nLocal products include dried persimmons, fruit juices and other orchard produce. Zhuquan also has traditional craft activities; ask whether the item you are buying was made locally.\nEat.\nLook for Yimeng-style stir-fried chicken (\u6c82\u8499\u7092\u9e21) and thin pancakes (\u714e\u997c), often eaten with scallions. Zhuquan and the resorts have dining options. Ask for the menu and prices before ordering a shared set meal.\nDrink.\nCherry and grape juices are among Maquan's local products. Ask whether a fruit drink contains alcohol if ordering a locally made specialty.\nSleep.\nGuesthouse accommodation is also available at Zhuquan, while Hongshizhai offers cabins. Staying at one resort can reduce return travel, but check whether admission to the attraction is included in the room rate.\nConnect.\nKeep the Chinese attraction names and your accommodation's contact details available for drivers. For wider travel information, see Linyi."}
{"id": "220987", "revid": "2441213", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220987", "title": "Hongcheon", "text": "Hongcheon is in Gangwon.\nUnderstand.\nHongcheon has the largest area among all municipalities (si/gun) in South Korea, approximately three times the size of Seoul. It is bordered by Chuncheon and Inje to the north, Gapyeong and Yangpyeong (Gyeonggi-do) to the west, Hoengseong and Pyeongchang to the south, and Yangyang and Gangneung to the east.\nGet in.\nBy train + bus.\nBy train + bus: Take the ITX-Cheongchun from Yongsan Station (\uc6a9\uc0b0\uc5ed) or Cheongnyangni Station to Chuncheon Station (\ucd98\ucc9c\uc5ed), then transfer to an intercity/local bus bound for Hongcheon.\nBy bus.\nExpress or intercity buses run frequently from Dong Seoul Terminal (\ub3d9\uc11c\uc6b8\ud130\ubbf8\ub110) directly to Hongcheon Intercity Bus Terminal.\nHongcheon is in Chuncheon.\nEat.\n=Buy=="}
{"id": "220989", "revid": "2221667", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220989", "title": "Majiang", "text": "Majiang is a county in Qiandongnan in Guizhou, China.\nUnderstand.\nMajiang is a county in southeastern Guizhou Province. It is located in the Qiandongnan Miao and Dong Autonomous Prefecture and is known for its natural scenery and local ethnic culture."}
{"id": "220994", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220994", "title": "San Gabriel (La Union)", "text": "\"This article is about the municipality in the Philippines. For the city in California, see San Gabriel\"\nSan Gabriel is a town in La Union. \nUnderstand.\nSan Gabriel is a landlocked municipality established along the Baroro River in the triboundary between the provinces of La Union, Ilocos Sur and Benguet. It is a highland town in the Cordillera mountains and has significant Igorot communities. Formerly a part of the Amburayan subdistrict of Mountain Province, it was annexed by La Union in 1920.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nSan Gabriel is bypassed by Manila North Road (Route 2), the main thoroughfare in La Union. Instead, it is connected to the highway by roads leading to the neighboring coastal municipalities of Bacnotan and San Juan, which merge at a junction in San Gabriel between the public market and the municipal hall.\nThere are jeepneys connecting San Gabriel with San Juan.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in San Juan and Bacnotan.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2513."}
{"id": "220995", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220995", "title": "Hungduan", "text": "Hungduan is a town in Ifugao, a province in the Cordillera mountain range in the north of the island of Luzon, in the Philippines. It has some of the famous Rice Terraces of the Cordilleras inscribed in the UNESCO World Heritage List.\nUnderstand.\nHungduan forms the northwestern corner of Ifugao and makes up the triboundary with Benguet and Mountain Province. It has some of the famous Rice Terraces of the Cordilleras inscribed in the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1995, but it is frequently overlooked by tourists, who stay in neighboring Banaue due to Hungduan's relative isolation and limited infrastructure.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nExcept for the northern barangay of Lubo-ong which borders Bontoc, Hungduan is bypassed by the Nueva Vizcaya-Ifugao-Mountain Province Road, which is traversed by buses from Metro Manila. Instead, travelers disembark in neighboring Banaue and take jeepneys heading to Tinoc-Hungduan-Banaue Road leading to downtown Hungduan or to barangay Hapao, where the UNESCO-inscribed terraces can be seen along the highway. Services tend to be few though given the low population density.\nGet around.\nThere are tricycles in town, but a 4WD or any vehicle suitable for mountain roads is best used. Most attractions in town are accessible following extended hikes.\nBuy.\nAll transactions are in cash. There are no banks or ATMs in town. \nSleep.\nThere are several homestays in Hungduan, particularly in Poblacion and Hapao.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition.\nPermits are required from local authorities to undertake any hikes or climb mountains. The hiking season runs during the dry season from November to May. While Ifugao is often known for its cool weather, do not underestimate the effects of heat and exhaustion while climbing, especially as many of the slopes are directly exposed to sunlight.\nConnect.\nThe town\u2019s postal code is 3603.\nInternet and phone signals in town can be erratic."}
{"id": "220996", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220996", "title": "Hingyon", "text": "Hingyon is a town in Ifugao, a province in the Cordillera mountain range in the north of the island of Luzon, in the Philippines. Often dismissed as a stopover on the way to Banaue, it offers some of the typical attractions of the province but with lesser tourists to share space with.\nUnderstand.\nHingyon one of Ifugao\u2019s youngest municipalities, having been officially founded in 1982 and cobbled together from parts of Banaue and the provincial capital Lagawe. Despite this, it is one of the important places to see traditional Ifugao culture and heritage and offers a quick getaway from the sometimes congested sites of Banaue.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nThe western barangays of Hingyon are along the Nueva Vizcaya-Ifugao-Mountain Province Road, which is traversed by buses from Metro Manila. The interior barangays are connected to the highway through secondary roads. There are also secondary road connections leading to the provincial capital, Lagawe.\nGet around.\nThere are tricycles in town, but a 4WD or any vehicle suitable for mountain roads is best used. Most attractions in town are accessible following extended hikes.\nBuy.\nAll transactions are in cash. There are no banks or ATMs in town. \nSleep.\nThere are several homestays in Hingyon.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition.\nPermits are required from local authorities to undertake any hikes or climb mountains. The hiking season runs during the dry season from November to May. While Ifugao is often known for its cool weather, do not underestimate the effects of heat and exhaustion while climbing, especially as many of the slopes are directly exposed to sunlight.\nConnect.\nThe town\u2019s postal code is 3607.\nInternet and phone signals in town can be erratic."}
{"id": "220997", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=220997", "title": "Sabtang", "text": "Sabtang is an island municipality of Batanes province in the northern Philippines. It is famous for its villages composed of traditional Ivatan stone houses that form the stereotypical postcard images of the province.\nUnderstand.\nSabtang is the southern-most island and municipality in the Batanes archipelago. It is one of the three (out of 10) islands in the province that are inhabited, the others being Batan and Itbayat. It also has jurisdiction over the islands of Dequey and Ivuhos, the latter used as a pastureland for grazing cattle.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nThe best time to visit is during the dry season between March and June, when the winds and waves are calm. Otherwise, expect frequent cancellations at the last minute.\nSabtang is only accessible from the daily ferry on the \"faluwa\", the traditional Ivatan fishing boat, that disembark from the port of Ivana in the southwestern part of Batan Island at 7:30AM. Travel time takes 30-40 minutes in fair weather. If you are in a hurry to get back to Batan island and the provincial capital Basco, take the return ferry before noon, as the seas tend to become rough afterwards and the ferries may get cancelled. Expect to share space with goods being imported from Batan and even livestock. \nGet around.\nSabtang Island is ringed by a circumferential road, except for its uninhabited southern tip. Tricycles and motorcycles are the predominant form of transportation in town.\nBuy.\nAll transactions are in cash due to the absence of banks and ATMs. Prices tend to be higher than in Batan Island due to the costs of transporting goods by sea, and can increase further when bad weather halts the ferries.\nBased in Chavayan, the Sabtang Weavers\u2019 Association is where most of the indigenous Ivatan fashions such as the lung and flowig \"vakul\" headdress, the jacket-like \"kanay'i\" vest and the wide-brimmed \"salakot\" hat are made from the leaves of the endemic palm species known as \"vuyavuy\". You can buy these garments or rent them for photo-ops.\nSleep.\nThere are homestays and inns across the island, with the prize catch being a stay on a traditional stone house in Chavayan.\nStay safe.\nLike in the rest of Batanes, crime is abysmally low, and the residents know each other. In any case, there is no escape route other than the once-daily \"faluwa\" ferry back to Batan Island.\nStay healthy.\nTry not to get into a serious medical emergency, as there is no hospital and you will need to be evacuated to Basco or worse, to mainland Luzon due to a lack of medicines and other resources.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3904. \nLike in the rest of Batanes, electricity is on a rotational basis, with generators and solar panels being the norm. Cellphone and internet signals are often sketchy, although things are improving with the proliferation of Starlink terminals, while the reception from radio stations in Taiwan can be clearer than the domestic ones."}
{"id": "221015", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221015", "title": "Panay (Capiz)", "text": "\"This article is about the municipality in Capiz. For the island in the Philippines, see Panay\"\nPanay (pronounced \"pan-ay\" with an interruption in the dash) is a town in Capiz.\nUnderstand.\nSituated at the mouth of the Panay River, the town was the second place in the Philippines to be conquered by the Spanish after Cebu in 1569, only to be overshadowed when the Spanish moved on to Manila in 1571 and made it their capital. Panay gave its name to the wider island in which it stands and became the capital of the province of Capiz until the 18th century, when it was moved to the neighboring settlement of Capiz (now Roxas City), which had better port facilities. This resulted in a slow economic decline that in the long run enabled it to retain its cultural and natural heritage. Today, the town sustains itself through, agriculture, fishing, tourism and commercial spillover from Roxas City.\nGet in.\nPanay is along the Iloilo East Coast-Capiz Road, part of the circumferential highway network that encircles Panay Island. The nearest airport is in Roxas City, a 20-minute drive away.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation around town.\nBuy.\nThere is a branch of Banco de Oro (BDO) in downtown Panay.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 5801."}
{"id": "221016", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221016", "title": "Pantabangan", "text": "Pantabangan is a municipality in Nueva Ecija. Having been relocated after its original site was submerged by the construction of the Pantabangan Dam in the 1970s, it is now a prime glamping spot on the foothills of the Caraballo mountains and a destination for 20th century archaeology, if you can afford to wait years for the old town to reappear from the Pantabangan Reservoir.\nUnderstand.\nPantabangan was founded by the Spanish in 1645 as an outpost to subjugate the indigenous Ilongot, Isinay and Dumagat tribes in the Caraballo and Sierra Madre mountains. During the 1970s, the old town center was evacuated and its inhabitants resettled after the site, located in a valley in the upper reaches of the Pampanga River basin, was earmarked to be submerged by the rising waters of the reservoir created by the construction of the Pantabangan Dam, the largest of its kind in Central Luzon, and a major lifeblood for agriculture and electricity in the region. Since the 21st century, the shores of what is now Pantabangan Lake have become a popular location for camping, glamping and fishing activities similar to those in Lake Caliraya in Eastern Laguna.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nPantabangan is along the Pantabangan-Baler Road, also known as the Casile Trail, which connects Nueva Ecija with the southeastern portion of Nueva Vizcaya and Baler, the capital of the province of Aurora. The road begins in the municipality of Rizal to the south, which in turn is directly connected by highways to the major cities of San Jose and Cabanatuan.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays. Boats can be hired for trips on Pantabangan Lake.\nSleep.\nMost accommodation is around Pantabangan Lake.\nStay safe.\nThere may be a few New People's Army rebels hiding in the mountains.\nRespect.\nOld Pantabangan is a municipally-designated heritage zone; climbing on the ruins and other kinds of horseplay is strictly prohibited, as well as extracting any kind of debris from the site.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3124."}
{"id": "221017", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221017", "title": "Le jardin des tortues", "text": "The Jardin des Tortues is an animal park and botanical garden located in Les Avirons, on the western side of R\u00e9union Island. \nOpened to the public in 2019, the park is dedicated to the discovery and observation of various species of land, freshwater and semi-aquatic turtles. Set in a tropical garden, it is home to several hundred turtles, including Seychelles giant tortoises, Madagascar radiated tortoises and African spurred tortoises. \nThe park offers an educational trail designed to introduce visitors to these animals and raise awareness of their protection and conservation. \nVisitors can also learn more about the different species, their habitats and their way of life through educational activities and encounters with the park's animal keepers."}
{"id": "221019", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221019", "title": "Carranglan", "text": "Carranglan is a municipality in Nueva Ecija. It is a highland town in the middle of the Caraballo Mountains that is dubbed the \u201cGateway to Cagayan Valley\u201d and the \u201cLittle Baguio\u201d and \u201cSummer Capital\u201d of Nueva Ecija for its cool climate.\nUnderstand.\nCarranglan was founded by the Spanish in the 17th century along the Digdig River, an tributary of the Pampanga River, as a base camp to subjugate the indigenous Ilongot and Isinays in Nueva Vizcaya, on the other side of the Caraballo Mountains that separate the Cagayan Valley from the Central Luzon plains. Nestled in a mountain valley, its landscape provides a contrast to the flat plains of Nueva Ecija, with parts of the municipality being dubbed as \"Little Baguio\" for its cool climate. It gained prominence for its strategic location at the terminus of the Balete (Dalton) Pass, which led to its inclusion into the Philippine highway network and a bloody battle in the closing days of World War II in 1945 that saw American and Filipino forces engage in months of protracted uphill fighting against Japanese holdouts in the area during the Liberation of Cagayan Valley.\nTucked into the mountainous triboundary region of Nueva Vizcaya, Nueva Ecija and Pangasinan, Carranglan is an ethnically mixed town inhabited by Ilocano and Tagalog migrants, Igorot and Ilongot tribespeople and indigenous Kalanguya. While not exactly a tourist town, its main attractions are from a natural and logistical perspective, being a midway point in the journey between Metro Manila and Cagayan Valley with loads of roadside restaurants and gas stations serving tired motorists who are also pondering the mountain views.\nGet in.\nCarranglan lies along the Maharlika Highway, formally known as the Pan-Philippine or Asian Highway (AH 26). The main entrance to Cagayan Valley, it is a two-lane thoroughfare that is vulnerable to traffic jams caused by landslides, vehicular accidents and vehicles sustaining mechanical breakdowns, particularly in the mountainous section leading from Barangay Capintalan to Balete (Dalton) Pass at the boundary with Santa Fe in Nueva Vizcaya. The highway itself bypasses downtown Carranglan; going there requires taking a junction in Barangay Digdig.\nThere are plans to build a bypass road that would avoid the high peaks of Dalton Pass using tunnels and directly connect downtown Carranglan with the national highway network.\nCarranglan is a midway point and stopover for buses travelling between Metro Manila and Cagayan Valley.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the most common transport between barangays, but due to uneven terrain, an SUV or any 4WD is recommended, particularly when driving along Dalton Pass.\nDrink.\nMany highland barangays rely on natural springs, with the result being a lucrative business in providing long convoys of trucks with water to cool engines that overheat during the ascent/descent through Dalton Pass.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in San Jose.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition, and bring enough supplies to outlast a traffic jam, which can range from an hour at best to 18 and longer in the event of a major vehicular pileup or landslip.\nCarranglan is located smack in the middle of the Philippine Fault System, which runs along the stretch of Maharlika Highway running through the municipality, and was devastated in the 1990 Luzon earthquake.\nAt night, look out for ropes raised across the middle of the highway by residents or itinerant robbers targeting motorists. These can be very dangerous for motorcyclists driving at high speed.\nThere may be a few New People's Army rebels hiding in the mountains. There are also more security checkpoints in town than usual, with a permanent police presence in Dalton Pass and agricultural and environmental agencies maintaining their own checkpoints elsewhere to guard against contraband products given Carranglan's location in a regional boundary.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3123.\nInternet and phone signals in town can be erratic."}
{"id": "221020", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221020", "title": "Nahuatl phrasebook", "text": "Nahuatl is the most widely spoken indigenous language in Mexico. It was the language of several Mexica cultures in Central Mexico, including the Aztecs, who dominated much of Central Mexico at the time of the Spanish Conquest. It is spoken by about 1.6 million people in Mexico, though most of those will also understand Spanish. \nNahuatl is most likely to be heard in Central Mexico, particularly in the state of Veracruz, but the language is very widespread and even modern states that are very far from Central Mexico may have significant numbers of Nahuatl speakers. There are as many as 30 significant dialects and variants of Nahuatl, and it is possible that the word or phrase used in one community may be different than those commonly used in other areas or spoken by different Mexica populations. "}
