{"id": "221025", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221025", "title": "Kiffa", "text": "Kiffa (Arabic: \u0643\u064a\u0641\u0647) is the capital of the Assaba region of southern Mauritania. It lies on the Route de l'Espoir (N3), the country's principal east\u2013west highway, roughly midway between Nouakchott and N\u00e9ma.\nKiffa is a busy regional trading centre rather than a conventional tourist town. Its large market receives goods from across Mauritania and neighbouring countries, while the city is particularly famous for colourful Kiffa beads, traditionally handmade from powdered glass.\nFor travellers, Kiffa is also the principal base for exploring the mountains, dunes, oasis valleys, lakes and pastoral landscapes of Assaba.\nUnderstand.\nKiffa sits on a plateau at the edge of the Assaba Massif, where the Sahara begins to merge into the more seasonally green Sahel.\nThe city occupies an important strategic position on the Route de l'Espoir, Mauritania's principal paved highway linking Nouakchott in the west with the Hodh regions and N\u00e9ma in the east.\nKiffa has consequently developed into one of the main commercial centres of interior Mauritania. Traders, herders and travellers from across the region pass through its markets.\nThe surrounding landscape is much more varied than the flat desert image often associated with Mauritania. Within Assaba are:\nTourism remains lightly developed. Outside the city, independent exploration generally requires a reliable 4WD, local knowledge and careful planning.\nHistory.\nLocal histories describe settlement in the Kiffa area before formal French occupation.\nThe modern administrative town developed around a French military and administrative post established in the early twentieth century.\nFrench forces reached the Kiffa area in late 1906, and an administrative post was formally established in 1907 near Hassi Babou.\nThe settlement subsequently became an important administrative centre for the surrounding Assaba region.\nKiffa's growth accelerated as pastoral families increasingly settled and road links improved.\nThe construction of the Route de l'Espoir transformed the city's commercial importance by placing it directly on the principal road linking western and eastern Mauritania.\nToday Kiffa remains the administrative capital and principal commercial centre of Assaba.\nCulture.\nKiffa is particularly famous for its traditional powdered-glass beads.\nThe beads are traditionally produced by women using finely crushed coloured glass combined with a binder such as gum arabic. Designs can be:\nwith intricate combinations of red, yellow, blue, white and black.\nOlder examples have become highly collectible, while contemporary artisans continue to produce jewellery inspired by the historic designs.\nOther crafts associated with Kiffa and Assaba include:\nArabic and Hassaniya are widely spoken. Pulaar, Soninke and other regional languages may also be heard because Kiffa serves a diverse trading hinterland.\nClimate.\nKiffa has an extremely hot Sahelian desert climate.\nApril through June is usually the hottest period. Daytime heat can become severe, particularly before the rains arrive.\nMost annual rainfall falls during approximately July through September.\nThe rainy season, known locally as the hivernage, transforms parts of Assaba. Dry valleys and grasslands can turn green surprisingly quickly, and lakes and seasonal water bodies expand.\nThis can be one of the most attractive periods for scenery, but heavy rain may also damage or flood minor roads.\nNovember through February generally provides the most comfortable temperatures for ordinary travel.\nCarry considerably more drinking water than you expect to need whenever leaving the city.\nGet in.\nBy road.\nRoad travel is the normal way to reach Kiffa.\nThe city lies directly on the paved Route de l'Espoir (N3).\nFrom Nouakchott, the road distance is roughly 600 km. Allow a full day rather than planning around a precise journey time.\nThe main west\u2013east route passes through or near:\nKiffa therefore makes a natural overnight stopping point on long journeys across southern Mauritania.\nRoad conditions on the N3 are much better than on desert tracks, but:\nAvoid long-distance driving at night.\nCarry spare water and ensure the vehicle is mechanically sound before leaving a major town.\nBy shared taxi or minibus.\nShared taxis and minibuses are the principal public transport for long-distance journeys.\nVehicles operate between Kiffa and:\nThere may be no precise published timetable. Vehicles commonly leave when enough passengers have assembled.\nAsk locally for the correct departure point for your destination.\nFor a very long journey, choosing a larger and better-maintained vehicle may be more comfortable than squeezing into the first available shared taxi.\nLuggage may be carried on the roof.\nBy plane.\nKiffa Airport () lies near the city and has a paved runway.\nHowever, do not plan a journey around a scheduled flight to Kiffa without checking directly beforehand.\nAs of September 2026, Mauritania Airlines does not list Kiffa among its scheduled domestic destinations, and current aviation schedules show no regular passenger service.\nCharter, government, medical or occasional special flights may still use the airport.\nFor ordinary travellers, road transport remains the dependable option.\nGet around.\nCentral Kiffa can be explored on foot, although the intense daytime heat makes even moderate distances tiring.\nShared taxis and local taxis are useful for:\nAgree on the fare before departure if it is not fixed.\nRoad names and conventional street addresses are not always useful. Hotels, shops and drivers may identify places by:\nSave destinations offline before travelling.\nFor excursions beyond the city, use a reliable vehicle and preferably a driver familiar with Assaba.\nA 4WD is strongly preferable for:\nDo not drive independently into remote desert or mountain country without sufficient fuel, water and navigation.\nBuy.\nKiffa is an excellent shopping centre by interior Mauritanian standards.\nThe central market sells:\nThe most distinctive purchase is a Kiffa bead.\nTraditional designs use powdered glass and intricate coloured decoration.\nWhen buying older beads, remember that:\nBuy a bead because you like it rather than treating a market seller's age claim as guaranteed authentication.\nOther regional products include:\nMauritania remains heavily cash-based.\nCarry Mauritanian ouguiya (MRU) in smaller denominations.\nATMs and banking services are available in larger regional towns such as Kiffa but should not be treated as completely dependable.\nForeign cards may fail.\nIt is illegal to export Mauritanian currency, so exchange remaining ouguiya before leaving the country.\nEat.\nKiffa has simple restaurants, grills, caf\u00e9s and roadside eating places.\nThe city's position on a major trade route means food options are generally better than in smaller Assaba settlements.\nCommon meals include:\nMauritanian meals may also be served communally from a large shared dish.\nLocal Assaba products and seasonal foods become more abundant after the rains.\nSmaller eateries may not display menus or fixed hours.\nIf travelling into rural Assaba, carry enough food for delays.\nDrink.\nMauritanian tea is central to social life.\nStrong green tea is traditionally prepared in three small glasses, usually very sweet and poured repeatedly to create foam.\nAccepting tea can turn a short stop into a much longer conversation.\nOther drinks include:\nThe tourism office specifically notes natural beverages produced using local ingredients.\nAlways carry bottled or treated drinking water.\nDo not drink untreated water from wells, lakes or seasonal pools unless a trusted local source confirms it is safe and it has been appropriately treated.\nMauritania is an Islamic republic and alcohol is prohibited. Do not bring or seek alcoholic drinks.\nSleep.\nKiffa has several hotels and simple auberges catering mainly to:\nExpect standards to be simpler than in Nouakchott.\nConfirm:\nbefore choosing a room if these are important.\nOther hotels and guesthouses operate around the city, and new properties appear periodically.\nFor an overland trip, telephone ahead rather than relying entirely on international booking sites.\nConnect.\nMobile-phone service is generally available in Kiffa. Coverage becomes much less dependable once leaving the city for remote mountain, oasis or desert areas.\nMauritania's principal mobile operators include Mauritel, Mattel and Chinguitel.\nLocal SIM cards and mobile data are easier to arrange in a large town such as Kiffa than in remote settlements.\nHotel Wi-Fi may be available but can be slow or unreliable.\nDownload:\nbefore continuing into rural Assaba.\nLong-distance travellers should not depend entirely on mobile coverage.\nFor remote off-road travel, consider:\nThe police can be reached at 117.\nCarry identification or a copy of your passport because security checkpoints occur on intercity roads.\nStay safe.\nSecurity conditions require more attention than they did in older travel guides.\nAs of September 2026, the UK government advises against all but essential travel throughout Assaba, citing instability and organized-crime risks extending from the conflict in neighbouring Mali.\nThe U.S. Department of State advises travellers to reconsider travel to Mauritania because of terrorism, crime and limited medical infrastructure.\nAreas close to the Mali border carry stronger warnings.\nCheck current official travel advice before making an Assaba itinerary.\nLong-distance journeys should be made during daylight.\nAvoid remote travel alone.\nFor journeys outside established towns:\nMedical facilities outside Nouakchott are limited.\nA serious illness or injury may require evacuation over a very long distance.\nCarry appropriate travel and medical-evacuation insurance.\nTraffic is another significant hazard.\nAvoid night driving because of:\nHeat can become life-threatening.\nDuring the hottest months, avoid strenuous activity around midday.\nWear conservative clothing appropriate to local customs.\nMauritania is an Islamic country. Respect Ramadan practices and local religious norms.\nAvoid photographing:\nwithout permission."}
{"id": "221031", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221031", "title": "Qinglong County (Guizhou)", "text": "Qinglong County (\u6674\u9686\u53bf) is a county in Qianxinan, Guizhou, China.\nUnderstand.\nQinglong County is in the southwestern part of Guizhou Province and is administered by Qianxinan Buyei and Miao Autonomous Prefecture. The county is mountainous and has a subtropical monsoon climate.\nQinglong is particularly known for the historic 24 Zigzags, a winding mountain road associated with the transportation history of southwestern China during the Second World War. The road was completed in the 1930s and has 24 bends.\nThe county also has several scenic areas associated with the 24 Zigzags scenic area, including the Panjiang Bridge, Hama Pass and Feishuiyan.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nQinglong is connected to other parts of Guizhou by major roads. The G60 Shanghai\u2013Kunming Expressway and G320 National Highway pass through the county.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses connect Qinglong with nearby towns and cities in southwestern Guizhou. Travelers should check current schedules locally before travelling.\nGet around.\nThe county seat can be explored by taxi and local transportation. A private car or taxi is more convenient for visiting attractions outside the county seat because the area is mountainous and attractions are spread across the county.\nBuy.\nLocal agricultural products and regional specialties can be found in markets and shops in Qinglong County.\nEat.\nRestaurants in Qinglong serve local Guizhou cuisine. Dishes in the region commonly feature spicy flavors and locally produced ingredients.\nDrink.\nTea and other non-alcoholic drinks are widely available in restaurants and shops.\nSleep.\nAccommodation is available in the county seat, with hotels and guesthouses serving visitors to Qinglong and the surrounding attractions.\nConnect.\nMobile phone services are available in populated areas. Coverage may be weaker in mountainous and rural areas."}
{"id": "221038", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221038", "title": "LA's 110 Freeways", "text": "Interstate 110 and California State Route 110, also known as the Harbor Freeway, Arroyo Seco Parkway and Pasadena Freeway is an itinerary in Los Angeles County.\nUnderstand.\nThe Arroyo Seco Parkway was originally built in 1940 as part of the City Beautiful parkway-building craze of the early and mid-20th century. In 1943, it was extended through the Figueroa Street Tunnels that had been constructed in 1931. It was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 2011. As a parkway, it is narrower and has shorter exits than later freeways, making driving at high speeds particularly unwise. Big trucks are not permitted on the Arroyo Seco Parkway. At various times, U.S. 6 and U.S. 66 were routed onto what has been the 110 since 1981.\nPrepare.\nBetween Central LA and San Pedro, I-110, along with I-710, is one of the main freight routes to the port. The portion north of I-10 is designated a state highway because it is not built to interstate highway standards.\nBetween CA-91 and Adams Boulevard are shared-use bus lanes and high-occupancy toll lanes. Buses, carpools and motorcycles are free but single-occupancy vehicles pay congestion pricing. Cars in the HOT lanes must have a FasTrak transponder. \nGet in.\nThe 110 Freeway connects with Interstate 5, which runs up and down the western United States and connects to the Burbank Airport. I-405 connects the 110 with LAX and the Long Beach Airport, and I-105 also connects to LAX. The 110 also connects with Interstate 10, which heads to Santa Monica, the Inland Empire, and points east; CA-91, which also goes to the Inland Empire; U.S. 101 (El Camino Real) and the Pacific Coast Highway, which both go up the California coast. Union Station, LA's main train station, is about a mile from the 110.\nBy public transit.\nThe Metro J Line busway (formerly the Silver Line) runs on the bus lanes of I-110 between Adams Blvd and San Pedro, stopping at Adams, 37th Street, Slauson, Manchester, the 105 Freeway, Rosecrans, 182nd Street, Carson Street and Pacific Coast Highway. The stop at the 105 connects to the Metro C Line toward LAX, while the stop at 182nd is a major bus depot. The J Line also connects the 110 to Union Station and other destinations in Downtown LA. \nThere are no lines that run along the Arroyo Seco Parkway, but the Metro A line runs within a few blocks of it in Northeast L.A., stopping at Lincoln/Cypress (Ave 26), Heritage Square (French Ave), Southwest Museum (Museum Drive), Highland Park (Ave 57), South Pasadena (Mission &amp; Meridian), Fillmore St, Del Mar Blvd and Memorial Park (Holly St).\nBy bike.\nBikes are not permitted on the freeway itself, but several bike paths, lanes and route run parallel to the freeway, such as the Arroyo Seco bike path, and the bike route on Broadway in South L.A.\nStay.\nAccommodations for this itinerary can be found in:\nThe areas around LAX and the Burbank Airport are several miles west of this itinerary, but are possible choices for lodging."}
{"id": "221041", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221041", "title": "Bagulin", "text": "Bagulin is a town in La Union. \nUnderstand.\nBagulin is a landlocked municipality established along the Balili River in the triboundary between the provinces of La Union, Ilocos Sur and Benguet. It is a highland town in the Cordillera mountains and has significant Igorot communities. Formerly a part of the Benguet subdistrict of Mountain Province, it was annexed by La Union in 1918.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nBagulin is bypassed by La Union's major highways. Instead, it is connected by roads leading to the neighboring municipality of Naguilian, which is along the Naguilian Road leading to Baguio and Bauang, and the provincial capital San Fernando, which is along Manila North Road (Route 2).\nThere are tricycles connecting Bagulin with Naguilian, which in turn is connected to Bauang by jeepney.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town.\nBuy.\nThe town is known for making brooms made from tiger grass that are exported mostly to the tourist markets of Baguio.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in San Fernando and Bauang.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nStay healthy.\nAvoid unnecessary and prolonged exposure in the Balili River, as the waterway is contaminated by pollution coming upstream from Baguio.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2512."}
{"id": "221042", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221042", "title": "Kayapa", "text": "Kayapa is a town in Nueva Vizcaya, Philippines. Situated within the Cordillera Mountains, it is the main point of entry leading to the Cagayan Valley region from Baguio and is dubbed the \u201cSummer Capital\u201d of Nueva Vizcaya for its highland location and cool climate.\nUnderstand.\nNestled within the Cordillera Mountains, Kayapa is a geographically large town that runs through the length of Nueva Vizcaya (except for its southern panhandle). However, it has only a small population composed of Igorots due to its rugged terrain. It was one of the few places in Nueva Vizcaya that was not conquered by the Spanish. It frequently switched provinces between Benguet and Nueva Vizcaya before finally coming under the latter's jurisdiction during the American occupation in 1905.\nLong overshadowed by neighboring Benguet, Kayapa is slowly developing its tourist potential as an alternative hiking and climbing spot to its neighbors in the Luzon Cordilleras. It is also the province\u2019s \u201cVegetable Bowl\u201d owing to its thriving agricultural sector focused on growing cabbages, potatoes, carrots and other staples.\nGet in.\nKayapa lies along the Benguet-Nueva Vizcaya Road. It is a winding two-lane thoroughfare that is vulnerable to blockages caused by landslides, vehicular accidents and vehicles sustaining mechanical breakdowns. There are few forms of public transportation to town apart from some jeepneys running from Bambang, Bayombong and other towns in lowland Nueva Vizcaya, and the passenger vans that connect Baguio with Nueva Vizcaya and Santiago City in Isabela. \nFrom Maharlika Highway, take the junction in Aritao if coming from Metro Manila. From the north, there is an access road in Bambang leading west towards a junction with Benguet-Nueva Vizcaya Road in Barangay Pingkian.\nGet around.\nThere are tricycles in the downtown, but due to uneven terrain, an SUV or any 4WD is recommended. There are also jeepneys connecting downtown Kayapa with the outlying barangays.\nBuy.\nKayapa is where one can buy vegetables directly from farms before their prices get jacked up in the market due to transportation costs.\nDrink.\nMany barangays rely on natural springs for their drinking water.\nSleep.\nThere are a few homestays in town.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition, and bring enough supplies to outlast a traffic jam.\nPermits are required from local authorities to undertake any hikes or climb mountains. The hiking season runs during the dry season from November to May. While Kayapa is often known for its cool weather, do not underestimate the effects of heat and exhaustion while climbing, especially as many of the slopes are directly exposed to sunlight.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3708.\nInternet and phone signals in town can be erratic."}
{"id": "221046", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221046", "title": "Aranese phrasebook", "text": "Aranese (\"aran\u00e9s\") is a Romance language. It is the official language of the county of the Aran Valley in Catalonia, and a co-official language in the autonomous community of Catalonia. Despite being a very small language (it is spoken only by around 5.100 people) it has also dialects, which differ in pronunciation, grammar and vocabulary. There are three main dialects:\nThis phrasebook is about Middle Aranese, spoken in Vielha (the capital of the valley), which serves as standard. Aranese is a relatively easy language, and it is similar to Catalan, French, Lengadocian Occitan, Spanish and Portuguese. Aranese pronunciation is very similar to Catalan, but not identical, and there are some characteristics that are even \"tricky\" for Catalan speakers.\nPronunciation.\nAranese pronunciation is more regular than that of Catalan, but trickier for English- or Catalan-speakers, as there are letters which don't sound like they would expect. \nSome Aranese sounds don't correspond to any sound in English, so this guide simplifies those sounds.\nVowels.\nAranese has 7 vowel sounds:\na, \u00e0 - as the 'a' in \"father\" [ah]\ne, \u00e9 - as the '\u00e9' in French \"\u00e9t\u00e9\" [ay]\n\u00e8 - as the 'e' in \"bed\" [eh]\ni, \u00ed, \u00ef - as the 'ee' in \"bee\" [ee] or as the 'y' on \"yell\" [y]\no, \u00f3 - as the 'oo' in \"cook\" [oo]\n\u00f2 - as the 'o' in \"pot\" [aw]\nu, \u00fa, \u00fc - as the 'ew' in British English \"new\" [ew] or as the 'w' in \"wing\" [w]\nConsonants.\nb - as the 'b' in \"elbow\" [b]\nc - before a, o, u it is pronounced as the 'c' in \"cut\" [k]; before e, i it is pronounced as the 's' in \"soap\" [s]\n\u00e7 - as the 's' in \"soap\" [s]\nch - as the 'ch' in \"touch\" [ch]\nd - as the 'd' in \"dog\" [d]\nf - as the 'f' in \"foot\" [f]\ng - before a, o, u it is pronounced as the 'g' in \"goat\" [g]; before e, i it is pronounced as the 'j' in French \"jour\" [zh]\ngu - before a, o, u it is pronounced as a 'gw'; before e, i it is pronounced as the 'g' in \"goat\" [g]\ng\u00fc - as a 'gw' [gw]\nh - mute\nj - as the 'j' in French \"jour\" [zh]\nl - as the 'l' in \"light\" [l]\nlh - similar to a 'l' followed by a 'y' [ly]\nm - as the 'm' in \"mouse\" [m]\nn - in most cases, it is pronounced as the 'n' in \"night\" [n]; at the end of a word it is pronounced as the 'ng' in \"thing\" [ng]\nnh - as the '\u00f1' in Spanish \"ni\u00f1o\" [ny]\np - as the 'p' in \"pot\" [p]\nqu - before a, o, u it is pronounced as a 'kw' [kw]; before e, i it is pronounced as the 'c' in \"cat\"\nq\u00fc - as a 'kw' [kw]\nr - at the starting of a word, as a rolled 'r' [rr]; in other contexts, as the 'tt' in American English \"kitty\". [\u027e]\nrr - as a rolled 'r' [rr]\ns - between vowels, as the 'z' in \"zone\" [z]; at the beginning of a word and in other contexts as the 's' in \"soap\" [s]\nss - as the 's' in \"soap\" [s]\nsh - as the 'sh' in \"shell\" [sh]; if preceded by vowel+'i', the 'i' is not pronounced.\nt - as the 't' in \"tower\" [t]\nth - identical to letter 't' [t]\nv - identical to letter 'b' [b]\nx - between vowels, it is pronouned as a 'd' followed by a 'z' [dz]; in other contexts as a 't' followed by a 's' [ts]\nz - as the 'z' in \"zone\" [z]\nStress.\nAn accent marks the stressed vowel: aran\u00e9s \"[ah-rah-NAYS]\". If there is not any accent, stress is positioned:\nPhrase list.\nIf a prase can be polite or informal, the informal form is written before the polite, with the sign (inf./pol.) at the end.\nBasics.\nHello. - \u00d2la. \"[AW-lah.]\"\nHow are you? - Com va? \"[koom-BAH?]\"\nFine, thank you. - Ben, gr\u00e0cies. \"[BAYNG, GRAH-syays.]\"\nWhat is your name? - Com te dides? \"[KOOM tay DEE-days?]\" / Com vos didetz? \"[KOOM boos dee-DAYTS?]\" (inf./pol.)\nMy name is ____. - Me digui ____. \"[may DEE-gee ___.]\"\nPleased to meet you. - Encantat (masc.) / Encantada (fem.) \"[ayn-kahn-TAHT / ayn-kahn-TAH-dah]\"\nPlease. - Se vos platz. \"[say-boos-PLAHTS]\"\nThank you. - Gr\u00e0cies. \"[GRAH-syays.]\"\nYou're welcome. - De res \"[day-RRAYS]\"\nYes. - \u00d2c \"[AW]\" / Si \"[SEE]\"*\nNo. - Non \"[NOO]\"\nExcuse me. (\"getting attention\") - Desencusatz. \"[day-zayn-kew-ZAHTS.]\"\nSorry. / Excuse me. (\"begging pardon\") - Perdon. \"[payr-DOONG]\" / Perdonatz \"[payr-doo-NAHTS]\" (inf./pol.)\nGoodbye - Adiu \"[ah-DEE-oo]\" / Adishatz \"[ah-dee-SHAHTS]\"\nI can't speak Aranese well. - Non parli ben aran\u00e9s. \"[NOO PAHR-lee BAYNG ah-rah-NAYS.]\"\nDo you speak English? - Parles angl\u00e9s? \"[PAHR-lays ahn-GLAYS?]\" / Parlatz angl\u00e9s? \"[pahr-LAHTS ahn-GLAYS?]\"\nIs there someone here who speaks English? - I a quauquarr\u00e9s aciu que parle angl\u00e9s? \"[YAH kah-oo-kah-RRAYS ah-SEE-oo kay PAHR-lay ahn GLAYS?]\"\nI (only) speak English. - (Sonque) parli angl\u00e9s. \"[SOON-kay PAHR-lee ahn-GLAYS.]\"\nHelp! - Ajuda! \"[ah-ZHEW-dah!]\"\nHelp me! - Ajudatz-me! \"[ah-zhew-DAHTS-may!]\"\nLook out! - Atencion! \"[ah-tayn-SYOONG!]\"\nGood morning - Bon dia \"[BOONG DEE-ah]\""}
{"id": "221047", "revid": "2197649", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221047", "title": "Poissy", "text": "Poissy is in Yvelines.\nUnderstand.\nPoissy is in the Yvelines department about 10 km west of central Paris. It is located next to the Seine.\nGet around.\nBuses leave from both sides of the Poissy train station. There is a bus station behind the station and through buses stop on Av. Maurice Berteaux at the front of the station."}
{"id": "221057", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221057", "title": "Jungle Mahal", "text": "Jungle Mahal (Bengali: \u099c\u0999\u09cd\u0997\u09b2\u09ae\u09b9\u09b2 \"Jongolmohol\", Hindi: \u091c\u0902\u0917\u0932 \u092e\u0939\u0932 \"Jangal Mahal\"), literally the \"jungle estates\", was a district of British India, comprising the present-day Jharkhand and West Bengal in eastern India. The region is known for its deep forests, undulating landscapes and tribal cultures.\nRegions.\nJungle Mahal consists of the following regions:\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nBefore the Jungle Mahal district was created in 1805, there was a series of uprisings by the tribal communities in the region in 1798-99, against the oppressive policies of the British East India Company. The rebels were called \"Chuars\", meaning \"barbarians\". The district was created as a response to the unrest. It comprised the areas around Bishnupur, Dhanbad, Ghatshila, Jhalda, Jhargram and Purulia.\nIn 1833, the district was split into Burdwan and Manbhum districts within the South-West Frontier Agency. The Bankura district was created on 16 March 1881 out of the former Jungle Mahal areas of Burdwan and Manbhum. Manbhum eventually became a part of Bihar, leaving Burdwan and Bankura under Bengal.\nIn the 1950s, the Bengali speakers of Manbhum in Bihar demanded for the recognition of Bengali in the region. In 1956, the Bengali-speaking Purulia district was carved out of Manbhum, with the rest remaining part of Bihar as Dhanbad district. In 2000, southern Bihar got split into its own state called \"Jharkhand\", literally the \"land of forests\".\nGeography.\nJungle Mahal is a forested region on the eastern fringe of the Chota Nagpur Plateau. It is home to hills like Ajodhya, Baranti, Biharinath, Dalma, Joychandi, Panchkot and Susunia.\nCulture.\nJungle Mahal is home to various Adivasi tribes, including Bagal, Baiga, Binjhari, Kharia, Ho, Korwa, Kudmi, Kurukh, Lohra, Munda and many others.\nThere are many festivals observed in the region, including Bhadu, Karam and Tusu.\nBhadu is a month-long festival during the Hindu lunisolar month of Bhadra.\nKaram is a local harvest festival held on Bhadra Ekadashi in the Hindu lunisolar calendar, which falls between August and September. Unmarried women fast and grow seedlings for 7\u20139 days. Then next day, groups of young villagers go to the jungle and collect wood, fruits and flowers. During this period, people sing and dance together in groups.\nTusu is anothet harvest fesitval held during Makar Sankranti (14-15 January)."}
{"id": "221058", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221058", "title": "Cheongyang", "text": "Cheongyang (\uccad\uc591) is a rural county in South Chungcheong Province, South Korea. Known for its clean natural environment, it is famous nationwide for its spicy Cheongyang chili pepper and as the setting of the popular Korean folk song \"Chilgapsan.\"\nUnderstand.\nCheongyang is the smallest county in South Chungcheong Province. The area is centered around Chilgapsan (\uce60\uac11\uc0b0), a mountain considered sacred locally, said to embody the \"seven origins of creation\" (hence the name). The county has been actively developing tourism infrastructure in recent years, including a large park golf course built on a former mine site and new observation facilities on Chilgapsan.\nBuy.\nCheongyang chili pepper, both marketed as health products. \nEat.\nchamge maeuntang (\ucc38\uac8c\ub9e4\uc6b4\ud0d5, spicy freshwater crab stew), both considered signature Cheongyang dishes. "}
{"id": "221066", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221066", "title": "Jhalda", "text": "Jhalda (\u099d\u09be\u09b2\u09a6\u09be \"Jh\u0101ld\u0101\") is a town surrounded by hills, in the Purulia district of West Bengal."}
{"id": "221073", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221073", "title": "Itbayat", "text": "Itbayat is an island municipality of Batanes province in the northern Philippines. \nUnderstand.\nItbayat is the northern-most island and municipality in the Batanes archipelago and also in the Philippines. It is one of the three (out of 10) islands in the province that are inhabited, the others being Batan and Sabtang. It also has jurisdiction over six uninhabited islands, including Mavulis, also known as Y'ami, the most northerly point of the Philippines and a mere 142 km away from Taiwan.\nItbayat island is believed to be the oldest inhabited settlement in Batanes, with archaeological evidence suggesting that humans first arrived there in 4000 BC. However, is the most difficult to access among the Batanes islands, as its coastline is entirely composed of rocky cliffs that are constantly battered by the rough seas while the mainland is constantly battered by winds strong enough to swipe off hats and knock down visitors even in sunny days. As such, it sees the least number of tourists and the most unspoiled views in the province. These consist of windswept landscapes covered with grass and a few bent trees in between, with the occasional goat, cow and other livestock grazing freely.\nGet in.\nThe best time to visit is during the dry season between March and June, when the winds and waves are calm. Otherwise, expect frequent cancellations at the last minute.\nItbayat is only accessible from the daily ferry on the \"faluwa\", the traditional Ivatan fishing boat, that disembarks from the provincial capital, Basco, in Batan Island. Travel time takes 3\u20134 hours in fair weather and 5-6 hours at other times. Expect to share space with goods being imported from Batan and even livestock. There are six seaports around the island, with Chinapoliran port, basically a long and steep set of concrete stairs, being the primary one. Be careful when boarding and disembarking, as the waves frequently throw unsuspecting passengers overboard. Often, you may need to jump to avoid this.\nThe island also has its own airport, a 15-minute flight by light charter aircraft from Basco.\nA mandatory environmental fee is collected from visitors at the municipal hall in downtown Itbayat.\nGet around.\nThe road network is less developed than in other islands, so expect lots of hiking. Get a guide with you at all times as the terrain and winds in town can be daunting. Motorcycles, tricycles and some trucks are the predominant form of transportation in town. There are no cars.\nBuy.\nThere is no organized market. Instead, all trades are conducted in the mom-and-pop \"sari-sari\" stores typical of the Philippines. \nAll transactions are in cash due to the absence of banks and ATMs. Prices tend to be higher than in Batan Island due to the costs of transporting goods by sea, and can increase further when bad weather halts the ferries.\nA popular souvenir in Itbayat includes handicrafts made from the endemic \"nito\" vine.\nSleep.\nThere are no hotels in Itbayat, only homestays.\nStay safe.\nLike in the rest of Batanes, crime is abysmally low, and the residents know each other. In any case, there is no escape route other than the once-daily \"faluwa\" ferry back to Batan Island.\nStay healthy.\nTry not to get into a serious medical emergency, as there is the town's District Hospital is not fully equipped and you will need to be evacuated to Basco or worse, to mainland Luzon due to a lack of medicines and other resources.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3905. \nLike in the rest of Batanes, electricity is on a rotational basis, with generators and solar panels being the norm. Cellphone and internet signals are often sketchy, although things are improving with the proliferation of Starlink terminals, while the reception from radio stations in Taiwan can be clearer than the domestic ones."}
{"id": "221074", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221074", "title": "Los Angeles/Harbor", "text": "There are several neighborhoods in the Harbor area of Los Angeles, in the South Bay part of L.A. County: \nThis article also deals with the incorporated city of Lomita (pop. 20,921) and the unincorporated region of West Carson.\nUnderstand.\nWilmington was founded by Phineas Banning of Wilmington, Delaware. It was incorporated in 1863 and annexed to Los Angeles in 1909. The Wilmington/Long Beach/Torrance area is part of a massive oil field, with oil refining and shipbuilding being the area's traditional industries.\nHarbor City and Harbor Gateway, then sparsely populated, were annexed by city of Los Angeles during the construction of the Port of Los Angeles. The Harbor Gateway is a narrow strip of land connecting Central and South Los Angeles to San Pedro and Wilmington. Harbor City and Lomita were developed in earnest during and after World War II, Lomita incorporated in 1964. \nGet in.\nThe closest airports to these communities are LAX and Long Beach airport. The closest freeway is I-110, which runs down the Harbor Gateway, and between Harbor City and Wilmington. A small section of I-405 runs east-west through the Harbor Gateway. The Pacific Coast Highway also goes through Wilmington and Harbor City. The J Line/Silver Line busway has stations at Rosecrans, 182nd Street, Carson Street and Pacific Coast Hwy."}
{"id": "221087", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221087", "title": "Ramnagar", "text": "Ramnagar is a city on the Ganges, opposite Varanasi, in eastern Uttar Pradesh."}
{"id": "221090", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221090", "title": "Luna (Apayao)", "text": "\"For the municipality in La Union, see Luna.\"\nLuna is a town in Apayao. It is the province's economic hub, \"de facto\" administrative capital and primary port of entry.\nUnderstand.\nFormerly known as Macatel, it was renamed as Luna in the 20th century in honor of the Luna brothers, Filipino nationalist heroes, who belonged to the same ethnic group as the Ilocano migrants who arrived in town during that same period. In the 1970s and 80s, the Marag Valley, in the western part of town, became known as a stronghold of the New People's Army, which contributed to the image of Apayao being a \"no-go\" zone during this period.\nSince the creation of Apayao as a province in 1995, Luna has become the \"de facto\" capital of Apayao, as the legal capital, Kabugao, has historically been difficult to access due to its secluded location in the mountainous and forested interior. In contrast, Luna, which is at the extension of a coastal plain along the Apayao River, has been more favored due to its proximity to the Manila North Road and the town of Abulug, the commercial hub of northwestern Cagayan. The subsequent government operations against the NPA have reduced violence in the Marag Valley, which is now the main jewel in Luna's efforts to promote itself as a ecotourism destination.\nGet in.\nLuna is along the Kabugao-Junction Luna Road, the main thoroughfare in Apayao. Buses from Metro Manila and Laoag, mostly run by GV Florida, stop in Junction Luna (Libertad Junction) in Abulug, from where transfers via jeepney or tricycle can be made to Luna. There are also passenger vans linking Luna to Tuguegarao, the capital of Cagayan province.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town.\nSleep.\nThere are a few inns and homestays in town, while more conventional accommodations can be found in Abulug and Pamplona, which are also beach destinations.\nStay safe.\nPermits are required from local authorities to undertake most hikes or mountain climbing.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3813."}
{"id": "221101", "revid": "111383", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221101", "title": "Central Minnesota", "text": "Central Minnesota is in Minnesota and consists of the counties around St. Cloud: Benton, Morrison, Sherburne, Stearns and Todd."}
{"id": "221109", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221109", "title": "Pudtol", "text": "Pudtol is a town in Apayao. \nUnderstand.\nPudtol is the oldest municipality in Apayao, having been established by the Spanish in the 17th century as a missionary outpost and garrison at the end of the coastal plain formed by the Apayao River valley to subjugate the Isneg tribes in the mountainous interior of the province. Its name derives from an Isneg verb meaning \"to cut\", a reference to the reputation of the inhabitants as headhunters. Pudtol was repeatedly destroyed or abandoned during the frequent Isneg revolts against the Spanish, and it took until the 20th century when American occupiers finally imposed lasting control over the area.\nGet in.\nPudtol is along the Kabugao-Junction Luna Road, the main thoroughfare in Apayao. Buses from Metro Manila and Laoag, mostly run by GV Florida, stop in Junction Luna (Libertad Junction) in Abulug, from where transfers via jeepney can be made to Pudtol, passing through Luna. There are also passenger vans linking Pudtol to Tuguegarao, the capital of Cagayan province.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town.\nSleep.\nThere are a few inns and homestays in town.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3812."}
{"id": "221114", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221114", "title": "Abulug", "text": "Abulug is a coastal municipality in Cagayan on Luzon, the Philippines. It is the main population hub and commercial center of northwestern Cagayan and the main point of entry into much of Apayao province.\nUnderstand.\nAbulug is located at the mouth of the Apayao aka Abulug River at the northwestern Cagayan panhandle. It was founded in the late 16th century as a Spanish base to conquer the Isneg tribes of the Cordillera mountains of Apayao to the south. The town was repeatedly destroyed and rebuilt due to the volatile changes in course by the Abulug River over the centuries, and today's downtown is in situated further inland in Barangay Libertad, on the boundary with the town of Luna in Apayao.\nAbulug is dominated by Ilocano migrants, with a substantial minority of native Ibanag speakers.\nGet in.\nAbulug is at a crossroads between Manila North Road and and Kabugao-Junction Luna Road, which leads to Apayao. Their meeting point in Barangay Libertad is where the downtown has relocated since the 2000s and is colloquially known as Junction Libertad or Junction Luna. Buses traveling between Cagayan's capital Tuguegarao pass through the town on their way to Ilocos Norte's capital Laoag and other destinations in Ilocos. It is also served by passenger vans and minibuses from Tuguegarao. Travel time from either Tuguegarao or Laoag takes between 3-5 hours. \nGV Florida operates daily bus trips to Junction Luna from Metro Manila. The junction is also where jeepneys to various municipalities in Apayao can be taken. Further east, a separate junction in Barangay Lucban leads to the Apayao towns of Santa Marcela and Flora, on the east bank of the Abulug River.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town.\nBuy.\nAs a coastal and estuarine town, a common souvenir is handicrafts made from the nipa palms that grow at the Abulug River delta.\nEat.\nThe most northerly branches of Jollibee and McDonald's in the Philippines can be found in Junction Luna.\nSleep.\nMost accommodations are concentrated in Junction Luna/Libertad and also accommodate travelers visiting Apayao.\nConnect.\nThe town\u2019s postal code is 3519."}
{"id": "221116", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221116", "title": "Maddela", "text": "Maddela (pronounced \"mad-dela\") is a municipality in Quirino.\nUnderstand.\nMaddela was originally an Ilongot settlement named Pinappagan, literally a place where payment was made, either a reference to its position as a trading site, or as a local legend says, a place were transgressors paid for their offenses with blood under tribal laws.\nThe town was renamed Maddela in 1950, the same year it became a municipality through the help of a congressman of Nueva Vizcaya (which then ruled over present-day Quirino) who pushed for its status and now shares his surname with the town in recognition for his efforts. It used to cover two-thirds of Quirino's land area until 1983, when much of its southern portion was separated to create the municipality of Nagtipunan.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nMaddela is at a junction between Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare in Quirino, and the Maddela-Echague Road which leads to Cauayan in Isabela province and its airport. There are passenger vans connecting the town with Santiago, while the bus from Santiago City to Dinapigue via Aurora also passes through town. Five Star and Northern Luzon Bus Line (NLBL) have bus terminals in town offering once a day services to and from Metro Manila.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3404."}
{"id": "221122", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221122", "title": "Nagtipunan", "text": "Nagtipunan is a municipality in Quirino.\nUnderstand.\nEstablished in 1983 from several barangays of Maddela, Nagtipunan is the largest municipality in Quirino, occupying the province's entire southern half. Its name means \"gathering place\", either for its mixed population of indigenous Bugkalot and Aeta tribes and Ilocano migrants, or the convergence of multiple tributaries of the upper Cagayan River in the area. Much of the town is covered in forested mountains.\nGet in.\nNagtipunan is along Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare in Quirino. There are passenger vans connecting the town with Santiago, while the bus from Santiago City to Dinapigue via Aurora also passes through town.\nSince the 2020s, construction has been ongoing to connect Nagtipunan with Dupax del Norte in Nueva Vizcaya.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town. Access to the outlying barangays, where most natural attractions are located, requires a 4WD and bouts of hiking.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3405."}
{"id": "221123", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221123", "title": "Cabarroguis", "text": "Cabarroguis is a the capital of the province of Quirino.\nUnderstand.\nEstablished in 1969 and named after a local politician, Cabarroguis has been the capital of Quirino since it was established in 1971. Unlike other provincial capitals, it is a mostly quiet place that relies on agriculture, tourism and the presence of bureaucrats in the provincial government complex for its economy.\nGet in.\nCabarroguis is along Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare in Quirino. There are passenger vans connecting the town with Santiago, while the bus from Santiago City to Dinapigue via Aurora also passes through town. A rudimentary trail links Cabarroguis with Kasibu in Nueva Vizcaya, but can only be traversed through a 4WD.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town.\nEat.\nMost restaurants in town are in close proximity to the provincial capitol compound and are frequented by government employees, especially during lunch time.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3400."}
{"id": "221125", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221125", "title": "Santa Giusta", "text": "Santa Giusta is a town in Oristano province."}
{"id": "221126", "revid": "2218862", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221126", "title": "Montresta", "text": "Montresta is in Oristano province, 15 km from Bosa.\nUnderstand.\nMontresta is known as the \"village of the Greeks\" due to its 18th-century origins linked to a colony of Greek-Maniot refugees.\nGet around.\nThe small size of Montresta allows you to explore the entire village by foot."}
{"id": "221127", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221127", "title": "Diffun", "text": "Diffun (pronounced \"dif-foon\") is a municipality in Quirino. It is the most populated town in the province and its main port of entry.\nUnderstand.\nDiffun was originally a barrio of Santiago City in Isabela. It became an independent municipality in 1950, the same year the town, along with the five other municipalities that make up present-day Quirino, was transferred to Nueva Vizcaya. Largely settled by Ilocano migrants and indigenous Ilongot tribes, the town saw an influx of Igorot settlers displaced by the damming of several rivers in the Luzon Cordilleras in the 1970s and 80s, resulting in the town's mixed ethnic makeup today. \nBeing the nearest town of Quirino to the Maharlika Highway, the main route to Cagayan Valley from Metro Manila, it has become the most-populated town and economic hub of Quirino, with over 56,000 residents and the main campus of the Quirino State University system.\nGet in.\nDiffun is along Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare in Quirino that connects with Maharlika Highway in the neighboring town of Cordon in Isabela. At a junction in Diffun Public Market is a road directly leading to Santiago City. \nThere are passenger vans connecting the town with Santiago, while the bus from Santiago City to Dinapigue via Aurora also passes through town.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town. Access to some of the outlying barangays, where most natural attractions are located, may require a 4WD and bouts of hiking.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3401."}
{"id": "221128", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221128", "title": "Lone Tree", "text": "Lone Tree is a residential suburb located in the Denver Metropolitan Area of the U.S state of Colorado."}
{"id": "221129", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221129", "title": "Sigay", "text": "Sigay is a municipality in Ilocos Sur. Situated within the Cordillera mountains, it is home to the tallest waterfall in the Ilocos Region.\nUnderstand.\nSigay is a landlocked municipality established along a tributary of the Amburayan River. It is a highland town in the Cordillera mountains and has significant Igorot communities. Formerly a part of the Amburayan subdistrict of Mountain Province, it was annexed by Ilocos Sur in 1920. The area saw heavy fighting in World War II during the Battle of Bessang Pass in 1945, when Allied forces struggled to break through Japanese defenses in Bessang Ridge on their way to liberate Baguio and the Luzon Cordilleras.\nSigay is the least populated municipality of Ilocos Sur, with a population of only 2,577 people as of 2024. The main economic activities are coffee and rice terrace farming, and nature-based tourism, through poor road infrastructure tends to keep most visitors at bay.\nGet in.\nSigay is one of the more isolated towns of Ilocos Sur, being completely bypassed by its major highways. Instead, it is connected to a secondary road leading south towards Urzadan Bridge in the neighboring municipality of Suyo, which is along Tagudin-Cervantes-Sabangan Road running between Mountain Province and Manila North Road in the coastal town of Tagudin. To the northwest, another secondary road leads to the neighboring municipality of Salcedo and onwards to Candon, the most populated city in Ilocos Sur. Both roads feature scenic mountain drives and frightening hairpin curves.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town, but due to the uneven terrain, a 4WD is recommended. Most tourist attractions are only accessible through extended hikes.\nBuy.\nThere is no market to speak of, only a few mom-and-pop \"sari-sari\" stores. There are also no banks or ATMs, so bring plenty of cash.\nSleep.\nThere are a few homestays in town.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nMedical clearance is required to climb the area's mountains due to their extreme terrain.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2719."}
{"id": "221133", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221133", "title": "Aglipay", "text": "Aglipay is a municipality in Quirino. \nUnderstand.\nAglipay was founded in 1950 and was named after Gregorio Aglipay, a Filipino nationalist and religious leader from Ilocos Norte who was said to have taken refuge in the town's caves during the Philippine Revolution and the Philippine-American War before breaking away from the Roman Catholic Church to establish the Philippine Independent Church (IFI, or Aglipayan Church) half a century before. Its population mostly consists of Ilocano settlers and a few Bugkalot (formerly known as Ilongot) tribes in its extensive sliver of territory extending south to the boundary with Kasibu in Nueva Vizcaya.\nGet in.\nAglipay is along Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare in Quirino. There are passenger vans connecting the town with Santiago, while the bus from Santiago City to Dinapigue via Aurora also passes through town.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town. Access to the southernmost barangays, where most natural attractions are located, requires a 4WD and bouts of hiking.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3403."}
{"id": "221134", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221134", "title": "Saguday", "text": "Saguday is a municipality in Quirino. \nUnderstand.\nSaguday was founded in 1959 and was named after an Ilocano term for a virtuous person. Its population mostly consists of Ilocano settlers. Unlike other towns of Quirino, Saguday is on a fertile plain, which explains its title as the \"Rice Granary of Quirino\".\nGet in.\nThe southernmost barangays of Saguday are along Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare in Quirino, while downtown Saguday is directly connected to Santiago City in Isabela through a road that merges with Cordon-Diffun-Aurora Road in neighboring Cabarroguis. There are passenger vans connecting the town with Santiago, while the bus from Santiago City to Dinapigue via Aurora also passes through town.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of public transportation within town. \nBuy.\nA common souvenir in Saguday is fossilized flowers. Contrary to what most people would think, these have nothing to do with dinosaurs, but are instead \"alibangbang\" leaves (\"Piliostigma malabaricum\") and flower petals that have been dried, bleached and dyed in various colors. These are mostly sold in stalls along the national highway in Barangay Gamis.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3402."}
{"id": "221135", "revid": "771685", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221135", "title": "Ordesa y Monte Perdido National Park", "text": "Ordesa y Monte Perdido National Park is in Aragon of Spain.In the heart of the Pyrenees in Aragon, this Park is a natural masterpiece that enchants hikers and mountaineers alike. Towering peaks, lush valleys, and cascading waterfalls create a dramatic backdrop for exploration. The park's crown jewel is Monte Perdido, one of the highest peaks in the Pyrenees. Traverse well-maintained trails that lead to viewpoints offering awe-inspiring panoramas. Discover the iconic Ordesa Valley with its sheer cliffs and emerald forests. From wildflower meadows to glacial lakes, this UNESCO World Heritage Site is a haven for outdoor enthusiasts seeking both adventure and tranquility amidst breathtaking alpine beauty.\nUnderstand.\nOrdesa y Monte Perdido National Park lies in the central Pyrenees of Huesca, Arag\u00f3n, near the French border. Established in 1918 to protect the Ordesa Valley, it was enlarged in 1982 to include the valleys and canyons of A\u00f1isclo, Escua\u00edn and Pineta, covering about 156 km\u00b2. Its spectacular limestone landscape is centred on Monte Perdido (\"Lost Muntain\" in spanish), which rises to 3,348 m and forms part of the Tres Sorores massif. Glaciers and erosion have shaped deep gorges, cirques, caves and dramatic U-shaped valleys, while waterfalls and alpine meadows add to the scenery. The park contains forests of beech, fir, Scots pine and mountain pine, together with high-altitude plants such as edelweiss and several Pyrenean endemics. Wildlife includes chamois, marmots, wild boar, the Pyrenean desman and important raptors, notably the bearded vulture, golden eagle and griffon vulture. The former Pyrenean ibex, or bucardo, became extinct in 2000. Since 1997, the park has formed part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9es\u2013Mont Perdu and the Ordesa-Vi\u00f1amala Biosphere Reserve. Its well-known valleys and trails make it one of Spain\u2019s principal destinations for hiking, mountaineering and observing high-mountain landscapes. there are 4 main areas in the park: Valle de Ordesa, Valle de A\u00f1isclo, Valle de Escua\u00edn, Valle de Pineta\nThe interpretive design of the Casa Oliv\u00e1n Center\u2014dedicated to people with disabilities\u2014incorporates information displays rarely seen in conventional exhibitions, without creating separate display areas for specific groups. Both the exhibit structures and the layout of the Center ensure comfortable, fluid visitor flow, although brief pauses are necessary at certain modules to better grasp the concepts being presented.\nThe exhibition aims to introduce people with visual and intellectual disabilities to a wide range of topics, including:\nDiscovering the key elements that make up the Park's various ecosystems and understanding their connection to the surrounding region. \nEngaging visitors by conveying the sensory experience of these ecosystems, fostering a sense of immersion within them. \nEnsuring visitors leave the Center with an awareness of the importance of respecting this natural environment. \nIntroducing visitors to the geological origins of the Pyrenees in general and the Park in particular\u2014highlighting the intense erosive processes that shaped every valley and mountain, as well as the materials that compose the landscape. \nShowcasing how flora and fauna have adapted to harsh environmental conditions, and highlighting the presence of endangered species. \nIllustrating the relationship between humans and the land where the Park is located, spanning both the past and the present.\n* Valle de A\u00f1isclo\n* \n* Valle de Escua\u00edn\n* \n* Valle de Pineta\n* \nVisitor information.\n For disabled xxxxx\nHistory.\nOrdesa y Monte Perdido, Spain's second-oldest national park, was established on 16 August 1918 to protect the Ordesa Valley and its wildlife, particularly the Pyrenean ibex. Initially covering only 21 km\u00b2, it was substantially expanded to 156 km\u00b2 in 1982 to include the valleys of A\u00f1isclo, Pineta, and Escua\u00edn. In 1977 it became part of the Ordesa-Vi\u00f1amala Biosphere Reserve, and in 1997 it was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site within the transboundary Pyr\u00e9n\u00e9es\u2013Mont Perdu property, recognizing both its exceptional geology and its long-standing pastoral traditions.\nLandscape.\nThe park is dominated by the Monte Perdido (Mont Perdu) massif, the highest limestone mountain range in Europe at 3,355 m, known locally as the Tres Sorores. Repeated Quaternary glaciations carved deep U-shaped valleys, spectacular cirques, and sheer cliffs, while rivers such as the Arazas, Cinca, Bell\u00f3s, and Yaga have since cut dramatic canyons and gorges. At high elevations, extensive karst plateaus feature sinkholes, fissures, and caves where surface water disappears underground, contrasting with the lush, waterfall-rich valleys below.\nFlora and fauna.\nOrdesa y Monte Perdido hosts exceptional biodiversity, with more than 1,500 plant species recorded, including around 83 endemics unique to the Pyrenees. Its fauna includes 32 mammal species, 65 breeding birds, and numerous reptiles, amphibians, and fish, among them emblematic and threatened species such as the bearded vulture, golden eagle, Pyrenean desman, and the now-extinct Pyrenean ibex subspecies. Brown bear and roe deer occur in the wider region, while the park remains a key refuge for rare alpine and karst-dwelling organisms\nClimate.\nSituated in the central Pyrenees, the park experiences a high-mountain climate with strong altitudinal contrasts and marked seasonality. Winters are long and cold, with heavy snowfall above 1,500\u20132,000 m that sustains glaciers, snowfields, and seasonal streams, while summers are mild in the valleys but can bring intense solar radiation and sudden storms at higher elevations. Precipitation is generally abundant, especially in spring and autumn, feeding the numerous waterfalls, torrents, and underground karst systems that shape the landscape.\nFees and permits.\nPark rules.\nIt is NOT permitted inside the National Park:\n*An area dedicated to witchcraft and magical aspects within traditional mountain cultures. Its centerpiece is a scene featuring a \"witch\" figure preparing various concoctions. This space serves as an introduction to the building's main theme\u2014plants and their benefits to humanity\u2014and to ethnobotany (the traditional use of plants) as a cultural heritage to be preserved, understood, and shared. \n*Areas dedicated to ethnobotany and its significance as a cultural legacy at risk of disappearing. This approach aims to raise awareness among visitors to the national park and its surrounding area regarding the conservation of its unique flora and vegetation. Furthermore, the interpretive elements serve a clear educational purpose: the preservation and understanding of traditions, legends, and respectful practices associated with natural resources help ensure their conservation.\n* \nDo.\nBirdwatching.\nThe paerk has at least eighty nesting birds spices.\nThe bearded vulture\u2014a threatened, bone-eating bird of prey\u2014has several breeding pairs within the Park. The golden eagle, the yellow-billed chough, and the griffon vulture are cliff-dwelling birds that constantly defy\nvertigo."}
{"id": "221137", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221137", "title": "Al Juwain", "text": "Al Juwain\nAl Juwain (Arabic: \u0627\u0644\u062c\u0648\u064a\u0646) is a mountainous tourist village and administrative center in Harub, Jazan Region, Saudi Arabia, postal code 87631, Geonames ID 13157454, at 1200m elevation, famous for its dense fog and agricultural terraces.\nUnderstand.\nAl Juwain is famous for its dense morning fog, agricultural terraces, and Al-Dohain Waterfall near Jabal Al-Aswad (Black Mountain). It was officially established as an administrative center in Harub Governorate and includes Al Juwain Public Park.\nGet in.\nVia road 1575 from Harub, about 40 minutes by car."}
{"id": "221142", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221142", "title": "Cervantes (Ilocos Sur)", "text": "Cervantes is a municipality in Ilocos Sur. Situated within the Cordillera mountains, it is dubbed the province's summer capital.\nUnderstand.\nEstablished in 1879 as a Spanish outpost, Cervantes is a landlocked municipality established along the upper reaches of the Abra River. It is a highland town in the Cordillera mountains that forms the province's triboundary with Benguet and Mountain Province and has significant Igorot communities. \nDuring the American occupation, it became the capital of the Lepanto-Bontoc province from its establishment in 1902 until its annexation by Mountain Province in 1908. It then became capital of the Lepanto subprovince until it was annexed by Ilocos Sur in 1920. The area saw heavy fighting in World War II during the Battle of Bessang Pass in 1945, when Allied forces struggled to break through Japanese defenses in Bessang Ridge on their way to liberate Baguio and the Luzon Cordilleras.\nThe main economic activities are agriculture and nature-based tourism, through poor road infrastructure tends to keep most visitors at bay.\nGet in.\nCervantes is at a junction between Tagudin-Cervantes-Sabangan Road running between Mountain Province and Manila North Road in the coastal town of Tagudin, and a perpendicular road that connects Ilocos Sur with Halsema Highway in Benguet. Both roads meet in downtown Cervantes and feature scenic mountain drives through the Bessang Pass and frightening hairpin curves. There are passenger vans connecting Cervantes with Tagudin.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation within town, but due to the uneven terrain, a 4WD is recommended. Most tourist attractions are only accessible through extended hikes.\nSleep.\nThere are a few homestays and inns in town.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nMedical clearance is required to climb the area's mountains due to their extreme terrain.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2718."}
{"id": "221144", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221144", "title": "Bergensbanen", "text": "Bergensbanen, translating literally to Bergen Line, is no doubt one of the most scenic train journeys one can take anywhere in the world. The 371-kilometre-long train journey connects the Norwegian capital of Oslo to the country's second-largest city of Bergen, taking about 6\u20137 hours in total. \nUnderstand.\nThe Bergensbanen is one of Norway's most scenic railway lines. Running from the capital, Oslo, it cuts across the Hardangervidda Plateau, all the way to the West Coast city of Bergen. For a good 100 km, the route runs through the northern part of the Hardangervidda above the tree line. This gives travellers a glimpse of a largely inaccessible high plateau that, in its central core, can otherwise only be reached on foot or by bike.\nHistorically, the railway line \u2013 which, both at its completion and in its ongoing maintenance, especially in winter, is quite complex \u2013 stemmed from an effort to connect the two most important trading cities, Bergen and Stockholm, of the then-united kingdoms of Norway and Sweden. Following Norway's independence from Sweden in 1905, the plans were changed to connect Norway's two most important cities instead. The railway line opened in 1909, making relatively fast travel between the two locations possible for the first time. In 1941, the branch line to the Sognefjord \u2013 the Fl\u00e5m Railway \u2013 was opened, running from Myrdal (at 866 m) down to Fl\u00e5m on the Aurlandsfjord, more than 800 m below. Until then, travellers arriving by ship via the Aurlandsfjord had to make the arduous journey up the Rallarvegen to the high plateau on foot or by horse.\nWhile the Bergensbanen initially winds its way out of Oslo through a more densely populated region, you can see numerous small abandoned railway stations. In the upper Hallingdal valley, the route becomes increasingly remote, even though it passes through numerous settlements and towns. From Geilo, the line climbs up onto the high plateau of the Hardangervidda, where the most scenic section of the Bergensbanen begins. During the approximately 2-hour journey from Geilo to Voss, which includes the trip through the most pristine and remote region, travellers should focus in particular on the section from Haugast\u00f8l to Finse. Here, the view is largely unobstructed by tunnels and snow galleries, giving a true impression of the expanse and rugged nature of this subpolar region.\nPrepare.\nTickets.\nFor travel on regional trains operated by Vy (formerly NSB) along the Oslo\u2013Bergen route, an advance (free) seat reservation is mandatory and is purchased alongside your ticket. If you decide to travel on the spur of the moment, you can ask the conductor if space is available, which is usually straightforward, though trains frequently sell out during the peak tourist season. Book tickets in advance online or by phone. When booking online, you can select your own seat using a carriage map showing availability.\nDiscounted MiniPris tickets become available up to 90 days before departure. Prices start at 249 kr and can rise relatively quickly depending on demand. Standard tickets are significantly more expensive, starting around 829 kr (prices as of April 2014). Standard tickets can be cancelled right up until departure, whereas MiniPris tickets are non-refundable. Upgrading to the Comfort section costs an extra 90 kr on top of the ticket price, and on night trains, you can book a sleeping compartment (\"sove\").\nChildren aged 5 to 15 travel free of charge on a parent's standard ticket, but require their own ticket when travelling on a MiniPris fare, which can sometimes negate the price advantage. Children aged 0 to 4 travel free in all cases. If you are travelling with young children, it is recommended to reserve a seat in the family carriage (\"familievogn\") during booking.\nBringing a bicycle on the Bergensbanen must be booked in advance. During the peak tourist season, you should do this well ahead of time, as bike spaces on some trains are strictly limited. A bicycle ticket on the Bergensbanen costs 179 kr.\nInterrail.\nInterrail passes and Scandinavia Railpass are valid on the Bergensbanen, but seat reservations (usually about 100 kr or \u20ac9) are mandatory year-round. They cannot be booked online; you must either call Entur Customer Service (\u260f) or visit a staffed station. See vy.no for detailed information.\nInterrail passes are \"not\" valid on the Fl\u00e5msbana (Flam Railway), branching out from Myrdal, but Interrail passholders do get a 30% discount, provided you have an active travel day. Tickets can be purchased on entur.no. As per the Interrail website, prices also vary by season: the train costs 245 kr in Oct\u2013Dec, 260 kr in Jan\u2013Apr and 315 from May\u2013Sept.\nGet in.\nIf you want to travel the entire route, the journey begins either in Oslo at Oslo Sentral station or in Bergen. Both cities have international airports and are reachable by various ferry lines. See the Get in sections of both articles for detailed information on getting to the main stations.\nFor shorter sections of the journey, Geilo and Voss are also good starting points, especially if you want to travel only the route across the Hardangervidda Plateau. \nThe one-way trip between Geilo and Voss takes about 2 hours, while a one-way trip from Oslo to Bergen takes 6 to 7 hours, depending on the service. Both directions offer 4\u20135 daily connections; one is usually a night train.\nGo.\nStations.\nThe Bergensbanen has 21 stations along its route. Some are suburban stations within Oslo, others serve reasonably-sized towns (for Norwegian standards), while some truly drop you in the middle of the countryside.\nOslo\u2013H\u00f8nefoss.\nThe first section of the route the train takes is mostly winding out of the Oslo area on the Drammensbanen (which is shared with other routes). For the first few kilometres, the route winds its way along Oslofjord and passes through largely suburban areas until Akser. After Akser, the train enters, a 10.7-kilometre-long tunnel. This specific tunnel sees over 400 trains a day, making it one of the most utilised rail tunnels in Norway. After passing the city of Drammen, the railway then makes a near-180-degree turn and heads back north along the Drammenselva to H\u00f8nefoss via Vikersund. From Vikersund, you should be able to see Norway's fifth-largest lake, around 330 metres deep.\nH\u00f8nefoss\u2013Myrdal.\nH\u00f8nefoss is officially where the Bergensbanen track begins. The route climbs up the mountains, passing by and. The scenery here starts to shift from a typical Boreal/tundra forest to rugged high-plateau mountains, which you will notice all the more past \u00c5l.\nThe route from Haugast\u00f8l to Myrdal is the most spectacular section of the Bergensbanen as it crosses the northern. For the best views of the landscape, sit on the left-hand side when travelling towards Myrdal (or the right-hand side if you're travelling eastbound).\nLeaving Haugast\u00f8l, the railway parts ways with Route 7 (which mostly runs alongside it up to this point) and ventures fully into the high plateau. Here, heavy snowfall and particularly snowdrifts can severely disrupt travel in winter. Summer travellers can see the extensive protective measures put in place, including wooden avalanche and snowdrift deflectors, as well as numerous snow sheds\u2014partial or complete tunnels made of wood and aluminium enclosing the tracks. This section offers the best views of the mountain slopes and peaks (which are partially or fully snow-covered year-round), as well as the lakes and rivers crisscrossing the region. In summer, you can also easily spot the path of the Rallarvegen and the many cyclists riding it. Along this stretch, the cycle path runs right next to or at least within sight of the Bergen Railway.\nAfter Finse, the route enters the 10-km-long, meaning passengers miss out on the dramatic scenery around Fagernuten and Fagervatnet. About half of the 12-minute journey to Hallingskeid is spent inside the tunnel, and little can be seen on the subsequent stretch either due to the many snow sheds.\nEven after Hallingskeid, several avalanche sheds block unobstructed views, yet you still get lovely and spectacular vistas\u2014including of Nedre Gr\u00f8ndalsvatnet and Klevavatnet lying below the railway, accompanied by the Rallarvegen. Just before Myrdal, you can see the Reinungavatnet down below.\nAt the small station of Myrdal, travellers heading to Fl\u00e5m, the Aurlandsfjord, or the Sognefjord region can transfer to the Fl\u00e5m Railway (Fl\u00e5msbana), which overcomes a vertical drop of a good 860 m over a mere 20 km stretch down through the Fl\u00e5m Valley.\nMyrdal\u2013Bergen.\nBetween Myrdal and Voss, the Bergensbanen passes through, a 5.3-km-long tunnel, and starts to descend to sea again; it is very picturesque, though this can be said for everything in Norway. \nAfter Voss (Vossevangen), the Bergensbanen drops significantly closer to sea level. Between Voss and Arna, the railway parallels the E16 highway alongside Seimsvatnet (though with more tunnelled sections than the road). \nThe final section of the Bergensbanen past Arna uses the 7.67-km-long (Ulriken Tunnel) and is also one of the only sections of the route where the train reaches speeds of 160 km/h. The tunnel was previously single-track when it opened in 1964, but was later duplicated in the late 2010s, with the twin tunnel opening in 2020.\nOnce out of the tunnel, within minutes, you will have reached the terminus station, Bergen.\nEat and drink.\nAll Vy services, including the Bergensbanen, have a dedicated caf\u00e9 car. The specific English menu can be found on Vy's website. Beer, cider and wine are also sold in the caf\u00e9 car.\nIf you're taking the night service, the caf\u00e9 will be open all night, but be aware that the caf\u00e9 closes 30 minutes before arrival into Oslo or Bergen.\nSleep.\nIf you're taking the once-daily sleeper train, you have four options, with the cheapest usually starting at about 400 kr. \nThe cheapest tickets are standard seats, as you would get on a day train. If you're on a budget, this will be the cheapest option and is usually half the price of day trains. However, the seats barely recline; only choose this if you really do not care how much sleep you will get.\nIf you want to pay an extra ~500 kr, you can alternatively opt for a lie-flat seat, which is \"far\" more comfortable than the standard seats. You'll get a blanket provided. These are particularly great if you're solo travelling, as you don't have to pay the price of two. \nFor a private sleeper berth with 2 beds, you can pay an extra ~1300 kr. All compartments come with a washbasin.\nIf you're travelling as a group of up to 6 people, you can also get an entire compartment for around 5000 kr. However, you need 6 people for this to be worth the price."}
{"id": "221148", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221148", "title": "Kasibu", "text": "Kasibu is a town in Nueva Vizcaya, Philippines. It is the province's \"citrus capital\" due to its extensive production of the fruit, and also has multiple natural attractions and indigenous tribes coexisting uneasily with one of the largest gold mining operations in the country.\nUnderstand.\nFor much of its history, Kasibu was a small village inhabited by Ilongot (now known as Bugkalot) tribes along the headwaters of the Magat River along the Namamparang mountains separating Nueva Vizcaya from Quirino province. Since the 20th century, it has been one of the country's leading producers of oranges and other citrus fruits on account of its mild temperature, fertile soil and elevation.\nKasibu is an ethnically mixed town inhabited by Ilocano migrants, Bugkalot tribespeople and Igorots relocated there following the damming of several rivers along the Luzon Cordilleras in the 1970s. Its main economic activities are agriculture, ecotourism, and controversially, the extraction of gold and other precious minerals in Barangay Didipio by the Australian company OceanaGold since 1994, a topic that arouses intense debates in Nueva Vizcaya.\nGet in.\nKasibu is bypassed by the Maharlika Highway, the main thoroughfare in Nueva Vizcaya. Instead, it is connected to the road through a two-lane provincial roads linking the area from Bambang. The only forms of public transportation heading to town are jeepneys and tricycles. There are also rudimentary road connections from Solano via Quezon municipality, and Cabarroguis, the provincial capital of Quirino.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the most common transport between barangays, but due to uneven terrain, an SUV or any 4WD is recommended.\nBuy.\nKasibu is where one can buy citrus fruits directly from farms before their prices get jacked up in the market due to transportation costs. Some farms also offer direct harvesting to visitors. Most citrus farms are concentrated in the Malabing Valley, east of downtown Kasibu and in the same area as Capisaan Cave.\nAnother souvenir from Kasibu is \"mun-abol\" textiles made by indigenous weavers.\nEat.\nApart from citrus, local delicacies include Igorot dishes such as \"piniwak\" (chicken stew) and \"binakle\" (glutinous rice cake with brown sugar, coconut milk and ground peanuts).\nDrink.\nKasibu is a major producer of coffee in the region, marketed under the Kasibrew brand.\nSleep.\nThere are a few homestays in town.\nStay safe.\nParts of Kasibu along the Namamparang Range are vulnerable to landslides, especially during the rainy season. There may also be a few New People's Army rebels hiding in the those areas.\nRespect.\nThe presence of a gold mine operated by OceanaGold in Barangay Didipio has been a divisive environmental and political issue in Nueva Vizcaya. Caution is advised when discussing the topic.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3703."}
{"id": "221150", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221150", "title": "Dingalan", "text": "Dingalan (pronounced as \"ding-galan\") is a municipality on the Pacific coast of Aurora province in the central part of the island of Luzon in the Philippines. Cut off from the rest of the country by the Sierra Madre mountains, it is one of the least explored areas of the province, but is being dubbed as the \"Batanes of the East\" due to similarities in their natural setting.\nUnderstand.\nDingalan sits on a gap in the Sierra Madre mountains formed by the Dingalan River, which then leads to Dingalan Bay, an extension of the Pacific Ocean. It forms the southern half of Aurora and shares boundaries with the provinces of Nueva Ecija, Bulacan and Quezon, but is separated from the rest of Aurora by the Mingan Range, a subsection of the Sierra Madre.\nThe people of Dingalan are a mix of indigenous Dumagats and migrants from Central Luzon, Quezon Province and the Ilocos. From the logging heydays of the 1970s, they are now primarily engaged in farming, fishing and tourism. They speak a variety of languages including Tagalog, Ilocano and Dumagat.\nGet in.\nThe only way of entry to Dingalan is through a highway that goes through the narrow pass from the neighboring town of Gabaldon in Nueva Ecija province. From Metro Manila, take the Nueva Ecija-Aurora Road at Cabanatuan and then Palayan-Gabaldon-Dingalan Road. Since the 2020s, construction has been ongoing to connect the town with the provincial capital Baler to the north, and to the town of General Nakar in Quezon province to the south.\nThere are limited once-a-day bus services from Metro Manila, while a more frequent option is to disembark in Cabanatuan and ride another bus or passenger van heading to Dingalan. Travel time from Metro Manila is between 5-6 hours, while it takes 2 hours to drive from Cabanatuan. Travelers from northern Luzon can also take a passenger van at San Jose in northern Nueva Ecija, with a 2-hour travel time.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transport in the downtown. Due to the uneven terrain and poor roads, a 4WD is recommended. Some attractions are only accessible by hired boat from Dingalan Port.\nStay safe.\nCrime is minimal in town, as the few residents seem to know each other and there are no escape routes for offenders to escape apart from the road back to Gabaldon. \nStay healthy.\nAvoid getting into a major medical emergency during your stay at all costs, as facilities and resources at the town's community hospital are limited. The nearest standard hospitals are in Cabanatuan in Nueva Ecija.\nRespect.\nThe indigenous Dumagat communities still adhere to their precolonial customs and belief systems and are widely respected even by the non-Dumagat resident population. You are expected to exercise maximum respect for their traditions and when visiting them.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3207.\nCellphone and internet signals can be quite patchy."}
{"id": "221152", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221152", "title": "Coutts", "text": "Coutts is a town on the Alberta side of the Sweetgrass\u2013Coutts Border Crossing connecting to Sweet Grass, Montana on the US side. It connects Highway 4 in Canada to Interstate 15 in the United States, an important trade route along the CANAMEX Corridor.\nUnderstand.\nCoutts, in Warner County, Alberta, is one of the busiest ports of entry on the Canada\u2013United States border in western Canada. The 2021 population was 224.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe Sweetgrass-Coutts border crossing is where Interstate 15 meets Alberta Highway 4. \nThe border crossing is open 24/7."}
