{"id": "221181", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221181", "title": "Suyo", "text": "Suyo is a municipality in Ilocos Sur.\nUnderstand.\nSuyo is a landlocked municipality established along a tributary of the Amburayan River. It is a highland town in the Cordillera mountains and has significant Igorot communities. Formerly part of the Amburayan subdistrict of Mountain Province, it was annexed by Ilocos Sur in 1920. The area saw heavy fighting in World War II during the Battle of Bessang Pass in 1945, when Allied forces struggled to break through Japanese defenses in Bessang Ridge on their way to liberate Baguio and the Luzon Cordilleras.\nThe main economic activities are agriculture and nature-based tourism, through poor road infrastructure tends to keep most visitors at bay.\nGet in.\nSuyo is along Tagudin-Cervantes-Sabangan Road running between Mountain Province and Bitalag Junction intersecting with Manila North Road in the coastal town of Tagudin. It features scenic mountain drives through the Bessang Pass and frightening hairpin curves. A junction beside Urzadan Bridge leads north towards the municipality of Sigay.\nThere are passenger vans connecting Cervantes with Tagudin passing through Suyo. Another option is to take tricycles from Bitalag Junction.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation within town, but due to the uneven terrain, a 4WD is recommended. Most tourist attractions are only accessible through extended hikes.\nSleep.\nThere are a few homestays and inns in town.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nMedical clearance is required to climb the area's mountains due to their extreme terrain.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2715."}
{"id": "221182", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221182", "title": "Sugpon", "text": "Sugpon is a municipality in Ilocos Sur.\nUnderstand.\nSugpon is a landlocked municipality established along the upper reaches of the Amburayan River. It is the southernmost municipality of Ilocos Sur and appears on maps as a wedge between the provinces of La Union and Benguet. It is a highland town in the Cordillera mountains and has significant Igorot communities. Formerly part of the Amburayan subdistrict of Mountain Province, it was annexed by Ilocos Sur in 1920. The area saw heavy fighting in World War II during the Battle of Bessang Pass in 1945, when Allied forces struggled to break through Japanese defenses in Bessang Ridge on their way to liberate Baguio and the Luzon Cordilleras.\nThe main economic activities are agriculture and nature-based tourism, through poor road infrastructure tends to keep most visitors at bay.\nGet in.\nSugpon has no direct road connection with the rest of Ilocos Sur. Instead, travelers enter the town through a junction in Sudipen in La Union province that leads to Alilem-Sugpon-Sudipen Road. It features scenic mountain drives and vistas of the Amburayan River valley, as well as frightening hairpin curves. \nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation within town, but due to the uneven terrain, a 4WD is recommended. Most tourist attractions are only accessible through extended hikes.\nThere is a tramline in Barangay Banga.\nSleep.\nThere are a few homestays and inns in town.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2717."}
{"id": "221183", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221183", "title": "Bangar (La Union)", "text": "\"For the town in Brunei, see Bangar.\"\nBangar (pronounced \"buhng-gar\") is a town in La Union.\nUnderstand.\nBangar was founded during the Spanish era in 1700 along the southern half of the Amburayan River estuary and the South China Sea. Formerly a part of Tagudin in Ilocos Sur, it became a separate municipality in 1850 and became the northernmost town of the newly-established province of La Union that same year.\nGet in.\nBangar is along Route 2 (Manila North Road), the main thoroughfare in La Union. Buses heading to Candon, Vigan and Laoag also ply this highway. From a junction in Bangar Public Plaza, a coastal road leads to the town's beaches and Luna.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2519."}
{"id": "221184", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221184", "title": "Sasmuan", "text": "Sasmuan is a town in southwestern Pampanga, Philippines. It is a quiet fishing town with extensive wetlands bypassed by the province's major highways.\nUnderstand.\nSasmuan is known to have existed in the precolonial era, with its name having been derived from a Kapampangan term for a meeting place of the ancient rulers known as \"datus\". During the Spanish occupation, its named was written as \"Sexmoan\", with the spelling blamed on the colonizers\u2019 inability to pronounce the native name. During the American period, the town was a subject of ridicule by English speakers, particularly US soldiers stationed in Clark, for the sexual connotations inadvertently created when the town was renamed, until in 1994, the prudish locals decided to change the name back to its indigenous spelling. \nBypassed by major highways and notwithstanding damage caused by the Mount Pinatubo eruption in 1991, Sasmuan is a quiet fishing community sustained by the extensive wetlands of the Pampanga and Pasig-Potrero River deltas. But since the 21st century, a combination of factors such as climate change, rising sea levels, typhoons, high tide, improper land conversion and excessive groundwater extraction have irreversibly submerged numerous barangays and triggered ground subsidence across town, resulting in an increase of abandoned homes and edifices surrounded by brackish estuarine water. Looking at maps of Sasmuan, one could be forgiven to think that the town eerily resembles Venice, with thinly-stretched settlements gathered around spider web-like roads that double as dikes and fishpond fences stretching southwards into Manila Bay, where many barangays are in danger of disappearing altogether.\nGet in.\nSasmuan is bypassed by the major highways of Pampanga. From Manila, take the San Fernando exit along the North Luzon Expressway (NLEX) leading to the Gapan-Olongapo Road, also known as Jose Abad Santos Avenue (JASA). From San Fernando, go west towards Guagua or Lubao, where both towns have roads connecting to Sasmuan, with the one from Lubao doubling as a dike.\nFrom Lubao and Guagua, there are tricycles servicing downtown Sasmuan. Fares from Guagua cost between P70-80, while Lubao is cheaper due to its shorter distance.\nGet around.\nDowntown Sasmuan is compact and easily accessible on foot. Tricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays.\nWhile downtown Sasmuan is accessible by motor vehicle, a trip to the barangays and wetlands further south requires a boat.\nEat.\nA local delicacy is \"polvoron\", a crumbly shortbread made flour, sugar, milk, nuts, and sometimes rice crispies.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in Guagua and Lubao.\nStay safe.\nA low-lying town, Sasmuan is regularly subjected to flooding from the Pampanga River system during the rainy season, which is exacerbated by typhoons and high tide from Manila Bay.\nStay healthy.\nDue to chronic flooding, medication against leptospirosis (a disease carried by rat urine) is necessary after wading into floodwaters.\nRespect.\nBe respectful when discussing the chronic flooding situation, as many residents have had enough from the long-term disruptions and displacement. Since 2025, protests have erupted in town after it emerged that public funds meant for the construction of dikes and flood control measures have been embezzled by corrupt politicians, engineers and contractors.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2004."}
{"id": "221195", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221195", "title": "Bamban", "text": "\"Not to be confused with Bambang, Nueva Vizcaya.\"\nBamban is a town in southern Tarlac.\nUnderstand.\nSituated on the eastern slopes of the Zambales mountain range, Bamban used to be part of Pampanga province, resulting in a significant number of Kapampangan speakers and Aeta tribes people in town. In 1874, it was transferred to the province of Tarlac. \nA primarily agrarian town, Bamban also became a satellite town of Angeles City following the expansion of the US military presence at Clark Field during the 20th century. The town was rebuilt following the 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo and its subsequent lahar flows. Its western areas are gradually being redeveloped into commercial centers as part of urbanization from neighboring Angeles and Mabalacat in Pampanga.\nIn 2024, Bamban gained international notoriety after it was found that its mayor, Alice Guo, was actually a Chinese national who faked her Filipino citizenship documents and was ultimately imprisoned on criminal charges following a raid on a cybercrime hub at the very back of the municipal hall whose operations she approved as mayor.\nGet in.\nFrom Manila, take the Concepcion or Clark North exit of the Subic-Clark-Tarlac Expressway (SCTEX). A toll-free option is to take the MacArthur Highway which connects Central Luzon with the Ilocos Region and passes right through Bamban. This is served by buses running from Metro Manila to Tarlac City and Pangasinan.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in Clark in Pampanga.\nRespect.\nAmid the controversy over Alice Guo, many residents would rather prefer their town be known for something else rather than her, the cybercrime hub, and jokes from outsiders about them electing a Chinese \u201cspy\u201d. At the same time, many of them look fondly upon Guo for bringing about a change from the regular personalities that have governed the town, while many of her allies in local politics remain in office.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3217."}
{"id": "221199", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221199", "title": "Alfonso Casta\u00f1eda", "text": "Alfonso Casta\u00f1eda is a town in southern Nueva Vizcaya, Philippines. Situated at the headwaters of the Cagayan and Pampanga Rivers and the junction of two mountain ranges, it forms the lever in the pan-shaped province and has multiple natural attractions and indigenous tribes.\nUnderstand.\nAlfonso Casta\u00f1eda occupies the southernmost edge of the Nueva Vizcaya panhandle and forms the province's boundary with Aurora, Quirino and Nueva Ecija provinces. For much of its history, it was a set of settlements inhabited by Ilongot (now known as Bugkalot) tribes along the headwaters of the Cagayan River at the junction of the Caraballo and Sierra Madre mountain ranges. It frequently switched ownership between Dupax in Nueva Vizcaya and Pantabangan in Nueva Ecija until it was finally made its own municipality in 1979 under Nueva Vizcaya and named after a local politician.\nAlfonso Casta\u00f1eda is an ethnically mixed town inhabited by Ilocano and Tagalog migrants and Bugkalot and Isinay tribespeople. Its main economic activities are agriculture, a burgeoning ecotourism sector and revenues from the hydropower projects built to harness its position on the watershed of the Cagayan and Pampanga Rivers.\nno population data for this page, making it the least populated municipality of Nueva Vizcaya.\nGet in.\nAlfonso Casta\u00f1eda is the most isolated municipality of Nueva Vizcaya. It has no direct connections with the rest of the province, forcing travelers to make a lengthy detour through Carranglan, San Jose and Rizal in Nueva Ecija before taking along Pantabangan-Baler Road connecting Nueva Ecija with Aurora, passing through Alfonso Casta\u00f1eda. The only forms of public transportation heading to town are a few jeepneys and tricycles. \nGet around.\nTricycles are the most common transport between barangays, but due to uneven terrain, an SUV or any 4WD is recommended.\nStay safe.\nThe highland areas of town are vulnerable to landslides, especially during the rainy season. There may also be a few New People's Army rebels hiding in the those areas.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3703."}
{"id": "221205", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221205", "title": "Bongabon", "text": "Bongabon is a municipality in Nueva Ecija. Situated on the slopes of the Sierra Madre, it forms one of the gateways to neigboring Aurora province.\nUnderstand.\nFormerly known as Santol or Santor, Bongabon was founded by the Spanish in 1659 as an outpost of Pantabangan to subjugate the indigenous tribes in the Sierra Madre mountains. It became a separate municipality in 1760. It is a largely quiet municipality that suffered some the occasional peasant revolt and guerrilla warfare during World War II, the Huk Rebellion and the New People's Army insurgency during the 20th century. \nBongabon's main lifeline is agriculture, particularly its production of onions that have made it the \"Onion Capital of Nueva Ecija\". There is also a growing ecotourism sector seeking advantage of its position on the watershed of the Pampanga River. Bongabon's mountains are also included in the Do\u00f1a Aurora Memorial National Park, a 5676 ha protected area straddling the boundaries of Nueva Ecija and Aurora that is home to some endemic species such as the Philippine eagle, the country's national bird.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nBongabon is the western terminus of the Nueva Ecija-Aurora Road, the main thoroughfare between Nueva Ecija and Baler, the capital of Aurora province. It passes through the Sierra Madre and features hairpin turns and forested areas. From Bongabon, the road is directly connected by highways to the major cities of San Jose and Cabanatuan.\nThere are passenger vans and buses passing through Bongabon on their way between Baler and Cabanatuan, the largest city in Nueva Ecija.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays.\nStay safe.\nThere may be a few New People's Army rebels hiding in the mountains.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3128."}
{"id": "221206", "revid": "2440688", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221206", "title": "Taean", "text": "Taean County (\ud0dc\uc548\uad70, Taean-gun) is a coastal county in Chungcheongnam-do (South Chungcheong Province), South Korea, known for its beaches, coastal sand dunes, and its location within Taeanhaean National Park.\nUnderstand.\nTaean sits on a peninsula on the west coast of South Korea and is largely surrounded by the sea. The county is best known for its many beaches and its role as part of Taeanhaean National Park, which stretches along 230\u00a0km of coastline and includes 26 beaches, sand dunes, and 72 islands.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nIntercity buses connect Taean Bus Terminal with Seoul, Daejeon, and other major cities.\nBy car.\nTaean is reachable by car from Seoul via the Seohaean Expressway, a drive of roughly 2 hours.\nGet around.\nA car or rental car is recommended, as many beaches and attractions are spread out along the coastline. Local buses connect the county seat with the main beach areas but run infrequently."}
{"id": "221211", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221211", "title": "Santo Domingo (Ilocos Sur)", "text": "Santo Domingo is a coastal town in Ilocos Sur. \nUnderstand.\nSanto Domingo was founded by the Spanish in 1742.\nGet in.\nSanto Domingo is along Route 2 (Manila North Road). Buses heading to Laoag also ply this highway.\nThe nearest cruise ship terminal is in Salomague Port in Cabugao, 24 km to the north.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town, particularly as downtown Santo Domingo is further inland from the coast.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2729."}
{"id": "221213", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221213", "title": "Aringay", "text": "Aringay (pronounced \"uh-ring-guy\") is a town in La Union.\nUnderstand.\nAringay is one of the oldest settlements in La Union, having existed in the precolonial era as a coastal trading post for Cordilleran traders selling their gold at the mouth of the Aringay River. It was conquered by the Spanish in 1572 and became a municipality in 1741 after it was separated from Agoo to the south. The town was rebuilt after World War II, when it was the site of amphibious landings along Lingayen Gulf by Japanese and then American forces in the battle to control Luzon.\nGet in.\nAringay is along Route 2 (Manila North Road), the main thoroughfare in La Union. Buses heading to San Fernando and other locations further north in the Ilocos Region also ply this highway.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town.\nEat.\nAringay is a leading producer of milkfish (\"bangus\"), with many fish farms along the Aringay River and its estuary.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2503."}
{"id": "221220", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221220", "title": "La Michil\u00eda Biosphere Reserve", "text": "La Michil\u00eda Biosphere Reserve is a protected natural area in the Sierra Madre Occidental mountains in the\nstate of Durango in Northern Mexico.\nUnderstand.\nHuman incursions into the once wild and inaccessible area have caused the extinction of large mammals in the area. An important\npart of La Michilia's mission is the preservation of species and the re-introduction of animals that have become extinct in\nthe area due to human activity. The park's operates a facility to breed endangered animals and re-introduce them to their natural\nhabitats. They have shown particularly good results breeding lobos (Mexican gray wolf) and black bears.\nTourist information.\nOfficial information is available from CONANP or UNESCO.\nHistory.\nThe reserve began as a biological research project in the early 1970s. In 1977 it was declared a Mexican Biosphere Reserve. Two years later it was declared a UNESCO Biosphere Reserve.\nThe reserve is administered for the federal government by the \"Comisi\u00f3n Nacional de \u00c1reas Naturales Protegidas\" (CONANP), the agency that manages many of Mexico's parks and natural resources.\nLandscape.\nThe park's mountainous landscape is blanketed in green forests dominated by pine-oak forests. Higher elevations\nhave evergreen species including cypress, fir and juniper. The forests lie at an elevation between 2,000 meters and\n2,800 meters at the peaks. Two separate sub-ranges sandwich a lower-lying valley of rolling hills and deep ravines.\nThe sub-ranges are called the \"Sierra Urica\" and the \"Sierra Michis\".\nDurango is known for its vast arid deserts, but La Michilia is an exception: it gets a fair amount of precipitation and acts\nas a water catchment area to replenish local aquifers. The reserve includes two rivers (Rio Laurel and Rio Temascal), along with\nseveral smaller screens and the occasional lagoon or marshy meadow.\nFlora and fauna.\nUNESCO says that more than 770 vascular plants have been identified in the reserve. Conifer forests (that's \"pine\" in human English) dominate\nat higher elevations, giving way to mixed pine and oak at mid elevations, and various grasses and smaller shrubs in the lowland\nvalleys and marshes.\nMammals include white-tail deer as well as pumas, coyotes, pronghorn antelope and a number of smaller woodland mammals like\nrabbits and squirrels.\nBird watchers will find a variety of mountain birds like the golden eagle. Most oprevalent is a large\npopulation of wild turkeys. Also expect to find woodpeckers, nuthatches and vultures. Two particularly notable avian residents\nare the thick-billed parrot and the military macaw. Both of these are are unique members of the parrot family that you are\nunlikely to spot elsewhere.\nThe reserve was once the home of the world's largest woodpecker species, the Imperial Woodpecker, but there have been no\nconfirmed sightings in several decades and the species is likely extinct.\nClimate.\nThe park is cooler than normal for Durango with a temperate climate that is typically humid. During the summer months\nof June through October, afternoon showers or thunderstorms are common. Winters have cold nights and the occasional\nsnowfall is likely.\nGet in.\nThe reserve is in a remote, relatively unpopulated area so there is no public transportation to the park. The best option\nfor most travelers is to rent a 4WD truck when you land at the Durango International Airport (Aeropuerto Internacional General\nGuadalupe Victoria). It is about 160 km from Durango to the park. The best road is via Suichil (52 km from the reserve) or\nVicente Guerrero (55 km from the reserve). Once you get past these towns the road is winding mountain roads and dirt trails ascending\ninto the mountains.\nBy car.\nAs you exit the airport, drive west on federal highway MEX-40 for about 15 minutes, then take the exit heading southeast on\nfederal highway MEX-45 towards Vicente Guerrero. There are three main entrances to the reserve in the towns of\nVicente Guerrero, Suchil or Mezquital.\nFees and permits.\nCONANP does not charge entry fees for this reserve.\nGet around.\nDirt roads lead into the reserve, but once there, the only way to get around is by walking. Established trails lead to points of interest and are best navigated with the services of a local guide. Guides can be hired in the towns on the edge of the reserve.\nSleep.\nBackcountry.\nNo formal campgrounds are in the reserve but backcountry camping is allowed. Everything you need must be packed in\nand strict Leave No Trace protocols must be observed including packing out all garbage."}
{"id": "221221", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221221", "title": "Tlalnepantla", "text": "Tlalnepantla is a suburb of Mexico City about 10 km east of the Centro Historico on the edge of Mexico State.\nToday, it is an industrial and residential area visited by few foreign tourists, though it does have important historical sites that a serious student of Mexican history and archaeology will want to explore.\nUnderstand.\nTlalnepantla (occasionally called \"Tlalnepantla de Baz\" by pompous local officials and hair-splitting Wikipedia editors) but foreigners usually refer to it simply as \"Tlalnepantla\". Chilangos usually call if \"Tlalne\" and hip younger residents call it \"Portlan\". Whatever you call it, know that it was settled before 1000 AD by Mesoamerican cultures first, by the Amaquemecan, then later by other cultures including the Toltec and the\nChichimeca. The archaeological site of Tenayuca was the largest capital city of the Chichimec people. It was built around\n1100 AD.\nDuring the colonial era, Spanish conquistadors conquered Mesoamerican cultures across Mexico. In Tlalnepantal,\nchange came in 1550 when Franciscan missionaries moved in and unslaved indigenous people to dismantle the historic structures of Tenayuca and\nuse the stones to build their Eurocentric settlement. Over the next two centuries they would expand their economic power by building\nhaciendas as agricultural centers. During the 20th century, the Tlalnepantla became heavily\nindustrialized and the population shifted from an indigenous agrarian population to a predominantly working class mestizo population of\nblue-collar factory workers.\nGet in.\nFrom downtown Mexico City use Metro or Metrobus to Buenavista. Inside the Buenavista train terminal you can purchase a farecard\nfor the Tren Suburbano (Metro farecards are not valid on Tren Suburbano). Board the train going toward \"Cuautitl\u00e1n\". Get off the\ntrain at Tlalnepantla.\nBe aware that the evening rush hour (\"hora pica\") starts at about 17:00 and doesn't die down until after 20:00. Taxis and Uber rides can become quite expensive and easily take twice as long as using Tren Suburbano.\nGet around.\nTlalnepantla is a large municipality where walking is impractical and can be unsafe. Uber and Didi are the best way to get around, but be\nsure to use specific destination names and addresses so you are not left having to walk through unfamiliar areas.\nStay Safe.\nTlalnepantla is not a touristy area. It is an industrial and working-class urban residential area. The tourist sites are generally safe\nduring the day, but travelers are advised not to stray into unfamiliar neighborhoods. It is best to be discreet and avoid flashy or extravagant\nclothing. Use ride-share apps like Uber or Didi to get around and avoid hailing taxis or using \"peseros\" (small local buses)."}
{"id": "221226", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221226", "title": "Ivana", "text": "Ivana is a municipality of Batanes province in the northern Philippines. \nUnderstand.\nIvana is in the southwestern part of Batan Island, the main island in the Batanes archipelago. It is the main port of entry leading to neighboring Sabtang island. With a population of only 1,368 people as of 2024, it is the second least populated municipality in the Philippines; only Kalayaan in Palawan is less populous.\nGet in.\nThe best time to visit is during the dry season between March and June, when the winds and waves are calm. Otherwise, expect frequent cancellations at the last minute.\nIvana is connected to the rest of Batan Island through a scenic highway system, with jeepneys and tricycles running between the town and the provincial capital Basco.\nGet around.\nThere are not much cars or heavy vehicles due to high costs, logistical difficulties when it comes to importation, and short distances. Motorcycles and tricycles are more common. \nBuy.\nAll transactions are in cash due to the absence of banks and ATMs.\nSleep.\nThere are homestays and inns in town.\nStay safe.\nLike in the rest of Batanes, crime is abysmally low, and the residents know each other. \nStay healthy.\nTry not to get into a serious medical emergency, as there is no hospital and you will need to be evacuated to Basco or worse, to mainland Luzon due to a lack of medicines and other resources.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3902. \nLike in the rest of Batanes, electricity is on a rotational basis, with generators and solar panels being the norm. Cellphone and internet signals are often sketchy, although things are improving with the proliferation of Starlink terminals, while the reception from radio stations in Taiwan can be clearer than the domestic ones."}
{"id": "221227", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221227", "title": "Uyugan", "text": "Uyugan is a municipality of Batanes province in the northern Philippines. \nUnderstand.\nUyugan is in the southeastern part of Batan Island, the main island in the Batanes archipelago. Being the nearest place to Luzon, it became the first place in Batanes to be conquered by the Spanish in the 17th and 18th centuries.\nno population data for this page\nGet in.\nThe best time to visit is during the dry season between March and June, when the winds and waves are calm. Otherwise, expect frequent cancellations at the last minute.\nUyugan is connected to the rest of Batan Island through a scenic highway system, with jeepneys and tricycles running between the town and the provincial capital Basco.\nGet around.\nThere are not much cars or heavy vehicles due to high costs, logistical difficulties when it comes to importation, and short distances. Motorcycles and tricycles are more common. \nBuy.\nAll transactions are in cash due to the absence of banks and ATMs.\nSleep.\nThere are homestays and inns in town.\nStay safe.\nLike in the rest of Batanes, crime is abysmally low, and the residents know each other. \nStay healthy.\nTry not to get into a serious medical emergency, as there is no hospital and you will need to be evacuated to Basco or worse, to mainland Luzon due to a lack of medicines and other resources.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3903. \nLike in the rest of Batanes, electricity is on a rotational basis, with generators and solar panels being the norm. Cellphone and internet signals are often sketchy, although things are improving with the proliferation of Starlink terminals, while the reception from radio stations in Taiwan can be clearer than the domestic ones."}
{"id": "221232", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221232", "title": "Santol", "text": "Santol is a town in La Union. \nUnderstand.\nNamed after the local term for \"Sandoricum koetjape\", a fruit-bearing tree, Santol is a landlocked municipality established at the foothills of the Luzon Cordilleras. Its area extends through down to the Amburayan River bordering the highland town of Sugpon in Ilocos Sur and Benguet, and is home to significant Igorot communities. Formerly a part of the Amburayan subdistrict of Mountain Province, it was annexed by La Union in 1920.\nGet in.\nSantol is bypassed by Manila North Road (Route 2), the main thoroughfare in La Union. Instead, it is connected to the highway by a road leading to the neighboring coastal municipality of Balaoan.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town. However, some of the mountain areas can only be reached by either 4WD or hired motorcycle.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in Balaoan.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2505."}
{"id": "221234", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221234", "title": "Burgos (La Union)", "text": " \"For other cities by the name, see Burgos (disambiguation).\"\nBurgos is a town in La Union. \nUnderstand.\nBurgos was originally an Igorot settlement in the Luzon Cordilleras known as Disdis that was administered from the town of Naguilian. Formerly a part of Benguet province, it was annexed by La Union in 1920. In 1925, it was renamed Burgos in honor of Filipino nationalist priest Jose Burgos, who was said to have taken refuge in town while fleeing from his Spanish persecutors despite lack of historical evidence to prove this.\nGet in.\nBurgos is along Naguilian Road (Quirino Highway, Route 54), which connects the coastal town of Bauang with Baguio and its the main thoroughfare to the latter city for those coming from the Ilocos provinces. Buses also ply this highway.\nGet around.\nTricycles are the main form of transportation in town. However, some of the mountain areas can only be reached by either 4WD or hired motorcycle.\nBuy.\nThe town is known for making brooms made from tiger grass that are exported mostly to the tourist markets of Baguio.\nSleep.\nThe nearest accommodations are in Bauang.\nStay safe.\nLandslides are common during the monsoon season from June to October. During this time, authorities suspend tourism activities.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2510."}
{"id": "221262", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221262", "title": "San Luis (Pampanga)", "text": "\"For other places by the name, see San Luis.\"\nSan Luis is a town in Pampanga, Philippines. It is a quiet agricultural town bypassed by the province's major highways.\nUnderstand.\nFormerly known as San Nicolas de Cabagsac, San Luis was established along a bend of the Pampanga River and the southern edge of the Candaba Swamp in 1742. It was renamed and rededicated in honor of Saint Aloysius Gonzaga. It is a generally quiet town reliant on agriculture, particularly rice farming, with a few upheavals throughout its history such as peasant revolts, World War II guerrillas and the Huk Rebellion, led by its native son Luis Taruc.\nGet in.\nSan Luis is bypassed by the major highways of Pampanga. From Manila, take the San Fernando exit along the North Luzon Expressway (NLEX) leading to the Gapan-Olongapo Road, also known as Jose Abad Santos Avenue (JASA). From NLEX, go east towards Mexico, then take the Santo Domingo Circle with signage pointing to San Luis. A road built over the Arnedo Dike also connects San Luis to other towns of eastern Pampanga such as San Simon and its eponymous exit along NLEX, Apalit, Candaba and Arayat. A secondary road connects the city of Baliwag in Bulacan province. \nFrom Robinsons Starmills in the provincial capital San Fernando, there is a jeepney heading to San Luis, but as these run in irregular intervals due to the practice of waiting for the vehicle to fill up at the terminal, many commuters take jeepneys heading to Arayat and then disembark at Santo Domingo Circle before taking a tricycle to San Luis instead. A one-way solo trip by trike costs P90.\nGet around.\nDowntown San Luis is compact and easily accessible on foot. Tricycles are the main form of transportation between barangays.\nEat.\nA rare delicacy that will only be offered in some peasant homes is the field rat, usually served in a stew (\"adobo\") by farmers taking revenge on the critters ravaging their rice fields. While still risky, these rats are relatively safer to eat than their urban counterparts as they live on a somewhat \"organic\" diet in the farm.\nStay safe.\nA low-lying town, San Luis is regularly subjected to flooding from the Pampanga River and the Candaba Swamp during the rainy season.\nStay healthy.\nDue to chronic flooding, medication against leptospirosis (a disease carried by rat urine) is necessary after wading into floodwaters.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 2014."}
{"id": "221265", "revid": "2442154", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221265", "title": "Gyeryong", "text": "Gyeryong (\uacc4\ub8e1\uc2dc) is a city in the southern region of South Chungcheong Province, South Korea. Known as the official military capital of South Korea, it hosts \"Gyeryongdae,\" the joint headquarters of the Republic of Korea Army, Navy, and Air Force. Nestled at the foot of the scenic Gyeryongsan National Park, Gyeryong offers a unique mix of national defense history, serene nature, and Confucian heritage.\nUnderstand.\nGyeryong is one of the newest and smallest cities in South Chungcheong Province. Historically part of Nonsan, it was officially established as an independent city in 2003 due to its specialized role as the headquarters for South Korea's armed forces.\nThe name \"Gyeryong\" literally translates to \"Rooster Dragon,\" named after the main ridge of Gyeryongsan Mountain, which local lore says resembles a dragon wearing a rooster's crest. According to traditional Korean geomancy (Feng Shui / Pungsu-jiri), the land around Gyeryong is considered exceptionally auspicious and energetically powerful. During the founding of the Joseon Dynasty in the late 14th century, King Taejo initially considered Gyeryong as the candidate site for the new national capital before ultimately choosing Hanyang (modern-day Seoul). Remnants of the original capital construction project, known as *Sindoan*, can still be found in the area.\nToday, Gyeryong is a quiet, clean, and well-organized city home to military personnel, families, and outdoor enthusiasts exploring Gyeryongsan National Park.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nGyeryong is easily accessible via the **Honam Expressway Branch Line** (Route 251) using the Gyeryong IC. It takes about 2 hours from Seoul and 20 minutes from central Daejeon.\nGet around.\nGyeryong is a compact city, making navigation straightforward:"}
{"id": "221270", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221270", "title": "Flying to India", "text": "With a lack of viable overland routes available along with the dearth of international ferry services in modern times, flying is the only realistic option to enter India from outside South Asia.\nThere are plently of flights from Asia and Europe, but no direct flights from Pakistan. Direct flights are also available from the major cities in Africa and North America, but none from South America. From Oceania, the only direct flights are from Australia.\nIn addition, Gulf carriers like Emirates, Etihad Airways and Qatar Airways offer a plethora of one-stop connections to many Indian cities from their hubs. Consider connecting via those hubs to save on ticket prices.\nDelhi ('), Mumbai (') and Bengaluru (') are India's main aviation hubs. There are many nonstop, direct and connecting choices to these cities from Europe, North America, Middle East, Southeast Asia, Africa and Australia. There are also many long-haul international flights to Kolkata ('), Hyderabad ('), Chennai (') and Ahmedabad ().\nBe sure to hold on to your boarding pass, as it will be checked on arrival in India. Some Indian airports, like Delhi, offer Wi-Fi access with a passport scan, and offer short\u2013term tourist SIM cards, but the majority offer no Wi-Fi or SIM card options.\nSee the respective city or airport article for more specific information on flights. Bear in mind that many medium-sized cities offer only a handful of international flights, especially the seasonal ones.\nUnderstand.\nBackground.\nIndia once had various overland routes to Africa, Europe and China as part of the Silk Road, which got abandoned due the rise of the Ottoman Empire and its poor relations with maritime Europe. This led to the discovery of the maritime Cape Route around Africa, replacing the overland routes to India. However, the advances in aviation in the mid-20th century led to the decline of maritime ferry services worldwide. Although the overland route from Europe to India via Persia (present-day Iran) became popular in the 1960s and 70s as the Hippie Trail, it has become increasingly challenging since the 1980s, with the 2025 closure of the India\u2013Pakistan border being the final nail in the coffin. This makes flying as a mode of transport more important than ever for India to connect to the outside world.\nIndian airlines.\nDue to bankruptcies and mergers, there are only five Indian airlines that operate international flights.\nThese airlines offer daily flights from major hubs around the world. However, most Indian airlines have a very unfriendly policy towards upgrading weight allowance and changing dates of flights, and so if you feel that you may need to change anything regarding your flight, it is best to check the company's policy before making a booking.\nAlso, different airlines have different standards for what they allow as cabin baggage, so check with your airline\u2019s website on the side of caution, especially if you are travelling by a low-cost airline. Usually, the allowed free baggage limit is 15 kg on most airlines.\nIndian-operated or Indian-flagged planes are banned from flying into Pakistani airspace and this may cause ticket prices to increase. Also, depending on the security situation in the Middle East or in Europe (which may cause airspace closures at short notice), your flight may be affected and cause delays or cancellations.\nFrom rest of Asia.\nThere are more flights to India from the rest of Asia than any other continent, mainly due to the Indian diaspora in the Middle East as well as the holiday destinations in Southeast Asia.\nNeighbouring countries.\nThere are numerous direct flights from neighbouring countries in South Asia, like Sri Lanka, Bangladesh, Bhutan, Nepal and the Maldives. However, most of them are operated by Indian airlines. Plus, there are no direct flights from Pakistan due to tensions with India.\nNeighbouring carriers flying to India are:\nEast Asia.\nCathay Pacific, the flag carrier of Hong Kong, is the only East Asian airline with connections to multiple Indian cities, like Bengaluru, Chennai, Delhi, Hyderabad and Mumbai. Other East Asian countries can use the one-stop connections from Cathay Pacitic to access more Indian destinations.\nOther East Asian carriers flying to India are:\nMiddle East.\nMost major cities have connections to the Middle East, especially the Gulf region. Additionally, regions with large Muslim populations are likely to have a connection to Jedda/Mecca either year-round or seasonal (e.g. during Hajj).\nChief Middle Eastern carriers flying to India are:\nOther airlines include:\nSoutheast Asia.\nChief Southeast Asian carriers flying to India are:\nFrom Europe.\nChief European carriers flying to India are:\nOther airlines include:\nFrom other continents.\nAlmost all American carriers prefer Delhi as their sole entry point to India, with Air Canada providing additional flights to Mumbai.\nIf you're coming from Africa, your best bet could be the direct flights to Delhi or via Gulf carriers. While it isn't difficult to get direct or one-stop flights from South Africa, Kenya, Nigeria or Egypt, it can be more challenging if you're from Malawi or Togo.\nQantas, the flag carrier of Australia, is the only Oceanian airline providing direct flights to India, with connections to Bengaluru and Delhi. So, it's better to rely on Gulf or Southeast Asian carriers.\nThere are no direct flights from South America, so be prepared for very long journeys via the Middle East, Europe or North America.\nSecurity.\nIndia\u2019s customs regime is notoriously complex, and could easily result in you facing seizure of goods, fines, deportation or imprisonment. Illegal drugs and importing or exporting significant amounts of money or jewellery may arouse suspicion from customs.\nYou must carry either a valid air ticket (electronic or printed) or valid boarding pass. Additionally you must carry an identification card (passport, OCI or Aadhaar Card) to even enter the terminal. They are checked and matched compulsorily at the entry gate by security guards. If you possess neither a printout or a soft copy, you can get a copy at the airline offices just outside the entry gate. Some airlines have started to charge for this privilege.\nIf you are bringing any new packaged items, it is a good idea to carry the invoices for them to show their value. You are also allowed to bring in 200 cigarettes or 50 cigars or 250 g of tobacco and 1L (2L for Indians) of alcohol duty-free. If you do not have anything to declare, you can go through the \"green channel\" clearly marked at various airports and generally you will not be harassed. Your bags upon arrival at all international airports in India will be screened when leaving through the green channel and red channel.\nDon't hesitate to ask someone if you are unsure about anything. Most staff in Indian airports are very helpful to passengers.\nFTI-TTP.\nFTI\u2011TTP (Fast Track Immigration \u2013 Trusted Traveller Programme)[dead link] is India\u2019s system for speeding up immigration checks for travellers who have been approved by the government. After applying online and completing identity verification, travellers are added to a trusted traveller database. When they arrive at one of the participating airports, they can use dedicated fast track immigration lanes instead of the regular queues. At these lanes, their passport and biometric details are checked using automated systems, allowing them to clear immigration much faster than usual. It\u2019s designed to make international travel smoother for frequent travellers, families, and anyone who wants a more efficient arrival experience. It is available at 13 major international airports across India."}
{"id": "221273", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221273", "title": "Dinapigue", "text": "Dinapigue is a municipality on the Pacific coast of Isabela province in the northern part of the island of Luzon in the Philippines. Cut off from the rest of the country by the Sierra Madre mountains, it is one of the least explored areas of the province, but is being geared as its future beach tourism destination.\nUnderstand.\nFounded in 1968, Dinapigue sits on the southeastern corner of Isabela. It occupies the eastern slopes of the Sierra Madre, which separates it from the rest of the Cagayan Valley.\nDinapigue is among four municipalities of Isabela that have a coastline, the others being Palanan, Divilacan and Maconacon. It is the only one among the four that is connected to the Philippine highway network, albeit only through the neighboring province of Aurora. Its electricity needs also pass through the province. The municipality is also part of the Northern Sierra Madre National Park (NSMNP), encompassing the entirety of the mountain range within Isabela. \nThe people of Dinapigue are primarily engaged in farming, fishing and tourism, which coexist uneasily with an extensive mining project. They speak a variety of languages including Tagalog, Ilocano and Ibanag. The Dumagats also subsist on hunting, fishing and gathering from the forests.\nGet in.\nDinapigue has its own airstrip at the southern edge of downtown, which accommodates private and chartered flights using light, turboprop aircraft originating from Cauayan and Tuguegarao. \nDinapigue is the northern terminus of a coastal road that runs the length of neighboring Aurora province from its capital Baler up to Dilasag, the last town of Aurora before Dinapigue. There are a few buses and jeepneys connecting Dinapigue with Baler. There is also an infrequent bus service from Santiago City in Isabela, passing through Quirino and entering Aurora at Dinadiawan junction in Dipaculao. Travel time is 5-6 hours.\nGet around.\nTricycles and motorcycles are the main form of transport in the downtown. Hiring a boat or motorcycle is necessary to reach some destinations.\nDo.\nBeing inside the NSMNP, expect to see rare marine species on the coast and even rarer plant and animal species such as the pitcher plant while hiking inland.\nBuy.\nDue to erratic phone signals and the absence of banks and ATMs, always pay in cash. While there are small-scale stores, make sure to bring enough food, water, batteries and other necessary supplies whenever you travel in the area. The nearest market is in Dilasag, across the border in Aurora province.\nEat.\nFreshly-bought seafood can be bought on the beaches. \nSleep.\nThere are no large-scale hotels to speak of, just some homestays and inns.\nStay safe.\nCrime is minimal in town, as the few residents seem to know each other and there are few escape routes for offenders to escape. \nThere may be a few NPA rebels in the mountains and saltwater crocodiles swimming along the beaches.\nStay healthy.\nAvoid getting into a major medical emergency during your stay at all costs, as facilities and resources at the town's Community Hospital are limited. You will need to be taken to Aurora province at the very least for such an event.\nRespect.\nThe indigenous Dumagat communities still adhere to their precolonial customs and belief systems and are widely respected even by the non-Dumagat resident population. As they are the most experienced in dealing with the natural settings of Dinapigue, you are expected to exercise maximum respect for their traditions and when visiting them.\nConnect.\nThe town's postal code is 3336.\nCellphone and internet signals are often weak despite the presence of celltowers in the area."}
{"id": "221275", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221275", "title": "Itogon", "text": "Itogon is a municipality in the province of Benguet, Philippines.\nUnderstand.\nItogon is at the southeastern corner of Benguet, at its triboundary with Nueva Vizcaya and Pangasinan provinces. Situated at the upper reaches of the Agno River, it has historically been a mining town home to some of the country's biggest reserves of gold, copper and other precious metals. It is also undergoing a slow urbanization from neighboring Baguio, resulting in some attractions being mislabeled as being in Baguio itself. Despite this, Itogon, like the rest of the Cordilleras, is a tourist destination in its own right on account of its mountain landscapes alone.\nGet in.\nItogon is primarily connected to Baguio via the Baguio-Bua-Itogon Road, previously the only way in or out of town. It is also along Benguet-Nueva Vizcaya Road, but that highway is useful only if going to the northern barangays. Both jeepneys and passenger vans ply the said routes. Parts of Itogon bordering Baguio are also accessible by taxi.\nGet around.\nThere are tricycles in town, but a 4WD or any vehicle suitable for mountain roads is best used. Most attractions in town are accessible following extended hikes.\nDo.\nThere are several hot spring resorts in the downtown area (Poblacion) and in Barangay Dalupirip.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition.\nPermits are required from local authorities to undertake any hikes or climb mountains. The hiking season runs during the dry season from November to May. While Itogon is often known for its cool weather, do not underestimate the effects of heat and exhaustion while climbing, especially as many of the slopes are directly exposed to sunlight.\nConnect.\nThe town\u2019s postal code is 2604."}
{"id": "221276", "revid": "2441446", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221276", "title": "Xuancheng", "text": "Xuancheng is a city in Anhui.\nUnderstand.\nXuancheng (\u5ba3\u57ce) is a city in southeastern Anhui, near the borders with Jiangsu and Zhejiang. It has a long history and is known for its traditional culture, especially its connection with Chinese calligraphy and the Four Treasures of the Study. The city is also a gateway to the scenic areas of southern Anhui.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nXuancheng Railway Station is the main railway station for Xuancheng urban area, serving both high\u2011speed D/G trains and conventional trains. It offers frequent direct services to major Yangtze River Delta cities including Nanjing (~1\u202fhour), Shanghai (1.5\u20112\u202fhours), Hangzhou and Hefei.\nOther railway stations within Xuancheng\u2019s administrative area serve surrounding counties:\n- Jingxian Railway Station for Jing County, Chaji Ancient Village and Taohuatan\n- Jixi North Railway Station for Jixi County and Longchuan scenic site.\nCheck real\u2011time timetables via China Railway 12306 before departure.\nBy bus.\nXuancheng Central Bus Station operates intercity bus routes to cities across Anhui and neighbouring provinces such as Shanghai, Nanjing, Hangzhou and Suzhou. Buses also run to county\u2011level destinations inside Xuancheng municipality.\nBy plane.\nThere is no large\u2011scale airport in central Xuancheng.\n- Wuhu\u2011Xuanzhou Airport (WHU) is the closest airport, about 21\u202fkm from downtown Xuancheng; airport shuttle buses run between the airport and Xuancheng city centre.\n- Most international travellers fly into Nanjing Lukou International Airport (NKG) or Hangzhou Xiaoshan International Airport (HGH), then transfer to high\u2011speed trains to reach Xuancheng.\nBy car.\nMajor expressways link Xuancheng with Nanjing, Hangzhou and other cities. Self\u2011driving is a popular choice for tourists planning to visit scattered ancient villages and mountain sites across southern Anhui.\nGet around.\nXuancheng is a relatively compact city, and taxis and buses are the main ways to get around. Ride-hailing services are also available.\nDo.\nXuan paper making experience \u2013 Join a hands-on workshop in Jing County to learn traditional papermaking techniques and make your own piece of Xuan paper.\nAncient village hiking \u2013 Walk through Chaji or Longchuan village to explore Huizhou architecture, local life and countryside scenery.\nBuy.\nXuan paper \u2013 High-quality traditional Chinese painting and calligraphy paper, produced in Jing County of Xuancheng.\nXuan writing brush \u2013 One of China's famous traditional writing brushes, handmade with local materials.\nEat.\nXuancheng pot stickers \u2013 Local pan-fried dumplings with crispy bottoms, a popular breakfast and street snack.\nWater Town three treasures \u2013 A local specialty dish with duck feet, fish head and tofu, typical of Xuancheng home-style cuisine.\nDrink.\nHuangshan Maofeng tea \u2013 One of China's top green teas, commonly served in local restaurants and teahouses.\nSouthern Anhui rice wine \u2013 Traditional sweet rice wine, often paired with home-style village dishes.\nSleep.\nDowntown Xuancheng \u2013 Budget and mid-range business hotels near the railway station and city centre, convenient for transport.\nJing County / Chaji area \u2013 Homestays and boutique inns inside or near ancient villages, ideal for slow travel and scenery."}
{"id": "221280", "revid": "2282829", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221280", "title": "Tuba (Benguet)", "text": "\"For the city in Arizona, see Tuba.\"\nTuba is a municipality in the province of Benguet, Philippines.\nUnderstand.\nTuba is at the southwestern corner of Benguet, at its triboundary with La Union and Pangasinan provinces. It has long been dismissed as the penultimate stopover on the way to neighboring Baguio and is slowly undergoing urbanization from the latter city, resulting in some attractions being mislabeled as being in Baguio itself. Despite this, Tuba, like the rest of the Cordilleras, is a tourist destination in its own right on account of its mountain landscapes alone.\nGet in.\nTuba is along the main highways running to Baguio. Kennon Road, a highway built during the American period runs through town on its way from Rosario in La Union to Baguio. It is the shortest and most scenic route from Metro Manila, but is frequently subjected to accidents due to its narrow lanes and treacherous curves, and closures to natural disasters such as rockslides, especially during the rainy season. Further north, Marcos Highway (Aspiras\u2013Palispis Highway, Route 208) connects Baguio with Agoo in La Union, and passes through downtown Tuba. Most buses and private vehicles from Metro Manila ply this route, which is better-maintained and has wider lanes compared to Kennon Road.\nThere are also jeepneys and passenger vans going to Tuba from Baguio. Parts of the town bordering Baguio are also accessible by taxi.\nGet around.\nThere are tricycles in town, but a 4WD or any vehicle suitable for mountain roads is best used. Most attractions in town are accessible following extended hikes.\nDo.\nThere are multiple waterparks in town using either cold spring water or geothermically heated ones from the numerous hot springs scattered across town.\nBuy.\nThere are strawberry farms across town offering visitors a chance to harvest them directly and pay by the kilo.\nStay safe.\nAccidents and landslides are common along the mountain roads, with the danger compounded by low or nonexistent guard rails and fog. Ensure that your vehicle is in good condition.\nPermits are required from local authorities to undertake any hikes or climb mountains. The hiking season runs during the dry season from November to May. While Tuba is often known for its cool weather, do not underestimate the effects of heat and exhaustion while climbing, especially as many of the slopes are directly exposed to sunlight.\nStay healthy.\nAvoid unnecessary and prolonged exposure in the Bued River, which forms the pathway along Kennon Road, as the waterway is contaminated by pollution coming upstream from Baguio.\nConnect.\nThe town\u2019s postal code is 2603."}
{"id": "221294", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=221294", "title": "Coca (Ecuador)", "text": "Coca is in the Amazonian region of Ecuador.\nUnderstand.\nThe city serves as the main gateway in Ecuador for travellers heading into the Amazon rainforest.\nThe weather is hot and humid with heavy rainfall all year round. However, June to August are the rainiest months."}
